window.COURSE={"modules":[{"id":"foundations","title":"Foundations","lessons":[{"slug":"request-lifecycle","title":"How a Request Travels","subtitle":"Follow one page load from the Enter key to the last byte, and see which hops eat the time.","minutes":10,"level":"Beginner","learn":["What a DNS lookup does and when it is skipped","Why TCP and TLS setup cost round trips before any data moves","The path browser → load balancer → app server → database and back","Why distance and slow queries dominate page load time"],"nodes":[{"id":"browser","label":"Browser","kind":"client","x":10,"y":55},{"id":"dns","label":"DNS resolver","kind":"dns","x":30,"y":18},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":42,"y":60},{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":64,"y":60},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":86,"y":30},{"id":"cdn","label":"CDN edge","kind":"cdn","x":30,"y":88}],"edges":[{"from":"browser","to":"dns"},{"from":"browser","to":"lb"},{"from":"lb","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"db"},{"from":"browser","to":"cdn"}],"metrics":[{"id":"dns","label":"DNS","unit":"ms"},{"id":"connect","label":"Connect","unit":"ms"},{"id":"server","label":"Server","unit":"ms"},{"id":"total","label":"Total","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"You press Enter","body":"You type shop.example and press Enter. Roughly half a second later a page appears. That half second is not one thing: it is a chain of separate conversations between machines, and each one has to finish before the next can start. This lesson walks the chain in order and times each link.","idea":"A page load is a sequence of dependent waits; the total is their sum.","caption":"The browser knows a name, not an address.","focus":["browser"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"DNS","title":"Find the address","body":"Computers route by IP address, so the browser first asks a DNS resolver which address belongs to shop.example. If the answer is already cached in the browser, the OS, or the resolver, this costs almost nothing. A cold lookup can walk root, TLD, and authoritative servers and take tens of milliseconds.","idea":"DNS answers are cached according to their TTL; a warm cache makes this step nearly free.","caption":"Resolver returns an IP address.","focus":["browser","dns"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"dns","kind":"request","label":"A shop.example?"},{"from":"dns","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"203.0.113.7"}],"metrics":{"dns":"20","total":"20"}},{"tag":"CONNECT","title":"Open a secure connection","body":"Before sending the request the browser opens a TCP connection (one round trip) and negotiates TLS encryption (one more round trip with TLS 1.3). No page data moves yet. If the server is 50 ms away, that is already about 100 ms spent just agreeing to talk.","idea":"Handshakes cost round trips, so physical distance multiplies directly into latency.","caption":"TCP + TLS handshakes complete.","focus":["browser","lb"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"SYN / TLS hello"},{"from":"lb","to":"browser","kind":"handshake","label":"ACK / keys"}],"metrics":{"connect":"100","total":"120"}},{"tag":"REQUEST","title":"Send the request","body":"The browser sends GET / with headers and cookies. A load balancer receives it and forwards it to one healthy app server. The load balancer exists so any single server can fail or be replaced without the user noticing.","idea":"The load balancer hides how many servers exist and which one answers.","caption":"Request forwarded to an app server.","focus":["lb","app"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /"},{"from":"lb","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"GET /"}],"metrics":{"total":"145"}},{"tag":"SERVER","title":"Fetch the data","body":"The app server runs the handler, which usually needs data: the product list, the user's cart, prices. It queries the database. A well-indexed query returns in a few milliseconds; the handler then renders HTML or JSON from the rows.","idea":"Server time is application logic plus every dependency it waits on.","caption":"App queries the database.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT ..."},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"rows"}],"metrics":{"server":"40","total":"185"}},{"tag":"RESPONSE","title":"Bytes come back","body":"The HTML travels back through the load balancer to the browser. The first byte arriving (time to first byte, TTFB) is a common health metric. The browser starts parsing immediately, before the whole response has downloaded.","idea":"TTFB captures everything up to here: DNS, connect, and server time.","caption":"HTML streams back to the browser.","focus":["browser","lb","app"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"},{"from":"lb","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"HTML"}],"metrics":{"total":"235"}},{"tag":"ASSETS","title":"More files","body":"The HTML references CSS, JavaScript, fonts, and images. The browser fetches these too, usually from a CDN edge server close to the user, reusing connections and downloading several in parallel. Static files are cached aggressively so repeat visits skip most of this.","idea":"Serving static assets from the edge removes distance from most of the bytes.","caption":"Static assets arrive from a nearby edge.","focus":["browser","cdn"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"cdn","kind":"request","label":"app.css, app.js"},{"from":"cdn","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"cached files"}],"metrics":{"total":"310"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A slow query","body":"Suppose the product query scans the whole table instead of using an index and takes 900 ms. Every other step is unchanged, yet the page now takes over a second. One slow dependency on the critical path dominates the whole request, which is why you measure each hop instead of guessing.","idea":"The slowest hop on the critical path sets the user's experience.","caption":"The database is now the bottleneck.","focus":["db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"full scan"}],"metrics":{"server":"900","total":"1170"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A faraway user","body":"Now put the user on another continent, 150 ms away. Each round trip costs 150 ms, so the handshakes alone cost about 300 ms and the request another 150 ms. Code cannot fix the speed of light; moving servers or caches closer to users can.","idea":"Latency from distance is paid per round trip, so fewer round trips and closer servers both help.","caption":"Each round trip now costs 150 ms.","focus":["browser","lb"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"150 ms RTT"}],"metrics":{"connect":"300","total":"650"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Where the time went","body":"A request is DNS, connect, request, server work, response, and assets, in that order. Caching cuts DNS and asset time, connection reuse cuts handshakes, indexes cut server time, and CDNs or regional servers cut distance. To make a page faster, first find which hop is slow.","idea":"Measure each hop, then fix the biggest one.","caption":"Every hop added to the total.","focus":["browser","dns","lb","app","db","cdn"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"dns":"20","connect":"100","server":"40","total":"310"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A page's DNS lookup takes 0 ms on a repeat visit. What is the most likely reason?","options":["DNS is skipped for HTTPS sites","The answer was cached and its TTL had not expired","The CDN resolved it","The load balancer stores IP addresses"],"answer":1,"why":"Resolvers, the OS, and browsers cache DNS answers until their TTL expires, so repeat lookups are near-instant."},{"q":"The server is 80 ms away (round trip). Roughly how long do a fresh TCP + TLS 1.3 handshake take before the request can be sent?","options":["About 40 ms","About 80 ms","About 160 ms","About 320 ms"],"answer":2,"why":"TCP needs one round trip and TLS 1.3 needs one more, so about two round trips: 2 × 80 ms."},{"q":"TTFB jumps from 200 ms to 1.2 s after a deploy, but DNS and connect times are unchanged. Where should you look first?","options":["The CDN cache","DNS TTLs","Server-side work such as a slow database query","The user's browser"],"answer":2,"why":"If DNS and connection setup are unchanged, the added time is inside the server's handling, typically a slow dependency like a query."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"RTT","def":"Round-trip time: how long a message takes to reach a server and come back. Handshakes cost whole RTTs."},{"term":"TTFB","def":"Time to first byte: from sending the request until the first response byte arrives. Includes DNS, connect, and server time."},{"term":"TLS 1.3 handshake","def":"One round trip to agree on keys (zero with session resumption) before encrypted data can flow."},{"term":"Critical path","def":"The chain of steps that must finish in order; its slowest step bounds total latency."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"latency-throughput","title":"Latency & Throughput","subtitle":"Watch one service answer requests, then see why averages lie, tails hurt, and Little's Law predicts overload.","minutes":12,"level":"Beginner","learn":["The difference between latency, throughput, and bandwidth","Why p50 and p99 tell different stories and the average hides both","How fan-out turns a rare slow call into a common slow page","How to use Little's Law (L = λ × W) to size concurrency and spot overload"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Clients","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":30,"y":50},{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":53,"y":50},{"id":"cache","label":"Cache","kind":"cache","x":80,"y":20},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":80,"y":50},{"id":"search","label":"Search service","kind":"service","x":80,"y":80}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"lb"},{"from":"lb","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"cache"},{"from":"app","to":"db"},{"from":"app","to":"search"}],"metrics":[{"id":"p50","label":"p50 latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"p99","label":"p99 latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"rps","label":"Throughput","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"inflight","label":"In flight","unit":"reqs"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Fast, or fast enough?","body":"Your service feels quick when you test it alone. Then launch day comes, thousands of users arrive, and some of them wait several seconds. Nothing is broken. You are simply measuring the wrong thing, or only one of the several things that make a system feel fast.","idea":"Speed is not one number: how long one request takes and how many you can handle are different questions.","caption":"A service sits between many clients and its backends.","focus":["client","app"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Latency: one request's wait","body":"Latency is the time one request takes from being sent to being answered. Here a client call goes through the load balancer, the app checks the cache, and returns. Assume the whole trip takes 40 ms. Latency is measured per request, in milliseconds, and users feel it directly.","idea":"Latency is how long one request waits; lower is better and users feel it.","caption":"One request, 40 ms start to finish.","focus":["client","lb","app","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /item"},{"from":"lb","to":"app","kind":"request"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"get item"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"app","to":"lb","kind":"response"},{"from":"lb","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"p50":"40"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Throughput: requests per second","body":"Throughput is how many requests the system completes each second. If each request takes 40 ms and the app handles 20 at the same time, it finishes about 20 / 0.04 = 500 requests per second. Latency and throughput are related but separate: you can raise throughput by doing more work in parallel without making any single request faster.","idea":"Throughput is the count of completed requests per second; parallelism raises it without cutting latency.","caption":"Many requests flow through at once: 500 per second.","focus":["client","lb","app"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"500 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"20 at a time"},{"from":"app","to":"lb","kind":"response"},{"from":"lb","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"500 req/s"}],"metrics":{"rps":"500","inflight":"20"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Bandwidth is the pipe, not the speed","body":"Bandwidth is the maximum data a link can carry per second, such as 1 Gbit/s. It sets an upper bound on throughput for large responses, but a fat pipe does not shorten the trip. A 1 KB reply on a 10 Gbit/s link and on a 100 Mbit/s link both wait about the same 40 ms for the round trip. Bandwidth matters when the payload is big, latency when it is small.","idea":"Bandwidth is capacity, latency is delay; a wider pipe never makes a small message arrive sooner.","caption":"A wide pipe still waits for the round trip.","focus":["client","lb"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"1 KB, 40 ms"},{"from":"lb","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"1 KB reply"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Percentiles beat averages","body":"Latency varies from request to request, so one number cannot describe it. The p50 (median) is the value half of requests beat: 40 ms here. The p99 is the value 99% of requests beat, so 1 in 100 is slower: 400 ms here. An average would say about 44 ms and hide the slow ones entirely. Report percentiles, not means.","idea":"p50 shows the typical request, p99 shows the worst 1 in 100; the average hides both.","caption":"Most requests are fast; a few are ten times slower.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"99 fast"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"40 ms"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"1 slow"}],"metrics":{"p50":"40","p99":"400"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Why the tail exists","body":"Slow outliers have ordinary causes: a garbage collection pause, a noisy neighbour on the same host, a cold cache miss that hits the database, a retry after packet loss, or a lock held by another query. They are rare per request, but they never fully go away. The slow end of the distribution is called tail latency.","idea":"Tail latency comes from many small, random stalls; you manage it, you cannot eliminate it.","caption":"A cache miss sends one request down the slow path.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"get item"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"miss"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT ..."},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"400 ms"}],"metrics":{"p99":"400"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Fan-out multiplies the tail","body":"A single page often calls many backends. Suppose each call has a 1% chance of being slow, and a page waits on 20 of them in parallel. The chance that at least one is slow is 1 - 0.99^20, about 18%. So a backend's p99 becomes roughly your page's p82. The more calls per request, the more the tail dominates what users see.","idea":"With N parallel calls, the page is only as fast as its slowest one, so rare slowness becomes common.","caption":"The app waits on every backend; one slow reply delays all.","focus":["app","cache","db","search"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"call 1"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"call 2"},{"from":"app","to":"search","kind":"request","label":"call 3"},{"from":"search","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"slow reply"}],"metrics":{"p99":"400"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Little's Law","body":"Little's Law says the average number of requests in the system equals the arrival rate times the average time each spends there: L = λ × W. At 500 req/s and 0.04 s each, L = 500 × 0.04 = 20 requests in flight. It holds for any stable system and needs no assumptions about the distribution. Use it to size thread pools, connection pools, and queues.","idea":"In-flight requests = arrival rate × time in system, so latency and concurrency are linked.","caption":"500 req/s × 0.04 s = 20 requests in flight.","focus":["lb","app"],"flows":[{"from":"lb","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"λ = 500/s"},{"from":"app","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"W = 0.04 s"}],"metrics":{"rps":"500","inflight":"20","p50":"40"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Latency rises, the pool runs out","body":"Now the database slows and each request takes 400 ms. Same 500 req/s arrive, so L = 500 × 0.4 = 200 requests in flight. If the app has only 50 worker threads, 150 requests queue up, waiting even longer. Waiting makes W bigger, which makes L bigger: a feedback loop. Slowness spreads and can topple the service.","idea":"When W grows at a fixed arrival rate, concurrency explodes past capacity and queues feed on themselves.","caption":"200 needed in flight, only 50 slots: requests pile up.","focus":["lb","app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"lb","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"500 req/s"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"400 ms each"},{"from":"app","to":"lb","kind":"fail","label":"queue full"}],"metrics":{"p50":"400","p99":"2000","rps":"125","inflight":"200"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What to remember","body":"Latency is one request's wait, throughput is requests completed per second, and bandwidth is the pipe's capacity. Track p50 and p99, never only the average, and expect fan-out to make tails matter more. Use L = λ × W to check that your pools cover your latency. To protect the tail, add timeouts, cache hot data, and shed load before queues grow.","idea":"Measure percentiles, watch concurrency with Little's Law, and design for the tail.","caption":"Healthy service: fast median, bounded tail, enough slots.","focus":["client","lb","app","cache","db","search"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"p50":"40","p99":"400","rps":"500","inflight":"20"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A link is upgraded from 100 Mbit/s to 10 Gbit/s. Users loading a tiny 2 KB JSON response from a server 80 ms away barely notice. Why?","options":["Bandwidth only matters for uploads","The 80 ms round trip dominates; the payload was already tiny, so more capacity saves almost nothing","Faster links increase latency for small messages","JSON is compressed automatically so link speed is irrelevant"],"answer":1,"why":"Bandwidth is capacity. For a 2 KB reply the transfer time is a fraction of a millisecond; the round-trip delay is what the user waits on."},{"q":"Your dashboard shows average latency of 60 ms, but users complain about occasional multi-second hangs. What should you add first?","options":["A bigger average window","p95/p99 latency per endpoint","More bandwidth","A second load balancer"],"answer":1,"why":"The mean is dominated by the many fast requests and hides rare slow ones. Percentiles like p99 expose the tail users are complaining about."},{"q":"A page makes 50 parallel backend calls, each with a 99th-percentile latency of 300 ms and a typical latency of 30 ms. About how often will the page wait roughly 300 ms or more?","options":["About 1% of the time","About 5% of the time","About 40% of the time","Almost never"],"answer":2,"why":"The chance at least one of 50 calls is slow is 1 - 0.99^50, about 40%. Fan-out makes a backend's tail the page's common case."},{"q":"A service gets 200 requests per second and the average request spends 250 ms inside it. About how many requests are in flight on average?","options":["5","50","200","800"],"answer":1,"why":"Little's Law: L = λ × W = 200 × 0.25 = 50 requests in flight, so you need at least that many worker slots plus headroom."},{"q":"Traffic is steady at 300 req/s, but a dependency slows and your average time per request triples. With a fixed pool of workers, what happens?","options":["Throughput rises because workers stay busy","Nothing, because arrival rate did not change","Needed concurrency triples, so the pool saturates, queues form, and latency climbs further","Latency drops because fewer requests finish"],"answer":2,"why":"L = λ × W, so tripling W triples required concurrency. Once it exceeds the pool, requests queue, which raises W again and worsens the overload."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Latency","def":"Time for one request to complete, from send to response. Measured in milliseconds; lower is better."},{"term":"Throughput","def":"Requests (or bytes) completed per second. Raised by parallelism, caching, and efficiency, not only by faster single requests."},{"term":"Bandwidth","def":"Maximum data a link can move per second. It caps throughput for big payloads but does not reduce the round-trip delay."},{"term":"p50 / p95 / p99","def":"The value that 50% / 95% / 99% of requests beat. p50 is the typical case; p99 means 1 in 100 requests is slower."},{"term":"Tail latency","def":"The slow end of the latency distribution (p99 and beyond), caused by GC pauses, cache misses, retries, and contention."},{"term":"Fan-out amplification","def":"With N parallel calls, P(at least one slow) = 1 - (1 - p)^N. A 1% tail across 20 calls hits about 18% of pages."},{"term":"Little's Law","def":"L = λ × W: average in-flight requests = arrival rate × average time in system. Use it to size pools and queues."},{"term":"Defences for the tail","def":"Timeouts, hedged requests, caching hot data, bounded queues, and load shedding keep slow outliers from spreading."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"napkin-math","title":"Napkin Math","subtitle":"Turn 'ten million daily users' into requests per second, terabytes, and gigabits in a few lines of arithmetic.","minutes":12,"level":"Beginner","learn":["Convert daily active users into average and peak requests per second","Estimate how fast storage grows and what a year of data costs in space","Convert photo or video sizes into bandwidth, and avoid the bits-versus-bytes trap","Recall the latency numbers that separate memory, SSD, network, and distance","Use powers of two and round numbers to do all of this in your head"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"10M daily users","kind":"user","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"cdn","label":"CDN","kind":"cdn","x":35,"y":18},{"id":"api","label":"API servers","kind":"server","x":38,"y":58},{"id":"cache","label":"Cache","kind":"cache","x":88,"y":52},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":65,"y":28},{"id":"store","label":"Photo storage","kind":"storage","x":65,"y":80}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"cdn"},{"from":"users","to":"api"},{"from":"api","to":"cache"},{"from":"api","to":"db"},{"from":"api","to":"store"},{"from":"cdn","to":"store"}],"metrics":[{"id":"avg","label":"Average QPS","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"peak","label":"Peak QPS","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"storage","label":"Storage / year","unit":"TB"},{"id":"egress","label":"Egress","unit":"Gbps"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Will it fit on one machine?","body":"You are designing a photo-sharing app with 10 million daily active users (DAU). Before drawing any boxes, a good engineer asks: how many requests per second, how much storage, how much bandwidth? Napkin math answers in minutes, using rough numbers, and tells you whether you need one server or a fleet.","idea":"Estimate first: the answer decides the architecture, and being within 2x to 3x is good enough.","caption":"Ten million people, and we know nothing else yet.","focus":["users"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Round numbers are your friend","body":"A day has 86,400 seconds, which we round to about 100,000 (10^5). So 1 million requests per day is roughly 10 per second (really 11.6). Powers of two give the sizes: 2^10 is about a thousand (KB), 2^20 a million (MB), 2^30 a billion (GB), 2^40 a trillion (TB). Rounding errors of 10 to 20 percent do not matter here.","idea":"Memorise 86,400 s/day and the 2^10 ladder; most estimates then reduce to moving zeros.","caption":"We state assumptions: 100 photo views per user per day, 10% of users upload one photo.","focus":["users","api"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"100 views / day"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"From DAU to QPS","body":"Reads per day: 10M users x 100 views = 1 billion. Divide by about 86,400 seconds and you get roughly 12,000 reads per second on average. Uploads: 10% of users, one photo each, is 1M per day, about 12 writes per second. The read-to-write ratio is around 1000 to 1, which says this system should be built around fast reads.","idea":"QPS = (DAU x actions per user per day) / 86,400. The read/write ratio shapes the design.","caption":"About 12,000 reads per second hit the system on average.","focus":["users","api"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"12k reads/s"},{"from":"users","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"12 uploads/s"}],"metrics":{"avg":"12000"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Design for the peak","body":"Traffic is not flat. People look at photos in the evening, so the busiest hour can be several times the average. A common rule of thumb is peak = 2x to 5x average; we use 3x, giving about 36,000 reads per second. Servers must survive the peak, not the average, or the app falls over exactly when the most people are watching.","idea":"Capacity is sized for peak load, so multiply the average by a peak factor (a rule of thumb, often 2x to 5x).","caption":"Peak is about 36,000 reads per second.","focus":["users","api"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"36k reads/s peak"}],"metrics":{"peak":"36000"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Storage grows every day","body":"One million uploads a day at about 2 MB each is 2 TB per day. Over 365 days that is roughly 730 TB, close to three quarters of a petabyte, before replication. Keeping 3 copies for durability makes it about 2.2 PB. Notice that thumbnails and metadata are tiny by comparison: the raw photos dominate the bill.","idea":"Storage per year = writes per day x size per item x 365, then multiply by the replication factor.","caption":"Uploads pile up in object storage at 2 TB per day.","focus":["api","store"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"2 MB photo"}],"metrics":{"storage":"730"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Bandwidth: watch bits and bytes","body":"Each view serves a resized image of about 200 KB. A billion views a day is 200 TB, or about 2.3 GB per second. Network links are quoted in bits, so multiply by 8: roughly 18 Gbps on average and 55 Gbps at peak. That is far too much for the origin, which is why a CDN serves most of these bytes from the edge.","idea":"Bandwidth = QPS x bytes per response. Multiply bytes by 8 to compare with link speeds in bits.","caption":"About 18 Gbps leaves the system, mostly served from CDN caches.","focus":["users","cdn","store"],"flows":[{"from":"store","to":"cdn","kind":"response","label":"cache misses"},{"from":"cdn","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"200 KB images"}],"metrics":{"egress":"18"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Latency numbers to remember","body":"Rough orders of magnitude: reading from memory takes about 100 nanoseconds, an SSD random read about 100 microseconds, a round trip inside one data center about 0.5 ms, a spinning-disk seek about 10 ms, and a round trip across continents about 150 ms. Each step is roughly 10 to 1000 times slower than the last, so avoid slow tiers on the hot path.","idea":"Memory is about 1000x faster than SSD, and a cross-continent round trip is about 300x slower than one inside a data center.","caption":"A cache lookup is cheaper than a database or storage read.","focus":["api","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"~0.5 ms"},{"from":"cache","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"api","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"~5 ms miss"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Turning numbers into decisions","body":"Suppose one API server handles about 2,000 requests per second. Peak 36,000 needs 18 servers, so plan around 25 for headroom. A single database comfortably serves a few thousand simple reads per second, say 5,000. To keep misses under that, the cache must hit at least 85 percent of the time: 15 percent of 36,000 is 5,400, so aim for 90 percent or more.","idea":"Divide the estimate by what one machine can do; the quotient tells you how many machines or what cache hit rate you need.","caption":"The cache absorbs reads so the database sees only misses.","focus":["api","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"36k lookups/s"},{"from":"api","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"~3.6k misses/s"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"What if they were videos?","body":"Change one assumption: each upload is a 50 MB video instead of a 2 MB photo. Storage becomes 1M x 50 MB = 50 TB per day, about 18,000 TB (18 PB) per year, 25 times more. Nothing else in the estimate moved, yet the cost and design of the storage layer changed completely. One input can dominate everything, so check the big ones.","idea":"Find the assumption that moves the result most and sanity-check that one first.","caption":"Same upload rate, 25x the bytes written.","focus":["api","store"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"50 MB video"}],"metrics":{"storage":"18250"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The napkin recipe","body":"State assumptions, then convert: DAU to QPS by dividing by 86,400, average to peak with a 3x factor, items to storage by multiplying size and days, responses to bandwidth in bits. Compare each result with what one machine does and with the latency ladder. Rough answers within a factor of 2 to 3 are enough to choose a design.","idea":"Write assumptions down, round aggressively, and use the results to choose between one box and a fleet.","caption":"Our photo app: 12k QPS, 36k peak, 730 TB a year, 18 Gbps.","focus":["users","cdn","api","cache","db","store"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"avg":"12000","peak":"36000","storage":"730","egress":"18"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"An app has 5 million DAU, and each user makes 20 requests a day. What is the average QPS, roughly?","options":["About 12","About 120","About 1,200","About 12,000"],"answer":2,"why":"5M x 20 = 100M requests per day. Dividing by about 100,000 seconds per day gives about 1,000, or 1,160 with 86,400. Peak would be several times that."},{"q":"300,000 uploads a day at 5 MB each, kept for one year with no replication. About how much storage is that?","options":["About 1.5 TB","About 55 TB","About 5.5 PB","About 550 TB"],"answer":3,"why":"300,000 x 5 MB = 1.5 TB per day. Times 365 is about 550 TB. Replication would multiply that further."},{"q":"You serve 1.2 GB per second on average and your network link is 10 Gbps. What does the napkin math say?","options":["It barely fits on average and cannot handle peak, since 1.2 GB/s is about 9.6 Gbps","It fits easily, since 1.2 is far smaller than 10","It fits only if you compress to bits","It is fine as long as peak is under 2x"],"answer":0,"why":"Link speeds are in bits: 1.2 GB/s x 8 = 9.6 Gbps, already about 96 percent of the link. Any peak above average would saturate it."},{"q":"Peak reads are 30,000 per second and your database safely handles 5,000. About what cache hit rate do you need at minimum?","options":["70 percent","85 percent","50 percent","99.9 percent"],"answer":1,"why":"The database may see at most 5,000 of 30,000 requests, which is about 17 percent misses, so a hit rate of roughly 83 percent. 85 percent meets it; 70 percent leaves 9,000 misses, and 50 percent leaves 15,000."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Seconds per day","def":"86,400, rounded to about 100,000 (10^5). So 1 million events per day is about 12 per second."},{"term":"Powers of two","def":"2^10 is about 1 thousand (KB), 2^20 about 1 million (MB), 2^30 about 1 billion (GB), 2^40 about 1 trillion (TB), 2^50 about 1 quadrillion (PB)."},{"term":"QPS formula","def":"Average QPS = DAU x actions per user per day / 86,400. Peak QPS = average x 2 to 5 (rule of thumb; 3 is a common default)."},{"term":"Storage formula","def":"Per year = items per day x bytes per item x 365. Multiply by the replication factor, usually 3, for the real footprint."},{"term":"Bandwidth","def":"QPS x bytes per response gives bytes per second. Multiply by 8 for bits per second, which is how network links are rated."},{"term":"Latency ladder","def":"Memory about 100 ns, SSD read about 100 us, same-data-center round trip about 0.5 ms, disk seek about 10 ms, cross-continent round trip about 150 ms."},{"term":"Capacity check","def":"Divide peak load by what one machine handles to get a server count, then add headroom. Do the same for a database against cache miss rate."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"dns","title":"DNS in Depth","subtitle":"Watch a cold DNS lookup walk from resolver to root, TLD and authoritative server, then see how TTLs, GeoDNS and failover bend the answer.","minutes":12,"level":"Beginner","learn":["The difference between a recursive resolver and an authoritative server","How root, TLD and authoritative servers cooperate to answer one question","What A, AAAA, CNAME, MX and TXT records are for","How TTL and caching trade speed against how fast changes spread","How GeoDNS and DNS-based failover steer users, and where they lag"],"nodes":[{"id":"browser","label":"Browser + OS","kind":"client","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"resolver","label":"Recursive resolver","kind":"dns","x":33,"y":50},{"id":"root","label":"Root servers","kind":"dns","x":58,"y":12},{"id":"tld","label":".com TLD servers","kind":"dns","x":82,"y":12},{"id":"auth","label":"Authoritative NS","kind":"dns","x":84,"y":42},{"id":"us","label":"Origin US","kind":"server","x":60,"y":78},{"id":"eu","label":"Origin EU","kind":"server","x":86,"y":78}],"edges":[{"from":"browser","to":"resolver"},{"from":"resolver","to":"root"},{"from":"resolver","to":"tld"},{"from":"resolver","to":"auth"},{"from":"browser","to":"us"},{"from":"browser","to":"eu"},{"from":"auth","to":"us"},{"from":"auth","to":"eu"}],"metrics":[{"id":"lookup","label":"Lookup time","unit":"ms"},{"id":"hops","label":"Servers asked","unit":""},{"id":"ttl","label":"TTL left","unit":"s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Names are not addresses","body":"Your browser wants shop.example, but networks route packets to IP addresses. Something must translate the name into a number, and no single computer holds every name on the internet. DNS solves this with a hierarchy of servers that each know a small slice, plus layers of caching so most lookups never travel far.","idea":"DNS is a distributed, cached, hierarchical phone book, not one big database.","caption":"The browser has a name and needs an IP address.","focus":["browser"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Ask the recursive resolver","body":"The OS hands the question to a recursive resolver, usually run by your ISP or a public provider. Its job is to do the whole chase for you and return one final answer. It keeps a cache, so if anyone nearby asked recently, it answers instantly. Here the cache is cold, so it has to start from the top.","idea":"A recursive resolver does the legwork on behalf of clients and remembers the results.","caption":"Browser asks the resolver; cache miss.","focus":["browser","resolver"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"resolver","kind":"request","label":"A shop.example?"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"2","hops":"1"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Root: who runs .com?","body":"The resolver already knows the addresses of the 13 root server identities (hundreds of anycast machines behind them). It asks one. The root does not know shop.example, but it knows who is responsible for each top-level domain, so it replies with a referral: here are the servers for .com.","idea":"The root only points to the right TLD servers; it never stores individual sites.","caption":"Root refers the resolver to the .com servers.","focus":["resolver","root"],"flows":[{"from":"resolver","to":"root","kind":"request","label":"A shop.example?"},{"from":"root","to":"resolver","kind":"response","label":"ask .com TLD"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"22","hops":"2"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"TLD: who runs shop.example?","body":"The resolver repeats the question to a .com TLD server. The TLD servers hold one thing for every registered domain: its NS records, naming the servers the owner chose. They reply with another referral to the domain's own name servers. Still no IP address yet, just a closer signpost.","idea":"The TLD tier maps each registered domain to its authoritative name servers.","caption":"TLD refers the resolver to shop.example's servers.","focus":["resolver","tld"],"flows":[{"from":"resolver","to":"tld","kind":"request","label":"A shop.example?"},{"from":"tld","to":"resolver","kind":"response","label":"ask ns1.shop"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"44","hops":"3"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Authoritative: the real answer","body":"An authoritative server holds the actual records for the zone, set by the domain owner. It answers with the A record 203.0.113.7 and a TTL of 300 seconds. The resolver caches it, returns it to the browser, and the whole cold lookup took about four network exchanges, roughly 70 ms in this example.","idea":"Only the authoritative server is the source of truth; everything else is a cached copy.","caption":"Authoritative server answers; resolver caches and replies.","focus":["resolver","auth","browser"],"flows":[{"from":"resolver","to":"auth","kind":"request","label":"A shop.example?"},{"from":"auth","to":"resolver","kind":"response","label":"203.0.113.7"},{"from":"resolver","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"203.0.113.7"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"70","hops":"4","ttl":"300"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Record types","body":"The zone holds more than one kind of record. A maps a name to an IPv4 address and AAAA to IPv6. CNAME says one name is an alias for another, so www can follow shop.example. MX names the mail servers. TXT holds free text, used for domain verification and email policy such as SPF. A CNAME cannot sit at the zone apex.","idea":"Pick the record type by purpose: address (A, AAAA), alias (CNAME), mail (MX), metadata (TXT).","caption":"Resolver asks for www and follows a CNAME to an A record.","focus":["resolver","auth"],"flows":[{"from":"resolver","to":"auth","kind":"request","label":"www CNAME?"},{"from":"auth","to":"resolver","kind":"response","label":"CNAME + A"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CACHE","title":"TTL and caching","body":"Two seconds later another user behind the same resolver asks for shop.example. The resolver answers from cache in about 2 ms, no root, TLD or authoritative traffic needed. Each cached answer carries a TTL that counts down; at zero it is discarded and re-fetched. Browsers and operating systems cache too, so the real lookup count is far lower.","idea":"A longer TTL means faster lookups and less load, but slower propagation of any change you make.","caption":"Warm cache: one hop, answer in about 2 ms.","focus":["browser","resolver"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"resolver","kind":"request","label":"A shop.example?"},{"from":"resolver","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"cached, 298s"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"2","hops":"1","ttl":"298"}},{"tag":"GEODNS","title":"GeoDNS: different answers by place","body":"The owner runs one site in the US and one in Europe. A GeoDNS authoritative server looks at who is asking, usually the resolver's IP or a client-subnet hint, and returns the nearest origin. A resolver in Frankfurt gets the EU address, so the user's later connections travel a few ms instead of 90 ms. It is a rough guess: a user on a distant public resolver can be steered wrongly.","idea":"GeoDNS picks the answer by the asker's location, which is only as accurate as the resolver tells it.","caption":"Authoritative returns the EU address; browser connects there.","focus":["resolver","auth","eu","browser"],"flows":[{"from":"resolver","to":"auth","kind":"request","label":"A (from Frankfurt)"},{"from":"auth","to":"resolver","kind":"response","label":"EU IP"},{"from":"browser","to":"eu","kind":"request","label":"GET /"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"60","hops":"4","ttl":"60"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"DNS-based failover","body":"Now the EU origin dies. The authoritative server health-checks both origins, notices, and stops returning the EU address. But resolvers and browsers still hold the old answer until its TTL expires. With a 60 s TTL, some users keep hitting a dead server for up to a minute or more, since some clients ignore TTLs. That is why failover-critical records use short TTLs.","idea":"DNS failover works, but it is only as fast as the TTL, so short TTLs buy faster recovery at the cost of more lookups.","caption":"Health check fails; clients with cached answers still hit the dead origin.","focus":["auth","eu","browser"],"flows":[{"from":"auth","to":"eu","kind":"fail","label":"health check"},{"from":"browser","to":"eu","kind":"fail","label":"cached IP, timeout"},{"from":"browser","to":"us","kind":"request","label":"retry new IP"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"60","ttl":"60"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What to remember","body":"A recursive resolver chases the answer through root, TLD and authoritative servers, then caches it. Records express different facts: addresses, aliases, mail, metadata. TTL is the dial between speed and agility. GeoDNS and failover both build on this, and both are limited by cached answers you cannot recall.","idea":"DNS is a cached hierarchy; every clever trick on top inherits the TTL delay.","caption":"The full lookup path and the origins it steers to.","focus":["browser","resolver","root","tld","auth","us","eu"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"lookup":"70","hops":"4","ttl":"300"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A resolver has no cached data for shop.example. What is the order of servers it contacts?","options":["Authoritative, then TLD, then root","Root, then TLD, then authoritative","TLD, then root, then authoritative","Root, then authoritative, skipping the TLD"],"answer":1,"why":"The root points to the .com TLD servers, which point to the domain's authoritative servers, which hold the actual records."},{"q":"You must move your site to a new IP tomorrow at noon. Your A record has a 24-hour TTL. What should you do beforehand?","options":["Nothing; DNS updates instantly worldwide","Raise the TTL so resolvers cache the new value longer","Lower the TTL to a few minutes well in advance, then change the IP","Switch the record to a TXT record"],"answer":2,"why":"Caches keep the old answer until the TTL expires, so the TTL must be lowered at least one old-TTL period before the change."},{"q":"You want example.com (the zone apex) to alias to a provider hostname, and also receive mail. Which is the real problem?","options":["MX records cannot point to hostnames","TXT records block aliases","CNAME records only work for IPv6","A CNAME cannot coexist with other records such as MX and NS at the apex"],"answer":3,"why":"A CNAME means the name is entirely an alias, so no other record types may exist at that name; apex names need NS and SOA records."},{"q":"Your EU origin fails and DNS failover removes it, yet some users still see errors for a minute. Most likely cause?","options":["Clients and resolvers are using cached answers until the TTL expires","The authoritative server has not noticed","GeoDNS cannot return US addresses","Root servers are overloaded"],"answer":0,"why":"DNS changes only reach users as cached copies expire, so the TTL sets a floor on failover time for those clients."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Recursive resolver","def":"Server that answers clients by chasing the lookup through root, TLD and authoritative servers, and caches results."},{"term":"Authoritative server","def":"Holds the real records for a zone. Its answers are the source of truth; all other copies are caches."},{"term":"Root / TLD","def":"Root refers to TLD servers (.com, .org); TLD servers refer to each domain's name servers via NS records."},{"term":"A / AAAA","def":"Map a name to an IPv4 / IPv6 address. A name can have several, which gives crude load spreading."},{"term":"CNAME","def":"Makes a name an alias of another name. Cannot share a name with other records, so not allowed at the zone apex."},{"term":"MX / TXT","def":"MX lists mail servers with priorities. TXT holds text for SPF, DKIM, DMARC and domain-ownership checks."},{"term":"TTL","def":"Seconds a cached answer may be reused. Typical values: 60 s for failover records, 300 to 3600 s for stable ones."},{"term":"GeoDNS / failover","def":"Authoritative server varies answers by asker location or origin health. Limited by resolver accuracy and TTL lag."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"http-tls","title":"HTTP, TLS & Connections","subtitle":"Load the same 31-file page over HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 and watch round trips and blocking change the clock.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why a new connection costs round trips, and what TLS 1.3 saves","How keep-alive and connection pools avoid repeating that cost","What head-of-line blocking is, at the HTTP layer and at the TCP layer","How HTTP/2 multiplexing and HTTP/3 over QUIC each remove one kind of blocking"],"nodes":[{"id":"browser","label":"Browser","kind":"client","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"edge","label":"Edge proxy (TLS)","kind":"gateway","x":40,"y":50},{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":74,"y":24},{"id":"api","label":"Auth service","kind":"service","x":74,"y":76}],"edges":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","label":"50 ms RTT"},{"from":"edge","to":"app"},{"from":"edge","to":"api"}],"metrics":[{"id":"setup","label":"Setup RTTs","unit":"RTT"},{"id":"conns","label":"Connections","unit":""},{"id":"load","label":"Page load","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"31 files, one slow wire","body":"A page needs one HTML file plus 30 small assets (CSS, scripts, images). The user is 50 ms away by round trip, and we will ignore download time so only round trips and waiting matter. The question: how should the browser and server talk so those 31 requests finish fast? Each protocol answers differently, and the numbers show why.","idea":"With small files, the cost is mostly round trips, not bandwidth.","caption":"One browser, one edge proxy, 31 resources to fetch.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"setup":"0","conns":"0","load":"0"}},{"tag":"CONNECT","title":"A new connection is not free","body":"Before any HTTP byte moves, the browser opens a TCP connection: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK, which costs one round trip. Then TLS encrypts it. With TLS 1.3 that is one more round trip, so the first request cannot leave until about 100 ms have passed. Older TLS 1.2 needed two round trips for TLS, making it 150 ms.","idea":"TCP plus TLS 1.3 costs 2 RTTs before the first request; TLS 1.2 costs 3.","caption":"Two round trips of setup, zero page data.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"SYN"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"handshake","label":"SYN-ACK"},{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"ACK"}],"metrics":{"setup":"2","conns":"1"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Inside the TLS 1.3 handshake","body":"In TLS 1.3 the client's first message already carries its key-exchange share. The server replies with its share, its certificate and a Finished message, and both sides can derive keys. The client then sends its own Finished together with the first HTTP request. On a repeat visit, a resumption ticket can allow 0-RTT early data, but that data can be replayed by an attacker, so it should carry only safe, idempotent requests.","idea":"TLS 1.3 needs one round trip; resumption can skip it but only for replay-safe requests.","caption":"Keys agreed in a single round trip.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"ClientHello+key"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"handshake","label":"ServerHello+cert"},{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"Finished + GET /"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"KEEP-ALIVE","title":"HTTP/1.1 reuses the connection","body":"HTTP/1.1 keeps the TCP connection open after a response (keep-alive), so later requests skip the handshakes. But a connection carries one request at a time: the next request waits for the previous response. With 31 requests at 50 ms each on one connection, the page needs about 100 ms setup plus 31 x 50 ms, roughly 1650 ms.","idea":"Keep-alive removes repeated handshakes, but HTTP/1.1 still handles one request per connection at a time.","caption":"31 requests queue one after another.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"GET /app.css"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"200 app.css"},{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"GET /app.js"}],"metrics":{"conns":"1","load":"1650"}},{"tag":"HOL BLOCKING","title":"Head-of-line blocking and the six-connection hack","body":"Head-of-line blocking means one slow item holds up everything queued behind it. On an HTTP/1.1 connection, a 2-second API call stalls every request after it, because responses must come back in order. Browsers work around it by opening about six parallel connections per host. That gives roughly 100 ms setup plus 5 rounds of 50 ms, so about 500 ms, at the price of six handshakes and six sets of server memory.","idea":"HTTP/1.1 fights head-of-line blocking by multiplying connections, which is costly.","caption":"Six connections share the 31 requests.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"6 connections"},{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"6 requests at once"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"6 responses"}],"metrics":{"conns":"6","load":"500"}},{"tag":"POOLING","title":"Pools behind the proxy","body":"The same idea applies server side. The edge proxy keeps a pool of already-open, already-encrypted connections to the app server and the auth service, and borrows one per request instead of dialing fresh. Inside a data center a handshake is only about 0.5 ms, but at thousands of requests per second it still burns CPU and can exhaust ports. Pools need a size limit and an idle timeout shorter than the server's, or you get requests sent on connections the server already closed.","idea":"A connection pool pays the setup cost once and shares it; size it and expire it deliberately.","caption":"Proxy reuses warm connections to backends.","focus":["edge","app","api"],"flows":[{"from":"edge","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"reused conn"},{"from":"edge","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"reused conn"},{"from":"app","to":"edge","kind":"response","label":"200"},{"from":"api","to":"edge","kind":"response","label":"200"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"HTTP/2","title":"HTTP/2 multiplexes streams","body":"HTTP/2 splits traffic into small frames tagged with a stream ID, so many requests and responses interleave on one TCP connection. The browser sends all 30 asset requests at once without waiting. Cost: 100 ms setup, 50 ms for the HTML, 50 ms for the burst of assets, about 200 ms, using a single connection and header compression. This fixes head-of-line blocking at the HTTP layer.","idea":"Multiplexing lets one connection carry many independent requests at the same time.","caption":"31 streams interleave on one connection.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"30 streams"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"interleaved frames"}],"metrics":{"conns":"1","load":"200"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"One lost packet stalls every stream","body":"HTTP/2 still rides on TCP, which delivers bytes strictly in order. If one packet is lost, TCP holds back everything after it, including frames for streams that were not affected, until the retransmission arrives. On a clean wired network this is rare, but on lossy mobile links with 1 to 2 percent loss, HTTP/2 can end up slower than six HTTP/1.1 connections. Time here is illustrative: roughly one extra round trip per stall.","idea":"HTTP/2 removes blocking in HTTP, but TCP's in-order delivery brings it back at the transport layer.","caption":"A dropped packet freezes all streams.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"fail","label":"packet lost"},{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"dup ACKs"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"replicate","label":"retransmit"}],"metrics":{"load":"300"}},{"tag":"HTTP/3","title":"HTTP/3 and QUIC","body":"HTTP/3 runs over QUIC, which uses UDP and builds reliability, encryption and streams into one protocol. Transport and TLS 1.3 handshakes are combined, so setup takes 1 round trip instead of 2, and resumption can send data in the first flight. Each stream recovers losses on its own, so a lost packet only delays its own stream. QUIC connections are identified by an ID, so they survive a phone switching from Wi-Fi to cellular. Browsers learn about HTTP/3 from an Alt-Svc header or DNS record and fall back to TCP if UDP is blocked.","idea":"QUIC cuts setup to 1 RTT and gives each stream independent loss recovery.","caption":"One-round-trip setup, streams recover separately.","focus":["browser","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"QUIC Initial+TLS"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"handshake","label":"keys + cert"},{"from":"browser","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"30 streams"},{"from":"edge","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"independent"}],"metrics":{"setup":"1","conns":"1","load":"150"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Fewer round trips, less blocking","body":"Every new connection costs round trips, so reuse them: keep-alive on the client side, pools behind the proxy. HTTP/1.1 does one request at a time per connection, HTTP/2 multiplexes streams over one TCP connection, and HTTP/3 moves to QUIC to remove TCP-level blocking and shave a round trip. TLS 1.3 is the baseline for all of them. The numbers here are idealized, but the ordering holds in practice.","idea":"Reuse connections, multiplex requests, and avoid a single in-order pipe shared by unrelated streams.","caption":"Same page: 1650 ms, 500 ms, 200 ms, 150 ms.","focus":["browser","edge","app","api"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"setup":"1","conns":"1","load":"150"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A server is 40 ms away (round trip). How long before the first HTTP request can be sent on a brand-new TCP + TLS 1.3 connection?","options":["About 40 ms","About 80 ms","About 120 ms","About 160 ms"],"answer":1,"why":"TCP needs one round trip and TLS 1.3 needs one more, so 2 x 40 ms = 80 ms. TLS 1.2 would add a third round trip."},{"q":"Why does HTTP/1.1 keep-alive alone not make a 30-asset page fast?","options":["Keep-alive only works for HTML","Each connection still handles one request at a time, so requests queue","Keep-alive disables encryption","The server closes the connection after each response"],"answer":1,"why":"Keep-alive removes repeated handshakes, but responses on a connection come back in order, so requests wait on each other."},{"q":"On a lossy mobile network, one packet is dropped during an HTTP/2 page load. What happens?","options":["Only the stream owning that packet waits","HTTP/2 automatically opens a second connection","All streams on the TCP connection stall until the retransmission arrives","The browser switches to HTTP/1.1 immediately"],"answer":2,"why":"TCP delivers bytes in order, so a gap blocks everything behind it, including unrelated streams. QUIC avoids this with per-stream recovery."},{"q":"Your proxy sometimes fails requests with 'connection reset' right after sitting idle. The app server closes idle connections after 60 s. What is the best fix?","options":["Disable the connection pool","Set the proxy's pool idle timeout below 60 s","Switch the proxy to HTTP/1.0","Raise the app server's timeout to 24 hours"],"answer":1,"why":"If the proxy holds a pooled connection longer than the server does, it can send on a connection already closed. Expiring idle connections first avoids that race."},{"q":"Which statement about HTTP/3 is correct?","options":["It runs over TCP with a faster handshake","It runs over QUIC on UDP and recovers lost packets per stream","It removes the need for TLS","It requires one connection per request"],"answer":1,"why":"HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP, with TLS 1.3 built in and independent loss recovery per stream."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Handshake cost","def":"TCP 1 RTT + TLS 1.3 1 RTT = 2 RTTs before the first request. TLS 1.2 adds a third. QUIC combines both into 1 RTT."},{"term":"Keep-alive","def":"Leaving a connection open for more requests, so later ones skip TCP and TLS setup. Default in HTTP/1.1."},{"term":"Head-of-line blocking","def":"A slow or lost item delays everything queued behind it. It occurs at the HTTP layer (1.1) and the TCP layer (2)."},{"term":"HTTP/2 multiplexing","def":"Many request/response streams interleaved as frames on one TCP connection, with header compression (HPACK)."},{"term":"HTTP/3 / QUIC","def":"HTTP over UDP-based QUIC: built-in TLS 1.3, per-stream loss recovery, connection IDs that survive network changes."},{"term":"Connection pool","def":"A set of warm connections reused across requests. Tune max size and set idle timeout below the peer's timeout."},{"term":"0-RTT resumption","def":"Sending data in the first flight on a repeat visit. Replayable by attackers, so use only for idempotent requests."},{"term":"Alt-Svc","def":"Header (or DNS HTTPS record) telling clients that HTTP/3 is available; clients fall back to TCP if UDP fails."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"hld-lld","title":"High-Level vs Low-Level Design","subtitle":"Walk a design from vague idea to boxes, API, data model and one component's internals, using a link shortener.","minutes":12,"level":"Beginner","learn":["The order a design process follows: requirements, estimates, high-level boxes, API, data model, deep dive","How a back-of-envelope estimate shapes the architecture before you draw anything","What high-level design (HLD) decides versus what low-level design (LLD) decides","How to design one component in detail, including its failure case","Why every added box (cache, queue) is a trade-off you should be able to defend"],"nodes":[{"id":"user","label":"Users","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"gw","label":"API gateway","kind":"gateway","x":29,"y":50},{"id":"svc","label":"Link service","kind":"service","x":52,"y":50},{"id":"cache","label":"Cache","kind":"cache","x":76,"y":20},{"id":"db","label":"Link database","kind":"db","x":76,"y":50},{"id":"q","label":"Click queue","kind":"queue","x":52,"y":84},{"id":"worker","label":"Click counter","kind":"worker","x":80,"y":84}],"edges":[{"from":"user","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"svc"},{"from":"svc","to":"cache"},{"from":"svc","to":"db"},{"from":"svc","to":"q"},{"from":"q","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"writes","label":"Writes","unit":"/sec"},{"id":"reads","label":"Reads","unit":"/sec"},{"id":"storage","label":"5-yr storage","unit":"TB"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"\"Design a link shortener\"","body":"You are handed one sentence: build something like a short-link service. That is not yet a design, because it does not say how big it is, who uses it, or what matters most. A good process turns the vague sentence into decisions in a fixed order, starting wide (high-level design, HLD) and ending narrow (low-level design, LLD). We will follow that order.","idea":"Design is narrowing: broad shape first, details only after the shape is agreed.","caption":"One vague sentence, no boxes yet.","focus":["user"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"REQUIREMENTS","title":"Ask before you draw","body":"Start by asking questions. Functional requirements say what it does: create a short link, redirect when someone visits it. Non-functional requirements say how well: redirects must feel instant, links must not break, the service should survive a server failure. Also agree on what is out of scope, such as custom domains or accounts, so you do not design forever.","idea":"Requirements decide the design; unstated requirements become surprises later.","caption":"Scope agreed with the person asking.","focus":["user","gw"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"scope?"},{"from":"gw","to":"user","kind":"response","label":"create + redirect"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"ESTIMATES","title":"Size it with napkin math","body":"Assume 100 million new links a month and 100 redirects for every link created. That is about 40 writes per second and about 4,000 reads per second on average, perhaps 12,000 at peak. At roughly 500 bytes per link, five years is around 3 TB. These are rule-of-thumb numbers, but they already tell you the system is read-heavy and fits on a small database cluster.","idea":"A few minutes of arithmetic tells you what to optimize: here, reads, not storage.","caption":"Read-heavy, modest storage.","focus":["svc","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"writes":"40","reads":"4000","storage":"3"}},{"tag":"HLD","title":"Draw the boxes","body":"Now sketch the high-level design: clients talk to an API gateway, which forwards to a stateless link service, which stores links in a database. Keep it to boxes and arrows. HLD answers which major components exist and how they talk, not how any one of them works inside. You can add boxes later when the numbers demand it.","idea":"HLD is the map: components and connections, with no internals yet.","caption":"Minimum viable architecture: gateway, service, database.","focus":["user","gw","svc","db"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"request"},{"from":"gw","to":"svc","kind":"request","label":"route"},{"from":"svc","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"read/write"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"API","title":"Define the contract","body":"Next, write the API, because it is the contract between boxes. POST /links takes a long URL and returns a short code. GET /{code} looks the code up and answers 301 or 302 with the long URL in the Location header. Choosing 302 keeps every click visible to you, while 301 lets browsers cache the redirect and skip you next time.","idea":"Fix the interfaces early; they are hard to change once clients depend on them.","caption":"Create a link, then follow it.","focus":["user","gw","svc"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"POST /links"},{"from":"gw","to":"svc","kind":"request","label":"create"},{"from":"svc","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"code: aB3xK9"},{"from":"gw","to":"user","kind":"response","label":"201 Created"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"DATA MODEL","title":"Decide what is stored","body":"The data model lists what you store and how you look it up. Here a link has a code, a long URL, a creation time and an owner. Every read asks one question, give me the URL for this code, so the code is the primary key and a simple key-value lookup is enough. Knowing the access pattern first is what lets you pick a sensible database.","idea":"Choose storage from the access pattern, not from habit.","caption":"Single-key lookups: code to long URL.","focus":["svc","db"],"flows":[{"from":"svc","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"put aB3xK9"},{"from":"db","to":"svc","kind":"response","label":"stored"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"LLD","title":"Zoom into one component","body":"Low-level design opens one box. Take code generation inside the link service. Seven characters from a 62-letter alphabet give about 3.5 trillion codes, far more than 6 billion links. If the service picks random codes, two links can collide, so it inserts only if the code is absent and retries on a collision. LLD is classes, algorithms and edge cases like this.","idea":"LLD is the inside of a box: algorithms, data structures and the awkward edge cases.","caption":"Collision detected, retry with a new code.","focus":["svc","db"],"flows":[{"from":"svc","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"insert if absent"},{"from":"db","to":"svc","kind":"fail","label":"code taken"},{"from":"svc","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"retry new code"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Add a cache, pay for it","body":"At 4,000 reads per second, most traffic hits the same popular links, so a cache in front of the database absorbs most of it and makes redirects faster. The price is complexity: cached data can be stale, and you must decide what happens when a link is deleted. Say that aloud. A box without a stated cost is a guess, not a design.","idea":"Every added component buys something and costs something; name both.","caption":"Hot links served from the cache.","focus":["svc","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"svc","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET aB3xK9"},{"from":"cache","to":"svc","kind":"response","label":"hit: long URL"}],"metrics":{"reads":"4000"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Count clicks off the hot path","body":"Owners want click counts. Writing a counter to the database on every redirect would slow the fast path and add 4,000 writes per second. Instead the service drops an event on a queue and returns the redirect immediately. A worker reads the queue and updates counts in batches. The cost is that counts are a few seconds behind, which the requirements allow.","idea":"Move non-urgent work to a queue so the user-facing path stays fast.","caption":"Redirect first, count later.","focus":["svc","q","worker","db"],"flows":[{"from":"svc","to":"q","kind":"async","label":"click event"},{"from":"q","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"batch"},{"from":"worker","to":"db","kind":"async","label":"add counts"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Wide first, then deep","body":"The order was requirements, estimates, HLD boxes, API, data model, a deep dive into one component, then trade-offs. HLD decides what the parts are and how they connect. LLD decides how one part works inside. Later lessons cover numbers, caching and queues in depth; here the point is the sequence and defending each choice with a reason.","idea":"Decide the shape first, the details second, and justify every box.","caption":"The finished design, built in layers.","focus":["user","gw","svc","cache","db","q","worker"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"writes":"40","reads":"4000","storage":"3"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do you do rough estimates before drawing the architecture?","options":["Interviewers require numbers on the whiteboard","They show what the system must optimize for, such as read-heavy traffic","They let you skip asking requirements questions","They fix the exact database vendor"],"answer":1,"why":"Estimates reveal the shape of the load (read-heavy, small storage), which decides which components are worth adding. They do not replace requirements or pick vendors."},{"q":"Which question belongs to low-level design rather than high-level design?","options":["Should we put a cache between the service and the database?","Which services does the gateway route to?","How does the code generator avoid or handle two links getting the same code?","Is the system read-heavy or write-heavy?"],"answer":2,"why":"Handling collisions is an algorithm inside one component. The other options are about which components exist or the overall load shape."},{"q":"A teammate adds a cache to the design but says nothing else. What is the most important thing missing?","options":["The cache's brand name","A diagram with colors","What it costs: staleness, invalidation and extra failure modes","A second cache for backup"],"answer":2,"why":"Every component is a trade-off. A cache speeds reads but can serve stale data, so a good design states when it may be wrong and how that is handled."},{"q":"Click counts may lag by a few seconds. How does that requirement shape the design?","options":["Counting can go through a queue, keeping redirects fast","Counts must be written to the database before redirecting","Counting needs a second database region","Redirects should use 301 instead of 302"],"answer":0,"why":"Because slightly late counts are acceptable, the service can enqueue an event and respond at once. A strict real-time requirement would push you toward synchronous writes."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Functional requirement","def":"What the system does, such as create a short link or redirect to the long URL."},{"term":"Non-functional requirement","def":"How well it does it: latency, availability, durability, scale, consistency."},{"term":"HLD","def":"High-level design: the major components, how they connect, and what each is responsible for. No internals."},{"term":"LLD","def":"Low-level design: the inside of one component, its classes, algorithms, data structures and edge cases."},{"term":"Access pattern","def":"How the data is read and written. Decide this before choosing a database or schema."},{"term":"Order of attack","def":"Requirements, estimates, HLD boxes, API, data model, deep dive on one part, then trade-offs and failure cases."},{"term":"Trade-off","def":"Every component gains something and costs something. State both, plus when your choice would flip."}],"isNew":false}]},{"id":"scaling","title":"Scaling","lessons":[{"slug":"vertical-horizontal","title":"Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling","subtitle":"Watch one overloaded server get fixed two ways, a bigger box or more boxes, and see what each choice costs.","minutes":10,"level":"Beginner","learn":["The difference between scaling up (vertical) and scaling out (horizontal)","Why a single big machine has both a hard ceiling and a single point of failure","How adding servers behind a load balancer raises capacity and survives failures","Why cost per unit of capacity often rises at the top of the instance range","When a bigger box is the right and simplest answer, especially for databases"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Users","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":32,"y":50},{"id":"s1","label":"Server 1 (4 vCPU)","kind":"server","x":57,"y":14},{"id":"s2","label":"Server 2 (4 vCPU)","kind":"server","x":57,"y":36},{"id":"s3","label":"Server 3 (4 vCPU)","kind":"server","x":57,"y":58},{"id":"big","label":"Big server (16 vCPU)","kind":"server","x":57,"y":84},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"lb"},{"from":"users","to":"big"},{"from":"users","to":"s1"},{"from":"lb","to":"s1"},{"from":"lb","to":"s2"},{"from":"lb","to":"s3"},{"from":"s1","to":"db"},{"from":"s2","to":"db"},{"from":"s3","to":"db"},{"from":"big","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"cap","label":"Capacity","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"cpu","label":"Peak CPU","unit":"%"},{"id":"cost","label":"App cost","unit":"$/mo"},{"id":"avail","label":"Availability","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One server is running hot","body":"Your shop runs on one 4 vCPU server that can handle about 800 requests per second (an assumption for this lesson). Traffic has doubled and now peaks at 680 requests per second, so the CPU sits at 85 percent. One more good week and requests will queue and time out. You need more capacity. There are two basic moves.","idea":"Capacity problems have two fixes: make the machine bigger, or add more machines.","caption":"Peak load is close to the server's limit.","focus":["users","s1"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"s1","kind":"request","label":"680 req/s"},{"from":"s1","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"}],"metrics":{"cap":"800","cpu":"85","cost":"150","avail":"99.9"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Scale up: a bigger box","body":"Vertical scaling means replacing the server with a larger one: more CPU cores, more RAM, faster disks. Here you move from 4 to 16 vCPU. Capacity rises roughly four times, to about 2,800 requests per second (a bit less than 4x because of contention). Cloud prices in this range grow about in line with size, so cost goes to about $600 a month. Your code does not change at all.","idea":"Scaling up is the simplest fix because the application stays exactly the same.","caption":"Traffic now goes to one much larger server.","focus":["users","big","db"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"big","kind":"request","label":"680 req/s"},{"from":"big","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"}],"metrics":{"cap":"2800","cpu":"24","cost":"600","avail":"99.9"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"The limits of one machine","body":"Keep scaling up and you hit walls. The largest instances top out at a few hundred cores, and software often stops using extra cores well (locks and shared memory limit the gain). Prices also bend upward near the top, since rare hardware costs more per core. Resizing usually needs a restart, and the one box is still a single point of failure: if it dies, the site is down.","idea":"Scale up has a hard ceiling, rising unit cost at the top, and no protection from failure.","caption":"Even the big server is one failure away from an outage.","focus":["users","big"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"big","kind":"fail","label":"box crashed"}],"metrics":{"cap":"9000","cpu":"8","cost":"2400","avail":"99.9"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Scale out: more boxes","body":"Horizontal scaling adds more machines of ordinary size and spreads the work across them. Here a load balancer fronts three 4 vCPU servers. Capacity is about 2,400 requests per second for roughly $450 of servers plus about $20 for the load balancer. Adding a fourth box later adds another 800, so growth is in small, cheap, reversible steps. The catch: your app must work when requests land on different servers.","idea":"Scale out grows capacity in small steps by adding ordinary machines behind a load balancer.","caption":"The load balancer now fronts three servers.","focus":["users","lb","s1","s2","s3"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"680 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"s1","kind":"request"},{"from":"lb","to":"s2","kind":"request"},{"from":"lb","to":"s3","kind":"request"}],"metrics":{"cap":"2400","cpu":"28","cost":"470","avail":"99.9"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Work is shared","body":"Each request lands on one of the three servers, so each carries about 227 requests per second, around 28 percent CPU. Every server talks to the same database, which is where shared data lives. For this to work, servers must not keep user state in local memory, or the next request may land somewhere that does not know the user. That design (statelessness) and the balancing rules are covered in their own lessons.","idea":"Scale out works when any server can handle any request, so state must live outside the servers.","caption":"All three servers read and write one database.","focus":["lb","s1","s2","s3","db"],"flows":[{"from":"s1","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"},{"from":"s2","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"},{"from":"s3","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"},{"from":"db","to":"s1","kind":"response"},{"from":"db","to":"s2","kind":"response"},{"from":"db","to":"s3","kind":"response"}],"metrics":{"cap":"2400","cpu":"28"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"One server dies","body":"Now server 2 crashes. The load balancer notices through health checks and stops sending it traffic. The remaining two servers handle about 1,600 requests per second, well above the 680 you actually have, so users see nothing except maybe a few failed requests during detection. Spare capacity beyond peak, often called N+1, turns a hardware failure into a non-event. The single big server could not do this.","idea":"Many small servers survive failures; spare capacity is what makes that survival real.","caption":"Server 2 fails, the other two absorb its traffic.","focus":["lb","s1","s2","s3"],"flows":[{"from":"lb","to":"s2","kind":"fail","label":"unhealthy"},{"from":"lb","to":"s1","kind":"request","label":"340 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"s3","kind":"request","label":"340 req/s"}],"metrics":{"cap":"1600","cpu":"43","avail":"99.99"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"What scale out costs you","body":"Horizontal scaling moves complexity from hardware into software and operations. You need a load balancer, health checks, deployment across many machines, shared session storage, and monitoring of a fleet. Data is the hardest part: three app servers can all be cloned, but they still share one database, which now carries three servers' worth of queries. Scaling data out (replicas, sharding) is much harder than scaling stateless app code.","idea":"Scaling out adds operational and design complexity, and shared data stores become the next bottleneck.","caption":"Three servers now funnel into one database.","focus":["s1","s2","s3","db"],"flows":[{"from":"s1","to":"db","kind":"request"},{"from":"s2","to":"db","kind":"request"},{"from":"s3","to":"db","kind":"request"},{"from":"db","to":"s2","kind":"fail","label":"connections full"}],"metrics":{"cap":"2400","cpu":"28"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"When the bigger box wins","body":"A relational database is the classic case. It keeps data consistent in one place, and splitting it across machines is hard, so the usual first move is a larger instance with more RAM to keep hot data in memory. Small products also win with one box: at 680 requests per second, two 16 vCPU servers or one big one are simpler than a fleet. Scale up first while it is cheap and easy, then scale out when you hit the ceiling or need availability.","idea":"Scale up first for databases and small systems; scale out when you need failure tolerance or outgrow one box.","caption":"A larger database absorbs the load without redesign.","focus":["big","db"],"flows":[{"from":"big","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"queries"},{"from":"db","to":"big","kind":"response","label":"rows from RAM"}],"metrics":{"cap":"2800","cpu":"24","cost":"600"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick by the constraint","body":"Scaling up is simple and needs no code change, but it has a ceiling, rising cost at the top, and one point of failure. Scaling out adds capacity in small steps and survives failures, but requires stateless services, a load balancer, and a plan for shared data. Most real systems do both: scale the database up, and scale the stateless app tier out.","idea":"Scale up for simplicity, scale out for headroom and resilience, and combine them.","caption":"Both paths shown: one big box and a pool of servers.","focus":["users","lb","s1","s2","s3","big","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"cap":"2400","cpu":"28","cost":"470","avail":"99.99"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which statement best describes the main weakness of scaling up a single server, even a very large one?","options":["It always requires rewriting the application","It has a hard ceiling and remains a single point of failure","It cannot use more than 4 CPU cores","It makes the database slower by definition"],"answer":1,"why":"A bigger box needs no code change, but there is a largest machine you can buy, and if that one machine fails everything it serves goes down."},{"q":"You run three servers, each at 75 percent CPU at peak. One fails. What is the most likely result?","options":["Nothing changes because load balancers add capacity","The other two run near 90 percent, which is tight but workable","The other two need about 112 percent each, so they overload and requests fail","The failed server's traffic is automatically dropped"],"answer":2,"why":"Three servers at 75 percent carry 2.25 servers' worth of work. Two servers can only supply 2.0, so they cannot keep up. This is why N+1 headroom matters: size the fleet so peak load still fits after losing one."},{"q":"Your stateless web tier is at its limit, and your single database is at 40 percent CPU with room to spare. What is the sensible first move?","options":["Shard the database across four servers","Add more web servers behind the load balancer","Replace the web tier with one very large server","Switch the database to a different vendor"],"answer":1,"why":"The web tier is the bottleneck and is stateless, so adding servers is cheap and low risk. The database has headroom, so sharding it would add heavy complexity for no benefit."},{"q":"A team wants to scale a primary relational database for the next year with minimal risk. Why is a larger instance often a good first choice?","options":["Databases cannot run on more than one machine ever","It keeps data in one place with no new consistency problems and needs no application changes","Bigger instances are always cheaper per core","Scaling out is impossible in the cloud"],"answer":1,"why":"A single primary avoids splitting data and the coordination problems that follow. Scaling up is often the simplest path until you outgrow it, though replicas and sharding exist for later."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Vertical scaling (scale up)","def":"Make one machine bigger with more CPU, RAM, or faster disks. Simple, no code change, but has a ceiling and one point of failure."},{"term":"Horizontal scaling (scale out)","def":"Add more machines and spread work across them. Grows in small steps and tolerates failures, but needs stateless design and a load balancer."},{"term":"Single point of failure","def":"A component whose failure takes the whole system down. One big server is the typical example."},{"term":"N+1 headroom","def":"Keep enough spare capacity that losing any one server still leaves you above peak load. A rule of thumb, not a guarantee."},{"term":"Cost curve","def":"Mid-range cloud instances cost roughly linearly with size, but the very largest cost more per core and gains flatten."},{"term":"Default order of operations","def":"Scale up first while it is cheap and simple. Scale out the stateless tier when you need headroom or resilience. Scale databases out last."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"stateless","title":"Stateless Services","subtitle":"Watch a login break when a server dies, then see how moving session state out of the servers fixes it.","minutes":9,"level":"Beginner","learn":["What state a server holds between requests and why that blocks scaling","Why round-robin load balancing logs users out of stateful servers","How sticky sessions work and what they cost you","Two ways to go stateless: a shared session store and signed tokens","Why stateless servers can be added, removed or killed freely"],"nodes":[{"id":"browser","label":"Browser","kind":"user","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":32,"y":50},{"id":"appa","label":"App server A","kind":"server","x":57,"y":22},{"id":"appb","label":"App server B","kind":"server","x":57,"y":78},{"id":"sessions","label":"Session store","kind":"cache","x":84,"y":22},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":84,"y":78}],"edges":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb"},{"from":"lb","to":"appa"},{"from":"lb","to":"appb"},{"from":"appa","to":"sessions"},{"from":"appb","to":"sessions"},{"from":"appa","to":"db"},{"from":"appb","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"servers","label":"App servers","unit":"up"},{"id":"failed","label":"Users re-login","unit":"%"},{"id":"lookup","label":"Session read","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Why did my login vanish?","body":"You log in to a shop and fill your cart. A minute later the site shows the login page again, cart gone. Nothing was wrong with your password. Behind the scenes, the server that knew you was replaced, or a different server answered. This lesson is about what that server was remembering, and where it should have kept it instead.","idea":"If a request only works on the one server that handled the last request, scaling out and failing over both break.","caption":"Two identical servers sit behind a load balancer.","focus":["browser","lb","appa","appb"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart"},{"from":"lb","to":"appa","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart"}],"metrics":{"servers":"2","failed":"0"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"State lives in server memory","body":"HTTP forgets you between requests, so apps keep a session: a small record such as user 42, cart with 3 items, stored under a random ID. The browser holds only the ID in a cookie. In the simplest design the record sits in the memory of the server that handled the login. That server is now stateful: it holds data that the next request depends on.","idea":"A stateful server remembers something between requests that no other server knows.","caption":"Login lands on A, so the session exists only in A's RAM.","focus":["browser","lb","appa"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"POST /login"},{"from":"lb","to":"appa","kind":"request","label":"POST /login"},{"from":"appa","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"Set-Cookie sid=9f2"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"Round robin hits the wrong server","body":"The load balancer spreads requests evenly, so the next click goes to server B. B receives the cookie sid=9f2 but has never seen that ID, so it treats you as logged out. With two servers about half your requests miss; with ten servers about nine in ten do. Adding capacity made the site worse, which is the opposite of what scaling should do.","idea":"Per-server state makes more servers mean more cache misses on your own users.","caption":"Server B has no record of sid=9f2 and rejects the request.","focus":["lb","appb"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart sid=9f2"},{"from":"lb","to":"appb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart"},{"from":"appb","to":"lb","kind":"fail","label":"401 login again"}],"metrics":{"failed":"50"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Sticky sessions: a patch","body":"The cheap fix is stickiness: the load balancer remembers that sid=9f2 belongs to A, usually through a cookie or a hash of the client IP, and always routes you there. It works, but you pay in other ways. Load can skew when a few heavy users pile onto one server. A deploy must drain each server slowly. And if A crashes, every user pinned to A loses their session.","idea":"Sticky sessions hide the problem but keep it: the server still holds the only copy, so its failure loses data.","caption":"Pinned to A, requests work, until A dies and takes its sessions along.","focus":["lb","appa"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart sid=9f2"},{"from":"lb","to":"appa","kind":"request","label":"pinned to A"},{"from":"lb","to":"appa","kind":"fail","label":"A crashed"}],"metrics":{"failed":"50"}},{"tag":"FIX","title":"Move sessions to a shared store","body":"Instead of RAM, any app server writes the session to a fast shared store such as Redis or Memcached, keyed by the session ID. On login, server A saves the record there with an expiry, say 30 minutes since last use. The store is a separate component that all servers talk to, so the app servers themselves no longer hold anything the others lack.","idea":"Externalize the state: the servers keep logic, and a shared store keeps memory.","caption":"A writes the session to the shared store at login.","focus":["appa","sessions"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"sessions","kind":"request","label":"SET sess:9f2 30m"},{"from":"sessions","to":"appa","kind":"response","label":"OK"}],"metrics":{"failed":"0","lookup":"1"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Any server can answer","body":"Now the next click can land on B. B reads the cookie, fetches sess:9f2 from the store in roughly 1 ms on the same network, finds user 42, then queries the database for the cart. The load balancer can use plain round robin or least connections, with no pinning. The price is one extra network hop per request, plus a new component you must keep highly available, usually with a replica and automatic failover.","idea":"Stateless servers cost one cheap lookup per request and gain free routing.","caption":"B looks up the session in the store and serves the cart.","focus":["lb","appb","sessions","db"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart sid=9f2"},{"from":"lb","to":"appb","kind":"request","label":"any server"},{"from":"appb","to":"sessions","kind":"request","label":"GET sess:9f2"},{"from":"sessions","to":"appb","kind":"response","label":"user 42"},{"from":"appb","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT cart"},{"from":"db","to":"appb","kind":"response","label":"3 items"},{"from":"appb","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"200 cart"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"1"}},{"tag":"ALTERNATIVE","title":"Signed tokens skip the lookup","body":"Another route is to put the session data in the client. After login the server issues a signed token, often a JWT, holding user ID and expiry, signed with a secret key all servers share. Any server verifies the signature in microseconds and needs no store at all. The cost: you cannot easily revoke one token before it expires, so keep tokens short-lived (say 15 minutes) with a refresh mechanism, and never put secrets inside them.","idea":"Tokens trade revocability and size for zero server-side session storage.","caption":"B verifies the token's signature itself; no session store is read.","focus":["browser","lb","appb","db"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"Bearer eyJ..."},{"from":"lb","to":"appb","kind":"request","label":"verify signature"},{"from":"appb","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT cart"},{"from":"db","to":"appb","kind":"response","label":"3 items"}],"metrics":{"lookup":"0"}},{"tag":"PAYOFF","title":"Kill a server, nobody notices","body":"With no state on the servers, they become interchangeable. If A crashes, the load balancer's health check marks it down and sends everything to B; users keep their sessions. You can deploy by replacing servers one at a time, and autoscale by adding or removing them at will. This is what lets you treat servers as disposable, which the next lessons on load balancing and autoscaling build on.","idea":"Stateless servers can be added, removed, or killed at any time with no user impact.","caption":"A fails its health check; B carries the traffic and sessions survive.","focus":["lb","appa","appb","sessions"],"flows":[{"from":"lb","to":"appa","kind":"fail","label":"health check fail"},{"from":"browser","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart sid=9f2"},{"from":"lb","to":"appb","kind":"request","label":"GET /cart"},{"from":"appb","to":"sessions","kind":"request","label":"GET sess:9f2"},{"from":"appb","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"200 cart"}],"metrics":{"servers":"1","failed":"0","lookup":"1"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Keep state out of the servers","body":"State held in a server's memory pins users to it and is lost when it dies. Sticky sessions patch the routing but not the loss. A shared session store or signed tokens move the state elsewhere, so any server can answer any request. The state did not vanish: it now lives in a store you must make durable and fast. Stateless means the app tier is disposable, not that the system has no data.","idea":"Stateless app servers scale and fail over easily; the state moves to a small number of well-protected places.","caption":"Servers hold logic only; sessions and data live in shared stores.","focus":["browser","lb","appa","appb","sessions","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"servers":"2","failed":"0","lookup":"1"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"You run 5 stateful servers with in-memory sessions and plain round robin. Roughly what share of a logged-in user's requests land on a server that does not know them?","options":["About 0%, round robin remembers users","About 20%","About 80%","About 100%"],"answer":2,"why":"Only the server that handled the login has the session. Each later request picks one of 5 servers evenly, so 4 in 5 (80%) miss."},{"q":"A team adds sticky sessions and calls the problem solved. Which failure still loses users' logins?","options":["A user opens the site in a second tab","The load balancer adds a sixth server","The server holding a user's session crashes","The user refreshes the page"],"answer":2,"why":"Stickiness only controls routing. The session still exists in one server's memory, so if that server dies or is replaced the session is gone."},{"q":"Which statement about signed tokens (JWTs) versus a shared session store is correct?","options":["Tokens avoid a per-request lookup but are harder to revoke before expiry","Tokens are always more secure because the client holds them","A session store requires sticky sessions to work","Tokens let the server change a user's data without the client noticing"],"answer":0,"why":"A signed token is verified locally with no store read, but the server holds no record to delete, so early revocation needs extra machinery such as short lifetimes or a deny list."},{"q":"After moving sessions to Redis, the app servers are stateless. What new risk have you taken on?","options":["Servers can no longer be restarted","The session store is now a shared dependency that must be fast and highly available","Load balancing must become sticky","Cookies no longer work"],"answer":1,"why":"State did not disappear, it moved. If the session store is down or slow, every server is affected, so it needs replication, failover, and monitoring."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Stateful server","def":"Keeps data between requests (such as a session in RAM) that other servers lack, so requests must reach that same server."},{"term":"Stateless server","def":"Holds nothing a later request depends on; everything needed arrives with the request or comes from a shared store."},{"term":"Sticky session","def":"Load balancer pins a user to one server via cookie or IP hash. Easy, but skews load, slows deploys, and loses sessions on a crash."},{"term":"Shared session store","def":"Redis or Memcached keyed by session ID with a TTL. Costs about 1 ms per request and must be replicated for availability."},{"term":"Signed token (JWT)","def":"Client-held claims signed with a key. No lookup needed, but hard to revoke, so use short expiry plus refresh tokens."},{"term":"Disposable servers","def":"The payoff of statelessness: autoscale, rolling deploys, and failover all work by freely adding or removing app servers."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"load-balancing","title":"Load Balancing","subtitle":"Watch a load balancer spread traffic across servers, pick the right one, drop a dead one, and survive its own failure.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["The difference between Layer 4 and Layer 7 load balancing, and when each fits","How round robin, least connections, and consistent hashing choose a server, and where each one goes wrong","How health checks detect a dead server and how long detection really takes","Why the load balancer itself needs a standby, and how failover works"],"nodes":[{"id":"clients","label":"Clients","kind":"client","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"lb1","label":"LB (active)","kind":"lb","x":40,"y":30},{"id":"lb2","label":"LB (standby)","kind":"lb","x":40,"y":76},{"id":"a","label":"Server A","kind":"server","x":74,"y":14},{"id":"b","label":"Server B","kind":"server","x":74,"y":50},{"id":"c","label":"Server C","kind":"server","x":74,"y":86}],"edges":[{"from":"clients","to":"lb1"},{"from":"clients","to":"lb2"},{"from":"lb1","to":"lb2","label":"heartbeat"},{"from":"lb1","to":"a"},{"from":"lb1","to":"b"},{"from":"lb1","to":"c"},{"from":"lb2","to":"a"},{"from":"lb2","to":"b"},{"from":"lb2","to":"c"}],"metrics":[{"id":"ca","label":"Conns A","unit":""},{"id":"cb","label":"Conns B","unit":""},{"id":"cc","label":"Conns C","unit":""},{"id":"healthy","label":"Healthy","unit":"of 3"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One server is not enough","body":"Your API gets about 6,000 requests per second at peak, but one server tops out near 2,500. You run three servers, yet clients only know one address. Something must sit in front, accept every connection, and decide which server handles it. That something is a load balancer, and it also decides what happens when a server dies.","idea":"A load balancer gives many servers one front door and chooses who answers each request.","caption":"Three servers, one entry point, and a choice to make.","focus":["clients","lb1"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"ca":"0","cb":"0","cc":"0","healthy":"3"}},{"tag":"LAYER 4","title":"L4: route by connection","body":"A Layer 4 balancer works on TCP or UDP. It sees addresses and ports, picks a server when the connection opens, and then passes bytes through without reading them. It is very fast and protocol-agnostic, so it suits databases, gRPC streams, and raw TLS. The catch: it cannot see URLs, headers, or cookies.","idea":"L4 decides once per connection using only IPs and ports, so it is fast but blind to content.","caption":"A TCP connection is pinned to one server.","focus":["clients","lb1","a"],"flows":[{"from":"clients","to":"lb1","kind":"handshake","label":"TCP SYN :443"},{"from":"lb1","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"same TCP stream"}],"metrics":{"ca":"1"}},{"tag":"LAYER 7","title":"L7: route by request","body":"A Layer 7 balancer terminates the connection, reads the HTTP request, and routes on what it finds: path, host, header, or cookie. So /api can go to one pool and /images to another, and it can retry a failed request on a different server. It costs more CPU per request, since it parses HTTP and often decrypts TLS.","idea":"L7 understands requests, enabling smart routing and retries, at the price of more work per request.","caption":"The path /api/orders picks the API pool.","focus":["clients","lb1","b"],"flows":[{"from":"clients","to":"lb1","kind":"request","label":"GET /api/orders"},{"from":"lb1","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"to API pool"},{"from":"b","to":"lb1","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"},{"from":"lb1","to":"clients","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"ca":"1","cb":"1"}},{"tag":"ALGORITHM","title":"Round robin: take turns","body":"The simplest policy sends request 1 to A, request 2 to B, request 3 to C, then starts over. It needs no state beyond a counter and works well when requests cost about the same and servers are equal. It breaks down when a few requests are very heavy, because the rotation keeps sending new work to a server that is already buried.","idea":"Round robin is fair in count, not in load, so it assumes similar requests and similar servers.","caption":"Requests rotate A, B, C evenly.","focus":["lb1","a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"lb1","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"req 1"},{"from":"lb1","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"req 2"},{"from":"lb1","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"req 3"}],"metrics":{"ca":"10","cb":"10","cc":"10"}},{"tag":"ALGORITHM","title":"Least connections: pick the idlest","body":"Now Server B is stuck on slow report exports and holds 48 open connections, while A has 12 and C has 9. Least connections sends the next request to the server with the fewest active ones, here C. Because it reacts to real load, it copes with uneven request cost far better than round robin. Weights can handle servers of different sizes.","idea":"Least connections follows actual load, so slow requests naturally steer new work elsewhere.","caption":"New request goes to C, the least busy.","focus":["lb1","a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"lb1","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"fewest conns"}],"metrics":{"ca":"12","cb":"48","cc":"10"}},{"tag":"ALGORITHM","title":"Consistent hash: same key, same server","body":"Sometimes you want a given user or cache key to land on the same server every time, for example because that server holds a warm in-memory cache. The balancer hashes the key and maps it onto a ring of servers. If a server is added or removed, only about 1/N of the keys move, instead of nearly all of them as with plain hash modulo N.","idea":"Consistent hashing gives stickiness by key while keeping reshuffling small when the pool changes.","caption":"User 42 always hashes to Server B.","focus":["lb1","b"],"flows":[{"from":"lb1","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"hash(user 42)"},{"from":"lb1","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"hash(user 42)"}],"metrics":{"ca":"12","cb":"49","cc":"10"}},{"tag":"HEALTH","title":"Health checks find the dead","body":"Server C crashes. The balancer probes every server on a schedule, for example GET /healthz every 5 seconds, and marks a server out after 3 straight failures. That means up to roughly 15 seconds of bad traffic before removal. Passive checks help: if real requests to C time out, the balancer counts those too and can react sooner.","idea":"Detection takes time: probe interval times failure threshold sets how long users may hit a dead server.","caption":"Probes to C time out and C is pulled.","focus":["lb1","c"],"flows":[{"from":"lb1","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"GET /healthz"},{"from":"c","to":"lb1","kind":"fail","label":"timeout x3"}],"metrics":{"cc":"0","healthy":"2"}},{"tag":"FAILOVER","title":"Traffic reroutes around C","body":"With C out of rotation, the balancer spreads load over A and B only. Each now carries about 3,000 requests per second at peak, above the 2,500 each can sustain, so this is where headroom matters. A common rule of thumb is to size the pool so it survives losing one server (N+1), and the balancer keeps probing C so it rejoins once it passes again.","idea":"Removing a server shifts its load to the rest, so capacity planning must leave room for failure.","caption":"Only A and B receive requests now.","focus":["lb1","a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"lb1","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"req"},{"from":"lb1","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"req"}],"metrics":{"ca":"20","cb":"30","cc":"0","healthy":"2"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The load balancer dies","body":"You removed one single point of failure and created another: if the only balancer dies, everything is down. So you run two. The active one sends heartbeats to a standby; when they stop for a few seconds, the standby takes over the shared virtual IP (for example with VRRP) and clients reconnect to the same address. Managed cloud balancers do this across zones for you.","idea":"Run the load balancer as a redundant pair or managed multi-zone service so it is not the weak link.","caption":"Heartbeat stops, standby takes the traffic.","focus":["lb1","lb2","clients"],"flows":[{"from":"lb1","to":"lb2","kind":"fail","label":"heartbeat lost"},{"from":"clients","to":"lb2","kind":"request","label":"same virtual IP"},{"from":"lb2","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"req"}],"metrics":{"ca":"21","cb":"30"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What to remember","body":"L4 balances connections quickly; L7 balances requests intelligently. Round robin suits uniform work, least connections suits uneven work, and consistent hashing suits key-based affinity. Health checks remove dead servers after a delay you can tune, and the balancer needs its own redundancy. Keep spare capacity so a lost server does not overload the survivors.","idea":"Pick the layer and algorithm for your traffic, check health, and make the balancer redundant.","caption":"Pool, policy, health, and redundancy together.","focus":["clients","lb1","lb2","a","b","c"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"ca":"21","cb":"30","cc":"0","healthy":"2"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"You must route /api/* to one server pool and /static/* to another, and retry failed GETs on a different server. Which balancer type do you need?","options":["Layer 4, because it is faster","Layer 7, because it can read the HTTP path and retry requests","Layer 4, because paths are in the TCP header","Either one, routing by path works the same at both layers"],"answer":1,"why":"Path-based routing and per-request retries need the balancer to parse HTTP, which is Layer 7. Layer 4 only sees IPs and ports."},{"q":"Requests vary wildly: most take 10 ms but some take 20 seconds. Which policy handles this best?","options":["Round robin","Consistent hashing on user ID","Least connections","Always send to the first server"],"answer":2,"why":"Least connections reacts to servers that are tied up with slow requests and routes new work away from them. Round robin ignores load."},{"q":"A balancer probes every 10 s and removes a server after 3 failed probes. Roughly how long can users hit a server that died just after a successful probe?","options":["About 10 s at most","Up to about 30 s","Exactly 3 s","No time, removal is instant"],"answer":1,"why":"Three consecutive failures at 10 s intervals take about 30 s to confirm. Lowering the interval or threshold shortens this but risks false alarms."},{"q":"Why is consistent hashing preferred over hash(key) mod N when servers are added or removed?","options":["It makes every request faster","It removes the need for health checks","Only about 1/N of keys move to a new server, so caches stay mostly warm","It balances load perfectly with no skew"],"answer":2,"why":"With mod N, changing N remaps almost every key. A hash ring moves only the keys adjacent to the changed server."},{"q":"Your three servers each handle 2,500 req/s, and peak traffic is 6,000 req/s. One server fails. What happens?","options":["Nothing, the two survivors easily cope","The survivors need 3,000 req/s each, above their limit, so they overload","The standby load balancer absorbs the extra load","Traffic drops to 5,000 req/s automatically"],"answer":1,"why":"6,000 / 2 = 3,000 per server, above the 2,500 limit. You need N+1 headroom so a single failure stays within capacity."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"L4 balancer","def":"Routes TCP/UDP connections by IP and port without reading content. Fast and protocol-agnostic, but cannot route by URL or header."},{"term":"L7 balancer","def":"Terminates the connection and routes each HTTP request by path, host, header, or cookie. Enables retries and canaries; costs more CPU."},{"term":"Round robin","def":"Rotate through servers in order. Simple and stateless; best when requests and servers are uniform."},{"term":"Least connections","def":"Send to the server with the fewest active connections. Handles uneven request cost; weights cover unequal servers."},{"term":"Consistent hashing","def":"Map a key onto a hash ring of servers so the same key hits the same server. Adding or removing a server moves only about 1/N of keys."},{"term":"Health check","def":"Active probe (such as GET /healthz) or passive error counting. Detection time is roughly interval times failure threshold."},{"term":"N+1 capacity","def":"Size the pool so the loss of any one server leaves enough capacity for peak load. Otherwise failover causes a cascade."},{"term":"Active/standby LB","def":"Two balancers share a virtual IP and exchange heartbeats. If the active one goes silent, the standby takes over within seconds."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"autoscaling","title":"Autoscaling","subtitle":"Watch a lunchtime traffic spike hit a fleet, and see why reacting late hurts and how schedules and forecasts help.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How reactive, scheduled, and predictive scaling differ and when each fits","Which metrics make good scaling signals (and which mislead)","Why scaling lag exists: metric delay + boot time + warm-up","How cooldowns stop the fleet from flapping up and down","The cost of over-provisioning versus the risk of under-provisioning"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Users","kind":"user","x":10,"y":55},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":30,"y":55},{"id":"fleet","label":"App instances","kind":"server","x":55,"y":55},{"id":"metrics","label":"Metrics store","kind":"service","x":30,"y":18},{"id":"scaler","label":"Autoscaler","kind":"service","x":55,"y":18},{"id":"sched","label":"Schedule + forecast","kind":"worker","x":82,"y":18},{"id":"cloud","label":"Cloud API","kind":"external","x":82,"y":55}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"lb"},{"from":"lb","to":"fleet"},{"from":"fleet","to":"metrics"},{"from":"metrics","to":"scaler"},{"from":"sched","to":"scaler"},{"from":"scaler","to":"cloud"},{"from":"cloud","to":"fleet"}],"metrics":[{"id":"traffic","label":"Traffic","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"instances","label":"Ready instances","unit":"count"},{"id":"cpu","label":"Avg CPU","unit":"%"},{"id":"p95","label":"p95 latency","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"How many servers do you need?","body":"An online shop gets 600 requests per second most of the day. Assume each instance comfortably handles 100 req/s at 60% CPU, so 6 instances is right. But the number you need changes through the day, and nobody wants to stare at a dashboard and add servers by hand. Autoscaling is software that changes the instance count for you.","idea":"The right fleet size moves with traffic; autoscaling is the control loop that follows it.","caption":"Steady state: 6 instances, 60% CPU.","focus":["users","lb","fleet"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"600 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"fleet","kind":"request","label":"100 each"}],"metrics":{"traffic":"600","instances":"6","cpu":"60","p95":"120"}},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"The lunch spike arrives","body":"At 11:45 a promotion email lands and traffic doubles to 1,200 req/s within a minute. Six instances now face twice the work they can take. CPU pins at 100%, requests queue up, and p95 latency (the time under which 95% of requests finish) jumps from 120 ms to about 1.8 seconds. Some requests start timing out.","idea":"Too little capacity does not degrade gently; queues build and latency explodes.","caption":"Demand doubles, capacity does not.","focus":["users","lb","fleet"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"1,200 req/s"},{"from":"lb","to":"fleet","kind":"fail","label":"overloaded"}],"metrics":{"traffic":"1200","cpu":"100","p95":"1800"}},{"tag":"METRICS","title":"Pick a signal to scale on","body":"Each instance reports numbers to a metrics store, usually averaged per minute. Good signals track load and move before users suffer: average CPU, requests per instance, or for queue workers the queue depth or age of the oldest message. Memory is a poor signal for most apps because it rarely falls after a spike. Latency is useful as an alarm, but it rises only after the damage is done.","idea":"Scale on a metric that rises with load and falls when capacity is added, ideally a leading one.","caption":"Instances publish CPU to the metrics store.","focus":["fleet","metrics"],"flows":[{"from":"fleet","to":"metrics","kind":"async","label":"CPU 100%"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"REACTIVE","title":"Reactive: react to what happened","body":"A reactive policy compares a metric to a target. With target tracking, you say keep average CPU near 60%. The autoscaler computes desired = current × (measured ÷ target), so 6 × 100 ÷ 60 = 10, and asks the cloud API for 4 more instances. Notice the catch: CPU cannot exceed 100%, so a saturated fleet hides how overloaded it truly is. The real need was 12.","idea":"Reactive scaling is simple and handles surprises, but it only acts after load has already arrived.","caption":"Autoscaler decides +4 and calls the cloud API.","focus":["metrics","scaler","cloud"],"flows":[{"from":"metrics","to":"scaler","kind":"request","label":"avg CPU 100%"},{"from":"scaler","to":"cloud","kind":"request","label":"launch +4"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"LAG","title":"Scaling lag","body":"Help is not instant. Metrics are aggregated per minute, so the spike is noticed about a minute late. The scaler may wait for a couple of readings to avoid false alarms. Then the cloud must start a VM and boot your app, often 60 to 120 seconds. All that time users still see 1.8 s latency. A typical total is 3 to 5 minutes from spike to relief.","idea":"Scaling lag = metric delay + decision delay + boot time + warm-up; reactive scaling always pays it.","caption":"New instances are booting; none are serving yet.","focus":["cloud","fleet"],"flows":[{"from":"cloud","to":"fleet","kind":"handshake","label":"boot ~90 s"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"WARM-UP","title":"Warm-up and the second round","body":"The 4 new instances join and the load balancer sends them traffic. They start cold: empty caches, unopened database connections, code not yet optimized by the runtime. So they serve less than full speed for a minute or so. CPU settles around 72%, still above target, so the scaler adds 2 more. Once warmed, 12 instances run at 60% CPU and p95 returns to about 130 ms.","idea":"A new instance is not full capacity the moment it boots; give it a warm-up period before counting its metrics.","caption":"Fleet grows 6 to 10 to 12 and latency recovers.","focus":["scaler","cloud","fleet"],"flows":[{"from":"cloud","to":"fleet","kind":"handshake","label":"4 join, warm up"},{"from":"scaler","to":"cloud","kind":"request","label":"launch +2"},{"from":"cloud","to":"fleet","kind":"handshake","label":"2 join"}],"metrics":{"instances":"12","cpu":"60","p95":"130"}},{"tag":"COOLDOWN","title":"Scaling in, slowly","body":"At 12:30 the spike ends and traffic falls back to 600 req/s. CPU drops to 30%. The scaler does not delete half the fleet at once. A cooldown (for example 10 minutes) and small step sizes make scale-in slower than scale-out. Without that, a brief dip would remove servers just before the next wave and the fleet would flap up and down, paying boot lag every time.","idea":"Scale out fast, scale in slow: removing capacity wrongly is costlier than keeping it a bit longer.","caption":"Traffic falls; the fleet shrinks gradually to 6.","focus":["metrics","scaler","cloud","fleet"],"flows":[{"from":"metrics","to":"scaler","kind":"request","label":"avg CPU 30%"},{"from":"scaler","to":"cloud","kind":"request","label":"terminate 1"},{"from":"cloud","to":"fleet","kind":"async","label":"drain, stop"}],"metrics":{"traffic":"600","instances":"6","cpu":"60","p95":"120"}},{"tag":"SCHEDULED","title":"Scheduled: you know the pattern","body":"The spike happens every weekday at 11:45, so waiting for CPU to rise is wasteful. A scheduled action raises the minimum to 12 instances at 11:35, leaving time to boot and warm up. The spike then meets full capacity with no lag. The cost is a few extra instance-hours, and the schedule goes stale if the pattern changes, such as a holiday or a new marketing time.","idea":"Scheduled scaling removes lag for predictable load, but it is only as good as your calendar.","caption":"Capacity is added before the spike.","focus":["sched","scaler","cloud","fleet"],"flows":[{"from":"sched","to":"scaler","kind":"request","label":"min=12 at 11:35"},{"from":"scaler","to":"cloud","kind":"request","label":"launch +6"},{"from":"cloud","to":"fleet","kind":"handshake","label":"ready by 11:45"}],"metrics":{"traffic":"1200","instances":"12","cpu":"60","p95":"120"}},{"tag":"PREDICTIVE","title":"Predictive: let history forecast","body":"Predictive scaling trains on past load, often a few weeks of it, to forecast the next hours and pre-launch capacity. It suits recurring daily and weekly shapes without hand-written schedules. It cannot see a surprise, like a viral post. So teams combine the three: forecast or schedule for the known shape, reactive scaling as the safety net, and a sensible minimum and maximum.","idea":"Forecasts handle regular rhythms; reactive scaling covers what the forecast cannot know.","caption":"A forecast feeds the autoscaler ahead of time.","focus":["sched","scaler"],"flows":[{"from":"sched","to":"scaler","kind":"async","label":"forecast: 12"},{"from":"metrics","to":"scaler","kind":"async","label":"live CPU"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Choosing a policy","body":"Reactive scaling follows live metrics and handles surprises but lags 3 to 5 minutes. Scheduled scaling kills lag for known events. Predictive scaling learns the rhythm. Keep a minimum so you survive the first seconds of a spike, a maximum so a bug cannot buy 500 servers, and headroom (the 60% target, not 90%) because capacity arrives late. Idle headroom is the price you pay for it.","idea":"Autoscaling trades a little idle cost for resilience; tune target, minimum, and lag to your traffic.","caption":"Three policies, one fleet.","focus":["metrics","scaler","sched","cloud","fleet"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"traffic":"1200","instances":"12","cpu":"60","p95":"120"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Six instances are overloaded and CPU reads 100% against a 60% target. Target tracking asks for 10 instances, but 12 are needed. Why is the estimate low?","options":["The cloud API caps launches at 4 instances","Memory usage was ignored","CPU saturates at 100%, so it hides how far demand exceeds capacity","The load balancer double counts traffic"],"answer":2,"why":"A saturated metric cannot show the true overload. The scaler under-adds, measures again, and adds more in a second round."},{"q":"Traffic jumps every weekday at 11:45 and new instances need about 4 minutes to boot and warm up. What fixes the latency spike best?","options":["Lower the CPU target from 60% to 50%","A scheduled action that raises the minimum before 11:45","A longer scale-in cooldown","Scaling on memory instead of CPU"],"answer":1,"why":"Reactive scaling always arrives after the spike. A predictable event is best handled by adding capacity ahead of time."},{"q":"Why do most autoscalers scale in more slowly and cautiously than they scale out?","options":["Terminating servers costs more than starting them","Cloud providers forbid fast termination","Idle servers are free, so there is no reason to hurry","Removing capacity too early causes overload and flapping, and re-adding it pays boot lag again"],"answer":3,"why":"Being a little over-provisioned costs money; being under-provisioned costs availability. The asymmetry justifies a cooldown on scale-in."},{"q":"A worker fleet processes jobs from a queue. Which scaling signal is most direct?","options":["Queue depth or age of the oldest message, per worker","Free disk space","Number of open browser tabs","Network packet size"],"answer":0,"why":"Backlog per worker measures unfinished work directly and responds to added workers. CPU can look fine while the queue grows if workers wait on I/O."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Reactive scaling","def":"Adds or removes instances when a live metric crosses a threshold or drifts from a target. Handles surprises but always lags."},{"term":"Scheduled scaling","def":"Changes capacity at set times for known patterns, such as a daily peak. No lag, but goes stale when the pattern shifts."},{"term":"Predictive scaling","def":"Forecasts load from history and launches capacity ahead of demand. Good for recurring rhythms, blind to surprises."},{"term":"Target tracking","def":"Keep a metric near a value (say CPU 60%). Desired count is roughly current count × measured ÷ target."},{"term":"Scaling lag","def":"Delay from load change to usable capacity: metric aggregation, decision time, instance boot, and warm-up. Often 3 to 5 minutes."},{"term":"Warm-up","def":"Period when a new instance is not yet at full speed (cold caches, fresh connections). Exclude it from metrics and traffic ramps."},{"term":"Cooldown","def":"Minimum wait after a scaling action before another. Longer for scale-in to prevent flapping."},{"term":"Min / max / headroom","def":"Minimum survives the first seconds of a spike, maximum caps cost and runaway bugs, headroom (a target well below 100%) buys time while scaling."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"cdn","title":"CDNs & Edge","subtitle":"Watch a cache miss, a hit, a shield, a purge and an edge function, and see how each one cuts distance and origin load.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why a CDN cuts latency (shorter round trips) and origin load (cache hits)","How a cache key decides whether two requests share one cached copy","What an origin shield does and why it protects the origin from many edges","How to get fresh content out: TTLs, purge, and versioned file names","When push beats pull, and what edge compute is good (and bad) at"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Users","kind":"user","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"edge","label":"Edge PoP Mumbai","kind":"cdn","x":34,"y":22},{"id":"edge2","label":"Edge PoP Tokyo","kind":"cdn","x":34,"y":78},{"id":"shield","label":"Origin shield","kind":"cdn","x":60,"y":50},{"id":"origin","label":"Origin server","kind":"server","x":87,"y":50},{"id":"fn","label":"Edge function","kind":"worker","x":62,"y":88}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"edge"},{"from":"users","to":"edge2"},{"from":"users","to":"origin","label":"direct, 200 ms"},{"from":"edge","to":"shield"},{"from":"edge2","to":"shield"},{"from":"shield","to":"origin"},{"from":"edge2","to":"fn"}],"metrics":[{"id":"latency","label":"Latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"hit","label":"Hit ratio","unit":"%"},{"id":"origin","label":"Origin load","unit":"req/s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"The origin is on the other side of the planet","body":"Your only server (the origin) sits in Virginia. A user in Mumbai is about 200 ms away per round trip. A fresh connection needs one round trip for TCP, one for TLS 1.3 and one for the request, so about 600 ms passes before any server work starts. Assume the origin also handles 1,000 requests per second of cacheable traffic.","idea":"Distance costs whole round trips, and the origin pays for every request that reaches it.","caption":"Every request crosses the planet to the origin.","focus":["users","origin"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"origin","kind":"request","label":"GET /product/42"},{"from":"origin","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"latency":"660","hit":"0","origin":"1000"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Put a server near the user","body":"A CDN (content delivery network) runs hundreds of small data centers called points of presence (PoPs). DNS or anycast routing sends the user to a nearby one, here Mumbai, about 20 ms away. The user's TCP and TLS handshakes now finish at the edge in roughly 40 ms instead of 400 ms, even before any caching happens.","idea":"Terminating connections near the user is a win by itself, because handshakes are the expensive part.","caption":"Handshake completes with the nearby edge.","focus":["users","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge","kind":"handshake","label":"TCP + TLS"},{"from":"edge","to":"users","kind":"handshake","label":"keys agreed"}],"metrics":{"latency":"40"}},{"tag":"MISS","title":"First request: a cache miss","body":"The edge has no copy of /product/42, so it forwards the request toward the origin (through a shield, covered soon). The origin renders the page in about 60 ms and sends it back with a header such as Cache-Control: max-age=300. The edge stores a copy for 300 seconds and relays it. This first user pays the long trip, roughly 320 ms in total.","idea":"A miss costs slightly more than no CDN; the payoff comes from every later request.","caption":"Miss: the request goes all the way to the origin.","focus":["users","edge","shield","origin"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"GET /product/42"},{"from":"edge","to":"shield","kind":"request","label":"miss"},{"from":"shield","to":"origin","kind":"request","label":"miss"},{"from":"origin","to":"shield","kind":"response","label":"200 + max-age"},{"from":"shield","to":"edge","kind":"response","label":"store copy"},{"from":"edge","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"latency":"320","hit":"0","origin":"1000"}},{"tag":"HIT","title":"Next request: a cache hit","body":"The next user in Mumbai asks for the same page. The edge finds a fresh copy and answers directly, so the origin never hears about it. Latency falls to about 60 ms including a fresh handshake, and less on a reused connection. If 95 percent of requests are hits, the origin sees 50 of the 1,000 requests per second.","idea":"Hit ratio is the CDN's main dial: each point you gain removes load from the origin and delay from users.","caption":"Hit: the edge answers without touching the origin.","focus":["users","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"GET /product/42"},{"from":"edge","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"200 (cached)"}],"metrics":{"latency":"60","hit":"95","origin":"50"}},{"tag":"CACHE KEY","title":"What counts as the same request?","body":"The edge stores each object under a cache key, by default host plus path plus query string. Two URLs that differ only by ?utm_source=a and ?utm_source=b get two separate copies unless you tell the CDN to ignore that parameter. Adding a cookie or User-Agent to the key is worse: every user becomes unique and the hit ratio collapses toward zero.","idea":"Keep the cache key as small as correctness allows, and strip anything that does not change the response.","caption":"Both tracking variants map to one cached copy.","focus":["users","edge"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"?utm_source=a"},{"from":"users","to":"edge","kind":"request","label":"?utm_source=b"},{"from":"edge","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"same cached copy"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"SHIELD","title":"Origin shield: one miss, not hundreds","body":"A user in Tokyo asks for the same page. That PoP has its own empty cache, so it misses. Without a shield, each of the CDN's ~200 PoPs would fetch the new object from the origin separately, up to 200 fetches for one file. With an origin shield, PoPs ask one designated cache first; it answers from its copy, and only it ever talks to the origin.","idea":"A shield is a cache in front of the origin that collapses many edge misses into one origin fetch.","caption":"Tokyo misses, but the shield answers.","focus":["users","edge2","shield"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge2","kind":"request","label":"GET /product/42"},{"from":"edge2","to":"shield","kind":"request","label":"miss"},{"from":"shield","to":"edge2","kind":"response","label":"shield hit"},{"from":"edge2","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"latency":"115","hit":"98","origin":"20"}},{"tag":"PURGE","title":"Changing content that is already cached","body":"The price of product 42 changes, but copies live for up to 300 seconds. You can wait for the TTL (time to live) to expire, or call the CDN's purge API to evict a URL or a tag everywhere within seconds. For static files the best fix is to avoid purging: name them app.3f9a1c.js, so a new build means a new URL and the old copy is simply never requested again.","idea":"Short TTLs, purge on change, and versioned file names are three ways to trade freshness against hit ratio.","caption":"Purge propagates from the shield to every edge.","focus":["origin","shield","edge","edge2"],"flows":[{"from":"origin","to":"shield","kind":"async","label":"purge /product/42"},{"from":"shield","to":"edge","kind":"replicate","label":"evict"},{"from":"shield","to":"edge2","kind":"replicate","label":"evict"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"PUSH VS PULL","title":"Who fills the cache?","body":"Everything so far was a pull CDN: the edge fetches from the origin on the first miss. It is simple and only stores what people ask for. In a push CDN you upload files to the CDN ahead of time. That suits large files with a known release moment, like a 2 GB game patch, because the first downloaders never hit a cold cache. The cost is that you manage uploads and deletions yourself.","idea":"Pull is the sensible default; push fits big, predictable releases where a cold miss would hurt.","caption":"Push: the content is placed before anyone asks.","focus":["origin","shield","edge","edge2"],"flows":[{"from":"origin","to":"shield","kind":"async","label":"upload patch.bin"},{"from":"shield","to":"edge","kind":"replicate","label":"pre-warm"},{"from":"shield","to":"edge2","kind":"replicate","label":"pre-warm"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"EDGE COMPUTE","title":"Run small code at the edge","body":"Some responses cannot be cached verbatim: an A/B test variant, a redirect by country, a signature check on a token. An edge function runs a short script in the PoP before or after the cache, in a few milliseconds, with no trip to the origin. Personalized pages can often be split into a cached shell plus a small dynamic piece. Keep edge code tiny and stateless; strongly consistent database writes belong near the origin.","idea":"Edge compute handles cheap per-request decisions near the user; heavy or stateful logic still belongs at the origin.","caption":"A function picks the variant without calling the origin.","focus":["users","edge2","fn"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"edge2","kind":"request","label":"GET /home"},{"from":"edge2","to":"fn","kind":"request","label":"pick variant"},{"from":"fn","to":"edge2","kind":"response","label":"variant B"},{"from":"edge2","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"cached B page"}],"metrics":{"latency":"30"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What the edge bought us","body":"The CDN moved handshakes next to the user, answered repeat requests from cache, and let a shield protect the origin. Cache keys decide what is shared, TTLs, purge and versioned names decide freshness, and edge functions handle small per-request logic. Result here: about 60 ms instead of 660 ms, and 20 origin requests per second instead of 1,000.","idea":"A CDN trades some freshness control for large gains in latency and origin capacity.","caption":"Fast for users, quiet for the origin.","focus":["users","edge","edge2","shield","origin","fn"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"latency":"60","hit":"98","origin":"20"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"After a release adds a per-user session cookie to the CDN's cache key, the hit ratio drops from 95% to about 2%. What happened?","options":["The TTL became shorter for everyone","The shield stopped forwarding misses","Every user now produces a unique key, so almost no two requests share a cached copy","The edge ran out of disk space"],"answer":2,"why":"A cache key that includes a per-user value makes each request unique, so copies cannot be shared and nearly every request misses."},{"q":"A new 5 MB video is requested by users near 150 different PoPs within a minute. What does enabling an origin shield change?","options":["Users get a faster first byte at every PoP","Roughly one fetch reaches the origin, and the other PoPs fill from the shield","The video is split into 150 smaller files","PoPs no longer need to cache the video"],"answer":1,"why":"Without a shield each PoP misses and goes to the origin (up to 150 fetches). With a shield, PoPs ask it first and only it contacts the origin."},{"q":"You deploy a new JavaScript bundle several times a day and want users to get it immediately while keeping a year-long cache lifetime. Which approach fits best?","options":["Put a content hash in the file name, such as app.3f9a1c.js, and reference the new name from the HTML","Keep one file name and purge it after each deploy","Set max-age to 0 so it is never cached","Add the User-Agent to the cache key"],"answer":0,"why":"A new hash means a new URL, so there is nothing stale to purge, and each versioned file can be cached for a very long time. The short-lived HTML points to the right one."},{"q":"Which task is the best fit for an edge function rather than the origin?","options":["Charging a card and writing the order to the primary database","Running a nightly report across all orders","Resizing a library of 4 TB of archived videos","Choosing an A/B test variant from a cookie and rewriting the request"],"answer":3,"why":"Choosing a variant is small, stateless, and benefits from running next to the user. Transactions, big batch jobs, and heavy processing need the origin's resources and consistent data."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"PoP (point of presence)","def":"A CDN data center near users, holding caches and often running edge code. Big CDNs run hundreds."},{"term":"Cache key","def":"The identity under which an object is stored, usually host, path and query. Anything extra in the key splits the cache and lowers hit ratio."},{"term":"TTL / Cache-Control","def":"How long a copy may be served without checking the origin, set by headers such as max-age. Longer TTL means more hits and staler content."},{"term":"Hit ratio","def":"Share of requests served from cache. Origin load is roughly (1 minus hit ratio) times total traffic, so 95% to 99% cuts origin traffic by 5x."},{"term":"Origin shield","def":"A designated mid-tier cache that edges consult before the origin, so many edge misses become one origin fetch."},{"term":"Purge / invalidation","def":"Explicitly evicting objects by URL, prefix or tag. Fast but must be wired into your publish flow; versioned file names avoid the need."},{"term":"Pull vs push CDN","def":"Pull fills the cache on first miss from the origin (default). Push means you upload content ahead of time, good for large scheduled releases."},{"term":"Edge compute","def":"Short, stateless code run in the PoP for redirects, auth checks, variants or header changes. Not a place for heavy or strongly consistent work."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"proxies-gateways","title":"Proxies & API Gateways","subtitle":"Watch one front door take over auth, routing, rate limits and fan-out for a fleet of backend services.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["The difference between a forward proxy and a reverse proxy","What an API gateway does: routing, authentication, rate limiting, aggregation","Why a Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) exists and who should own it","Why the gateway becomes a single point of failure and how to contain that risk"],"nodes":[{"id":"web","label":"Web app","kind":"client","x":8,"y":30},{"id":"mobile","label":"Mobile app","kind":"client","x":8,"y":72},{"id":"fproxy","label":"Corporate proxy","kind":"server","x":30,"y":30},{"id":"bff","label":"Mobile BFF","kind":"server","x":30,"y":72},{"id":"gw","label":"API gateway","kind":"gateway","x":52,"y":50},{"id":"auth","label":"Auth service","kind":"service","x":52,"y":12},{"id":"orders","label":"Orders service","kind":"service","x":84,"y":20},{"id":"users","label":"Users service","kind":"service","x":84,"y":50},{"id":"catalog","label":"Catalog service","kind":"service","x":84,"y":80}],"edges":[{"from":"web","to":"fproxy"},{"from":"fproxy","to":"gw"},{"from":"mobile","to":"bff"},{"from":"bff","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"auth"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders"},{"from":"gw","to":"users"},{"from":"gw","to":"catalog"}],"metrics":[{"id":"calls","label":"Client calls","unit":"calls"},{"id":"latency","label":"Server time","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Too many doors","body":"A shop is split into many small services: orders, users, catalog, auth. If every app talked to each service directly, each client would need every address, its own login check, and its own retry logic. Changing one service would break clients in the field. Is there one place to put all of that shared plumbing?","idea":"Without a front door, every client must know every backend and re-implement the same cross-cutting rules.","caption":"Clients would have to call three services directly.","focus":["web","mobile","orders","users","catalog"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"calls":"3"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Forward vs reverse proxy","body":"A proxy is a middleman that passes requests along. A forward proxy sits on the client side: a company routes employee traffic through it to filter sites, cache downloads or hide internal addresses, and the destination sees the proxy, not the employee. A reverse proxy sits on the server side: clients talk to it as if it were the site, and it forwards to hidden backends. The gateway in this lesson is a reverse proxy.","idea":"A forward proxy acts for clients; a reverse proxy acts for servers. Which side it protects is the difference.","caption":"The corporate proxy fronts the client; the gateway fronts the servers.","focus":["web","fproxy","gw"],"flows":[{"from":"web","to":"fproxy","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders/42"},{"from":"fproxy","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders/42"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"One entry, many routes","body":"The gateway terminates TLS and then routes by path or host: /orders goes to Orders, /users to Users, /catalog to Catalog. Clients only know one hostname, so services can be moved, split, or renamed behind it. Assume the gateway hop costs about 2 ms and each service answers in about 20 ms.","idea":"Routing hides the internal topology, so backends can change without clients noticing.","caption":"Path /orders/42 is routed to the Orders service.","focus":["gw","orders"],"flows":[{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"route /orders"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"200 order 42"},{"from":"gw","to":"fproxy","kind":"response","label":"200 order 42"}],"metrics":{"calls":"1","latency":"22"}},{"tag":"AUTH","title":"Check who is asking","body":"Instead of every service verifying logins itself, the gateway does it once at the edge. With signed tokens such as JWTs it can check the signature locally in microseconds; with opaque tokens it asks the auth service, shown here at about 5 ms. Either way, unauthenticated calls die at the door and services receive a trusted user id. Services still need their own authorization for sensitive actions.","idea":"Authenticate once at the edge, but do not make the gateway the only line of defence.","caption":"Gateway validates the token before routing.","focus":["gw","auth"],"flows":[{"from":"gw","to":"auth","kind":"request","label":"verify token"},{"from":"auth","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"ok, user 7"}],"metrics":{"latency":"27"}},{"tag":"RATE LIMIT","title":"Protect the backends","body":"A buggy client loops and sends 5,000 requests a second. The gateway counts requests per API key, for example with a token bucket allowing 100 per minute, and answers the excess with 429 Too Many Requests. The rejection costs about 1 ms and the backends never see it. Limits are usually per key or per IP, with stricter ones on expensive endpoints.","idea":"Shedding abusive traffic at the edge is cheaper than letting it reach services that cannot say no.","caption":"Excess requests are rejected with 429 at the gateway.","focus":["fproxy","gw"],"flows":[{"from":"fproxy","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"burst of calls"},{"from":"gw","to":"fproxy","kind":"fail","label":"429 slow down"}],"metrics":{"latency":"1"}},{"tag":"AGGREGATE","title":"One call, three services","body":"A home screen needs the user profile, recent orders and recommended products. Rather than make the phone send three requests over a slow mobile network, the gateway can fan out to all three in parallel and merge the answers into one response. Server time is then about the slowest call plus overhead: 2 + 5 + 20 = 27 ms, versus about 67 ms if the calls were chained one after another.","idea":"Aggregation trades one fat client-facing call for parallel internal calls on a fast network.","caption":"Gateway fans out to three services and merges the replies.","focus":["gw","orders","users","catalog"],"flows":[{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"recent orders"},{"from":"gw","to":"users","kind":"request","label":"profile"},{"from":"gw","to":"catalog","kind":"request","label":"recommended"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"orders"},{"from":"users","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"profile"},{"from":"catalog","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"products"}],"metrics":{"calls":"1","latency":"27"}},{"tag":"PATTERN","title":"A BFF per client type","body":"Web and mobile want different things: the phone wants small payloads and few calls, the web app wants rich data. Putting both shapes in one gateway makes it a tangle of special cases. A Backend-for-Frontend is a thin server per client type that shapes the response for that client, ideally owned by the same team that builds the client. It adds one more hop, here about 2 ms.","idea":"A BFF lets each client team shape its own API without bloating the shared gateway.","caption":"The mobile BFF trims the home-screen payload for the phone.","focus":["mobile","bff","gw"],"flows":[{"from":"mobile","to":"bff","kind":"request","label":"GET /home"},{"from":"bff","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /home-data"},{"from":"gw","to":"bff","kind":"response","label":"full data"},{"from":"bff","to":"mobile","kind":"response","label":"lean JSON"}],"metrics":{"calls":"1","latency":"29"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The front door fails","body":"Every request now passes through the gateway, so if it goes down, everything goes down even though all the services are healthy. This is a single point of failure (SPOF). The cure is to run several gateway instances across availability zones behind a load balancer, keep them stateless and thin, and avoid putting business logic in them. Set timeouts and circuit breakers so one slow service cannot tie up the gateway's workers.","idea":"A gateway concentrates risk; redundancy and keeping it thin are what make that acceptable.","caption":"With one gateway instance, both client paths fail together.","focus":["gw","fproxy","bff"],"flows":[{"from":"fproxy","to":"gw","kind":"fail","label":"timeout"},{"from":"bff","to":"gw","kind":"fail","label":"timeout"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"One door, with care","body":"A forward proxy fronts clients; a reverse proxy fronts servers. An API gateway is a reverse proxy that routes, authenticates, rate limits and can aggregate, so clients stay simple and services stay hidden. Add a BFF when client types need different shapes. Run the gateway redundantly and keep logic out of it so the front door does not become the single point of failure.","idea":"Centralise shared edge concerns, but keep the gateway thin and redundant.","caption":"Clients make one call; the gateway handles the rest.","focus":["web","mobile","fproxy","bff","gw","auth","orders","users","catalog"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"calls":"1","latency":"29"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a forward proxy from a reverse proxy?","options":["A forward proxy caches, a reverse proxy never does","A forward proxy represents the clients to the internet; a reverse proxy represents the servers to clients","A reverse proxy only works over HTTPS","A forward proxy sits inside the data center in front of the database"],"answer":1,"why":"The difference is which side the proxy acts for. Clients are configured to use a forward proxy; clients are unaware of a reverse proxy and think it is the site."},{"q":"A service behind your gateway accepts any request that reaches it, because the gateway checks tokens. What is the main risk?","options":["Tokens become too long","Anything that bypasses the gateway, such as another internal service or a misconfigured route, is unauthenticated","The gateway cannot route requests","Rate limits stop working"],"answer":1,"why":"Edge authentication is not a full defence. Services should still verify identity and authorize sensitive actions, since the network inside is not automatically trustworthy."},{"q":"A mobile home screen needs data from three services. Which design best cuts latency on a slow phone connection?","options":["The phone calls each service directly and merges the results","The gateway or a BFF calls the three services in parallel and returns one response","The gateway calls the three services one after another","Add a second gateway in front of the first"],"answer":1,"why":"Fanning out on the fast internal network replaces three slow client round trips with one, and parallel calls cost about the slowest service rather than the sum."},{"q":"Your team adds order-pricing rules and discount logic into the gateway because it is convenient. Why is this a poor idea?","options":["Gateways cannot run code","It couples every team's release to one shared component and makes the single point of failure bigger and riskier","It makes rate limiting impossible","It forces clients to use HTTP/1.1"],"answer":1,"why":"Business logic belongs in services. A fat gateway becomes a deployment bottleneck and a larger blast radius, so keep it to cross-cutting concerns."},{"q":"What is the best way to reduce the gateway as a single point of failure?","options":["Make the one instance a bigger machine","Run several stateless instances across zones behind a load balancer, with timeouts and circuit breakers","Let clients bypass it when it is slow","Move authentication into every client"],"answer":1,"why":"Redundancy removes the single instance as a failure point, and timeouts plus circuit breakers stop one slow service from exhausting the gateway's capacity."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Forward proxy","def":"Sits on the client side and makes requests on behalf of clients. Used for filtering, caching, anonymity and controlled egress."},{"term":"Reverse proxy","def":"Sits on the server side and receives requests meant for the site, then forwards them to backends. Hides topology, terminates TLS, can cache and balance load."},{"term":"API gateway","def":"A reverse proxy specialised for APIs: routing, authentication, rate limiting, request/response shaping, and sometimes aggregation, metrics and logging."},{"term":"Rate limiting","def":"Capping requests per key or IP over time, often with a token bucket. Over-limit requests get HTTP 429 and never reach backends."},{"term":"Aggregation (fan-out)","def":"The gateway or BFF calls several services in parallel and merges the results, so clients make one call instead of many."},{"term":"BFF","def":"Backend-for-Frontend: a thin server per client type (web, mobile) that shapes data for that client, ideally owned by that client's team."},{"term":"Gateway SPOF","def":"All traffic flows through the gateway, so one instance is a single point of failure. Run several stateless instances across zones and keep logic out of it."},{"term":"Circuit breaker","def":"Stops calling a failing service for a short period and fails fast, so a slow dependency cannot exhaust the gateway's workers."}],"isNew":true}]},{"id":"caching","title":"Caching","lessons":[{"slug":"caching","title":"Caching Basics","subtitle":"Watch one hot record get read and written through a cache, and see how each policy trades speed, freshness, and safety.","minutes":12,"level":"Beginner","learn":["How cache-aside, read-through, write-through, write-back, and write-around differ","How hit ratio turns into average latency and database load","How LRU and LFU choose what to throw out when the cache is full","Where caches live between the browser and the database"],"nodes":[{"id":"browser","label":"Browser","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"cdn","label":"CDN edge","kind":"cdn","x":30,"y":14},{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":38,"y":50},{"id":"cache","label":"Cache (Redis)","kind":"cache","x":64,"y":20},{"id":"worker","label":"Flush worker","kind":"worker","x":64,"y":84},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":90,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"browser","to":"cdn"},{"from":"browser","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"cache"},{"from":"app","to":"db"},{"from":"cache","to":"db"},{"from":"cache","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"hit","label":"Hit ratio","unit":"%"},{"id":"latency","label":"Avg read","unit":"ms"},{"id":"dbload","label":"DB reads","unit":"/s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"The same row, ten thousand times","body":"Your product page reads the same few popular rows again and again. Assume 10,000 reads per second, a database read that takes about 10 ms, and a cache read that takes about 1 ms. With no cache, every read hits the database, so it carries all 10,000 queries per second. Most of that work is repeated answers, and a cache is a small fast store that remembers them.","idea":"A cache trades a little memory and some freshness risk for far fewer slow reads.","caption":"Every read goes to the database.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT * ..."},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"row (10 ms)"}],"metrics":{"hit":"0","latency":"10","dbload":"10000"}},{"tag":"CACHE-ASIDE","title":"Cache-aside: the app does the work","body":"In cache-aside (also called lazy loading) the application code owns the logic. It asks the cache first. On a miss it reads the database, then stores the result in the cache with a time limit (TTL) for next time. This first read is slower, about 11 ms, because it pays for both lookups.","idea":"Cache-aside fills the cache on demand, so only data that is actually read gets cached.","caption":"Miss: the app falls back to the database, then fills the cache.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET user:42"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"miss"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT user 42"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"row"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"SET ttl=300s"}],"metrics":{"latency":"11"}},{"tag":"HIT RATIO","title":"A hit, and why the ratio matters","body":"The next read finds the key in the cache and returns in about 1 ms with no database work. Hit ratio is hits divided by total reads. At 90% with 1 ms hits and 11 ms misses, average latency is 0.9 x 1 + 0.1 x 11 = 2 ms, and the database sees only 1,000 reads per second instead of 10,000. Going from 90% to 99% cuts database reads by another 10 times.","idea":"Small gains in hit ratio near the top cut database load sharply, because misses are what you pay for.","caption":"Hit: served from memory, database untouched.","focus":["app","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET user:42"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"hit (1 ms)"}],"metrics":{"hit":"90","latency":"2","dbload":"1000"}},{"tag":"READ-THROUGH","title":"Read-through: the cache loads itself","body":"Read-through hides the miss logic. The app only ever talks to the cache. If the key is absent, the cache itself fetches from the database, stores the value, and returns it. The behavior matches cache-aside, but the loading code lives in one place (the cache layer or library) instead of in every caller.","idea":"Read-through and cache-aside differ in who loads on a miss: the cache or the application.","caption":"The cache fetches from the database on a miss.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"get(user:7)"},{"from":"cache","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"load user 7"},{"from":"db","to":"cache","kind":"response","label":"row"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"value"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"WRITE-THROUGH","title":"Write-through: update both, then confirm","body":"On a write, write-through updates the cache and the database together, and only acknowledges once both succeed. The cache never holds a value the database lacks, so later reads are fresh and nothing is lost on a cache crash. The price is that every write waits for the slower database, and you may cache values nobody reads again.","idea":"Write-through buys consistency and durability by making each write as slow as the database.","caption":"The write reaches the database before the app is told it worked.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"PUT user:42"},{"from":"cache","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"UPDATE user 42"},{"from":"db","to":"cache","kind":"response","label":"committed"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"WRITE-BACK","title":"Write-back: acknowledge now, save later","body":"Write-back (write-behind) updates only the cache, acknowledges immediately, and flushes dirty entries to the database later in batches. Writes become very fast and many updates to one key collapse into a single database write. The danger is that if the cache node dies before a flush, those acknowledged writes are gone.","idea":"Write-back is the fastest write path, and the only one that can lose data you already acknowledged.","caption":"Fast ack from the cache; the database catches up asynchronously.","focus":["app","cache","worker","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"PUT counter"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ok (1 ms)"},{"from":"cache","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"dirty batch"},{"from":"worker","to":"db","kind":"async","label":"bulk UPDATE"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"WRITE-AROUND","title":"Write-around: skip the cache on writes","body":"Write-around sends writes straight to the database and leaves the cache alone. It suits data that is written once and rarely read soon after, such as uploaded logs or a bulk import, because it keeps them from filling the cache. The catch is that the first read after a write is a miss, and an old cached copy of that key is stale until it expires or is removed.","idea":"Write-around protects the cache from write-heavy, read-light data at the cost of a cold first read.","caption":"The write goes to the database only.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT log row"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"EVICTION","title":"The cache is full: who goes?","body":"Memory is limited, so a full cache must evict something to admit a new key. LRU (least recently used) removes the key untouched the longest, betting that recent use predicts future use. LFU (least frequently used) removes the key with the fewest accesses, which protects long-term favorites. LRU is simple and a common default; a one-time bulk scan can flush hot keys out of it, which LFU resists.","idea":"Eviction is a bet about the future: LRU bets on recency, LFU bets on popularity.","caption":"A new key arrives and an old one is evicted.","focus":["cache"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"SET new key"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"fail","label":"evict LRU key"}],"metrics":{"hit":"92"}},{"tag":"WHERE","title":"Caches live in layers","body":"A request can be answered at many points. The browser caches files and responses, a CDN edge serves popular content near the user, the app may hold a small in-process cache, a shared cache like Redis serves all app servers, and the database keeps hot pages in its own memory. Closer to the user is faster, but harder to keep consistent and to clear.","idea":"Put each cache as close to the user as the data's freshness rules allow.","caption":"Browser, CDN, and shared cache can each answer before the database.","focus":["browser","cdn","app","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"browser","to":"cdn","kind":"request","label":"GET /logo.png"},{"from":"cdn","to":"browser","kind":"response","label":"edge hit"},{"from":"browser","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"GET /api/user"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET user:42"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the policy by the data","body":"Read side: cache-aside keeps logic in the app, read-through moves it into the cache. Write side: write-through is safe and slower, write-back is fast and risky, write-around keeps the cache clean of write-only data. Track hit ratio, since it sets both latency and database load, and pick an eviction policy that matches your access pattern. Keeping cached copies correct over time is covered in the invalidation lesson.","idea":"Choose read and write policies per data type, then watch the hit ratio.","caption":"Five policies, one goal: fewer slow reads.","focus":["browser","cdn","app","cache","worker","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"hit":"90","latency":"2","dbload":"1000"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Cache hits take 1 ms and misses take 11 ms (cache check plus database read). With an 80% hit ratio, what is the average read latency?","options":["About 1.2 ms","About 3 ms","About 5.5 ms","About 8 ms"],"answer":1,"why":"Average = 0.8 x 1 + 0.2 x 11 = 0.8 + 2.2 = 3 ms. Misses are rare but expensive, so they contribute most of the time."},{"q":"A counter service acknowledges each increment from the cache and flushes to the database every few seconds. The cache server crashes. What is the realistic loss?","options":["Nothing, the database always has every write first","Only reads are affected","The increments not yet flushed, even though they were acknowledged","The whole database is corrupted"],"answer":2,"why":"This is write-back. Unflushed dirty entries live only in the cache, so a crash loses writes the client was told succeeded."},{"q":"A nightly report reads 5 million rows exactly once, alongside normal traffic. Which cache behavior is most at risk?","options":["LRU, because the scan makes one-time keys look recently used and pushes hot keys out","LFU, because it evicts the newest key first","Write-through, because reads never touch the database","None, since eviction ignores access order"],"answer":0,"why":"LRU treats every just-read key as valuable, so a big scan evicts the genuinely popular keys. LFU keeps frequently used keys and resists this."},{"q":"Which data is the best fit for write-around?","options":["A user's shopping cart read on every page","A feature flag read by every request","A session token checked on each call","Audit log rows that are written constantly and rarely read back"],"answer":3,"why":"Write-around avoids filling the cache with data unlikely to be read soon. The cart, flag, and session are read-heavy and belong in the cache."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Cache-aside","def":"App checks the cache, on a miss reads the database and fills the cache itself. Most common pattern; only requested data gets cached."},{"term":"Read-through","def":"App talks only to the cache; the cache loads missing keys from the database. Same effect as cache-aside, loading logic lives in the cache layer."},{"term":"Write-through","def":"Write goes to cache and database together before the ack. Consistent and durable, but writes are as slow as the database."},{"term":"Write-back","def":"Write updates only the cache and flushes to the database later. Fastest writes, but unflushed data is lost if the cache fails."},{"term":"Write-around","def":"Write goes straight to the database, skipping the cache. Good for write-once, rarely read data; first read afterward is a miss."},{"term":"Hit ratio","def":"Hits divided by total reads. Average latency = hit% x hit time + miss% x miss time; database load = misses per second."},{"term":"LRU vs LFU","def":"LRU evicts the least recently used key; LFU evicts the least frequently used. LRU is simple but vulnerable to scans; LFU protects steady favorites."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"cache-invalidation","title":"Cache Invalidation & Stampedes","subtitle":"Watch a hot cache entry expire under load, see the database get flattened, then apply the fixes one by one.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How TTL expiry and explicit invalidation each trade freshness against complexity","Why a delete-then-refill race can leave stale data in the cache","What a cache stampede (thundering herd) is and why it hurts the database most","How request coalescing and probabilistic early expiry prevent stampedes","Why a single hot key can overload one cache node, and how to spread it"],"nodes":[{"id":"clients","label":"Clients","kind":"user","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"app","label":"App servers","kind":"server","x":36,"y":50},{"id":"cache","label":"Cache","kind":"cache","x":62,"y":20},{"id":"writer","label":"Writer / admin","kind":"worker","x":62,"y":84},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"clients","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"cache"},{"from":"app","to":"db"},{"from":"writer","to":"db"},{"from":"writer","to":"cache"}],"metrics":[{"id":"rps","label":"Requests/s","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"dbq","label":"DB queries/s","unit":"qps"},{"id":"hit","label":"Hit rate","unit":"%"},{"id":"lat","label":"p99 latency","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"A cache that works too well","body":"A shop's home page shows a product feed that takes the database about 200 ms to build. It is cached under one key and read 5,000 times per second. With a 99.9% hit rate, the database sees only about 5 queries per second. Everything looks calm. The question is what happens when that one entry goes away, and how it ever gets replaced with fresh data.","idea":"A cache hides load from the database, so anything that empties it can expose that load all at once.","caption":"Healthy: nearly every read is a cache hit.","focus":["clients","app","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"clients","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"GET /home"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET feed"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"hit"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"5"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"TTL: let it expire","body":"The simplest policy is a time to live (TTL). The app stores the feed with a 60 second TTL, and the cache silently drops it when the time is up. The next reader misses, rebuilds the value from the database, and stores it again. TTL needs no coordination between services, which is why it is the default. Its cost is that data can be up to 60 seconds old.","idea":"TTL is the cheapest invalidation: no coordination, but a built-in staleness window.","caption":"A miss rebuilds the entry from the database.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET feed"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"miss"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT feed"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"SET feed, ttl 60s"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"200"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Stale reads","body":"An admin changes a price in the database. The cache still holds the old feed, so for up to the remaining TTL every shopper sees the old price. For a news site that is fine. For a price, stock count, or permission change, it can mean selling at the wrong price or showing access a user no longer has. Shortening the TTL helps, but it raises database load because entries are rebuilt more often.","idea":"Staleness is bounded by the TTL; a shorter TTL buys freshness with more database work.","caption":"Database is updated but the cache still serves the old value.","focus":["writer","db","cache","app"],"flows":[{"from":"writer","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"UPDATE price"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET feed"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"old price"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"5"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Explicit invalidation","body":"To avoid waiting, the writer deletes the cache key right after committing the change. The next read misses and loads the new value. Delete, rather than overwrite, is the usual choice because it is idempotent and cannot store a wrong value. It is not airtight: a reader that loaded the old row just before the commit can write it back just after the delete. That is why teams keep a TTL as a safety net even when they also invalidate.","idea":"Invalidate by deleting after the write commits, and keep a TTL to repair the races you missed.","caption":"Writer commits, then deletes the key.","focus":["writer","db","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"writer","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"UPDATE price"},{"from":"db","to":"writer","kind":"response","label":"committed"},{"from":"writer","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"DEL feed"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"5"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The stampede","body":"Now the key disappears, by TTL or by a delete, while 5,000 requests per second arrive. Every request misses, and every one starts its own 200 ms query. In the time the first rebuild takes, about 1,000 identical queries have been sent. If the database comfortably handles around 500 queries per second, it queues, slows down, and requests time out. The failed rebuilds mean the key stays empty, so the pile-on continues. This is a cache stampede, also called a thundering herd.","idea":"A stampede turns one expired key into thousands of identical database queries, and the overload can prevent the refill that would end it.","caption":"Every miss goes straight to the database.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET feed"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"miss x 1000"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"SELECT x 1000"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5000","hit":"0","lat":"2000"}},{"tag":"FIX","title":"Request coalescing","body":"The first request to miss takes a short lock (in one process, a single-flight map; across servers, a cache entry set with a short expiry only if absent). It alone queries the database. Everyone else waits for that result, or briefly serves the previous value if you kept one. The database sees one query instead of a thousand, and waiters pay at most one rebuild time. Give the lock its own timeout so a crashed leader cannot block the key forever.","idea":"Coalescing collapses many identical misses into one rebuild; always give the lock an expiry.","caption":"One query rebuilds the entry for everyone.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"lock + GET"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"1 x SELECT"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"SET feed"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"200"}},{"tag":"FIX","title":"Refresh before it expires","body":"Coalescing still makes someone wait. Probabilistic early expiry avoids even that. Each request that reads the entry may decide to rebuild it early, with a chance that grows as the expiry approaches and grows with how long a rebuild takes. One lucky request refreshes in the background while everyone else keeps getting the still-valid value. The entry is replaced before it ever expires, so there is never a mass miss. Stale-while-revalidate, which serves the old value during a refresh, is a close cousin.","idea":"Rebuilding slightly early, at random, means the key is never empty when the crowd arrives.","caption":"One request refreshes early; the rest still hit.","focus":["app","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET feed"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"async","label":"early refresh"},{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"async","label":"SET feed"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"5"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Many keys, one deadline","body":"A related trap: if a deploy or a restart fills 100,000 keys all with a 60 second TTL, they all expire together and the database faces a stampede across many keys at once. Coalescing per key does not help, because each key is a different query. The cheap fix is jitter: add a random amount, say plus or minus 10 percent, to every TTL so expiries spread out instead of lining up.","idea":"Add random jitter to TTLs so keys written together do not expire together.","caption":"Keys expiring in the same second overwhelm the database.","focus":["cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"100k misses"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"many queries"}],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"3000","hit":"60","lat":"800"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Hot keys","body":"Even with no misses, one key can be the problem. Suppose a celebrity's profile is read 50,000 times per second. The key lives on one cache node, and a single node typically serves somewhere around 100,000 simple operations per second, so it is near its limit while other nodes sit idle. Common fixes are a tiny in-process cache on each app server with a 1 to 5 second TTL, or storing several copies under keys like feed#1 to feed#8 and picking one at random.","idea":"Hot keys are a load-spreading problem: copy the value so reads fan out across nodes.","caption":"Reads spread across copies of the hot key.","focus":["app","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"GET feed#3"},{"from":"cache","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"hit"}],"metrics":{"rps":"50000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"3"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Keep the herd away","body":"TTL is simple but leaves a staleness window. Deleting on write shrinks it, with a TTL kept as a backstop. An empty hot key sends every request to the database at once, so coalesce rebuilds, refresh early at random, and jitter TTLs. For hot keys, replicate the value across nodes or cache it locally for a second or two. Measure miss bursts, not just average hit rate.","idea":"Plan for the moment the cache is empty, not only for the moment it is full.","caption":"Healthy again: few DB queries, high hit rate.","focus":["clients","app","cache","writer","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"rps":"5000","dbq":"5","hit":"99.9","lat":"5"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A key with a 60 s TTL is read 2,000 times per second and takes 500 ms to rebuild. It expires with no protection. Roughly how many database queries fire for it before the first rebuild finishes?","options":["1","About 100","About 1,000","About 120,000"],"answer":2,"why":"Every request that arrives during the 500 ms rebuild also misses: 2,000 req/s x 0.5 s = about 1,000 duplicate queries."},{"q":"Why do many systems delete the cache key after a write instead of writing the new value into the cache?","options":["Deleting is faster than any write","A delete is idempotent and cannot leave a wrong value behind, and the next read loads fresh data","Caches cannot store updated values","It removes the need for a TTL"],"answer":1,"why":"Setting a value from the writer can race with other writers and store an out-of-order result. A delete is safe to repeat, and the next reader rebuilds from the source of truth. A TTL is still useful as a backstop."},{"q":"Which change best protects against 100,000 different keys all expiring in the same second after a bulk load?","options":["Coalescing requests per key","Adding random jitter to each TTL","Making the TTL shorter","Adding a larger cache"],"answer":1,"why":"Per-key coalescing cannot merge queries for different keys. Jitter spreads the expiry times so the rebuild work is spread out over time."},{"q":"One key receives 50,000 reads per second and its cache node is saturated, though the hit rate is 99.9%. What is the right fix?","options":["Raise the TTL so it expires less often","Add request coalescing","Spread reads over copies of the key or add a short-lived in-process cache","Switch to explicit invalidation"],"answer":2,"why":"This is a hot key problem, not a miss problem. The load is concentrated on one node, so you copy the value to spread reads across nodes or keep it in each app server's memory for a second or two."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"TTL","def":"Time to live: how long a cached entry may be served before it is dropped. Bounds staleness without any coordination."},{"term":"Explicit invalidation","def":"Writers delete (or update) the cache key when the source data changes. Fresher than TTL but needs every write path to remember to do it."},{"term":"Cache stampede","def":"Many requests miss the same key at once and all hit the database to rebuild it. Also called a thundering herd or dogpile."},{"term":"Request coalescing","def":"Only one request rebuilds a missing key; others wait or use the old value. Also called single-flight. Give the lock a timeout."},{"term":"Probabilistic early expiry","def":"Each reader may refresh early with a rising probability near expiry, so the key is replaced before it expires."},{"term":"Stale-while-revalidate","def":"Serve the old value while one background request fetches a new one. Trades a little staleness for no waiting."},{"term":"TTL jitter","def":"Add a random offset, such as plus or minus 10 percent, to TTLs so keys written together do not expire together."},{"term":"Hot key","def":"A single key with far more traffic than others. It can saturate one cache node. Fix by replicating it or caching locally with a short TTL."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"bloom-filters","title":"Bloom Filters & Sketches","subtitle":"Watch a tiny bit array skip useless disk reads, then see how two more sketches count events and unique users in kilobytes.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a Bloom filter answers 'definitely not here' or 'maybe here' using bits and hashes","Why false positives happen, why false negatives do not, and how bits per key set the error rate","How a filter in front of a database or cache avoids reads for keys that do not exist","How a Count-Min sketch estimates how often something happened, and which way it errs","How HyperLogLog counts unique items with about 12 KB, and why sketches merge across servers"],"nodes":[{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"bloom","label":"Bloom filter","kind":"cache","x":33,"y":18},{"id":"db","label":"Database (disk)","kind":"db","x":62,"y":18},{"id":"events","label":"Event stream","kind":"queue","x":33,"y":82},{"id":"cms","label":"Count-Min sketch","kind":"service","x":62,"y":58},{"id":"hll","label":"HyperLogLog","kind":"service","x":88,"y":84}],"edges":[{"from":"app","to":"bloom"},{"from":"bloom","to":"db"},{"from":"app","to":"db"},{"from":"app","to":"events"},{"from":"app","to":"cms"},{"from":"events","to":"cms"},{"from":"events","to":"hll"}],"metrics":[{"id":"reads","label":"Disk reads","unit":"per 1000"},{"id":"mem","label":"Memory","unit":"KB"},{"id":"err","label":"Error rate","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Asking for things that are not there","body":"A lookup service gets 1,000 requests for user IDs that do not exist: typos, scrapers, deleted accounts. Without help, each one checks the cache, misses, then reads the disk to learn there is nothing to find. Every one of those reads is wasted work. Can we answer 'not here' cheaply, without storing every key in memory?","idea":"Misses for absent keys are the most expensive lookups because they pay the full cost and return nothing.","caption":"Every absent key still costs a disk read.","focus":["app","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"get user:9"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"not found"}],"metrics":{"reads":"1000","mem":"0","err":"0"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A Bloom filter: bits and hashes","body":"A Bloom filter is an array of bits, all 0 at the start. To add a key, run it through k different hash functions and set the k bits they point to. For 1 million keys we use about 10 million bits, roughly 1.25 MB, and k = 7. The keys themselves are never stored, only their fingerprints in the bits.","idea":"Adding a key flips k bits to 1; the filter stores no keys, only bit patterns.","caption":"Each new key sets 7 bits in the filter.","focus":["app","bloom"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"bloom","kind":"async","label":"add user:42"}],"metrics":{"mem":"1250"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A 'no' is always true","body":"To check a key, hash it the same k ways and look at those bits. If even one bit is 0, the key was never added, because adding it would have set that bit. The filter says 'definitely not present' and the app skips the disk entirely. Bits are never cleared, so this answer can never be wrong.","idea":"One zero bit proves absence: Bloom filters have no false negatives.","caption":"A zero bit means: skip the disk read.","focus":["app","bloom"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"bloom","kind":"request","label":"has user:9?"},{"from":"bloom","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"no, skip disk"}],"metrics":{"reads":"10"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A 'yes' only means maybe","body":"If all k bits are 1, the key may have been added, or other keys may have set those same bits by coincidence. The filter says 'maybe present', so the app must still check the real store. When that check finds nothing, we have a false positive. It costs one wasted read, which is the price of the whole scheme.","idea":"All-ones means 'maybe': the filter can wrongly say yes, never wrongly say no.","caption":"A false positive costs one wasted disk read.","focus":["app","bloom","db"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"bloom","kind":"request","label":"has user:77?"},{"from":"bloom","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"maybe"},{"from":"app","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"get user:77"},{"from":"db","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"false positive"}],"metrics":{"reads":"10","err":"1"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Sizing the filter","body":"Error rate depends on bits per key. As a rule of thumb with the best k, about 5 bits per key gives roughly 10% false positives, 10 bits gives about 1%, and 14 to 15 bits gives about 0.1%. At 10 bits per key, our 1,000 absent lookups drop from 1,000 disk reads to about 10. Memory is paid once; saved reads are saved on every lookup.","idea":"Each extra 5 bits per key cuts false positives roughly ten-fold, at linear memory cost.","caption":"10 bits per key: about 1 in 100 slips through.","focus":["bloom"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"reads":"10","mem":"1250","err":"1"}},{"tag":"LIMIT","title":"What a Bloom filter cannot do","body":"You cannot delete a key, because clearing its bits could erase bits shared with other keys and create false negatives. You cannot list the keys or read a value back. And if you add far more keys than you sized for, more bits fill up and false positives climb. Variants such as counting Bloom filters or cuckoo filters add deletion, and many systems simply rebuild the filter periodically.","idea":"Size for the expected key count; a filter only grows more wrong as it fills, and it cannot forget.","caption":"Deleting a key is not supported.","focus":["app","bloom"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"bloom","kind":"fail","label":"delete user:42?"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Count-Min sketch: how often?","body":"A different question: how many times did each video get viewed? A Count-Min sketch is a small grid, say 5 rows by 2,719 counters. For every event, hash the item once per row and add 1 to one counter in each row. With those dimensions, memory is about 54 KB of 4-byte counters, no matter how many distinct videos exist.","idea":"A Count-Min sketch keeps approximate counts for unlimited items in fixed memory.","caption":"Each event increments one counter per row.","focus":["app","events","cms"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"events","kind":"async","label":"view video 7"},{"from":"events","to":"cms","kind":"async","label":"+1 in 5 rows"}],"metrics":{"mem":"54","err":"0.1"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Reading a count: take the minimum","body":"To estimate a count, hash the item in each row and take the smallest of the 5 counters. Other items collide into the same counters and only ever add to them, so every counter is at least the true count. The minimum is the least inflated one. The estimate can be too high but never too low, by at most about 0.1% of total events with 99% confidence.","idea":"Count-Min overestimates, never underestimates; the minimum across rows trims the damage.","caption":"Estimate = smallest counter across rows.","focus":["app","cms"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"cms","kind":"request","label":"count(video 7)?"},{"from":"cms","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"about 4,210"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"HyperLogLog: how many different?","body":"Counting unique visitors exactly needs a set of every ID: a billion 8-byte IDs is over 8 GB. HyperLogLog hashes each ID and watches for rare patterns such as many leading zero bits; seeing one with 20 zeros suggests about a million distinct items were hashed. It splits items into 16,384 buckets and averages them, giving about 0.8% error in 12 KB. Two HyperLogLogs from different servers merge by taking the max of each bucket.","idea":"HyperLogLog estimates unique counts in about 12 KB and merges cleanly across machines.","caption":"Unique users counted in 12 KB.","focus":["events","hll"],"flows":[{"from":"events","to":"hll","kind":"async","label":"hash(user id)"}],"metrics":{"mem":"12","err":"0.81"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the sketch by the question","body":"Is this key in the set? Use a Bloom filter: no is certain, yes is maybe. How many times did this item occur? Use Count-Min: counts can be high, never low. How many distinct items were there? Use HyperLogLog: a small relative error. All three trade a little accuracy for fixed, tiny memory. Use them where an approximate answer is acceptable, never where you need exactness.","idea":"Sketches trade bounded, one-directional error for memory that does not grow with the data.","caption":"Three questions, three small structures.","focus":["bloom","cms","hll"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A Bloom filter in front of a database answers 'no' for a key. What should the app do?","options":["Query the database anyway to confirm","Retry the filter with different hash functions","Skip the database: the key is definitely absent","Treat it as present with 50% probability"],"answer":2,"why":"A 'no' means at least one required bit is 0. Since bits are never cleared, the key was never added. Bloom filters have no false negatives."},{"q":"A filter with a 1% false-positive rate guards a store. 1,000,000 lookups arrive, all for keys that do not exist. About how many disk reads happen?","options":["About 10,000","About 1,000,000","About 100","Zero, since the filter is exact"],"answer":0,"why":"Only false positives pass through the filter, so about 1% of 1,000,000 = 10,000 reads, instead of 1,000,000 without the filter."},{"q":"Your Count-Min sketch says an item occurred 5,300 times. Which statement is correct?","options":["The true count is exactly 5,300","The true count is at most 5,300, possibly less","The true count may be above or below 5,300 equally","The true count is at least 5,300, possibly more"],"answer":1,"why":"Collisions only add to counters, so estimates are never below the true count. The true count is therefore at most 5,300."},{"q":"Fifty servers each track unique daily visitors. You need one global unique count without shipping all IDs. Which fits best?","options":["A Bloom filter per server, summing the 'yes' answers","A Count-Min sketch per server, summing the counters","A HyperLogLog per server, merged by taking the max of each bucket","A plain counter per server, added together"],"answer":2,"why":"Visitors seen on several servers would be double-counted by a plain sum. HyperLogLogs merge by per-bucket max, which removes duplicates, in about 12 KB each."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Bloom filter","def":"Bit array plus k hashes. Answers 'definitely not in set' or 'maybe in set'. No false negatives, some false positives, no deletes."},{"term":"False positive rate","def":"Rule of thumb with best k: about 10% at 5 bits per key, 1% at 10, 0.1% at 15. Optimal k is about 0.7 times bits per key."},{"term":"Where it is used","def":"In front of disk or SSTable reads, caches, and databases, so lookups for absent keys skip the expensive read."},{"term":"Count-Min sketch","def":"Grid of counters with one hash per row. Add: +1 in each row. Estimate: min across rows. Overestimates only."},{"term":"Heavy hitters","def":"The most frequent items. Count-Min plus a small top-N list finds trending items without a counter per item."},{"term":"HyperLogLog","def":"Estimates distinct count from leading-zero patterns of hashes. About 12 KB gives roughly 0.8% error, for billions of items."},{"term":"Mergeable","def":"Bloom filters merge by OR, Count-Min by adding counters, HyperLogLog by max per bucket, so shards can be combined."},{"term":"When not to use","def":"When you need exact answers, deletion, or the actual keys: money, inventory, billing, access control."}],"isNew":true}]},{"id":"data","title":"Data & Storage","lessons":[{"slug":"sql-nosql","title":"SQL vs NoSQL","subtitle":"Watch one online shop send each kind of data to the store whose shape fits its access pattern.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How relational tables, schemas and joins let you ask questions you did not plan for","What key-value, document, wide-column and graph stores each optimize","Why NoSQL stores usually require you to design around known queries","How to choose a store by access pattern instead of by hype","Why most systems start with one relational database and add others only when forced"],"nodes":[{"id":"app","label":"Shop app server","kind":"server","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"sql","label":"Relational (SQL)","kind":"db","x":42,"y":12},{"id":"kv","label":"Key-value store","kind":"cache","x":42,"y":32},{"id":"doc","label":"Document store","kind":"db","x":42,"y":52},{"id":"wide","label":"Wide-column store","kind":"db","x":42,"y":72},{"id":"graph","label":"Graph database","kind":"db","x":80,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"app","to":"sql"},{"from":"app","to":"kv"},{"from":"app","to":"doc"},{"from":"app","to":"wide"},{"from":"app","to":"graph"},{"from":"sql","to":"kv"}],"metrics":[{"id":"latency","label":"Typical read","unit":"ms"},{"id":"ops","label":"Ops per node","unit":"ops/s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One shop, five kinds of data","body":"An online shop stores orders, login sessions, a product catalog, a firehose of click events, and a 'people also bought' network. Each has a different shape and is read in a different way. Should they all live in one database, or does each deserve its own? The numbers in this lesson are rough single-node figures to show orders of magnitude, not benchmarks.","idea":"Pick a database by how the data is read and written, not by whether it is called SQL or NoSQL.","caption":"Five data shapes, five candidate stores.","focus":["app"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RELATIONAL","title":"Orders in tables with a schema","body":"A relational database stores rows in tables with fixed, declared columns: customers, orders, order_items. The schema is a contract the database enforces, including types, foreign keys and uniqueness. Placing an order touches several tables, and a transaction makes them succeed or fail together. A single well-tuned PostgreSQL node handles roughly 10 to 20 thousand simple queries per second.","idea":"A relational schema trades flexibility for guarantees: the database itself rejects bad or half-written data.","caption":"A checkout transaction writes three tables atomically.","focus":["app","sql"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"sql","kind":"request","label":"BEGIN; INSERT x3"},{"from":"sql","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"COMMIT ok"}],"metrics":{"latency":"3","ops":"15000"}},{"tag":"JOINS","title":"Ask a question nobody planned for","body":"Next quarter someone asks: revenue per product category for customers who signed up in Berlin. Because data is stored once in normalized tables, one SQL query can join customers, orders, items and products to answer it. No one had to predict this question when designing the tables. That ad hoc power is the main reason relational databases are the default.","idea":"Normalized tables plus joins let you answer new questions without changing how data is stored.","caption":"One query joins four tables on demand.","focus":["app","sql"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"sql","kind":"request","label":"JOIN 4 tables"},{"from":"sql","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"aggregated rows"}],"metrics":{"latency":"12","ops":"5000"}},{"tag":"KEY-VALUE","title":"Sessions: give me the value for this key","body":"A login session is read on every request and is only ever fetched by its session id. A key-value store such as Redis or DynamoDB treats the value as an opaque blob and offers just get, put and delete by key. Giving up queries, joins and rich schema buys speed: an in-memory store answers in about half a millisecond and serves 100 thousand or more operations per second per node.","idea":"A key-value store is the fastest design when the only question is 'what is stored under this key?'","caption":"GET by session id returns in well under 1 ms.","focus":["app","kv"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"kv","kind":"request","label":"GET sess:9f2c"},{"from":"kv","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"session blob"}],"metrics":{"latency":"0.5","ops":"100000"}},{"tag":"DOCUMENT","title":"Catalog: one product, one document","body":"A laptop has RAM and ports; a T-shirt has size and fabric. Forcing both into one table means many empty columns or many joined tables. A document store such as MongoDB keeps each product as one JSON-like document with whatever fields it needs, so a single read returns the whole product page. The cost is that the shape is enforced by your code rather than the database, and cross-document joins are limited or slow.","idea":"Documents fit data that is read and written as a self-contained unit with varying fields.","caption":"One read returns the full product document.","focus":["app","doc"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"doc","kind":"request","label":"find sku=L-204"},{"from":"doc","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"product JSON"}],"metrics":{"latency":"2","ops":"20000"}},{"tag":"WIDE-COLUMN","title":"Click events: huge write volume","body":"The shop logs 200 thousand click events per second during a sale. A wide-column store such as Cassandra or ScyllaDB spreads data over many nodes by a partition key (say user id) and keeps rows inside a partition sorted by a clustering key such as time. Writes are cheap appends, and 'latest 50 events for user 42' reads one sorted slice. You must choose the table layout per query, because queries on other columns are not supported efficiently.","idea":"Wide-column stores scale writes by making every query hit one partition, so the table is designed around the query.","caption":"Writes fan out by partition key across the cluster.","focus":["app","wide"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"wide","kind":"async","label":"append click"},{"from":"app","to":"wide","kind":"request","label":"last 50, user 42"},{"from":"wide","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"sorted slice"}],"metrics":{"latency":"2","ops":"40000"}},{"tag":"GRAPH","title":"Recommendations: follow the relationships","body":"'Customers who bought this also bought...' means walking from a product to buyers to their other purchases, two or three hops deep. In SQL each hop is another self-join that gets slower as the data grows. A graph database stores connections directly and walks them as pointers, so a traversal costs about the number of edges visited rather than the table sizes. It shines when relationships are the question, and is an odd choice for plain lookups.","idea":"Pick a graph store when queries are about paths and neighbours several hops deep.","caption":"A 3-hop traversal follows stored edges.","focus":["app","graph"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"graph","kind":"request","label":"3 hops from item"},{"from":"graph","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"related items"}],"metrics":{"latency":"5","ops":"8000"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Run the finance report on the document store","body":"Suppose orders had been put in the document store because it was fashionable. The revenue-by-category question now has no join. The app must fetch orders, then products, then customers, and combine them in its own code, or you must copy product and customer fields into every order. That duplication is fast to read but must be kept in sync, and it was designed for the queries you knew, not the new one.","idea":"NoSQL stores make known queries fast by pre-shaping data; unplanned queries then cost you code or copies.","caption":"No join available, so the app does the work.","focus":["app","doc"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"doc","kind":"fail","label":"no JOIN support"}],"metrics":{"latency":"400","ops":"50"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Start boring, add stores on evidence","body":"A common mistake is to adopt five databases on day one. A single relational database handles most products well past a million users, and it gives transactions, constraints and joins for free. Add a key-value cache when reads hurt, a wide-column or time-series store when write volume outgrows one node, a graph store when traversals dominate. Each added store means more operations work and a way to keep copies consistent.","idea":"Every extra database is a new thing to run, back up and keep in sync, so add one only for a measured need.","caption":"SQL is the source of truth; others serve special reads.","focus":["app","sql","kv"],"flows":[{"from":"sql","to":"kv","kind":"replicate","label":"cache hot rows"},{"from":"app","to":"kv","kind":"request","label":"GET first"}],"metrics":{"latency":"0.5","ops":"100000"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Choose by access pattern","body":"Relational suits structured data, joins and transactions, and answers questions you did not plan for. Key-value is fastest for lookups by key. Documents suit self-contained records with varying fields. Wide-column scales heavy writes when queries are known and partition-aligned. Graphs suit deep relationship queries. Ask how the data is read and written, how it must stay consistent, and how big it gets, then choose.","idea":"List your queries first; the store that serves them directly is usually the right one.","caption":"Each store matches one access pattern.","focus":["app","sql","kv","doc","wide","graph"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"latency":"3","ops":"15000"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your team must answer a brand-new analytics question tomorrow that combines customers, orders and products, and nobody planned for it. Which design makes that easiest?","options":["A key-value store keyed by order id","Normalized relational tables queried with joins","A wide-column table designed for the click-event query","Denormalized documents with product data copied into each order"],"answer":1,"why":"Normalized tables store each fact once, so joins can combine them in ways nobody anticipated. The other designs were shaped around specific known queries."},{"q":"A service only ever fetches a user's session blob by session id, millions of times per second. What is the best fit?","options":["A graph database","A relational database with many joins","A key-value store","A document store with secondary indexes on every field"],"answer":2,"why":"The only access pattern is lookup by key, which is exactly what key-value stores optimize, with no query planner or joins to pay for."},{"q":"In a wide-column store you need to list a user's latest events, and later also list all events from one country. Why is the second query a problem?","options":["Wide-column stores cannot store country values","The table is partitioned by user, so a country query would scan every partition unless you add a second table laid out by country","Country lookups require a graph traversal","Wide-column stores only support writes"],"answer":1,"why":"Data is placed by partition key and efficient queries must supply it. A different query needs a differently keyed copy of the data."},{"q":"Which statement about 'NoSQL scales, SQL does not' is most accurate?","options":["It is true: SQL databases cannot run on more than one machine","It is false: NoSQL stores are slower than SQL on every workload","It oversimplifies: NoSQL stores often scale out by limiting query types, while a single SQL node already handles very large workloads","It is true because NoSQL stores never need indexes"],"answer":2,"why":"Scale-out comes mostly from restricting queries to one partition, not from the data model's label. One relational node serves most products, and sharded or distributed SQL exists too."},{"q":"You store each order as a document that embeds a copy of the product name and price. A product is renamed. What is the main consequence?","options":["Old orders automatically update","Order reads become slower","Copies in existing orders may keep the old name, so you must decide whether that is correct or update them","The document store rejects the rename"],"answer":2,"why":"Embedding duplicates data for fast single-read access. Sometimes keeping the price paid at purchase time is desired, but other copies need explicit synchronization."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Schema","def":"The declared structure of data. SQL enforces it on write; many NoSQL stores leave it to application code (schema-on-read)."},{"term":"Normalization","def":"Storing each fact once across related tables to avoid duplication. Joins reassemble the data at query time."},{"term":"Denormalization","def":"Copying data into the place it is read to avoid joins. Reads get faster; writes must keep copies in sync."},{"term":"Key-value store","def":"Maps a key to an opaque value. Extremely fast lookups by key; no joins or ad hoc queries. Examples: Redis, DynamoDB."},{"term":"Document store","def":"Stores self-contained JSON-like records with flexible fields. Good for read-as-a-unit data such as catalogs. Example: MongoDB."},{"term":"Wide-column store","def":"Partitions rows by a key and sorts them within the partition. Built for heavy writes and known queries. Examples: Cassandra, ScyllaDB."},{"term":"Graph database","def":"Stores nodes and edges and traverses relationships directly. Best for multi-hop questions like recommendations or fraud rings."},{"term":"Choosing rule","def":"Write down your top queries and consistency needs first. Default to relational; add a specialized store only for a measured mismatch."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"indexes","title":"Indexes","subtitle":"Watch one query go from reading a million pages to reading four, then see what that speed costs on every write.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a B-tree index finds a row in a handful of page reads","Why the column order of a composite index decides which queries it can serve","How a covering index skips the table lookup entirely","Why every extra index slows down inserts, updates, and deletes","When the database correctly chooses a full scan over an index"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"client","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"app","label":"Database engine","kind":"service","x":31,"y":50},{"id":"index","label":"B-tree index","kind":"db","x":58,"y":20},{"id":"table","label":"Table (heap pages)","kind":"storage","x":58,"y":80},{"id":"pool","label":"Buffer pool (RAM)","kind":"cache","x":87,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"index"},{"from":"app","to":"table"},{"from":"index","to":"table"},{"from":"index","to":"pool"},{"from":"table","to":"pool"}],"metrics":[{"id":"pages","label":"Pages touched","unit":"pages"},{"id":"rows","label":"Rows examined","unit":"rows"},{"id":"ms","label":"Latency","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One query, ten million rows","body":"An orders table holds 10 million rows, about 100 bytes each, stored in 8 KB pages. That is roughly 1 GB across 125,000 pages. A user opens their order history, which runs: SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 42. The customer has about 50 orders. How should the database find those 50 rows?","idea":"Finding a few rows among millions is the problem an index exists to solve.","caption":"A query arrives for one customer's orders.","focus":["client","app"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"customer_id = 42"}],"metrics":{"pages":"0","rows":"0","ms":"0"}},{"tag":"FULL SCAN","title":"Without an index: read everything","body":"With no index on customer_id, the engine has no choice but to read every page of the table and test every row. That is 125,000 pages and 10 million row checks to return 50 rows. Sequential reads are fast, but about 1 GB of work still takes on the order of a second, and it grows linearly as the table grows.","idea":"A full scan costs time proportional to table size, no matter how few rows match.","caption":"Every table page is read to find 50 rows.","focus":["app","table","pool"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"table","kind":"request","label":"scan all pages"},{"from":"table","to":"pool","kind":"response","label":"125,000 pages"},{"from":"table","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"10M rows checked"}],"metrics":{"pages":"125000","rows":"10000000","ms":"800"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A B-tree is a sorted map of pages","body":"An index is a separate structure holding the indexed column's values in sorted order, each with a pointer to its table row. Most databases use a B-tree: a shallow tree where each page holds hundreds of keys. Internal pages guide you down, and leaf pages hold the entries in order. With around 300 keys per page, three levels can cover about 27 million entries.","idea":"A wide, shallow tree means any key is only a few page reads away, even in a huge table.","caption":"The index is a small sorted structure beside the table.","focus":["index","table"],"flows":[{"from":"index","to":"table","kind":"async","label":"key -> row pointer"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"SEEK","title":"With an index: seek, then fetch","body":"Now the engine walks the tree: root page, one internal page, then a leaf page, which lists the 50 entries for customer 42 side by side. For each entry it follows the pointer to the table page holding the full row. Upper levels are hot and stay in RAM, so the cost is about 3 index pages plus up to one table page per row. Here that is roughly 53 pages instead of 125,000.","idea":"An index seek reads a handful of pages instead of the whole table; cost grows with tree depth, not table size.","caption":"Tree walk to the leaf, then fetch matching rows.","focus":["app","index","table"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"index","kind":"request","label":"seek 42"},{"from":"index","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"50 pointers"},{"from":"app","to":"table","kind":"request","label":"fetch rows"},{"from":"table","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"50 rows"},{"from":"app","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"result"}],"metrics":{"pages":"53","rows":"50","ms":"3"}},{"tag":"COMPOSITE","title":"Column order in a composite index","body":"The page needs the latest 20 orders, so you create an index on (customer_id, created_at). Entries are sorted by customer_id first, then by created_at within each customer. The engine jumps straight to customer 42's block, already in date order, and reads the last 20 entries with no separate sort. But a query filtering only on created_at cannot use it well, because dates are scattered across all customers. This is the leftmost-prefix rule.","idea":"A composite index serves queries that use its leading columns; put equality columns first, then the range or sort column.","caption":"Entries sorted by customer, then date: 20 rows read.","focus":["app","index","table"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"index","kind":"request","label":"42, newest 20"},{"from":"index","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"20 pointers"},{"from":"app","to":"table","kind":"request","label":"fetch 20 rows"}],"metrics":{"pages":"23","rows":"20","ms":"1"}},{"tag":"COVERING","title":"Covering index: skip the table","body":"Suppose the page only shows order date and total. If the index also stores total (for example (customer_id, created_at) INCLUDE (total), or the equivalent in your database), the leaf entries already hold every column the query needs. The engine answers from the index alone and never touches the table pages. This saves the scattered row fetches, which are often the most expensive part of a seek.","idea":"A covering index answers a query entirely from the index, trading a larger index for fewer reads.","caption":"The index alone answers the query.","focus":["app","index","pool"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"index","kind":"request","label":"42, date, total"},{"from":"index","to":"pool","kind":"response","label":"leaf pages in RAM"},{"from":"index","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"20 rows"}],"metrics":{"pages":"3","rows":"20","ms":"1"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Every index taxes every write","body":"Now an order is inserted. The row goes into a table page, and a matching entry must go into each index, in its sorted position. With five indexes that is six places to write, and a full leaf page may split in two, touching its parent as well. Updates to indexed columns move entries, and deletes remove them. Each index also uses disk and RAM. Indexes buy read speed with write cost.","idea":"Write cost grows with the number of indexes, so add them for real query patterns, not by habit.","caption":"One insert updates the table and five indexes.","focus":["app","index","table"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"app","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order"},{"from":"app","to":"table","kind":"request","label":"write row"},{"from":"app","to":"index","kind":"replicate","label":"5 index entries"}],"metrics":{"pages":"6","rows":"0","ms":"3"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"When a scan is the right answer","body":"Suppose a query asks for orders with status = 'shipped', and 90 percent of rows match. Following the index would mean 9 million random row fetches, far worse than reading the table once in order. The query planner estimates this from statistics and chooses the full scan. Likewise, wrapping a column in a function, such as WHERE lower(email) = ..., usually stops a plain index on email from being used.","idea":"Indexes help selective queries; when most rows match, a sequential scan is cheaper and the planner knows it.","caption":"Index skipped: the scan is cheaper for 90 percent of rows.","focus":["app","table"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"index","kind":"fail","label":"not worth it"},{"from":"app","to":"table","kind":"request","label":"scan instead"}],"metrics":{"pages":"125000","rows":"10000000","ms":"800"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Index for the queries you run","body":"A B-tree turns a search over millions of rows into a few page reads. Composite indexes follow the leftmost-prefix rule, so column order matters. Covering indexes skip the table. Each index slows writes and uses space, and low-selectivity queries are better served by scans. Look at real slow queries and their query plans, then add the smallest index that fixes them.","idea":"Design indexes from your actual queries, check the query plan, and keep only the ones that earn their write cost.","caption":"Fast reads, paid for on every write.","focus":["client","app","index","table","pool"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"pages":"3","rows":"20","ms":"1"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A table has an index on (customer_id, created_at). Which query can use it most effectively?","options":["WHERE created_at > '2025-01-01'","WHERE customer_id = 42 AND created_at > '2025-01-01'","WHERE created_at = '2025-01-01' ORDER BY customer_id","WHERE total > 100"],"answer":1,"why":"The index is sorted by customer_id first, so a query with an equality on customer_id lets the engine jump to one block, then range over created_at inside it. A filter on created_at alone skips the leading column."},{"q":"Your table grows from 10 million to 100 million rows. How does a B-tree seek on a single key change?","options":["It becomes about 10 times slower","It stays almost the same, maybe one extra level","It becomes 10 times faster","It doubles in cost"],"answer":1,"why":"B-trees are wide and shallow, so a tenfold growth adds at most about one level. A full scan, by contrast, would take roughly ten times longer."},{"q":"A write-heavy table has 12 indexes, and inserts have become slow. What is the most sensible first step?","options":["Add another index on the primary key","Find indexes no query plan uses and drop them","Convert every index into a covering index","Run every query as a full scan"],"answer":1,"why":"Each insert updates all 12 indexes. Removing unused or redundant ones cuts write cost without hurting reads. Covering indexes would make each index larger and slower to maintain."},{"q":"A query matches 80 percent of a table and the planner ignores the index. What is the likely explanation?","options":["The index is corrupt","A sequential scan is cheaper than millions of random row fetches","Indexes only work on unique columns","The buffer pool is empty"],"answer":1,"why":"Following an index means one random table lookup per match. When most rows qualify, reading the table once in order is cheaper, and the planner picks that using its statistics."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"B-tree index","def":"Sorted, shallow tree of pages mapping key values to row locations. Good for equality, ranges, and ordering. Seek cost grows with depth, not table size."},{"term":"Full scan vs seek","def":"A scan reads every page and costs O(table size). A seek walks the tree to matching entries and costs a few page reads plus any row fetches."},{"term":"Leftmost-prefix rule","def":"A composite index on (a, b, c) serves filters on a, on a and b, or on a, b and c, but not on b or c alone."},{"term":"Column order rule of thumb","def":"Put equality-filtered columns first, then the range or sort column. Selectivity and your real queries break ties."},{"term":"Covering index","def":"An index that contains every column a query needs, so the table is never read. Costs a bigger index and more write work."},{"term":"Write cost","def":"Each insert, delete, or indexed-column update modifies every index on the table, and may split pages. More indexes means slower writes and more storage."},{"term":"Selectivity","def":"The fraction of rows a filter returns. Low fractions (few matches) benefit from indexes; when most rows match, a scan wins."},{"term":"Query plan","def":"The engine's chosen strategy for a query. Check it (EXPLAIN) to confirm an index is used instead of guessing."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"storage-engines","title":"Storage Engines: B-Trees vs LSM","subtitle":"Watch the same write travel through a B-tree and an LSM tree, and see where each one pays its cost.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a B-tree updates a page in place, and why a tiny change rewrites a whole page","How an LSM tree turns random writes into sequential ones using a memtable, SSTables, and compaction","What write amplification and read amplification mean, with realistic numbers","Why both designs need a write-ahead log (WAL) to survive crashes","How to pick an engine from the workload: read-heavy vs write-heavy"],"nodes":[{"id":"app","label":"Application","kind":"client","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"wal","label":"Write-ahead log","kind":"storage","x":34,"y":18},{"id":"mem","label":"Memtable (RAM)","kind":"cache","x":34,"y":50},{"id":"btree","label":"B-tree pages","kind":"db","x":34,"y":84},{"id":"sst","label":"SSTables (L0)","kind":"storage","x":62,"y":26},{"id":"comp","label":"Compaction","kind":"worker","x":62,"y":72},{"id":"deep","label":"Deeper levels L1+","kind":"storage","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"app","to":"wal"},{"from":"app","to":"mem"},{"from":"app","to":"btree"},{"from":"mem","to":"sst"},{"from":"app","to":"sst"},{"from":"sst","to":"deep"},{"from":"sst","to":"comp"},{"from":"comp","to":"deep"}],"metrics":[{"id":"wamp","label":"Write amp","unit":"x"},{"id":"reads","label":"Reads/lookup","unit":"disk"},{"id":"files","label":"L0 files","unit":"files"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One write, two philosophies","body":"Your app saves a 100-byte row. Disks, especially SSDs, like big sequential writes and dislike many small scattered ones. A storage engine must keep data sorted so reads are fast, yet absorb writes cheaply. The two dominant answers are the B-tree (update data in place) and the LSM tree (never update in place; merge later).","idea":"A storage engine balances three costs: write cost, read cost, and space.","caption":"Same row, two possible destinations.","focus":["app"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"B-TREE","title":"B-tree: edit the page in place","body":"A B-tree stores sorted rows in fixed-size pages (8 KiB in Postgres, 16 KiB in InnoDB). To change one row, the engine finds its leaf page, edits it, and eventually writes the whole page back, even for a 100-byte change. That is up to roughly 160x page-level write amplification, though several changes to one page often share a flush. Pages sit at scattered disk locations, so these are random writes.","idea":"B-trees make reads cheap and predictable but pay for random, page-sized writes.","caption":"Whole page rewritten for one small row.","focus":["app","btree"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"btree","kind":"request","label":"update row"}],"metrics":{"wamp":"160","reads":"1","files":"0"}},{"tag":"WAL","title":"Write-ahead log first","body":"Edited pages live in RAM for a while, so a crash could lose them or leave a page half-written. To prevent that, both engine types first append a small record to a write-ahead log and fsync it (force it to disk). That append is sequential and fast. Only then does the engine acknowledge the write. After a crash, the log is replayed to rebuild lost changes.","idea":"The WAL buys durability cheaply: one sequential append before anything else.","caption":"Log record made durable before the ack.","focus":["app","wal"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"wal","kind":"request","label":"append + fsync"},{"from":"wal","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"durable"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"LSM WRITE","title":"LSM: write to memory","body":"An LSM tree (log-structured merge tree) takes a different route. After the WAL append, the row goes into the memtable, a sorted structure in RAM such as a skip list. No data page is read or modified on disk. The write is acknowledged in well under a millisecond because it costs one sequential log append and one in-memory insert.","idea":"LSM writes touch only the log and RAM, so they are fast and sequential.","caption":"Row lands in the sorted in-memory memtable.","focus":["app","wal","mem"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"wal","kind":"request","label":"append + fsync"},{"from":"app","to":"mem","kind":"request","label":"put(key, val)"}],"metrics":{"wamp":"1","reads":"1","files":"0"}},{"tag":"FLUSH","title":"Flush to an SSTable","body":"When the memtable reaches its limit (RocksDB defaults to 64 MiB), it is frozen and written to disk in one sequential pass as an SSTable: a sorted, immutable file with an index and a Bloom filter. Immutable means never edited again, which makes files simple to cache and copy. The matching WAL segment can now be deleted. New writes go to a fresh memtable.","idea":"Flushing turns many random updates into one big sorted sequential file.","caption":"Memtable becomes an immutable level-0 SSTable.","focus":["mem","sst"],"flows":[{"from":"mem","to":"sst","kind":"async","label":"flush 64 MiB"}],"metrics":{"wamp":"2","files":"1"}},{"tag":"READ","title":"Reads must look in many places","body":"A key may live in the memtable or in any SSTable, and newer data shadows older data. A lookup checks the memtable, then level-0 files (which can overlap), then one file per deeper level. Each SSTable has a Bloom filter, a small structure that says 'definitely not here' quickly, so most files are skipped. Without it, a lookup could touch 8 or more files.","idea":"LSM reads pay read amplification; Bloom filters keep it to about 1 to 2 disk reads.","caption":"Lookup checks newest data first, skipping via Bloom.","focus":["app","mem","sst","deep"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"mem","kind":"request","label":"miss"},{"from":"app","to":"sst","kind":"request","label":"bloom? index"},{"from":"sst","to":"deep","kind":"request","label":"L1..L4"},{"from":"sst","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"value"}],"metrics":{"reads":"2","files":"4"}},{"tag":"COMPACT","title":"Compaction cleans up","body":"Background compaction merges overlapping SSTables into larger sorted ones at the next level, dropping overwritten values and deleted keys (tombstones). This keeps reads fast and reclaims space, but every byte gets rewritten several times as it moves down the levels. With a size ratio of 10, leveled compaction typically lands around 10 to 30x write amplification, a rule of thumb that varies with workload.","idea":"Compaction is the bill for fast writes: the same data is rewritten again and again.","caption":"Compaction merges files and rewrites them lower down.","focus":["sst","comp","deep"],"flows":[{"from":"sst","to":"comp","kind":"request","label":"merge sorted runs"},{"from":"comp","to":"deep","kind":"async","label":"write L1 file"}],"metrics":{"wamp":"20","reads":"2","files":"1"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A write burst outruns compaction","body":"Imagine a bulk import pushes writes faster than compaction can merge them. Level-0 files pile up, and each lookup must check more of them, so read latency climbs. To protect itself the engine eventually slows or stalls writers (a write stall) until compaction catches up. Tuning compaction speed and file limits is a core LSM operations task.","idea":"LSM trades smooth latency for throughput; a lagging compaction shows up as slow reads and write stalls.","caption":"Backlog: L0 files accumulate, reads get slower.","focus":["sst","comp"],"flows":[{"from":"sst","to":"comp","kind":"fail","label":"backlog"}],"metrics":{"reads":"6","files":"24"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick by workload","body":"B-trees update pages in place: strong, predictable reads and range scans, pricier random writes. LSM trees buffer writes in memory and merge sorted files later: very high write throughput and compact storage, but read amplification and compaction work. Both use a WAL for crash safety. Write-heavy ingest favors LSM; read-heavy transactional workloads usually favor B-trees.","idea":"No free lunch: you choose where to pay, at write time (B-tree) or in the background (LSM).","caption":"Two engines, same goal, different cost placement.","focus":["app","wal","mem","btree","sst","comp","deep"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"wamp":"20","reads":"2","files":"1"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A B-tree engine uses 16 KiB pages. An update changes a 100-byte row and the page is written back alone. Roughly how large is the page-level write amplification?","options":["About 1x, only the changed bytes are written","About 16x","About 160x","About 1,600x"],"answer":2,"why":"16,384 bytes written for 100 bytes changed is about 160x. In practice several changes to the same page may share one flush, so real numbers are lower."},{"q":"Why is an LSM write acknowledged so quickly compared with a B-tree update?","options":["It only needs a sequential log append and an in-memory insert, with sorting and merging deferred","It skips the write-ahead log","It writes directly to the final sorted position on disk","It waits for compaction to finish first"],"answer":0,"why":"The write touches the WAL (sequential) and the memtable (RAM). The expensive reorganization is postponed to background flush and compaction."},{"q":"An LSM database was fast, then after a huge bulk load its point lookups slow down and L0 file count keeps rising. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The Bloom filters were deleted","The WAL is too large","The memtable is too small to hold any rows","Compaction is falling behind the write rate"],"answer":3,"why":"Many overlapping L0 files mean each lookup checks more files. That points to compaction not keeping up, and writers may soon be stalled."},{"q":"The LSM engine holds recent writes in RAM. Why does it still write every change to a WAL?","options":["To make reads faster","RAM contents vanish on a crash, so the log lets the engine replay unflushed writes","To avoid ever needing compaction","To keep SSTables sorted"],"answer":1,"why":"The memtable is volatile. The WAL is a durable, sequential record of writes not yet flushed to an SSTable, replayed after a crash."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"B-tree","def":"Sorted tree of fixed-size pages updated in place. Predictable reads and range scans; random, page-sized writes."},{"term":"LSM tree","def":"Writes go to a memtable, then flush to immutable sorted files merged in the background. Great write throughput."},{"term":"WAL","def":"Write-ahead log: sequential, fsynced record of each change before it is applied, replayed after a crash."},{"term":"Memtable / SSTable","def":"Memtable is the sorted in-RAM buffer; an SSTable is an immutable sorted file on disk with an index and Bloom filter."},{"term":"Write amplification","def":"Bytes physically written divided by bytes the user wrote. Leveled LSM often runs 10 to 30x; B-trees pay it per page."},{"term":"Read amplification","def":"Number of places (files or pages) checked per lookup. Low for B-trees; LSM reduces it with Bloom filters and compaction."},{"term":"Compaction","def":"Background merge of SSTables that drops overwritten and deleted data. Keeps reads fast, costs CPU, I/O, and rewrites."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"transactions","title":"Transactions & Isolation","subtitle":"Watch two concurrent transactions corrupt a bank balance, then see how logs, row versions, and locks stop it.","minutes":14,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["What each letter of ACID promises, and which part of the engine delivers it","How lost updates, dirty reads, and write skew actually happen","How MVCC lets readers and writers work without blocking each other","When to reach for row locks, SELECT FOR UPDATE, or serializable isolation","Why stronger isolation costs waiting or retries"],"nodes":[{"id":"txa","label":"Transaction A","kind":"client","x":9,"y":24},{"id":"txb","label":"Transaction B","kind":"client","x":9,"y":78},{"id":"engine","label":"Transaction manager","kind":"service","x":37,"y":50},{"id":"lock","label":"Lock manager","kind":"service","x":62,"y":18},{"id":"versions","label":"Row versions","kind":"storage","x":62,"y":82},{"id":"wal","label":"Write-ahead log","kind":"storage","x":89,"y":24},{"id":"db","label":"accounts table","kind":"db","x":89,"y":70}],"edges":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine"},{"from":"engine","to":"lock"},{"from":"engine","to":"versions"},{"from":"engine","to":"wal"},{"from":"versions","to":"db"},{"from":"wal","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"total","label":"Balance","unit":"USD"},{"id":"versions","label":"Row versions","unit":""},{"id":"wait","label":"B waits","unit":"ms"},{"id":"bad","label":"Wrong results","unit":""}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Two people, one balance","body":"An account holds $100. Transaction A and Transaction B each want to withdraw $30 at the same moment, perhaps from two phones. The right final balance is $40. A database serving thousands of such requests per second must get that right every time, even when requests overlap or the machine dies mid-way. A transaction is the tool for this: a group of reads and writes treated as one unit.","idea":"A transaction groups several operations into one all-or-nothing unit, and the database must keep concurrent units from corrupting each other.","caption":"Two transactions about to touch the same $100 row.","focus":["txa","txb","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"total":"100","versions":"1","wait":"0","bad":"0"}},{"tag":"ATOMICITY","title":"All or nothing","body":"A transfer debits one account and credits another. If the server crashes between the two writes, money vanishes. Atomicity says both happen or neither does. The engine first appends every change to a write-ahead log (WAL), a sequential file, tagged with the transaction id. On a crash it replays or undoes from that log, so a half-finished transaction never survives restart.","idea":"Atomicity comes from logging changes first, so an unfinished transaction can always be rolled back.","caption":"Changes are logged before they count.","focus":["txa","engine","wal"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"BEGIN; 2 writes"},{"from":"engine","to":"wal","kind":"async","label":"log both writes"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"DURABILITY","title":"Commit means it stays","body":"When A sends COMMIT, the engine forces the log to disk (an fsync) and only then answers success. On a typical NVMe SSD that costs well under a millisecond; on a network disk, a few milliseconds. Durability means a committed transaction survives a power cut. Consistency, the C in ACID, means your rules (no negative balance, foreign keys) hold before and after; the database enforces declared constraints, but your code defines the rest.","idea":"Durability means the commit record is on disk before the client hears 'committed'.","caption":"Commit waits for the log to reach disk.","focus":["txa","engine","wal"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"COMMIT"},{"from":"engine","to":"wal","kind":"handshake","label":"fsync"},{"from":"engine","to":"txa","kind":"response","label":"committed"}],"metrics":{"total":"70"}},{"tag":"ANOMALY","title":"Lost update","body":"Now the overlap. Both transactions read the balance (100) into the application, subtract 30 in code, and write back 70. A writes 70, then B writes 70. Each transaction looked fine on its own, but one withdrawal vanished: the balance should be 40. This is a lost update, and it is the classic bug of read-modify-write done in application code without protection.","idea":"A lost update happens when two transactions read the same old value and the later write overwrites the earlier one.","caption":"Both read 100, both write 70.","focus":["txa","txb","engine","db"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"read 100"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"read 100"},{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"write 70"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"fail","label":"write 70 (wrong)"}],"metrics":{"total":"70","bad":"1"}},{"tag":"ANOMALY","title":"Dirty read","body":"A different problem: A writes 70 but has not committed yet. If B is allowed to read that uncommitted 70 and acts on it, and then A rolls back, B used a number that never officially existed. That is a dirty read. The Read Committed isolation level (the default in PostgreSQL and many others) forbids it: you only ever see data from transactions that have committed.","idea":"Isolation levels are promises about which anomalies you can never see; Read Committed rules out dirty reads.","caption":"B sees A's uncommitted write, then A rolls back.","focus":["txa","txb","engine"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"write 70 (open)"},{"from":"engine","to":"txb","kind":"fail","label":"dirty: 70"},{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"fail","label":"ROLLBACK"}],"metrics":{"total":"100","bad":"1"}},{"tag":"MVCC","title":"Keep old versions around","body":"Instead of blocking readers while A writes, most modern engines (PostgreSQL, MySQL InnoDB, Oracle) use multi-version concurrency control. A write creates a new version of the row and keeps the old one. Each transaction reads from a snapshot, so B keeps seeing 100 until A commits. Readers never block writers and writers never block readers. The price is storage for old versions and cleanup later (VACUUM in PostgreSQL).","idea":"MVCC gives each transaction a consistent snapshot by keeping old row versions, so reads do not wait for writes.","caption":"B reads the old version while A's new one is pending.","focus":["txb","engine","versions"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"write 70"},{"from":"engine","to":"versions","kind":"async","label":"new version v2"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"versions","to":"engine","kind":"response","label":"v1 = 100"},{"from":"engine","to":"txb","kind":"response","label":"100"}],"metrics":{"total":"100","versions":"2","bad":"0"}},{"tag":"LOCKING","title":"Make B wait its turn","body":"Snapshots stop dirty reads but not lost updates in app code. The fix is to lock the row you intend to change: SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 FOR UPDATE. A gets the row lock first. B asks for the same lock and is made to wait. When A commits, B's read returns 70, and B writes 40. B waited, say 12 ms, but the answer is correct. Often you can skip all this with a single statement: UPDATE ... SET balance = balance - 30.","idea":"Row locks serialize writers on the same row: correctness is bought with waiting.","caption":"B blocks on A's row lock, then sees 70.","focus":["txa","txb","engine","lock"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"SELECT FOR UPDATE"},{"from":"engine","to":"lock","kind":"request","label":"lock row 1 (A)"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"SELECT FOR UPDATE"},{"from":"lock","to":"engine","kind":"fail","label":"held: wait"},{"from":"lock","to":"engine","kind":"response","label":"A committed"},{"from":"engine","to":"txb","kind":"response","label":"balance = 70"}],"metrics":{"total":"40","versions":"3","wait":"12","bad":"0"}},{"tag":"ANOMALY","title":"Write skew","body":"Locks on one row do not help when two transactions write different rows based on a shared rule. Say checking and savings hold $60 each, and the rule is that the total must stay at or above 0. A withdraws $100 from checking, B withdraws $100 from savings. Each checks the total (120), sees room, and writes a different row. Snapshot isolation sees no conflict, so both commit and the total is -80.","idea":"Write skew breaks a rule spanning several rows even though no single row was written twice.","caption":"Different rows, shared rule, both commit.","focus":["txa","txb","engine","versions"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"sum=120, debit chk"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"sum=120, debit sav"},{"from":"engine","to":"versions","kind":"async","label":"chk v2, sav v2"},{"from":"versions","to":"engine","kind":"fail","label":"no conflict seen"}],"metrics":{"total":"-80","versions":"5","wait":"0","bad":"1"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Serializable and its price","body":"Serializable isolation promises results as if transactions ran one at a time. PostgreSQL does this by tracking which rows each transaction read and wrote; if it spots a dangerous pattern, one transaction is aborted with a serialization error, and the app must retry. Other engines use more locking and more waiting. Safety rises along Read Committed, Repeatable Read, Serializable, and so does the cost, so many teams stay at a lower level and protect only the risky paths.","idea":"Stronger isolation trades throughput for safety: more waiting, or more aborted transactions to retry.","caption":"The engine aborts B; the app retries and sees -40 is not allowed.","focus":["txb","engine","lock"],"flows":[{"from":"txa","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"COMMIT"},{"from":"engine","to":"lock","kind":"request","label":"check conflicts"},{"from":"engine","to":"txb","kind":"fail","label":"serial. error"},{"from":"txb","to":"engine","kind":"request","label":"retry: sum=20"}],"metrics":{"total":"20","wait":"3","bad":"0"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the weakest safe tool","body":"ACID: atomic via the log, consistent via your rules, isolated via MVCC and locks, durable via fsync on commit. Lost updates call for atomic UPDATEs or FOR UPDATE; dirty reads are gone at Read Committed; write skew needs Serializable or an explicit lock on a shared row. Keep transactions short, always retry on serialization or deadlock errors, and never hold one open across a user click. Spanning several databases needs more machinery, covered in later lessons.","idea":"Use the cheapest mechanism that rules out the anomaly you actually risk, and always be ready to retry.","caption":"Log, versions, and locks each cover a different failure.","focus":["txa","txb","engine","lock","versions","wal","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two requests each run SELECT balance, compute balance - 30 in app code, then UPDATE with the computed value. Both read 100. What final balance results at Read Committed with no locks?","options":["40, because the database serializes the updates","70, because the second write overwrites the first","100, because both transactions roll back","The second transaction always gets an error"],"answer":1,"why":"Read Committed does not stop two transactions reading the same old value. The later write of 70 overwrites the earlier one, a lost update."},{"q":"Why does MVCC let a long report query run while writers keep updating the same table?","options":["Writers are paused until the report ends","The report reads a snapshot made of older row versions, so it does not need locks held by writers","The report reads uncommitted data","The report runs on the write-ahead log"],"answer":1,"why":"MVCC keeps old versions, so the reader sees a consistent snapshot while writers create new versions. Neither blocks the other."},{"q":"On-call rule: at least one doctor must stay on call. Two doctors, both on call, each check the count (2) and then set only their own row to off. Both commit under snapshot isolation. What happened?","options":["A dirty read","A lost update","Write skew","A deadlock"],"answer":2,"why":"They wrote different rows, so there was no write-write conflict, yet the shared rule broke. That is write skew, prevented by Serializable or a lock on a shared row."},{"q":"Your service runs at Serializable isolation and sometimes gets a serialization error on commit. What is the correct response?","options":["Treat it as a bug and lower the isolation level silently","Ignore it, the data is already saved","Rerun the whole transaction from the start, usually with a small retry limit","Retry only the last statement"],"answer":2,"why":"A serialization failure means the transaction was aborted to keep results correct. The whole transaction must be retried, since its earlier reads may now be stale."},{"q":"What does the write-ahead log mainly provide?","options":["Faster reads through caching","Atomic rollback and durable commits after a crash","Protection against write skew","Automatic sharding"],"answer":1,"why":"Logging changes before applying them lets the engine undo unfinished transactions and replay committed ones after a crash."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"ACID","def":"Atomicity (all or nothing), Consistency (rules hold), Isolation (concurrent transactions do not interfere), Durability (committed data survives crashes)."},{"term":"Dirty read","def":"Reading another transaction's uncommitted data, which may later be rolled back. Prevented from Read Committed upward."},{"term":"Lost update","def":"Two read-modify-write cycles overlap and one overwrites the other. Fix with atomic UPDATE, SELECT FOR UPDATE, or version checks."},{"term":"Write skew","def":"Two transactions read overlapping data, write different rows, and together break a rule. Needs Serializable or an explicit shared lock."},{"term":"MVCC","def":"Multi-version concurrency control: writes add row versions and reads use snapshots, so readers and writers rarely block each other."},{"term":"Isolation levels","def":"Read Committed (default in PostgreSQL), Repeatable Read (default in MySQL InnoDB), Serializable. Exact guarantees vary by engine, so check yours."},{"term":"SELECT ... FOR UPDATE","def":"Reads rows and locks them until commit, so concurrent writers wait. Keep the transaction short to limit waiting and deadlocks."},{"term":"Retry loop","def":"Serialization failures and deadlocks are normal under contention. Rerun the whole transaction, with a cap and a little jitter."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"replication","title":"Replication","subtitle":"Watch one write fan out to copies of a database, see where stale reads come from, and survive the leader dying.","minutes":14,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How leader-follower replication ships a write log to copies of the data","What synchronous vs asynchronous replication trades: latency against durability","Why replication lag causes stale reads, and how read-your-writes fixes the worst case","How multi-leader and leaderless (quorum) designs differ, and what conflicts they create","What happens during failover, including lost writes and split brain"],"nodes":[{"id":"leader2","label":"Leader (region 2)","kind":"db","x":38,"y":14},{"id":"app","label":"App server","kind":"server","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"leader","label":"Leader (primary)","kind":"db","x":38,"y":50},{"id":"fa","label":"Follower A (sync)","kind":"db","x":74,"y":14},{"id":"fb","label":"Follower B (async)","kind":"db","x":74,"y":50},{"id":"fc","label":"Follower C (remote)","kind":"db","x":74,"y":86},{"id":"mon","label":"Failover monitor","kind":"service","x":38,"y":86}],"edges":[{"from":"app","to":"leader"},{"from":"leader","to":"fa"},{"from":"leader","to":"fb"},{"from":"leader","to":"fc"},{"from":"app","to":"fb"},{"from":"app","to":"fa"},{"from":"app","to":"fc"},{"from":"leader","to":"leader2"},{"from":"mon","to":"leader"},{"from":"mon","to":"fb"}],"metrics":[{"id":"wlat","label":"Write ack","unit":"ms"},{"id":"lag","label":"Replica lag","unit":"ms"},{"id":"copies","label":"Durable copies","unit":"copies"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One copy is a single point of failure","body":"Your database lives on one machine. If its disk dies, the data is gone. If it is busy, every read waits in the same queue as every write. Replication keeps copies of the same data on several machines to fix both problems. The hard part is keeping the copies in agreement while data keeps changing. Numbers here assume copies in the same region about 1 ms apart, and one copy in another region about 70 ms away.","idea":"Replication buys durability, read capacity and availability, and the price is keeping copies consistent.","caption":"One leader holds the data and the app has no backup.","focus":["app","leader"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Leader-follower: one place takes writes","body":"In the most common design, all writes go to one node, the leader. The leader applies the change, appends it to an ordered replication log, and ships that log to followers, which replay entries in the same order. Because there is a single writer and a single order, followers converge to the same state. This is how PostgreSQL streaming replication and MySQL replication work.","idea":"A single leader gives every follower the same ordered log, so copies converge without conflicts.","caption":"Write hits the leader, then the log streams to all followers.","focus":["app","leader","fa","fb","fc"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"leader","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order 41"},{"from":"leader","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"OK"},{"from":"leader","to":"fa","kind":"replicate","label":"log entry 41"},{"from":"leader","to":"fb","kind":"replicate","label":"log entry 41"},{"from":"leader","to":"fc","kind":"replicate","label":"log entry 41"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"2","lag":"10","copies":"1"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Synchronous vs asynchronous","body":"With asynchronous replication the leader answers as soon as its own disk has the write, so the ack is fast but only one copy is certain. If the leader dies before shipping the entry, that write is lost. With a synchronous follower the leader waits for its ack first. That costs one extra round trip (here about 5 ms total) and guarantees two copies. Waiting on every follower is fragile, since one slow node stalls all writes, so real systems keep one sync follower and the rest async. A sync follower 70 ms away would add that much to every write.","idea":"Synchronous replication trades write latency for durability; most systems make just one follower synchronous.","caption":"Leader waits for Follower A's ack before confirming.","focus":["app","leader","fa"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"leader","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order 42"},{"from":"leader","to":"fa","kind":"replicate","label":"log entry 42"},{"from":"fa","to":"leader","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"leader","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"committed"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","copies":"2"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Followers absorb the reads","body":"Most apps read far more than they write, often 10 to 1 or more. Followers can serve reads, so adding followers adds read capacity while the leader keeps handling writes. The app sends SELECT queries to a follower and writes to the leader. This scales reads nicely, but it does nothing for write throughput, since every write still goes through one leader. That limit is what sharding addresses in its own lesson.","idea":"Followers scale reads and not writes; every write still passes through the leader.","caption":"A read is served by Follower B instead of the leader.","focus":["app","fb"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"fb","kind":"request","label":"SELECT orders"},{"from":"fb","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"rows"}],"metrics":{"lag":"10"}},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"Replication lag shows you the past","body":"Async followers trail the leader. Usually that is milliseconds, but a busy follower, a big batch job or a network hiccup can push lag to seconds. Picture a user saving a new display name. The write goes to the leader, the page reloads, and the read lands on a follower that has not received the entry yet. The user sees the old name and thinks the save failed. The data is not lost; the read simply happened too early.","idea":"With async replication, a read from a follower can return data older than a write you just made.","caption":"The follower has not applied the write, so the read is stale.","focus":["app","leader","fb"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"leader","kind":"request","label":"UPDATE name"},{"from":"leader","to":"fb","kind":"async","label":"still in flight"},{"from":"app","to":"fb","kind":"request","label":"SELECT name"},{"from":"fb","to":"app","kind":"fail","label":"old name"}],"metrics":{"lag":"800"}},{"tag":"FIX","title":"Read-your-writes","body":"The guarantee users expect is read-your-writes: after I write something, I always see it. A simple fix is to send a user's reads to the leader for a short window, say 10 seconds, after they write. A more precise one records the log position of their last write and only reads from a follower that has replayed past it. Related is monotonic reads, meaning a user never sees data go back in time, which you get by pinning each user to one follower.","idea":"Route a user's reads to the leader, or to a caught-up follower, right after they write.","caption":"The read goes to the leader and sees the new name.","focus":["app","leader"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"leader","kind":"request","label":"SELECT name"},{"from":"leader","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"new name"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"VARIANT","title":"Multi-leader: a leader per region","body":"With one leader, users far from it pay a cross-region round trip (about 70 ms) on every write. Multi-leader runs a leader in each region. Each accepts local writes at around 3 ms and ships them to the other leaders asynchronously. The cost is conflicts: if two regions edit the same row at the same time, both succeed locally and the clash surfaces later. You must resolve it, for example last-write-wins (which silently drops one edit), merge rules, or CRDTs. Use it only when you need multi-region writes or offline editing.","idea":"Multi-leader makes writes local and fast, but concurrent writes to the same data become conflicts you must resolve.","caption":"Two leaders accept the same edit and a conflict appears.","focus":["leader","leader2"],"flows":[{"from":"leader","to":"leader2","kind":"replicate","label":"set title = A"},{"from":"leader2","to":"leader","kind":"fail","label":"set title = B"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"3","lag":"120","copies":"1"}},{"tag":"VARIANT","title":"Leaderless: quorum reads and writes","body":"Dynamo-style systems such as Cassandra have no leader. With n = 3 replicas, the client writes to all three and counts it done once w = 2 reply. A read asks r = 2 replicas and takes the newest version. Since w + r > n, every read set overlaps the write set by at least one node, so it normally sees the latest write. A replica that missed the write is fixed later by read repair or background anti-entropy. Quorums are not magic: concurrent writes and sloppy quorums can still return stale data.","idea":"With w + r > n, reads and writes overlap on at least one replica, so you usually read the latest value.","caption":"Write succeeds after two of three replicas acknowledge.","focus":["app","fa","fb","fc"],"flows":[{"from":"app","to":"fa","kind":"request","label":"write v2"},{"from":"app","to":"fb","kind":"request","label":"write v2"},{"from":"app","to":"fc","kind":"request","label":"write v2 (slow)"},{"from":"fa","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"fb","to":"app","kind":"response","label":"ack"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"4","lag":"10","copies":"2"}},{"tag":"FAILURE","title":"Failover when the leader dies","body":"Now the leader crashes. A monitor notices missed heartbeats, typically after a timeout of 10 to 30 seconds, picks the most up-to-date follower (B here), promotes it, and points the app at it. Two traps follow. Writes the old leader had not yet shipped are lost, which is the async risk from earlier. And if the old leader comes back believing it is still in charge, two nodes accept writes (split brain), so the old leader must be fenced off or demoted using an increasing epoch number.","idea":"Failover restores service but can lose unreplicated writes and risks split brain without fencing.","caption":"The monitor promotes Follower B and redirects the app.","focus":["mon","leader","fb","app"],"flows":[{"from":"mon","to":"leader","kind":"fail","label":"no heartbeat"},{"from":"mon","to":"fb","kind":"request","label":"promote, epoch 8"},{"from":"fb","to":"app","kind":"handshake","label":"I am leader"}],"metrics":{"copies":"1"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the weakest guarantee you can live with","body":"Leader-follower is the default: one ordered log, simple to reason about, with followers for reads and standby. Choose sync or async by how much data loss you can tolerate versus latency. Expect lag and handle it with read-your-writes. Reach for multi-leader or leaderless only when you need multi-region writes or very high availability, and then plan for conflicts. Rehearse failover before you need it.","idea":"Every replication choice trades latency, consistency and availability; decide which one you can give up.","caption":"One leader, a sync follower, async followers, and a failover plan.","focus":["app","leader","fa","fb","fc","mon"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","lag":"10","copies":"2"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A leader uses fully asynchronous replication and its disk is destroyed one second after it acknowledged a write. What is the realistic outcome?","options":["Nothing is lost, because followers always have every acknowledged write","That write may be gone if it had not reached any follower yet","The write is retried by the app and always recovered","All data on all followers is corrupted"],"answer":1,"why":"Async mode acknowledges after the leader's own write. Entries not yet shipped exist only on the dead leader, so acknowledged writes can be lost."},{"q":"A user posts a comment and immediately reloads, but the comment is missing. A minute later it appears. Which cause and fix fit best?","options":["The leader dropped the write; add more followers","Replication lag on the follower that served the read; route that user's reads to the leader briefly after writing","A cache bug; make followers synchronous for every write","The follower is leaderless; raise w to 3"],"answer":1,"why":"This is the classic read-your-writes violation from async lag. Reading from the leader (or a caught-up follower) after a write fixes it without slowing every write."},{"q":"A leaderless store has n = 3. Which setting guarantees read and write sets overlap by at least one replica?","options":["w = 1, r = 1","w = 2, r = 1","w = 1, r = 2","w = 2, r = 2"],"answer":3,"why":"The overlap rule is w + r > n. With n = 3, w = 2 and r = 2 gives 4 > 3. Both w = 2, r = 1 and w = 1, r = 2 give 3, which is not greater than 3, so neither guarantees overlap, and w = 1, r = 1 clearly fails."},{"q":"After failover, the old leader restarts and still accepts client writes alongside the new leader. What is this called, and what prevents it?","options":["Replication lag; add a sync follower","Split brain; fence the old leader with an epoch or token so it steps down","Read repair; run anti-entropy","A quorum overlap; raise r"],"answer":1,"why":"Two nodes both believing they are leader is split brain. Epoch numbers or fencing tokens make storage and followers reject the stale leader's writes."},{"q":"Why is making every follower synchronous usually a bad idea?","options":["Followers cannot apply logs in order","Any single slow or down follower would block or fail every write","It removes the ability to serve reads from followers","Synchronous replication works only across regions"],"answer":1,"why":"Writes wait for all acks, so availability and latency become that of the worst replica. Systems typically use one sync follower and keep the rest async."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Leader / follower","def":"The leader accepts all writes and ships an ordered log; followers replay it and can serve reads or take over if the leader fails."},{"term":"Sync vs async","def":"Sync waits for a follower's ack before confirming a write (more durable, slower). Async confirms immediately (fast, can lose recent writes on leader loss)."},{"term":"Semi-synchronous","def":"One follower is synchronous and the others async, so there are always at least two copies without one slow node blocking writes."},{"term":"Replication lag","def":"How far a follower trails the leader. Usually milliseconds, but it can grow to seconds under load and makes follower reads stale."},{"term":"Read-your-writes","def":"Guarantee that a user sees their own earlier writes. Implement by reading from the leader or a caught-up follower after a write."},{"term":"Multi-leader","def":"Several nodes accept writes, usually one per region. Local writes are fast, but concurrent edits create conflicts needing resolution."},{"term":"Quorum (w + r > n)","def":"In leaderless stores, require w write acks and r read replies so the sets overlap. Helps freshness but is not strict consistency."},{"term":"Failover and split brain","def":"Promote a follower when the leader dies. Risks: losing unreplicated writes, and two leaders if the old one returns, so fence with epochs."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"sharding","title":"Sharding","subtitle":"Split one huge table across machines, then watch hot keys, scatter-gather queries and resharding test the design.","minutes":13,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How range and hash partitioning place rows, and what each makes easy or hard","What makes a good shard key, and why it must appear in your hot queries","Why hot partitions happen even with hashing, and how to relieve them","What a cross-shard query costs and why tail latency grows with fan-out","How resharding moves data, and why fixed buckets beat hash mod N"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"App clients","kind":"client","x":8,"y":40},{"id":"router","label":"Shard router","kind":"gateway","x":32,"y":40},{"id":"map","label":"Shard map","kind":"service","x":32,"y":84},{"id":"a","label":"Shard A","kind":"db","x":64,"y":14},{"id":"b","label":"Shard B","kind":"db","x":64,"y":40},{"id":"c","label":"Shard C","kind":"db","x":64,"y":66},{"id":"d","label":"Shard D (new)","kind":"db","x":89,"y":40}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"router"},{"from":"router","to":"map"},{"from":"router","to":"a"},{"from":"router","to":"b"},{"from":"router","to":"c"},{"from":"a","to":"d"},{"from":"b","to":"d"},{"from":"c","to":"d"}],"metrics":[{"id":"shards","label":"Shards","unit":""},{"id":"hot","label":"Hottest shard","unit":"% load"},{"id":"fan","label":"Shards per query","unit":""},{"id":"moved","label":"Data moved","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One database is not enough","body":"Picture an orders table with 300 million rows, about 6 TB, taking 20,000 writes per second at peak. One machine runs out of disk, memory and write throughput. Read replicas help reads, but each copy still holds all 6 TB and every write still hits one leader. Sharding splits the rows themselves: here three shards hold about 2 TB and 6,700 writes per second each.","idea":"Replication copies the whole dataset; sharding divides it so each machine owns only a slice.","caption":"Three shards, each owning about a third of the rows.","focus":["a","b","c"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"shards":"3","hot":"33","fan":"1","moved":"0"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Route by shard key","body":"Every row carries a shard key, here user_id. A router looks the key up in a shard map and sends the query to the one shard that owns it. The app never needs to know how many shards exist. The map is tiny, so routers cache it and refresh it only when it changes.","idea":"The shard key decides where a row lives, so every query is routed by it.","caption":"User 8841 is routed to Shard B and nowhere else.","focus":["client","router","map","b"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"router","kind":"request","label":"user 8841"},{"from":"router","to":"map","kind":"request","label":"who owns it?"},{"from":"map","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"Shard B"},{"from":"router","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"SELECT orders"},{"from":"b","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"router","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"fan":"1"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Hash partitioning spreads load","body":"With hash partitioning the shard is chosen from hash(user_id), so neighbouring ids land on unrelated shards. Inserts and reads spread almost evenly across all three. The price is order: a range scan such as user ids 1000 to 2000 now touches every shard, because adjacent keys are deliberately scattered.","idea":"Hashing buys even spread by giving up range scans on the key.","caption":"Writes fan out evenly across A, B and C.","focus":["router","a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"INSERT"},{"from":"router","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"INSERT"},{"from":"router","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"INSERT"}],"metrics":{"hot":"34"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Range partitioning keeps order","body":"Range partitioning gives each shard a contiguous slice of keys, for example order dates by month. Scanning one month, or the newest 100 orders, hits one shard and is cheap. But if the key is a timestamp, every new insert lands in the newest range. That one shard takes nearly all the writes while the older shards sit almost idle.","idea":"Ranges make scans cheap but concentrate writes whenever the key grows over time.","caption":"Today's writes all pile onto the newest range, Shard C.","focus":["router","c","a"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"new orders"},{"from":"router","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"new orders"},{"from":"router","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"old reads"}],"metrics":{"hot":"90"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Choosing the shard key","body":"A good key has many distinct values, spreads traffic evenly, and appears in your most common queries. Sharding orders by user_id makes the query show me my orders hit exactly one shard. Sharding by country would give only about 200 values, wildly uneven sizes, and a few giant shards. Pick the key from your access pattern, not from the schema.","idea":"Pick a high-cardinality key that your hottest queries already filter on.","caption":"A query that carries the key touches one shard.","focus":["client","router","b"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"router","kind":"request","label":"my orders"},{"from":"router","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"user_id = 8841"},{"from":"b","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"20 rows"}],"metrics":{"hot":"34","fan":"1"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A hot partition","body":"Now one tenant, say a viral store, generates 40 percent of all traffic. Hashing cannot help: every request carries the same key, so they all land on the same shard. That shard saturates while the others idle. Fixes include a cache in front, giving the big tenant a dedicated shard, or splitting its key with a small suffix so its rows spread over several shards.","idea":"Hashing spreads different keys evenly, but it cannot spread one key that is itself hot.","caption":"One hot key overloads Shard A while B and C coast.","focus":["router","a"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"a","kind":"fail","label":"hot tenant"},{"from":"router","to":"a","kind":"fail","label":"hot tenant"}],"metrics":{"hot":"62"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A query without the key","body":"Someone asks for all orders from merchant 77. The merchant is not the shard key, so the router cannot tell which shard holds them. It sends the query to every shard and merges the answers, called scatter-gather. The result is only as fast as the slowest shard. Cross-shard joins and transactions are harder still, so design schemas to avoid them.","idea":"A query missing the shard key fans out to every shard, and the slowest shard sets the latency.","caption":"The router asks all three shards and waits for the last reply.","focus":["router","a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"router","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"merchant 77"},{"from":"router","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"merchant 77"},{"from":"router","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"merchant 77"},{"from":"a","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"partial"},{"from":"b","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"partial"},{"from":"c","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"partial"}],"metrics":{"hot":"34","fan":"3"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Resharding: add a fourth shard","body":"Growth means adding Shard D. With plain hash mod N, going from 3 to 4 shards changes the owner of about 75 percent of keys, which is a huge copy. The standard fix is many fixed buckets, say 256, mapped to shards by the shard map. Adding D moves only whole buckets, roughly one quarter of the data, taken evenly from A, B and C.","idea":"Hash into fixed buckets and move buckets, so a new shard takes only its fair share of the data.","caption":"A, B and C each hand some buckets to D.","focus":["a","b","c","d"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"d","kind":"replicate","label":"buckets"},{"from":"b","to":"d","kind":"replicate","label":"buckets"},{"from":"c","to":"d","kind":"replicate","label":"buckets"}],"metrics":{"shards":"4","moved":"25"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Switching over safely","body":"Live traffic keeps writing while buckets copy. The usual pattern is: bulk copy, replay the changes made during the copy until D is almost caught up, briefly pause writes to those buckets, then update the shard map so the router points them at D. Old copies are deleted later. Clients see at most a short blip, never a stop.","idea":"Copy, catch up, flip the map, then clean up; the map change is the moment ownership moves.","caption":"The router receives a new shard map and routes to D.","focus":["router","map","d"],"flows":[{"from":"b","to":"d","kind":"replicate","label":"catch-up log"},{"from":"router","to":"map","kind":"request","label":"map version?"},{"from":"map","to":"router","kind":"response","label":"v8: buckets to D"}],"metrics":{"hot":"25","moved":"25"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What to remember","body":"Sharding splits rows across machines by a shard key. Hash partitioning spreads load but loses range scans; range partitioning keeps order but can create a hot newest shard. Pick a key that is high-cardinality and present in your main queries. Expect hot keys and scatter-gather as costs, and reshard by moving fixed buckets. Shard last: indexes, caching and replicas are cheaper first.","idea":"Shard only when one machine truly cannot cope, and choose the key for your queries.","caption":"Four shards, one router, one map.","focus":["client","router","map","a","b","c","d"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"shards":"4","hot":"25","fan":"1","moved":"25"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A cluster uses hash(key) mod N to pick a shard and grows from 3 to 4 shards. About what share of keys change owner?","options":["About 25 percent","About 50 percent","About 75 percent","None, hashing is stable"],"answer":2,"why":"Only keys whose value mod 3 equals mod 4 stay put, about one in four. The rest move, which is why fixed buckets in a shard map are preferred."},{"q":"Orders are range-partitioned by created_at. Write latency spikes on exactly one shard at peak while the others are idle. What is the cause?","options":["Every new order falls in the newest range, so that shard takes all inserts","Range partitioning cannot handle writes","The shard map is out of date","The other shards are replicas"],"answer":0,"why":"A monotonically increasing key sends all fresh writes to the last range. A hashed key, or a compound key that leads with something well spread, avoids it."},{"q":"A multi-tenant app shards by tenant_id with hashing, but one tenant sends 40 percent of traffic and its shard is overloaded. What best addresses it?","options":["Add more shards to the cluster","Switch from hash to range partitioning","Hash the tenant_id with a stronger function","Give that tenant its own shard, or split its key with a suffix across several shards"],"answer":3,"why":"All of one tenant's requests share one key, so any hash maps them to the same shard. More shards or a better hash cannot split a single key; you must change how that key is placed."},{"q":"In an 8-shard cluster you query by a column that is not the shard key. What happens, and what limits the latency?","options":["The router guesses one shard and may miss rows","It scatters to all 8 shards, and the slowest shard sets the latency","It scans only the shard that was most recently used","It fails, because non-key queries are not allowed"],"answer":1,"why":"Without the key the router cannot narrow the target, so it queries every shard and merges. The query waits for the slowest reply, so the chance of hitting one slow shard grows with fan-out."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Shard key","def":"The column(s) whose value decides which shard owns a row. Choose high cardinality, even spread, and presence in your hot queries."},{"term":"Hash partitioning","def":"Shard = hash(key) mapped to a bucket. Spreads load evenly, but adjacent keys are scattered, so range scans hit every shard."},{"term":"Range partitioning","def":"Each shard owns a contiguous key range. Great for range scans; risks hot shards when keys grow over time, like timestamps."},{"term":"Hot partition","def":"One shard receiving far more traffic than its peers, often due to a single popular key. Fix with caching, key splitting or a dedicated shard."},{"term":"Scatter-gather","def":"Sending a query to all shards and merging results. Latency is the slowest shard's, and cost grows with shard count."},{"term":"Fixed buckets","def":"Hash into many logical buckets (for example 256) and map buckets to shards. Resharding moves whole buckets instead of rehashing every key."},{"term":"Shard map","def":"Small lookup table of which shard owns which bucket or range. Routers cache it; updating it is the cutover moment in resharding."},{"term":"Rule of thumb","def":"Shard last. Try indexes, caching, read replicas and a bigger machine first, since sharding permanently complicates joins and transactions."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"consistent-hashing","title":"Consistent Hashing","subtitle":"Watch a cache cluster gain and lose nodes, and see why a hash ring moves a quarter of the keys instead of three quarters.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why hash(key) mod N reshuffles almost every key when N changes","How a hash ring maps keys and nodes onto one circle and walks clockwise","Why adding or removing a node moves only about 1/N of the keys","How virtual nodes even out load and handle uneven machines","Where rings show up: cache clients and Dynamo-style stores"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"App / cache client","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"ring","label":"Hash ring lookup","kind":"service","x":32,"y":50},{"id":"a","label":"Cache A","kind":"cache","x":68,"y":14},{"id":"b","label":"Cache B","kind":"cache","x":68,"y":36},{"id":"c","label":"Cache C","kind":"cache","x":68,"y":58},{"id":"d","label":"Cache D (new)","kind":"cache","x":68,"y":80},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":92,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"ring"},{"from":"ring","to":"a"},{"from":"ring","to":"b"},{"from":"ring","to":"c"},{"from":"ring","to":"d"},{"from":"a","to":"b"},{"from":"b","to":"c"},{"from":"c","to":"d"},{"from":"a","to":"db"},{"from":"b","to":"db"},{"from":"c","to":"db"},{"from":"d","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"moved","label":"Keys moved","unit":"%"},{"id":"hit","label":"Hit rate","unit":"%"},{"id":"hot","label":"Busiest node","unit":"% of avg"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Which cache holds this key?","body":"Your site keeps 30 million cached items on three cache servers, A, B and C, because no single machine has the memory. Every client must agree, with no coordination, on which server owns which key. If two clients disagree, one will write a value the other can never find. How do you split keys so everyone agrees, and so growing the cluster later does not hurt?","idea":"Partitioning needs a rule that every client computes identically, and that survives the cluster changing size.","caption":"Three caches, one shared rule needed.","focus":["client","a","b","c"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"moved":"0","hit":"95","hot":"100"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"The simple rule: hash mod N","body":"The obvious answer is server = hash(key) mod 3. A good hash spreads keys evenly, so each server gets about a third, and every client computes the same answer. The key user:42 might hash to 7, and 7 mod 3 is 1, so it lives on B. This works well for as long as the number of servers never changes.","idea":"hash(key) mod N is stateless and even, but it bakes the server count into every key's address.","caption":"user:42 hashes to B; hit rate is a healthy 95%.","focus":["client","ring","b"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"ring","kind":"request","label":"GET user:42"},{"from":"ring","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"7 mod 3 = B"},{"from":"b","to":"ring","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"ring","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"value"}],"metrics":{"moved":"0","hit":"95","hot":"100"}},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"Add one server, lose most of the cache","body":"Traffic grows, so you add Cache D and change the rule to mod 4. Now 7 mod 4 is 3, not 1. A key stays put only if its hash gives the same answer under both moduli, which is about one key in four. Roughly 75% of keys now map to a server that does not hold them. They all miss at once and stampede the database.","idea":"With mod N, going from N to N+1 servers remaps about N/(N+1) of all keys, so scaling out causes a cache wipe.","caption":"75% of lookups miss and fall through to the database.","focus":["ring","d","db"],"flows":[{"from":"ring","to":"d","kind":"request","label":"7 mod 4 = D"},{"from":"d","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"miss, query"}],"metrics":{"moved":"75","hit":"25","hot":"100"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Put servers and keys on a ring","body":"Consistent hashing hashes both servers and keys into the same range, say 0 to 2^32, and bends that range into a circle. Each server sits at the point where its name hashes. To find a key's owner, hash the key, then walk clockwise until you hit the first server. Every client can build the same ring from the same server list, so no coordination is needed.","idea":"A key belongs to the first node clockwise from its hash, so ownership depends on neighbours, not on the total count.","caption":"user:42 lands between A and B and walks clockwise to B.","focus":["client","ring","b"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"ring","kind":"request","label":"hash(user:42)"},{"from":"ring","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"next clockwise"},{"from":"b","to":"ring","kind":"response","label":"hit"},{"from":"ring","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"value"}],"metrics":{"moved":"0","hit":"95","hot":"100"}},{"tag":"ADD NODE","title":"A new node steals one arc","body":"Cache D joins and hashes to a point on the ring, between C and A. Only the keys on the arc just before D, which used to walk past to A, now stop at D. Every other key still reaches the same server as before. With four nodes that arc covers about a quarter of the keys, so 25% move, and D can fetch them from A or let them refill.","idea":"Adding a node moves only the keys it takes over, about 1/N of the total, and they all come from one neighbour.","caption":"Only the keys between C and D move to D.","focus":["c","d","a"],"flows":[{"from":"c","to":"d","kind":"replicate","label":"hand over arc"},{"from":"ring","to":"d","kind":"request","label":"new keys"}],"metrics":{"moved":"25","hit":"80","hot":"100"}},{"tag":"REMOVE NODE","title":"A failed node hands off to its successor","body":"Now Cache B crashes. Its keys do not scatter everywhere. Walking clockwise from B's old arc, the next node, C, becomes the owner. Keys owned by A, C and D are untouched, so their cached data stays warm. Only B's share of about a quarter misses once, then refills from the database.","idea":"Removing a node affects only the keys it owned; every other node keeps its data.","caption":"B's keys fall to C; A and D are unaffected.","focus":["b","c","db"],"flows":[{"from":"ring","to":"b","kind":"fail","label":"B is down"},{"from":"ring","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"B's keys"},{"from":"c","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"refill"}],"metrics":{"moved":"25","hit":"75","hot":"100"}},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"Random arcs are uneven","body":"Hashing four server names onto a circle gives arcs of random length, so one node can easily own 40% of the ring while another owns 10%. Worse, when a node dies its whole arc lands on one successor, which may double its load and then also fail. With one point per server, a hot node can run about twice the average.","idea":"With one point per server, ring positions are luck, and a dead node's load goes to a single neighbour.","caption":"C absorbs all of B's load and runs far above average.","focus":["c"],"flows":[{"from":"ring","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"2x traffic"}],"metrics":{"moved":"25","hit":"75","hot":"190"}},{"tag":"FIX","title":"Virtual nodes smooth the load","body":"Instead of one point, each physical server gets many virtual nodes (vnodes), for example 128 points, each hashed from a name like C#17. The server owns many small arcs scattered around the ring. Load averages out to within roughly 10% of equal, and when a server dies its arcs pass to many different neighbours. A bigger machine can simply be given more vnodes.","idea":"Many small arcs per server turn random imbalance into a near-even split, and spread a failure across all survivors.","caption":"Each server owns many scattered arcs; load is nearly even.","focus":["a","b","c","d"],"flows":[{"from":"ring","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"vnodes A#1..128"},{"from":"ring","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"vnodes B#1..128"},{"from":"ring","to":"c","kind":"request","label":"vnodes C#1..128"},{"from":"ring","to":"d","kind":"request","label":"vnodes D#1..128"}],"metrics":{"moved":"25","hit":"78","hot":"110"}},{"tag":"IN PRACTICE","title":"Caches and Dynamo-style stores","body":"Cache clients such as memcached libraries use a ring so adding a server does not flush everything. Dynamo-style databases, such as Cassandra, Riak and DynamoDB's design ancestors, go further: a key is stored on the next several distinct servers clockwise, typically 3, giving replication on top of partitioning. Any node can then rebuild the ring and route a request, with no central router.","idea":"The same ring that places keys also picks replicas: the next few distinct nodes clockwise.","caption":"A key is copied to the next nodes clockwise.","focus":["a","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"replica 2"},{"from":"b","to":"c","kind":"replicate","label":"replica 3"}],"metrics":{"moved":"25","hit":"78","hot":"110"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Stable ownership under change","body":"Mod N is simple but remaps about N/(N+1) of keys when the count changes. A hash ring maps keys and nodes to one circle, so a key belongs to the next node clockwise and a topology change moves only about 1/N of the keys. Virtual nodes fix uneven arcs and let big machines carry more. Rebalancing is limited, not free: moved keys still need to be copied or refilled.","idea":"Consistent hashing makes cluster membership changes local instead of global.","caption":"Ring plus vnodes: small, even, local moves.","focus":["client","ring","a","b","c","d","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"moved":"25","hit":"80","hot":"110"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A cluster using hash(key) mod N grows from 4 to 5 servers. About what fraction of keys now map to a different server?","options":["About 20%","About 50%","About 80%","About 100% exactly"],"answer":2,"why":"A key stays only if its hash gives the same remainder mod 4 and mod 5, roughly 1 in 5. So about 4/5, or 80%, move. It is not exactly 100% because some hashes coincide."},{"q":"On a hash ring with 5 equal nodes, a sixth is added. Roughly what share of keys move, and where do they come from?","options":["About 1/6 of keys, taken from neighbouring nodes on the ring","About 5/6 of keys, from every node equally","About 1/6 of keys, all from the new node's own old data","None, because ownership is already set"],"answer":0,"why":"The new node takes the arcs just before its points, about 1/6 of the ring. Those keys come from the nodes that were next clockwise, and every other key keeps its owner."},{"q":"Why do virtual nodes help when a physical server fails?","options":["They make the hash function faster","They stop the server from ever failing","They reduce the number of replicas needed","Its many small arcs are inherited by many different survivors, not one neighbour"],"answer":3,"why":"With one point per server, a dead node's whole arc goes to one successor, which can overload. With many vnodes the arcs are scattered, so the extra load is spread across most surviving nodes."},{"q":"A store uses a ring and replicates each key to the next 3 vnodes clockwise. A subtle bug: all 3 turn out to be vnodes of the same physical machine. What went wrong?","options":["Replica choice must skip vnodes of an already chosen physical node","The ring has too many vnodes","Replication should always use mod N instead","Three replicas is too many for any ring"],"answer":0,"why":"Replicas on one machine die together, so replication is useless. Dynamo-style systems walk the ring and take the next distinct physical nodes, skipping extra vnodes of the same machine."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"hash mod N","def":"Simple partitioning, server = hash(key) mod N. Even, but changing N remaps about N/(N+1) of keys."},{"term":"Hash ring","def":"A circular hash space. Nodes and keys both hash onto it; a key belongs to the first node clockwise."},{"term":"Keys moved on change","def":"Adding or removing one node moves about 1/N of the keys, and only to or from its ring neighbours."},{"term":"Virtual node (vnode)","def":"One of many ring points owned by a physical server, such as 100 to 256 per node. Evens out load and spreads failure."},{"term":"Weighted vnodes","def":"Give larger machines more vnodes so their share of the ring matches their capacity."},{"term":"Preference list","def":"In Dynamo-style stores, the next N distinct physical nodes clockwise from a key's position, used as its replicas."},{"term":"Still your job","def":"Moved keys must be copied or refilled, hot keys stay hot (a ring spreads keys, not traffic per key), and clients need a consistent node list."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"files","title":"Files & Object Storage","subtitle":"Follow a 2 GB video upload straight into an object store, and see why your API server never touches the bytes.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How block, file, and object storage differ and which fits which job","Why big files go in an object store and their metadata in a database","How a presigned URL lets a client upload without passing through your API","How multipart upload makes huge uploads resumable and parallel","How a CDN serves reads and how an upload event triggers background work"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client app","kind":"client","x":10,"y":50},{"id":"api","label":"API server","kind":"server","x":34,"y":50},{"id":"meta","label":"Metadata DB","kind":"db","x":58,"y":16},{"id":"store","label":"Object store","kind":"storage","x":82,"y":50},{"id":"cdn","label":"CDN edge","kind":"cdn","x":58,"y":84},{"id":"worker","label":"Post-upload worker","kind":"worker","x":88,"y":84}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"api"},{"from":"api","to":"meta"},{"from":"client","to":"store"},{"from":"api","to":"store"},{"from":"client","to":"cdn"},{"from":"cdn","to":"store"},{"from":"store","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","to":"meta"}],"metrics":[{"id":"apibytes","label":"API bytes","unit":"MB"},{"id":"parts","label":"Parts","unit":"count"},{"id":"urlttl","label":"URL lifetime","unit":"min"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Where do the bytes go?","body":"A user taps Upload on a 2 GB video. Your app also needs to remember who owns it, its title, and whether it has been processed. Do you push 2 GB through your API servers into a database column? Almost nobody does. This lesson shows where the bytes live, where the facts about them live, and how they travel between the two.","idea":"Big binary data and small structured facts want different homes.","caption":"A client holds a large file; the system must store it safely.","focus":["client"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Block, file, object","body":"Block storage is a raw disk: fixed-size blocks you read and write at an offset, ideal for a database's data files or a VM disk. File storage adds folders and paths on top and lets many machines share them (NFS-style). Object storage, like S3, is a flat bucket of whole objects found by key: you PUT or GET the entire thing, and you cannot edit bytes in the middle. In exchange you get huge scale, cheap cost per GB, and very high durability (S3 advertises 11 nines).","idea":"Block is for databases and VMs, file is for shared folders, object is for blobs at scale.","caption":"The object store holds whole, immutable blobs addressed by key.","focus":["store"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"The naive path: proxy it","body":"The simplest design: client sends the file to the API, and the API forwards it to the object store. It works, but now every upload ties up an API connection for the whole transfer. Ten thousand users each uploading 2 GB means your API fleet moves 20 TB, pays for the bandwidth twice (in and out), and a slow phone on bad Wi-Fi holds a worker for minutes.","idea":"Proxying uploads makes your API a bandwidth pipe, which is the wrong thing to scale.","caption":"Every byte passes through the API server twice.","focus":["client","api","store"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"2 GB upload"},{"from":"api","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"2 GB forwarded"}],"metrics":{"apibytes":"2048"}},{"tag":"PATTERN","title":"Metadata DB + blob","body":"Split the job. The blob lives in the object store under a key such as videos/u42/9f3a.mp4. A row in the metadata database records the facts: owner, title, size, content type, the object key, and a status like pending. The database answers questions like list my videos by date, which an object store cannot do well (it only lists keys by prefix). The row is the index; the blob is the payload.","idea":"Store the key and facts in a database; store the bytes in the object store.","caption":"API records the file's facts as a pending row.","focus":["api","meta"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"meta","kind":"request","label":"INSERT pending"},{"from":"meta","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"id + key"}],"metrics":{"apibytes":"0"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Presigned URL","body":"The API now asks its storage library to sign a URL for exactly one operation: PUT this key, valid for 15 minutes. Signing is local math with a secret the client never sees, so it costs microseconds and no storage call. The client receives the URL and can upload to the store directly. Anyone holding the URL can use it until it expires, so keep lifetimes short and scope it to one key.","idea":"A presigned URL is a short-lived permission slip: the API grants it, the store honors it.","caption":"API hands the client a signed, expiring upload URL.","focus":["client","api"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"may I upload?"},{"from":"api","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"presigned PUT"}],"metrics":{"urlttl":"15"}},{"tag":"MULTIPART","title":"Upload in parts","body":"A single 2 GB PUT is fragile: if the connection drops at 95 percent, you start over. Multipart upload splits the file into parts (S3 needs 5 MiB or more per part except the last, up to 10,000 parts). The client starts an upload, sends 32 parts of 64 MB in parallel, collects an ETag for each, then sends a complete call listing them. A failed part is retried alone, and the store stitches the parts into one object.","idea":"Multipart makes big uploads parallel and resumable: retry a part, not the file.","caption":"Client uploads 32 parts straight to the object store.","focus":["client","store"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"parts 1..32"},{"from":"store","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"ETag per part"},{"from":"client","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"complete"}],"metrics":{"parts":"32"}},{"tag":"ASYNC","title":"React to the upload","body":"When the object appears, the store emits an event (for example ObjectCreated) to a queue or function. A worker picks it up, checks the file, generates thumbnails or transcodes the video, and updates the metadata row from pending to ready. The client never waits on this. If the worker crashes, the event is retried, so processing must be safe to run twice.","idea":"Use storage events to trigger background work and flip the row to ready.","caption":"Object arrives, event fires, worker marks the row ready.","focus":["store","worker","meta"],"flows":[{"from":"store","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"ObjectCreated"},{"from":"worker","to":"meta","kind":"request","label":"status=ready"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"READ","title":"Serve it from the edge","body":"To watch the video, the client looks up the key in the metadata DB through the API, then fetches the object. Public content usually goes through a CDN: the first viewer in a region causes a cache miss and the CDN pulls from the object store, and later viewers get the cached copy from nearby. Private files use a presigned GET URL instead. Reads of new objects are strongly consistent in S3, so a fresh upload is visible immediately.","idea":"Read through a CDN for speed and cost; use presigned GET for private files.","caption":"CDN fills from the store on a miss, then serves nearby.","focus":["client","cdn","store"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"cdn","kind":"request","label":"GET video"},{"from":"cdn","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"cache miss"},{"from":"store","to":"cdn","kind":"response","label":"object bytes"},{"from":"cdn","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"video"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The upload is abandoned","body":"The user closes the app after 20 of 32 parts. Those parts sit in the store, invisible but billed, and the row is stuck on pending. Two cleanups fix this: a lifecycle rule that aborts incomplete multipart uploads after, say, 7 days, and a job that deletes pending rows older than a day. The mirror problem is deleting a row but forgetting the blob, so deletes should remove both.","idea":"Two systems mean two cleanups: expire stale multipart parts and stale pending rows.","caption":"A dropped upload leaves orphaned parts behind.","focus":["client","store","meta"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"store","kind":"fail","label":"connection lost"}],"metrics":{"parts":"20"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The whole pattern","body":"Bytes go in an object store, facts go in a metadata database, and the API only brokers permission. A presigned URL lets the client upload directly, multipart makes huge files resumable, events trigger processing, and a CDN serves reads. Block storage stays for databases and VM disks. Object stores cannot edit in place, so changing a file means writing a new object.","idea":"Keep your API out of the data path: sign a URL, let the client talk to the store.","caption":"API handles tiny messages; the store handles gigabytes.","focus":["client","api","meta","store","cdn","worker"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"apibytes":"0","parts":"32","urlttl":"15"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do most designs hand the client a presigned URL instead of proxying the upload through the API?","options":["Object stores refuse requests that come from API servers","Presigned URLs encrypt the file, and the API cannot","The file bytes skip your API, so it only handles a tiny request and scales independently of upload volume","It removes the need for a metadata database"],"answer":2,"why":"The signature is created locally and the client sends bytes directly to the store, so API servers do not carry upload bandwidth. You still need a database for metadata."},{"q":"A 2 GB multipart upload of 32 parts fails on part 27 because of a network blip. What should the client do?","options":["Retry only part 27, then complete the upload","Restart all 32 parts","Switch to block storage","Delete the metadata row and ask the user to try again later"],"answer":0,"why":"Each part is uploaded and acknowledged independently with its own ETag, so only the failed part needs to be resent before the complete call."},{"q":"Why store the object key and attributes in a database row rather than relying on the bucket alone?","options":["Buckets cannot hold more than 1 million objects","Object keys cannot contain the user's name","Objects are encrypted so they cannot be read back","The database can query by owner, date or status, while a bucket mainly lists keys by prefix"],"answer":3,"why":"Object stores are good at get-by-key and prefix listing. Questions like all videos owned by user 42 this week belong in a database that can index those fields."},{"q":"You need to update a single 4 KB page in the middle of a large data file many times per second. Which storage fits best?","options":["Object storage, rewriting the object each time","Block storage, which supports reads and writes at an offset","A CDN with a short TTL","A presigned URL with a long lifetime"],"answer":1,"why":"Block storage allows small in-place writes at an offset, which is why databases use it. Objects are replaced whole, so a 4 KB edit would mean rewriting the entire object."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Block storage","def":"Raw fixed-size blocks read or written at an offset. Used for database files and VM disks. Low latency, in-place updates."},{"term":"File storage","def":"Hierarchical folders and paths, often shared by many machines over a network. Good for shared home directories and legacy apps."},{"term":"Object storage","def":"Flat bucket of whole objects found by key. Cheap, huge, durable, but no partial edits: replace the whole object."},{"term":"Metadata DB + blob","def":"Keep owner, size, status and the object key in a database row; keep the bytes in the object store. The row is how you search and list."},{"term":"Presigned URL","def":"A time-limited URL signed by your server for one operation on one key. Lets a client talk directly to the store. Keep expiry short."},{"term":"Multipart upload","def":"Split a large file into parts (S3: 5 MiB to 5 GiB each, max 10,000), upload in parallel, retry failed parts, then complete. Abort stale ones with a lifecycle rule."},{"term":"Storage events","def":"The store emits a notification when an object is created, so a worker can scan, resize or transcode it. Handlers must be idempotent."},{"term":"CDN in front","def":"Cache popular objects at edge locations. Cuts latency and egress cost; use presigned GET for private content."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"search-index","title":"Search & Inverted Indexes","subtitle":"Watch a text query hit a sharded search cluster, and see how data from the main database gets into it.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How text is tokenized and normalized so queries match documents","What an inverted index is and why it beats scanning every document","How TF-IDF and BM25 turn matches into a ranked list","How a query fans out to shards and the results are merged","How the search index stays in sync with the primary database, and what happens when it lags"],"nodes":[{"id":"user","label":"Search box","kind":"user","x":8,"y":40},{"id":"api","label":"Search service","kind":"service","x":31,"y":40},{"id":"s1","label":"Shard 1","kind":"storage","x":60,"y":14},{"id":"s2","label":"Shard 2","kind":"storage","x":60,"y":40},{"id":"s3","label":"Shard 3","kind":"storage","x":60,"y":66},{"id":"db","label":"Primary database","kind":"db","x":8,"y":88},{"id":"feed","label":"Change stream","kind":"queue","x":31,"y":88},{"id":"indexer","label":"Indexer","kind":"worker","x":60,"y":88}],"edges":[{"from":"user","to":"api"},{"from":"api","to":"s1"},{"from":"api","to":"s2"},{"from":"api","to":"s3"},{"from":"db","to":"feed"},{"from":"feed","to":"indexer"},{"from":"indexer","to":"s3"}],"metrics":[{"id":"query","label":"Query time","unit":"ms"},{"id":"shards","label":"Shards hit","unit":""},{"id":"lag","label":"Index lag","unit":"s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Find the words, fast","body":"A shop has 300 million product listings, about 1 KB of text each, so roughly 300 GB. A user types \"wireless headphones\". Reading every listing and checking for the words would mean scanning 300 GB; at 1 GB/s that is about five minutes. A B-tree index on one column does not help, because the user's words can be anywhere in the text. Search needs a different structure.","idea":"Scanning text per query is hopeless; search engines precompute where every word lives.","caption":"A query must not touch 300 million documents.","focus":["user","api"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"wireless headph."}],"metrics":{"query":"300000","shards":"0","lag":"0"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Tokenize and normalize","body":"Before anything is stored, text goes through an analyzer. It splits \"Wireless Headphones, 40% off!\" into tokens, lowercases them, drops punctuation, and often removes filler words like \"the\". Many analyzers also stem words, so \"headphones\" and \"headphone\" become one term. The same analyzer must run on the query, or \"Headphones\" would never match \"headphone\".","idea":"Index time and query time must use the same analysis, or terms will not line up.","caption":"Raw text becomes a list of normalized terms.","focus":["user","api"],"flows":[{"from":"user","to":"api","kind":"request","label":"Wireless Headph."}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"The inverted index","body":"An inverted index maps each term to a posting list: the sorted IDs of documents containing it. Say wireless appears in docs 3, 8, 21 and headphone in 8, 21, 40. For a query needing both words, the engine walks the two sorted lists and intersects them, giving docs 8 and 21. It reads two short lists instead of 300 million documents.","idea":"An inverted index flips documents-to-words into words-to-documents, so a lookup touches only relevant IDs.","caption":"One shard intersects two posting lists.","focus":["api","s2"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"s2","kind":"request","label":"wireless+headphone"},{"from":"s2","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"docs 8, 21"}],"metrics":{"query":"6","shards":"1"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Ranking the matches","body":"Matching is not enough; thousands of listings may contain both words, and the user wants the best first. TF-IDF scores a document higher when the term appears often in it (term frequency) and lower when the term is common everywhere (inverse document frequency), since \"the\" tells you nothing. BM25, the default in Lucene-based engines, refines this: repeated terms give diminishing returns and long documents are penalized.","idea":"Relevance rewards frequent-in-this-document and rare-in-the-corpus terms; BM25 adds saturation and length normalization.","caption":"The shard returns matches with scores attached.","focus":["api","s2"],"flows":[{"from":"s2","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"doc 21: 7.3"}],"metrics":{"query":"9"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Shard the index","body":"300 million documents will not fit well on one machine, so the index is split into shards. Each document goes to one shard by hashing its ID. The search service does not know which shard holds the best answers, so it sends the query to all of them in parallel (scatter). Each shard searches its own third of the data, about 100 million documents.","idea":"Sharding splits the data; every query fans out to every shard because any shard may hold matches.","caption":"The query is scattered to all three shards.","focus":["api","s1","s2","s3"],"flows":[{"from":"api","to":"s1","kind":"request","label":"query"},{"from":"api","to":"s2","kind":"request","label":"query"},{"from":"api","to":"s3","kind":"request","label":"query"}],"metrics":{"shards":"3","query":"12"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Gather and merge","body":"Each shard replies with only its own top 10 hits and their scores. The search service merges the three short lists and keeps the global top 10. Total time is set by the slowest shard, so one sluggish shard slows every query. Deep pages are costly: asking for results 1000 to 1010 forces each shard to return its top 1010.","idea":"Latency is the slowest shard plus merge cost, and deep pagination multiplies the work.","caption":"Shards reply; the service merges the top hits.","focus":["api","s1","s2","s3"],"flows":[{"from":"s1","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"top 10"},{"from":"s2","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"top 10"},{"from":"s3","to":"api","kind":"response","label":"top 10"},{"from":"api","to":"user","kind":"response","label":"10 results"}],"metrics":{"query":"35"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Syncing from the database","body":"The primary database is the source of truth; the search index is a derived copy. When a seller edits a listing, the change is read from the database's change log and put on a stream. An indexer consumes it, re-analyzes the text, and writes to the right shard. Writing to both database and index from the app is risky: if one write fails, they silently disagree.","idea":"Treat the index as a rebuildable copy fed by a change stream, not as a second place to write.","caption":"A DB change flows through the stream into a shard.","focus":["db","feed","indexer","s3"],"flows":[{"from":"db","to":"feed","kind":"async","label":"row changed"},{"from":"feed","to":"indexer","kind":"async","label":"listing 8841"},{"from":"indexer","to":"s3","kind":"async","label":"index doc"}],"metrics":{"lag":"2"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The indexer falls behind","body":"Suppose the indexer crashes or a bulk price update floods the stream. Searches still work, but they show stale data: a sold-out item still appears, a new listing is missing. Index lag climbs from seconds to minutes. Because the stream keeps the events, a restarted indexer replays them. If the index is badly wrong, rebuild it from the database into a fresh index and swap it in.","idea":"Search is eventually consistent with the database; monitor lag and keep a way to replay or rebuild.","caption":"The indexer stalls; changes pile up in the stream.","focus":["feed","indexer"],"flows":[{"from":"feed","to":"indexer","kind":"fail","label":"consumer down"}],"metrics":{"lag":"180"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"From words to ranked results","body":"Analyzers turn text into normalized terms. An inverted index maps each term to the documents containing it, so a query reads short posting lists instead of everything. BM25-style scoring ranks the matches. Sharding spreads the data, queries scatter to all shards and merge top hits, and a change stream keeps the index fed from the database, accepting some lag.","idea":"Search = analyze, invert, score, shard, and feed the index from the source of truth.","caption":"The full path: sync in the background, search in milliseconds.","focus":["user","api","s1","s2","s3","db","feed","indexer"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"query":"35","shards":"3","lag":"2"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your index stores the term \"headphone\", but a user searches \"Headphones\" and gets zero results. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The posting list for headphone is too long","The query was not run through the same analyzer (lowercasing and stemming) as the documents","There are too few shards","BM25 scored the document too low to show"],"answer":1,"why":"Matching happens on normalized terms. If the query skips the analysis used at index time, its terms never line up with the stored ones."},{"q":"A query for \"the red shoes\" returns plenty of documents. Why does the word \"the\" barely affect their ranking?","options":["Its inverse document frequency is very low because almost every document contains it","Its term frequency is always zero","Stemming removes it from the query","Posting lists cannot store common terms"],"answer":0,"why":"IDF measures rarity across the corpus. A term in nearly every document has a tiny IDF, so it adds almost nothing to the score."},{"q":"A 3-shard cluster has p50 shard latency of 10 ms, but one shard often takes 120 ms. What happens to search latency?","options":["It stays near 10 ms because results are averaged","It becomes about 40 ms, the mean of the shards","Many queries take about 120 ms, since the merge waits for the slowest shard","Only 1/3 of queries are affected"],"answer":2,"why":"A scattered query is not complete until every shard replies, so the slowest shard sets latency for every query it participates in, and that is all of them."},{"q":"Your app writes a product to the database, then to the search index. The index write fails after the DB write succeeds. Which design best prevents silent drift?","options":["Retry the index write forever inside the web request","Write to the index first, then the database","Feed the index from the database's change stream so missed updates can be replayed","Skip the database and store products only in the search index"],"answer":2,"why":"A change stream makes the database the single source of truth and lets an indexer retry or replay changes. Dual writes from the app have no such recovery path."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Tokenization / analysis","def":"Splitting text into terms and normalizing them (lowercase, strip punctuation, drop stop words, stem). Must be identical at index and query time."},{"term":"Inverted index","def":"Map from each term to a sorted posting list of document IDs that contain it. Multi-word queries intersect or union the lists."},{"term":"TF-IDF","def":"Score = how often a term appears in a document, weighted by how rare it is across all documents. Common words count for little."},{"term":"BM25","def":"Modern TF-IDF refinement: repeated terms saturate (parameter k1, often 1.2) and long documents are normalized (parameter b, often 0.75)."},{"term":"Scatter-gather","def":"Send the query to every shard, take each shard's top N, merge into the global top N. Latency follows the slowest shard."},{"term":"Deep pagination","def":"Asking for page 100 makes every shard return 100 pages of hits. Prefer search-after cursors over large offsets."},{"term":"Change stream / CDC","def":"A feed of row changes read from the database log, consumed by an indexer. Keeps the index a rebuildable copy of the source of truth."},{"term":"Index lag","def":"Delay between a database change and its visibility in search. Typically seconds; track it and alert when it grows."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"geospatial","title":"Geospatial Indexing","subtitle":"Watch a ride app find the nearest drivers by turning 2D space into cells it can look up directly.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why latitude and longitude do not work well with ordinary indexes","How geohash turns a location into a string whose prefix means 'same box'","How a quadtree adapts to crowded and empty areas","What S2 and H3 cells add, and why you search a cell plus its neighbours","How a live driver index is kept fresh and queried in a few milliseconds"],"nodes":[{"id":"rider","label":"Rider app","kind":"user","x":10,"y":22},{"id":"driver","label":"Driver app","kind":"client","x":10,"y":78},{"id":"search","label":"Nearby service","kind":"service","x":36,"y":22},{"id":"queue","label":"Location stream","kind":"queue","x":36,"y":78},{"id":"worker","label":"Index updater","kind":"worker","x":62,"y":78},{"id":"cache","label":"Cell index","kind":"cache","x":62,"y":26},{"id":"db","label":"Driver store","kind":"db","x":88,"y":52}],"edges":[{"from":"rider","to":"search"},{"from":"search","to":"cache"},{"from":"search","to":"db"},{"from":"driver","to":"queue"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker"},{"from":"worker","to":"cache"}],"metrics":[{"id":"candidates","label":"Candidates","unit":"drivers"},{"id":"cells","label":"Cells read","unit":"cells"},{"id":"latency","label":"Query time","unit":"ms"},{"id":"pings","label":"Location pings","unit":"/sec"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Who is closest to me?","body":"A rider taps 'request ride' at one point in a city. About 200,000 drivers are online in the metro area, each one a latitude and longitude that changes every few seconds. The app must return the 5 closest available drivers in well under a second. How do you search positions without checking every driver?","idea":"Proximity search asks for everything near a point, and a point has two coordinates, not one.","caption":"A rider asks for nearby drivers.","focus":["rider","search"],"flows":[{"from":"rider","to":"search","kind":"request","label":"near 12.97, 77.59"}],"metrics":{"candidates":"200000"}},{"tag":"PROBLEM","title":"The naive scan","body":"The simplest answer computes the distance to every driver and sorts. That is 200,000 distance calculations per request. An ordinary B-tree index on latitude does not rescue you: it narrows one axis to a long horizontal stripe across the whole city, and you still filter the rest. Assume this scan costs roughly 400 ms from a database; that is an assumption, but it is far too slow at thousands of requests per second.","idea":"A one-dimensional index cannot answer a two-dimensional 'nearby' question efficiently.","caption":"Scanning every driver is slow.","focus":["search","db"],"flows":[{"from":"search","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"scan 200k rows"}],"metrics":{"candidates":"200000","latency":"400"}},{"tag":"GEOHASH","title":"Geohash: space as a string","body":"Geohash repeatedly halves the map, alternating longitude and latitude, and writes the resulting bits as a short string such as 'tdr1v7'. Points in the same box share the same prefix. At 6 characters a cell is roughly 1.2 km by 0.6 km; at 7 it is about 150 m square. Now 'nearby' becomes an ordinary lookup: fetch the drivers stored under the rider's cell key.","idea":"Geohash turns a 2D position into a string key, so a plain key lookup or prefix scan finds a neighbourhood.","caption":"Look up one cell key, not every driver.","focus":["search","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"search","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"cell tdr1v7"},{"from":"cache","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"14 drivers"}],"metrics":{"cells":"1","candidates":"14","latency":"4"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"The boundary problem","body":"A rider standing at the edge of a cell may have the closest driver just across the line, in the next cell with a completely different prefix. Close points can have different hashes, and similar prefixes can be far apart. The standard fix is to query the rider's cell plus its 8 neighbours, then compute exact distances on those few results.","idea":"Cells are a coarse filter: search the cell and its neighbours, then rank by exact distance.","caption":"Query 9 cells, get a short candidate list.","focus":["search","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"search","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"own + 8 neighbours"},{"from":"cache","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"~70 candidates"}],"metrics":{"cells":"9","candidates":"70","latency":"8"}},{"tag":"QUADTREE","title":"Quadtree: cells that adapt","body":"A quadtree starts with the whole map as one box. Whenever a box holds more than a set number of points, say 100, it splits into four children, and so on. Dense downtown ends up in tiny boxes while an empty suburb stays one big box. Lookups descend the tree. The catch is that it usually lives in memory on one node, and moving drivers mean constant inserts and removals.","idea":"A quadtree splits only where data is dense, so every leaf holds a similar number of points.","caption":"Tree descends to a small leaf box.","focus":["search","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"search","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"descend tree"},{"from":"cache","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"leaf + siblings"}],"metrics":{"cells":"4","candidates":"60","latency":"5"}},{"tag":"S2 / H3","title":"S2 and H3: cells on a sphere","body":"Flat grids distort near the poles and edges. Google's S2 maps the globe onto a cube and orders cells along a space-filling curve, so each cell is a single 64-bit id and nearby cells get nearby ids. Uber's H3 uses hexagons, whose 6 neighbours are all the same distance away, which makes 'rings' of cells simple. At H3 resolution 8, a hexagon is about 0.7 square km.","idea":"S2 and H3 are geohash's idea done on the sphere: fixed ids per cell, with cheap neighbour and ring lookups.","caption":"Search the rider's hexagon plus a ring.","focus":["search","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"search","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"H3 res 8, ring 1"},{"from":"cache","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"ids in 7 cells"}],"metrics":{"cells":"7","candidates":"55","latency":"5"}},{"tag":"UPDATES","title":"Keeping drivers fresh","body":"Each driver app sends a GPS ping every 4 seconds, so 200,000 drivers produce about 50,000 writes per second. Pings go onto a stream, and an updater reads them. It writes only when a driver crosses into a new cell: remove the id from the old cell's set, add it to the new one. Entries expire after a short TTL so drivers who go offline drop out on their own.","idea":"The index changes constantly, so write only on cell changes and let stale entries expire.","caption":"Pings flow in; cell moves update the index.","focus":["driver","queue","worker","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"driver","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"GPS ping"},{"from":"queue","to":"worker","kind":"async","label":"batch"},{"from":"worker","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"move cell A to B"}],"metrics":{"pings":"50000"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Rank and answer","body":"With about 60 candidate ids in hand, the service loads each driver's current coordinates, rating, and availability from the driver store. It computes true distance, drops anyone who is busy or too far, sorts, and returns the top 5. The cell lookup shrank 200,000 drivers to a few dozen, so the expensive exact work runs on almost nothing.","idea":"Two phases win: a cheap cell filter to shortlist, then exact math on the shortlist.","caption":"Shortlist, exact distance, top 5 back.","focus":["rider","search","cache","db"],"flows":[{"from":"search","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"cells"},{"from":"cache","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"60 ids"},{"from":"search","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"load 60"},{"from":"db","to":"search","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"search","to":"rider","kind":"response","label":"top 5"}],"metrics":{"cells":"9","candidates":"60","latency":"12"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A stadium empties","body":"Fixed-size cells assume roughly even density. After a concert, 5,000 drivers and riders pile into a single cell, so one lookup returns a huge list while the quiet cell next door is empty. Fixes: use a finer resolution where cells are busy, let a quadtree split that area, or cap the shortlist and expand outward ring by ring only if you need more.","idea":"Uneven density breaks fixed cells; go finer or adaptive in hot spots.","caption":"One hot cell returns thousands of ids.","focus":["search","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"cache","to":"search","kind":"fail","label":"5,000 ids"}],"metrics":{"cells":"1","candidates":"5000","latency":"60"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"From 200,000 to a few dozen","body":"Proximity search is hard because 'near' is two-dimensional. Geohash, quadtrees, S2, and H3 all map space to cells so that a lookup replaces a scan. Always search the cell plus its neighbours, then rank by exact distance. Keep the index in memory, update it only when a driver changes cell, and choose cell size to match how crowded the area is.","idea":"Index space as cells, shortlist by cell and neighbours, then rank exactly.","caption":"Cells turn a scan into a lookup.","focus":["rider","driver","search","queue","worker","cache","db"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"cells":"9","candidates":"60","latency":"12","pings":"50000"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can't a plain B-tree index on latitude alone make 'drivers within 2 km' fast?","options":["B-trees cannot store decimal numbers","It narrows only one axis, leaving a long stripe of rows to filter by longitude","Latitude values are not sortable","It only works for exact matches"],"answer":1,"why":"A latitude range selects a band across the whole city. The longitude condition still has to be checked row by row inside it, so the index helps far less than for a one-dimensional query."},{"q":"A rider sits right on the edge of a geohash cell. What query avoids missing the closest driver?","options":["Search only the rider's own cell with a longer prefix","Search the rider's cell and its 8 neighbours, then sort by exact distance","Sort all drivers by their geohash string","Retry with a shorter hash only if the first result is empty"],"answer":1,"why":"Nearby points can fall in different cells. Reading the neighbours covers the boundary, and exact distance on the small shortlist picks the true closest."},{"q":"Which situation most favours a quadtree over fixed-size cells?","options":["Drivers spread evenly across a flat plain","A dense downtown next to large empty areas","Needing a 64-bit id per location","Storing the index only on disk"],"answer":1,"why":"A quadtree splits only where points are dense, so crowded and empty regions both end up with a manageable number of points per leaf. Fixed cells waste work on empty areas or overflow in busy ones."},{"q":"Drivers ping every 4 seconds, yet the index is written far less often than that. What makes this possible?","options":["Pings are dropped at random","The index is written only when a driver moves into a different cell","Cells are recomputed once per hour","Riders update the index instead"],"answer":1,"why":"Most pings keep the driver in the same cell, so the cell membership does not change. Writing only on cell changes, plus TTL expiry for stale entries, cuts index writes substantially."},{"q":"After a concert, one cell returns 5,000 candidates and queries slow down. What is the most sensible response?","options":["Use a coarser cell size everywhere","Use finer cells or an adaptive split in that hot area","Remove the neighbour lookup","Move the index to disk"],"answer":1,"why":"The cell is too coarse for that density. A finer resolution or a quadtree split keeps the shortlist small where the crowd is, without changing quiet areas."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Geohash","def":"String encoding of a location from interleaved lat/lon bits. Longer string means smaller box; a shared prefix means the same enclosing box."},{"term":"Boundary problem","def":"Close points can sit in different cells. Query the cell plus its neighbours, then rank by exact distance."},{"term":"Quadtree","def":"Recursive 4-way split of the map, splitting a box only when it holds too many points. Adapts to uneven density."},{"term":"S2","def":"Google's library: sphere mapped to a cube, cells ordered along a Hilbert curve, each cell a 64-bit id with 31 levels."},{"term":"H3","def":"Uber's hexagonal grid with 16 resolutions. Every neighbour is equidistant, so ring and distance queries are simple."},{"term":"Filter then rank","def":"Use cells as a cheap coarse filter to shortlist tens of candidates, then compute exact distance on just those."},{"term":"Cell size choice","def":"Rule of thumb: pick a size that holds tens of results, not thousands. Go finer in dense areas, widen rings in sparse ones."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"time-series","title":"Time-Series Data","subtitle":"Watch a metrics system absorb 100,000 points per second, roll old data up, and drop it on schedule.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why time-series workloads are append-heavy and mostly read as recent ranges","How time-partitioned chunks make writes, queries and deletes cheap","How rollups (downsampling) trade detail for speed and storage","Why retention is a policy of dropping whole chunks, not deleting rows","How high-cardinality labels blow up the number of series"],"nodes":[{"id":"agents","label":"App hosts","kind":"client","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"queue","label":"Ingest queue","kind":"queue","x":29,"y":50},{"id":"writer","label":"Ingest writer","kind":"worker","x":50,"y":50},{"id":"hot","label":"Raw chunks (hot)","kind":"db","x":72,"y":22},{"id":"rollup","label":"Rollup job","kind":"worker","x":72,"y":78},{"id":"cold","label":"Rollup store","kind":"storage","x":92,"y":78},{"id":"dash","label":"Dashboard","kind":"user","x":92,"y":22}],"edges":[{"from":"agents","to":"queue"},{"from":"queue","to":"writer"},{"from":"writer","to":"hot"},{"from":"hot","to":"rollup"},{"from":"rollup","to":"cold"},{"from":"dash","to":"hot"},{"from":"dash","to":"cold"}],"metrics":[{"id":"writes","label":"Writes","unit":"k pts/s"},{"id":"rawday","label":"Raw per day","unit":"GB"},{"id":"keep","label":"Raw kept","unit":"days"},{"id":"query","label":"Query time","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"A firehose of numbers","body":"Picture 10,000 servers, each reporting 100 metrics (CPU, latency, queue depth) every 10 seconds. That is 1 million separate series and 100,000 new points per second, forever. Nobody edits old points, and people mostly ask about the last hour. How do you store this so it stays fast and cheap? These numbers are an assumed scenario.","idea":"Time-series data is a never-ending stream of timestamped appends, read mostly by recent time range.","caption":"Many hosts, constant small writes.","focus":["agents"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"writes":"100","rawday":"17","keep":"0","query":"0"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Buffer the stream","body":"Each host sends points as (series name, timestamp, value). They land in an ingest queue first, so a slow database or a traffic spike delays points instead of losing them. The writer then pulls batches rather than handling 100,000 tiny writes one by one.","idea":"Buffer and batch: appends are cheap in bulk and painful one at a time.","caption":"Points flow through a buffer to the writer.","focus":["agents","queue","writer"],"flows":[{"from":"agents","to":"queue","kind":"async","label":"name,ts,value"},{"from":"queue","to":"writer","kind":"async","label":"batch of 5,000"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Append into the newest chunk","body":"The writer groups points by series and writes them into the chunk for the current time window, say one day. Within a chunk, timestamps and values compress well: consecutive timestamps differ by a steady 10 seconds, and values change slowly. A good scheme gets raw points down to roughly 1 to 2 bytes each, so a day is about 17 GB here. Slightly late points are still accepted into recent chunks.","idea":"Writes only touch the newest chunk, and regular timestamps compress extremely well.","caption":"Today's chunk takes all the writes.","focus":["writer","hot"],"flows":[{"from":"writer","to":"hot","kind":"request","label":"append to today"},{"from":"hot","to":"writer","kind":"response","label":"ack"}],"metrics":{"rawday":"17"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Query a time range","body":"A dashboard asks for one service's p95 latency over the last hour. Because data is partitioned by time, the database opens only today's chunk and skips every older one. This is partition pruning, and it is the main reason time-partitioned storage stays fast as history grows. A narrow query like this returns in tens of milliseconds.","idea":"Time partitioning lets a range query ignore almost all the data.","caption":"One chunk is read; the rest are skipped.","focus":["dash","hot"],"flows":[{"from":"dash","to":"hot","kind":"request","label":"last 1h, svc=api"},{"from":"hot","to":"dash","kind":"response","label":"360 points"}],"metrics":{"query":"40"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Long ranges hurt","body":"Now someone charts one metric across 90 days from raw data. The database must open 90 chunks and read every point for the matching series, yet the screen can only draw a couple thousand points anyway. Scanning far more detail than the chart can show wastes time and money. In a big fleet this kind of query can take many seconds.","idea":"Raw points answer long-range questions slowly, and the chart cannot show that detail anyway.","caption":"A 90-day raw scan is slow.","focus":["dash","hot"],"flows":[{"from":"dash","to":"hot","kind":"fail","label":"90 days raw"}],"metrics":{"query":"9000"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Roll up into coarser points","body":"A rollup job reads finished raw chunks and writes summaries: one point per series per minute, then one per hour. Each summary keeps count, sum, min and max, not just the average, so later math stays correct. A 1-minute rollup has 6 times fewer points than 10-second data, and an hourly one has 360 times fewer. Percentiles cannot be averaged, so they need stored histograms or sketches.","idea":"Downsampling trades detail for a huge cut in points; keep count, sum, min and max so it stays useful.","caption":"Raw data is summarized into minute and hour points.","focus":["hot","rollup","cold"],"flows":[{"from":"hot","to":"rollup","kind":"async","label":"read old chunk"},{"from":"rollup","to":"cold","kind":"async","label":"1m + 1h points"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Serve long ranges from rollups","body":"The 90-day chart now reads hourly rollups: about 2,160 points per series instead of over 700,000 raw ones. The query layer picks the coarsest resolution that still gives enough points for the screen. The chart that took seconds returns in tens of milliseconds, with the trade-off that you cannot zoom back into 10-second detail.","idea":"Pick the resolution to match the question: coarse for long ranges, raw for short ones.","caption":"Long queries are answered from rollups.","focus":["dash","cold"],"flows":[{"from":"dash","to":"cold","kind":"request","label":"90 days, 1h step"},{"from":"cold","to":"dash","kind":"response","label":"2,160 points"}],"metrics":{"query":"30"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Retention: drop, do not delete","body":"Nobody needs 10-second data from a year ago, so a retention policy might keep raw data for 15 days, 1-minute rollups for 13 months, and hourly ones for years. Because each chunk covers a fixed time window, expiry means dropping a whole old chunk, which is close to deleting a file. Running DELETE on billions of rows would be slow and leave the table bloated. These durations are examples; pick yours from alerting and audit needs.","idea":"Retention works best as dropping whole time chunks, not deleting individual rows.","caption":"Expired raw chunks are removed whole.","focus":["hot","rollup"],"flows":[{"from":"rollup","to":"hot","kind":"async","label":"safe to drop"}],"metrics":{"keep":"15"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A label explodes the series count","body":"A developer adds user_id as a label on a request-count metric. Every distinct label combination is its own series, so 1 million series can become 100 million. Indexes and memory grow with series count, not with points per second, so the system falls over even though traffic is unchanged. Keep labels low-cardinality (region, status code) and put per-user detail in logs or traces.","idea":"Cost grows with the number of distinct series, so unbounded labels are the classic way to break a metrics store.","caption":"Series count explodes and ingest stalls.","focus":["agents","queue","writer"],"flows":[{"from":"agents","to":"queue","kind":"fail","label":"100M series"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Design for time","body":"Buffer and batch the appends, store them in time-partitioned compressed chunks, and answer recent queries from the newest chunks. Roll old data up so long ranges read few points, and enforce retention by dropping whole chunks. Watch series cardinality, because it is the cost that grows quietly.","idea":"Time is the partition key, the query filter, and the retention unit all at once.","caption":"Ingest, chunk, roll up, expire.","focus":["agents","queue","writer","hot","rollup","cold","dash"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"writes":"100","rawday":"17","keep":"15","query":"30"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does partitioning data into daily chunks make 'last hour' queries fast?","options":["Each chunk is stored on a faster disk","The database can skip every chunk outside the time range","Daily chunks are never compressed","It removes the need for timestamps"],"answer":1,"why":"Partition pruning means only the chunk covering the queried window is read, so cost does not grow as history accumulates."},{"q":"You downsample raw data to 1-minute points storing only the average. Which question can you no longer answer correctly?","options":["What was the typical load in that minute?","What was the peak value in that minute?","How many hours of data exist?","Which host sent the data?"],"answer":1,"why":"An average hides spikes. Keeping min, max, count and sum in each rollup preserves peaks and lets you recompute averages correctly."},{"q":"You need to expire data older than 15 days from a 10 TB table. What is usually the best approach in a time-series store?","options":["Run DELETE with a timestamp filter every night","Rewrite the table without the old rows","Compress old rows more aggressively","Drop the whole chunk partitions outside the window"],"answer":3,"why":"Chunks align with time, so dropping one is a cheap metadata and file operation. Row-by-row deletes are slow and leave dead space."},{"q":"A metrics store runs out of memory after a release, though points per second is unchanged. A new metric now carries a request_id label. What is the cause?","options":["Too many readers on the dashboard","The rollup job runs too often","Series count exploded because each request_id is a new series","Timestamps are arriving out of order"],"answer":2,"why":"Memory and index size scale with distinct series. An unbounded label creates a new series per value, which is high cardinality."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Time series","def":"A sequence of values for one named thing, each with a timestamp, such as CPU% of host A every 10 seconds."},{"term":"Series cardinality","def":"The number of distinct series (metric name plus label values). Memory and index cost scale with it."},{"term":"Time-partitioned chunk","def":"A storage unit covering a fixed time window, so queries can skip chunks and retention can drop them whole."},{"term":"Downsampling / rollup","def":"Replacing many fine points with fewer coarse ones (for example 10s to 1m). Store count, sum, min and max."},{"term":"Retention policy","def":"Rules for how long each resolution is kept, for example raw 15 days, 1-minute 13 months."},{"term":"Delta-of-delta encoding","def":"Store the change in the gap between timestamps; steady intervals become near-zero values that compress to a bit or two."},{"term":"Out-of-order write","def":"A point arriving after newer ones for the same series. Stores usually accept it only within a recent window."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"oltp-olap","title":"OLTP vs OLAP & Warehouses","subtitle":"Watch one revenue report choke a checkout database, then see how a warehouse and a pipeline fix it.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How OLTP workloads (many tiny reads and writes) differ from OLAP workloads (few huge scans)","Why row storage suits transactions and column storage suits analytics","How ETL and ELT move data from the live database into a warehouse or lake","Why running analytics on the production database hurts customers","When to pick a warehouse, a data lake, or both"],"nodes":[{"id":"users","label":"Shoppers","kind":"user","x":8,"y":22},{"id":"oltp","label":"OLTP DB (row store)","kind":"db","x":30,"y":22},{"id":"etl","label":"ETL / ELT pipeline","kind":"worker","x":52,"y":50},{"id":"lake","label":"Data lake (files)","kind":"storage","x":52,"y":84},{"id":"wh","label":"Warehouse (columns)","kind":"db","x":74,"y":50},{"id":"analyst","label":"Analyst dashboard","kind":"client","x":88,"y":22}],"edges":[{"from":"users","to":"oltp"},{"from":"oltp","to":"etl"},{"from":"etl","to":"wh"},{"from":"etl","to":"lake"},{"from":"lake","to":"wh"},{"from":"wh","to":"analyst"},{"from":"analyst","to":"oltp"}],"metrics":[{"id":"p99","label":"Checkout p99","unit":"ms"},{"id":"scanned","label":"Data scanned","unit":"GB"},{"id":"query","label":"Report time","unit":"s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Why does checkout slow down at 9am?","body":"A shop keeps 500 million orders in one Postgres-style database. Checkout is fast: p99 latency is about 8 ms. Every morning an analyst opens a dashboard titled revenue by day, and suddenly shoppers see timeouts. Same database, same data. What changed? The kind of question being asked, not the amount of traffic.","idea":"Two workloads can be harmless alone and destructive together on the same database.","caption":"Healthy checkout, one analyst about to ask a big question.","focus":["users","oltp","analyst"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"p99":"8","scanned":"0","query":"0"}},{"tag":"OLTP","title":"Transactions: many tiny operations","body":"OLTP (online transaction processing) means thousands of small operations per second, each touching one or a few rows: insert this order, read this user's cart, update this stock count. Each must be fast and correct. Databases built for this store each row's fields together on disk, so one row is one cheap read or write, usually found through an index.","idea":"OLTP is point reads and writes by key, optimized for latency and correctness.","caption":"Shoppers write and read single rows in milliseconds.","focus":["users","oltp"],"flows":[{"from":"users","to":"oltp","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order"},{"from":"oltp","to":"users","kind":"response","label":"committed"}],"metrics":{"p99":"8"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Analytics hits the live database","body":"Now the analyst runs SELECT day, SUM(amount) FROM orders GROUP BY day. It must read every one of the 500 million rows. At roughly 200 bytes per row that is about 100 GB. Even at an optimistic 2 GB/s that is around 50 seconds of heavy disk and CPU use, evicting hot pages from the cache that checkout relies on. Checkout queries now queue behind it.","idea":"A full-table scan competes for the same disk, cache and CPU that transactions need.","caption":"The big scan starves checkout of cache and I/O.","focus":["analyst","oltp","users"],"flows":[{"from":"analyst","to":"oltp","kind":"fail","label":"SUM ... GROUP BY"},{"from":"users","to":"oltp","kind":"fail","label":"checkout slow"}],"metrics":{"p99":"900","scanned":"100","query":"50"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Row store vs column store","body":"A row store keeps all fields of one order side by side, which is perfect for fetching a whole order. A column store keeps all values of one column together, so the revenue query reads only the date and amount columns, about 16 bytes per row instead of 200. Similar values sit together too, so they compress very well, often 3 to 10 times. That is roughly 8 GB shrunk to 1 to 3 GB.","idea":"Columns let analytics read only what it needs, and compress better; rows make single-record access cheap.","caption":"Same data, laid out by column for scanning.","focus":["oltp","wh"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"scanned":"2","query":"1"}},{"tag":"ETL","title":"Copy data out: ETL","body":"We cannot just move everything to a column store, because checkout still needs fast row writes. So we keep both and copy. In classic ETL (extract, transform, load) a pipeline pulls changes from the OLTP database, cleans and reshapes them on its own workers, then loads finished tables into the warehouse. Reading changes from the database log (change data capture) puts far less load on it than rescanning tables.","idea":"The OLTP database stays the source of truth; analytics works on a copy.","caption":"Pipeline extracts changes and loads the warehouse.","focus":["oltp","etl","wh"],"flows":[{"from":"oltp","to":"etl","kind":"async","label":"change feed"},{"from":"etl","to":"wh","kind":"async","label":"cleaned tables"}],"metrics":{"p99":"8"}},{"tag":"ELT + LAKE","title":"Dump first, transform later: ELT","body":"Modern warehouses are powerful and cheap to scale, so many teams flip the order: ELT (extract, load, transform). Raw data lands first in a data lake, which is cheap object storage holding files such as Parquet or JSON in any shape. Transformations then run as SQL inside the warehouse. The lake also feeds machine learning and data nobody has modeled yet; the warehouse serves clean, governed tables.","idea":"A lake stores raw data cheaply and flexibly; a warehouse stores modeled data for fast SQL.","caption":"Raw files land in the lake, then the warehouse transforms them.","focus":["etl","lake","wh"],"flows":[{"from":"etl","to":"lake","kind":"async","label":"raw files"},{"from":"lake","to":"wh","kind":"async","label":"load + SQL"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"OLAP","title":"The same report, off the hot path","body":"OLAP (online analytical processing) means few queries that each scan huge amounts of data and aggregate it. Run the revenue report on the warehouse and it reads two compressed columns, spread across many nodes in parallel, in about a second. Checkout never notices, because the OLTP database is not involved at all.","idea":"OLAP is built for big scans and aggregates; isolating it protects transactions.","caption":"Report answered by the warehouse; checkout is untouched.","focus":["analyst","wh"],"flows":[{"from":"analyst","to":"wh","kind":"request","label":"SUM ... GROUP BY"},{"from":"wh","to":"analyst","kind":"response","label":"daily totals"}],"metrics":{"p99":"8","scanned":"2","query":"1"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"The price: freshness and complexity","body":"The copy is never instant. With change capture the warehouse may lag by seconds to minutes; with a nightly batch job, up to a day. You also now run a pipeline that can break, duplicate rows, or drift from the source. Warehouses are poor at single-row updates, so do not point your app at one. Pick the lag the business actually needs.","idea":"You trade data freshness and operational effort for isolation and fast analytics.","caption":"The warehouse is a slightly stale copy fed by a pipeline.","focus":["oltp","etl","wh"],"flows":[{"from":"oltp","to":"etl","kind":"async","label":"lag: ~1-5 min"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Two workloads, two systems","body":"OLTP serves many small, correct, low-latency row operations, so it uses row storage and indexes. OLAP serves few huge scans and aggregates, so it uses column storage and parallel scans. Do not run the second on the first. Move data with ETL or ELT, keep raw history in a lake if you want flexibility, and serve reports from a warehouse.","idea":"Match the storage layout and system to the access pattern, and keep analytics off the transaction path.","caption":"Transactions on the left, analytics on the right.","focus":["users","oltp","etl","lake","wh","analyst"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"p99":"8","scanned":"2","query":"1"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A report needs SUM(amount) over 500 million rows of a 40-column table. Why is a column store usually much faster than a row store here?","options":["It stores fewer rows overall","It reads only the amount column, and that column compresses well","It skips the need for aggregation","It keeps all data in memory by default"],"answer":1,"why":"A column store reads just the columns the query needs, and similar values in one column compress strongly, so far fewer bytes come off disk."},{"q":"Why can one heavy analytics query make checkout slow even though it only reads data?","options":["Reads always lock every table for writing","It competes for disk, CPU and buffer cache with transactions","Read queries are replicated to shoppers","It increases the number of rows in the table"],"answer":1,"why":"The scan saturates I/O and pushes hot pages out of cache, so small transactional queries wait. Reads do not need to block writes for this to hurt."},{"q":"A team lands raw JSON and CSV exports in cheap object storage first and models them into tables later, only when someone needs them. Which approach is this?","options":["ETL into an OLTP database","A data lake, often feeding ELT","Row-based sharding","A write-through cache"],"answer":1,"why":"Storing raw files first and transforming afterwards is the data lake plus ELT pattern; classic ETL transforms before loading."},{"q":"Your app needs to update one customer's address and show it back instantly. Where should that live?","options":["Only in the warehouse, since it holds all data","In the OLTP database, with the warehouse updated later by the pipeline","In the data lake as a new file each time","In the dashboard tool"],"answer":1,"why":"Warehouses are tuned for big scans, not frequent single-row updates. The OLTP database is the source of truth and the warehouse catches up afterwards."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"OLTP","def":"Online transaction processing: many small, fast reads and writes of individual rows, such as orders and carts. Needs low latency and strong correctness."},{"term":"OLAP","def":"Online analytical processing: few queries that scan and aggregate huge volumes of history, such as revenue by region. Needs throughput, not single-row speed."},{"term":"Row vs column store","def":"Row stores keep a record's fields together (good for point access). Column stores keep each column together (good for scans, compression)."},{"term":"ETL vs ELT","def":"ETL transforms data before loading it into the target. ELT loads raw data first and transforms it inside the warehouse using SQL."},{"term":"Data warehouse","def":"A column-oriented analytical database holding cleaned, modeled data, queried with SQL by analysts and dashboards."},{"term":"Data lake","def":"Cheap object storage of raw data in open file formats (for example Parquet). Flexible, but needs structure added before it is easy to query."},{"term":"Change data capture","def":"Reading a database's change log to stream inserts, updates and deletes downstream, with less load than repeated full table queries."},{"term":"Rule of thumb","def":"Never point heavy analytics at the production OLTP database. Use a copy, with a freshness lag the business can accept."}],"isNew":true}]},{"id":"distributed","title":"Distributed Systems","lessons":[{"slug":"cap","title":"CAP & PACELC","subtitle":"Cut the link between two replicas and watch the system be forced to choose between answering and being right.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["What CAP really says: during a network partition you give up consistency or availability","Why partitions are a fact of life, not an option you can decline","How PACELC adds the everyday trade-off: latency versus consistency when nothing is broken","The difference between strong, causal, and eventual consistency, with examples","How to pick a model per feature instead of per whole system"],"nodes":[{"id":"alice","label":"Alice (US user)","kind":"user","x":10,"y":22},{"id":"a","label":"Replica A (US)","kind":"db","x":34,"y":55},{"id":"b","label":"Replica B (EU)","kind":"db","x":66,"y":55},{"id":"bob","label":"Bob (EU user)","kind":"user","x":90,"y":22}],"edges":[{"from":"alice","to":"a"},{"from":"a","to":"b","label":"replication link"},{"from":"b","to":"bob"}],"metrics":[{"id":"wlat","label":"Write latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"rlat","label":"Read latency","unit":"ms"},{"id":"wok","label":"Writes accepted","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Two copies of one value","body":"A profile setting x is stored on two replicas: A in the US and B in Europe, so each user reads from a nearby copy. Both hold x = 4. Alice sets x = 5 on A. Bob, in Europe, reads x from B a moment later. What should Bob see, and what if the link between A and B is cut right now?","idea":"Copying data to two places creates a question: which copy is allowed to be behind?","caption":"Alice writes x = 5 to A; B still holds x = 4.","focus":["alice","a","b","bob"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x = 5"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","rlat":"5","wok":"100"}},{"tag":"BASELINE","title":"Healthy network: easy","body":"With the link up, A forwards x = 5 to B and waits for B's acknowledgement before telling Alice it worked. Now any read from either replica returns 5. This is strong consistency: the system behaves as if there were one copy. The price is that Alice waits for a transatlantic round trip, roughly 80 ms, on top of the local write.","idea":"When nothing is broken you can have both consistency and availability; you just pay latency for it.","caption":"A copies to B and waits for the ack before replying.","focus":["alice","a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x = 5"},{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"x = 5"},{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"a","to":"alice","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"85","rlat":"5","wok":"100"}},{"tag":"PARTITION","title":"The link breaks","body":"Now a fibre cut or a bad router config severs the path between the regions. Both replicas are healthy and both users can still reach their nearest copy, but A and B cannot talk. This is a network partition. Neither side can tell whether the other is dead or just unreachable. You cannot design partitions away: any system spread over a network will eventually see one.","idea":"Partitions are not a choice. CAP is about what you do once one happens.","caption":"A and B are both up but cannot reach each other.","focus":["a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"fail","label":"link down"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CHOOSE C","title":"Option 1: stay correct, refuse","body":"A consistency-first (CP) system protects the single-copy illusion. A cannot confirm that B will see the write, so it rejects Alice's request with an error. B likewise refuses reads it cannot prove are current. Nobody ever sees stale or conflicting data, but during the partition users get errors. Real CP systems with three or more replicas keep serving on the side that still has a majority; the minority side stops.","idea":"Choosing consistency means some requests fail while the partition lasts.","caption":"A returns an error rather than accept a write B cannot see.","focus":["alice","a"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x = 5"},{"from":"a","to":"alice","kind":"fail","label":"503 unavailable"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","wok":"0"}},{"tag":"CHOOSE A","title":"Option 2: stay up, diverge","body":"An availability-first (AP) system keeps answering. A accepts x = 5 and replies instantly, since it does not wait for B. But Bob's read from B returns the old value 4: stale, and still a valid answer because B is up. If Bob had also written x on B, the two copies would now hold different values that must be reconciled later.","idea":"Choosing availability means replicas can disagree while the partition lasts.","caption":"A accepts the write; B serves the old value x = 4.","focus":["alice","a","b","bob"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x = 5"},{"from":"a","to":"alice","kind":"response","label":"ok"},{"from":"bob","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"read x"},{"from":"b","to":"bob","kind":"response","label":"x = 4 (stale)"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","rlat":"5","wok":"100"}},{"tag":"HEALING","title":"The link comes back","body":"When the partition heals, an AP system must converge. A sends the missed update to B, and B applies it, so both hold 5 again. This is eventual consistency: if writes stop, all replicas end up equal. The hard case is two conflicting writes made on both sides. Deciding which wins, or merging them, is covered in the clocks and CRDT lessons.","idea":"Eventual consistency promises convergence, not when, and not which concurrent write survives.","caption":"A pushes the missed write to B; copies converge.","focus":["a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"x = 5"},{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"ack"}],"metrics":{"wok":"100"}},{"tag":"PACELC","title":"Even with no partition","body":"CAP only talks about failures, but partitions are rare and the trade-off is constant. PACELC extends it: if Partition, choose Availability or Consistency; Else, choose Latency or Consistency. In the healthy case, a strongly consistent write waits for the far replica. With about 80 ms between regions, every write pays about 80 ms extra, on every request, not only during outages.","idea":"PACELC: the everyday cost of consistency is latency, paid even when everything works.","caption":"Synchronous replication: Alice waits for B's ack.","focus":["alice","a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x = 5"},{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"x = 5"},{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"ack (80 ms)"},{"from":"a","to":"alice","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"85","rlat":"5","wok":"100"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Else: pick latency","body":"The alternative is asynchronous replication. A confirms to Alice after its own write, taking about 5 ms, then ships x = 5 to B in the background. Writes are 17 times faster, but for the next 80 ms or more Bob can still read 4. Databases in the Dynamo style are typically PA/EL (availability, then latency); Google Spanner is PC/EC (consistency both times).","idea":"Async replication buys speed by letting replicas briefly lag. Sync replication buys correctness with delay.","caption":"A replies at once and replicates in the background.","focus":["alice","a","b","bob"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"write x = 5"},{"from":"a","to":"alice","kind":"response","label":"ok (5 ms)"},{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"async","label":"x = 5, later"},{"from":"bob","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"read x"}],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","rlat":"5","wok":"100"}},{"tag":"MODELS","title":"A ladder of consistency","body":"Consistency is a spectrum. Strong (linearizable): every read sees the latest completed write, as if there were one copy. Causal: if one event could have influenced another, everyone sees them in that order. Alice posts a question and Bob replies; nobody may see the reply before the question. Eventual: replicas only converge over time, with no ordering promise. Weaker models are cheaper and faster.","idea":"Pick the weakest model your feature can tolerate; each step up costs latency or availability.","caption":"Post then reply must appear in order under causal consistency.","focus":["a","b","bob"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"post"},{"from":"bob","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"reply to post"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"How to think about it","body":"CAP: when a partition splits the replicas, choose consistency (reject some requests) or availability (accept and diverge). It is not pick-two-of-three, because partitions will happen. PACELC: even without one, choose latency or consistency. Decide per feature: a bank balance and a unique username need strong consistency; a like counter or a feed can be eventual.","idea":"Ask two questions: what happens during a partition, and what latency am I paying when it is healthy?","caption":"All four parts: clients, two replicas, and the link between.","focus":["alice","a","b","bob"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"wlat":"5","rlat":"5","wok":"100"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A three-replica database splits into a group of 2 and a lone replica. The lone replica rejects all writes. In CAP terms, what did the system choose?","options":["Availability on every node","Consistency, giving up availability on the minority side","It gave up partition tolerance","Neither; this is not a CAP situation"],"answer":1,"why":"Refusing writes that cannot be safely replicated preserves a single history (consistency) at the cost of the isolated node being unavailable. This is the CP choice."},{"q":"A teammate says: 'We chose CA, so we never have to worry about partitions.' What is the flaw?","options":["CA systems cannot have more than one replica","Network partitions happen regardless; you only decide how the system behaves when one occurs","Availability is impossible without partition tolerance","CAP only applies to NoSQL databases"],"answer":1,"why":"On any real network, partitions happen whether you plan for them or not. A 'CA' design just has undefined or accidental behavior when one occurs."},{"q":"Cross-region round trip is 80 ms. A service with no failures at all still sees 80 ms added to every write. Which idea explains this?","options":["The C in CAP, applied during partitions","PACELC's 'else' branch: consistency costs latency even when healthy","Eventual consistency","Causal ordering"],"answer":1,"why":"Waiting for a distant replica to acknowledge is the latency price of strong consistency, paid on every write even without a partition."},{"q":"In a social app, Bob sees a reply to Alice's comment but not the comment itself. Which guarantee would prevent this?","options":["Eventual consistency","Causal consistency","Higher availability","Faster replication hardware"],"answer":1,"why":"Causal consistency ensures that if one event could have caused another, every reader sees them in that order. Plain eventual consistency allows the reply to arrive first."},{"q":"Which feature most clearly needs strong consistency rather than eventual?","options":["View count on a video","Claiming a unique username at signup","Number of followers shown on a profile","Recently viewed items list"],"answer":1,"why":"Two replicas accepting the same username concurrently would break uniqueness. Counters and recency lists tolerate being briefly stale."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"CAP","def":"During a network partition, a replicated system can keep consistency or availability, not both. Partitions are not optional."},{"term":"Consistency (CAP sense)","def":"Linearizability: every read returns the latest completed write, as if there were a single copy. Not the same as ACID consistency."},{"term":"Availability (CAP sense)","def":"Every non-failing node answers every request with a non-error response, even if the data is stale."},{"term":"PACELC","def":"If Partition: Availability or Consistency; Else: Latency or Consistency. Captures the everyday cost of strong guarantees."},{"term":"Strong consistency","def":"Reads see the latest write. Needs coordination between replicas, so it costs round trips and can reject requests in a partition."},{"term":"Causal consistency","def":"Related events (a question and its reply) are seen in order by everyone; unrelated events may appear in any order."},{"term":"Eventual consistency","def":"If writes stop, replicas converge. No promise about when, or about reads in the meantime. Fast and highly available."},{"term":"Rule of thumb","def":"Choose consistency per feature: money, uniqueness, and inventory lean strong; counters, feeds, and caches lean eventual."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"quorums","title":"Quorums (N, R, W)","subtitle":"Watch a leaderless database write to three copies, survive a dead node, and heal itself afterwards.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["What N, R and W mean and why R + W > N makes reads overlap with the latest write","Why a leaderless system can keep accepting writes while a replica is down","How a sloppy quorum and hinted handoff trade guarantees for availability","How read repair and anti-entropy bring stale replicas back in sync","What quorums still do not give you, such as ordering of concurrent writes"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"client","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"coord","label":"Coordinator","kind":"service","x":34,"y":50},{"id":"ra","label":"Replica A","kind":"db","x":64,"y":14},{"id":"rb","label":"Replica B","kind":"db","x":64,"y":50},{"id":"rc","label":"Replica C","kind":"db","x":64,"y":86},{"id":"stand","label":"Node D (stand-in)","kind":"storage","x":89,"y":68}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"coord"},{"from":"coord","to":"ra"},{"from":"coord","to":"rb"},{"from":"coord","to":"rc"},{"from":"coord","to":"stand"},{"from":"stand","to":"rc"},{"from":"ra","to":"rb"}],"metrics":[{"id":"n","label":"Copies (N)","unit":"copies"},{"id":"need","label":"Acks needed","unit":"acks"},{"id":"got","label":"Acks received","unit":"acks"},{"id":"ver","label":"Version seen","unit":"ver"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"No leader, so who is right?","body":"In a leaderless database such as Dynamo, Cassandra or Riak, any node can take a write. Each key is stored on N replicas; here N = 3, on A, B and C. Replicas fail and come back at different times, so copies drift apart. If a client reads while they disagree, how many replicas must it ask to be sure it sees the latest write?","idea":"With no single leader, correctness comes from how many replicas you wait for, not from asking one special node.","caption":"One key lives on three replicas. Any of them may be stale.","focus":["ra","rb","rc"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"n":"3","need":"0","got":"0","ver":"1"}},{"tag":"WRITE","title":"Write with W = 2","body":"The client sends cart:42 = v2 to a coordinator (any node can play this role; it is drawn separately here). It forwards the write to all three replicas but only waits for W = 2 acknowledgements. A and B answer in about 2 ms. C is down and never answers. The write still succeeds, and C is simply behind for now.","idea":"A write is acknowledged once W replicas have it; the other N - W copies catch up later.","caption":"A and B ack; C is down. Two acks meet W = 2.","focus":["coord","ra","rb","rc"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"coord","kind":"request","label":"PUT cart v2"},{"from":"coord","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"v2"},{"from":"coord","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"v2"},{"from":"coord","to":"rc","kind":"fail","label":"no reply"},{"from":"ra","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"rb","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"coord","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"OK"}],"metrics":{"need":"2","got":"2","ver":"2"}},{"tag":"READ","title":"Read with R = 2","body":"C has restarted but still holds v1. The client now reads and the coordinator asks R = 2 replicas, say B and C. B returns v2, C returns v1, and the coordinator picks the newest, v2. Any two replicas must include at least one of the two that took the write, because 2 + 2 = 4 is greater than N = 3.","idea":"If R + W > N, every read set shares at least one replica with every write set, so the read sees the latest acknowledged write.","caption":"Read set {B, C} overlaps write set {A, B} at B.","focus":["coord","rb","rc"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"coord","kind":"request","label":"GET cart"},{"from":"coord","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"coord","to":"rc","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"rb","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v2"},{"from":"rc","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v1 (stale)"},{"from":"coord","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"v2"}],"metrics":{"need":"2","got":"2","ver":"2"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Turning the knobs","body":"R and W are per-request dials. W = 1, R = 1 is the fastest, but 1 + 1 is not greater than 3, so a read can land on C and return v1. W = 3, R = 1 gives fresh reads but one dead replica blocks every write. The common middle for N = 3 is W = 2, R = 2, which survives one failure on both paths. Larger quorums also mean waiting for slower replicas, which raises tail latency.","idea":"Pick R and W to trade freshness, write availability and latency; R + W > N buys overlap, and the cost is waiting for more nodes.","caption":"R = 1 asked only stale C and returned v1.","focus":["coord","rc"],"flows":[{"from":"coord","to":"rc","kind":"request","label":"read (R=1)"},{"from":"rc","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v1 (stale)"},{"from":"coord","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"v1"}],"metrics":{"need":"1","got":"1","ver":"1"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Too many replicas down","body":"Now a network problem cuts the coordinator off from A and C, leaving only B reachable, and W = 2. A strict quorum would reject the write because only 1 of the 3 designated replicas can ack. A sloppy quorum instead lets the coordinator write to a healthy node outside the set, D, tagged with a hint that the data belongs to C. B plus D give two acks and the write is accepted.","idea":"A sloppy quorum keeps writes available during failures by counting stand-in nodes, at the cost of the overlap guarantee.","caption":"D holds C's copy as a hint so the write can succeed.","focus":["coord","rb","stand","ra","rc"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"coord","kind":"request","label":"PUT cart v3"},{"from":"coord","to":"ra","kind":"fail","label":"unreachable"},{"from":"coord","to":"rc","kind":"fail","label":"unreachable"},{"from":"coord","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"v3"},{"from":"coord","to":"stand","kind":"async","label":"v3 + hint for C"},{"from":"rb","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"stand","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"ack"},{"from":"coord","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"OK"}],"metrics":{"need":"2","got":"2","ver":"3"}},{"tag":"CAVEAT","title":"The catch with sloppy quorums","body":"D is not one of the three replicas that reads consult. If a client now reads A and C, both of which missed v3, it gets v2 even though the write was acknowledged and R + W is still greater than N. The overlap promise held only for the designated replicas. Sloppy quorums give you eventual consistency and high write availability, not a read-your-writes guarantee.","idea":"With stand-ins, R + W > N no longer guarantees a fresh read until the hint is delivered.","caption":"Reading A and C returns old data; v3 sits on D.","focus":["coord","ra","rc","stand"],"flows":[{"from":"coord","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"coord","to":"rc","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"ra","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v2"},{"from":"rc","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v2"}],"metrics":{"need":"2","got":"2","ver":"2"}},{"tag":"HANDOFF","title":"Hinted handoff","body":"The partition heals and C is reachable again. D notices it is holding a hint for C and forwards v3 to it, then deletes its temporary copy. Systems usually cap how long they keep hints, often a few hours, so a node that stays down longer must be repaired another way. Handoff is a fast path, not a guarantee.","idea":"Hinted handoff replays missed writes to a recovered replica, but hints are best-effort and time-limited.","caption":"D hands v3 to C once C is back.","focus":["stand","rc"],"flows":[{"from":"stand","to":"rc","kind":"replicate","label":"hint: v3"},{"from":"rc","to":"stand","kind":"response","label":"ack, drop hint"}],"metrics":{"n":"3","got":"3","ver":"3"}},{"tag":"REPAIR","title":"Read repair","body":"A is still on v2 because it was cut off for the v3 write. A client reads from A and B. B returns v3, A returns v2. The coordinator answers the client with v3 and, in the same breath, writes v3 back to A. The read itself discovered the gap and fixed it, at the cost of one extra write on that path.","idea":"Read repair heals stale replicas as a side effect of reads, so popular keys converge quickly.","caption":"Coordinator spots A is behind and pushes v3 to it.","focus":["coord","ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"coord","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"coord","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"read"},{"from":"ra","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v2"},{"from":"rb","to":"coord","kind":"response","label":"v3"},{"from":"coord","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"v3"},{"from":"coord","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"repair: v3"}],"metrics":{"got":"2","ver":"3"}},{"tag":"ANTI-ENTROPY","title":"Background anti-entropy","body":"Read repair only touches keys that get read, and hints can expire or be lost. So replicas also compare data in the background. Each builds a Merkle tree, a tree of hashes over key ranges, and swaps the root hash with a peer. If roots match, nothing is copied. If not, they walk down to the differing ranges and exchange only those keys. This sweeps up cold data nobody reads.","idea":"Anti-entropy uses hash trees to find and fix differences cheaply, covering keys that read repair and hints miss.","caption":"A and B compare hash roots, then sync only the differing range.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"handshake","label":"Merkle root"},{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"handshake","label":"mismatch: range 7"},{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"keys in range 7"}],"metrics":{"got":"3","ver":"3"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What quorums give you","body":"Store N copies, wait for W acks on writes and R replies on reads. If R + W > N and you use the real replica set, reads overlap the latest write. Sloppy quorums and hinted handoff favor availability, while read repair and anti-entropy pull copies back together. Quorums do not order concurrent writes: that needs version tracking, covered in the clocks and conflict-resolution lessons.","idea":"Quorums tune the freshness versus availability trade; healing mechanisms provide eventual convergence.","caption":"Write to W, read from R, repair in the background.","focus":["client","coord","ra","rb","rc","stand"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"n":"3","need":"2","got":"3","ver":"3"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which setting does NOT guarantee that a read overlaps with the most recent acknowledged write (assume strict quorums and no failures during the read)?","options":["N = 3, W = 2, R = 2","N = 5, W = 3, R = 3","N = 5, W = 2, R = 3","N = 3, W = 3, R = 1"],"answer":2,"why":"Overlap needs R + W > N. For N = 5, W = 2 and R = 3 give 5, which is not greater than 5, so a read set of 3 can miss both replicas that took the write."},{"q":"A sloppy quorum accepts a write using a stand-in node outside the key's usual N replicas. Why can a later read with R + W > N still return old data?","options":["Stand-in nodes always reject reads","The read set may contain only designated replicas that never received the write, since the stand-in is not among them","R + W > N only applies to leader-based systems","Hinted handoff deletes the data after a write"],"answer":1,"why":"The overlap argument depends on both sets being drawn from the same N replicas. The acknowledged copy lives on a node reads do not consult until the hint is handed off."},{"q":"A rarely accessed key is stale on one replica, the hint meant for it expired, and nobody reads the key. What eventually repairs it?","options":["Read repair, because every key is read eventually","Raising R above W","Hinted handoff, which never expires","Anti-entropy, which compares replicas in the background using hash trees"],"answer":3,"why":"Read repair needs a read and the hint is gone, so a periodic background comparison such as Merkle-tree anti-entropy is what finds and copies the missing data."},{"q":"You set N = 3 and W = 3 to make writes as durable as possible. What is the main cost?","options":["If any one replica is down or slow, writes fail or stall","Reads can no longer see the latest write","The coordinator must be a leader","Data is stored only twice"],"answer":0,"why":"W = N means every replica must acknowledge, so a single failure blocks writes and the slowest replica sets write latency. Durability goes up but availability goes down."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"N, R, W","def":"N = replicas per key, W = acks required before a write succeeds, R = replies required before a read returns. Each request can pick its own R and W."},{"term":"R + W > N","def":"Read and write sets must share at least one replica, so a read sees the latest acknowledged write. Common choice for N = 3 is R = W = 2."},{"term":"Majority quorum","def":"floor(N/2) + 1 replicas, for example 2 of 3 or 3 of 5. Using it for both R and W tolerates the loss of a minority."},{"term":"Sloppy quorum","def":"Accepts writes on stand-in nodes outside the key's replica set when designated ones are unreachable. More available, but loses the overlap guarantee."},{"term":"Hinted handoff","def":"A stand-in stores the write with a hint naming the intended replica and forwards it when that node returns. Hints are time-limited and best-effort."},{"term":"Read repair","def":"When a read sees different versions, the coordinator returns the newest and writes it back to stale replicas. Only fixes keys that are read."},{"term":"Anti-entropy","def":"Background sync where replicas compare Merkle trees of their data and copy only the differing ranges. Catches cold keys and expired hints."},{"term":"What quorums do not do","def":"They do not order concurrent writes to the same key. You still need version metadata such as vector clocks or a merge rule to avoid silently losing updates."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"consensus","title":"Consensus (Raft)","subtitle":"Watch five servers agree on one write, lose their leader, elect a new one, and never lose a committed entry.","minutes":14,"level":"Advanced","learn":["Why a group of machines needs consensus instead of one trusted copy","How Raft elects a leader using terms, randomized timeouts and majority votes","How log replication and majority commit make a write durable","Why a minority partition can never commit and a stale leader is forced to step down","What etcd and ZooKeeper use consensus for, and what they are not for"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"client","x":9,"y":50},{"id":"s1","label":"S1 (leader)","kind":"server","x":38,"y":50},{"id":"s2","label":"S2","kind":"server","x":74,"y":12},{"id":"s3","label":"S3","kind":"server","x":74,"y":34},{"id":"s4","label":"S4","kind":"server","x":74,"y":66},{"id":"s5","label":"S5","kind":"server","x":74,"y":88}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"s1"},{"from":"client","to":"s3"},{"from":"s1","to":"s2"},{"from":"s1","to":"s3"},{"from":"s1","to":"s4"},{"from":"s1","to":"s5"},{"from":"s3","to":"s2"},{"from":"s3","to":"s4"},{"from":"s4","to":"s5"}],"metrics":[{"id":"term","label":"Term","unit":""},{"id":"acks","label":"Copies stored","unit":"of 5"},{"id":"need","label":"Majority needs","unit":"copies"},{"id":"commit","label":"Commit time","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Who decides what is true?","body":"You keep a cluster's critical settings, such as which node owns which job, on one server. That server dies and everything stops. So you keep copies on five servers. Now a harder problem appears: if two clients write different values at the same moment, or a server crashes mid-write, how do all five end up agreeing on one history? Making machines agree despite crashes and lost messages is called consensus.","idea":"Replication gives availability; consensus is what keeps the copies from disagreeing.","caption":"Five servers must act like one reliable machine.","focus":[],"flows":[],"metrics":{"term":"1","acks":"0","need":"3"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"One leader, many followers","body":"Raft simplifies the problem by electing a single leader. Only the leader accepts writes. Followers copy what the leader sends and reply. Time is cut into numbered terms; each term has at most one leader, and the term number acts as a logical clock. Here S1 leads term 1 and sends tiny heartbeats, every 100 ms or so, to prove it is alive.","idea":"One leader per term means writes are ordered in one place, so there is nothing to argue about.","caption":"S1 leads term 1 and sends heartbeats.","focus":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"flows":[{"from":"s1","to":"s2","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat T1"},{"from":"s1","to":"s3","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat T1"},{"from":"s1","to":"s4","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat T1"},{"from":"s1","to":"s5","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat T1"}],"metrics":{"term":"1"}},{"tag":"REPLICATE","title":"A write enters the log","body":"A client sends set x=5. The leader appends it to its own log, an ordered list of commands, and sends an AppendEntries message to every follower with the entry and its position. Each follower writes the entry to disk before answering. At this point the entry exists but is not yet committed, so the leader has not told the client anything.","idea":"A log entry is just a proposal until enough servers have stored it.","caption":"Leader appends x=5 and sends it to all followers.","focus":["client","s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"s1","kind":"request","label":"set x=5"},{"from":"s1","to":"s2","kind":"replicate","label":"append x=5"},{"from":"s1","to":"s3","kind":"replicate","label":"append x=5"},{"from":"s1","to":"s4","kind":"replicate","label":"append x=5"},{"from":"s1","to":"s5","kind":"replicate","label":"append x=5"}],"metrics":{"acks":"1","need":"3"}},{"tag":"COMMIT","title":"Majority means committed","body":"Followers reply as they finish their disk writes. The moment the leader plus two followers hold the entry, three of five, a majority has it. The entry is now committed: it will survive any failure the cluster is allowed to survive. The leader applies it, answers the client, and tells followers to apply it on the next message. In one data center this takes a few milliseconds, mostly the disk flush.","idea":"Commit means a majority has it on disk, so any future leader's majority must overlap and include it.","caption":"Three of five have it, so x=5 is committed.","focus":["s1","s2","s3","client"],"flows":[{"from":"s2","to":"s1","kind":"response","label":"stored"},{"from":"s3","to":"s1","kind":"response","label":"stored"},{"from":"s1","to":"client","kind":"response","label":"ok, committed"}],"metrics":{"acks":"3","need":"3","commit":"5"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The leader dies","body":"Next S1 crashes. Followers expect a heartbeat every 100 ms or so. When one does not arrive within the election timeout, each follower begins to worry. The timeout is randomized per server (etcd defaults to 1000 ms, papers often use 150 to 300 ms) so they do not all panic at the same instant. Until a new leader exists, the cluster accepts no writes.","idea":"Followers detect a dead leader by silence; random timeouts make simultaneous candidates unlikely.","caption":"Heartbeats stop; S3's timer fires first.","focus":["s1","s3","client"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"s1","kind":"fail","label":"no response"},{"from":"s3","to":"s1","kind":"fail","label":"no heartbeat"}],"metrics":{"acks":"0","commit":"1000"}},{"tag":"ELECTION","title":"S3 asks for votes","body":"S3's timer fires first. It increments the term to 2, votes for itself, and sends RequestVote to the others. Each server grants at most one vote per term, and only to a candidate whose log is at least as up to date as its own. S2 and S4 say yes. With its own vote that makes three of five, a majority, so S3 becomes leader of term 2 and immediately sends heartbeats.","idea":"One vote per server per term guarantees at most one leader per term.","caption":"S3 wins term 2 with votes from S2 and S4.","focus":["s3","s2","s4"],"flows":[{"from":"s3","to":"s2","kind":"request","label":"vote? term 2"},{"from":"s3","to":"s4","kind":"request","label":"vote? term 2"},{"from":"s2","to":"s3","kind":"response","label":"granted"},{"from":"s4","to":"s3","kind":"response","label":"granted"}],"metrics":{"term":"2","acks":"3","need":"3","commit":"1000"}},{"tag":"SAFETY","title":"Committed entries survive","body":"Why is x=5 not lost? It sits on a majority, and any winning candidate also needs a majority of votes. Two majorities of five must share at least one server, and that server refuses to vote for a candidate missing x=5. So the new leader always holds every committed entry. If old S1 wakes up still thinking it leads term 1, it sees term 2 in the first message, steps down, and its stray uncommitted entries are overwritten.","idea":"Quorum overlap plus the up-to-date vote rule means a committed entry can never be elected away.","caption":"Stale S1 sees term 2 and becomes a follower.","focus":["s3","s1"],"flows":[{"from":"s3","to":"s1","kind":"replicate","label":"append, term 2"},{"from":"s1","to":"s3","kind":"response","label":"I step down"}],"metrics":{"term":"2","acks":"4","need":"3","commit":"6"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A network partition","body":"Suppose a network split leaves S4 and S5 alone, cut off from the other three. They cannot reach a majority, so they cannot elect a leader or commit anything, even if S4 keeps trying new terms. The three-server side keeps working with its leader. The minority refuses to answer rather than risk two conflicting histories, which is the consistency choice from the CAP lesson.","idea":"Only the side holding a majority may make progress, so a partition can never produce two committed histories.","caption":"S4 and S5 are isolated and cannot commit.","focus":["s4","s5"],"flows":[{"from":"s4","to":"s3","kind":"fail","label":"unreachable"},{"from":"s4","to":"s5","kind":"request","label":"vote? term 3"},{"from":"s5","to":"s4","kind":"response","label":"granted"}],"metrics":{"term":"3","acks":"2","need":"3"}},{"tag":"IN PRACTICE","title":"What etcd and ZooKeeper do","body":"You rarely write Raft yourself. etcd (Raft) and ZooKeeper (a similar protocol called Zab) run a small cluster of 3 or 5 servers that stores a small, critical amount of data: configuration, who is leader of a service, cluster membership, locks. Kubernetes keeps all its state in etcd. These stores are slow for bulk data because every write pays a quorum round trip, so keep them for coordination, not for your application's rows.","idea":"Use consensus stores for small, high-value coordination state, and never as your main database.","caption":"A client reads and writes small coordination data.","focus":["client","s1","s2","s3"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"s3","kind":"request","label":"put /leader=A"},{"from":"s3","to":"s2","kind":"replicate","label":"append"},{"from":"s2","to":"s3","kind":"response","label":"stored"}],"metrics":{"term":"2","acks":"3","need":"3","commit":"5"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Agreement by majority","body":"Raft elects one leader per numbered term, replicates a log entry to followers, and commits it once a majority has stored it. A new leader needs a majority of votes, and those voters must hold every committed entry, so committed data survives leader crashes. Minorities stall instead of diverging. Pay for it with a quorum round trip per write, and use it for small coordination data.","idea":"Majority plus terms gives one history, at the cost of a quorum round trip per write and no progress without a majority.","caption":"Leader, terms, majority commit, safe failover.","focus":["client","s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"term":"2","acks":"3","need":"3","commit":"5"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A Raft cluster has 5 servers. The leader has stored an entry itself, and one follower has also stored it. Can the leader tell the client the write is committed?","options":["Yes, two copies is enough to survive one crash","No, it needs at least 3 of 5 servers to hold the entry","Yes, because the leader's own copy is authoritative","No, it needs all 5 servers to hold the entry"],"answer":1,"why":"Commit requires a majority, which is 3 of 5. Two copies might both be on servers that a different majority could outvote or isolate."},{"q":"Why must a server refuse to vote for a candidate whose log is behind its own?","options":["To keep elections short","To make sure the winner already holds every committed entry","To prevent followers from voting twice in a term","To stop the old leader from restarting"],"answer":1,"why":"Any committed entry sits on a majority, and any winning candidate also needs a majority of votes. The overlapping server blocks candidates that lack the entry."},{"q":"A 6-server cluster is split into groups of 3 and 3 by a network failure. What happens?","options":["Both groups elect leaders and keep accepting writes","The group containing the old leader keeps working","Neither group can reach a majority of 4, so neither can commit","The group with the lowest server IDs wins automatically"],"answer":2,"why":"A majority of 6 is 4. With 3 and 3, neither side has it, so writes stall. This is also why odd cluster sizes are preferred."},{"q":"An old leader crashed in term 1 and returns after S3 has won term 2. It tries to send an entry to followers. What happens?","options":["Followers accept it because it arrived last","Followers accept it if it has more entries than S3","Followers reject it because its term is lower, and it steps down","Followers start a new election in term 1"],"answer":2,"why":"Servers reject messages from a lower term, and a leader that sees a higher term immediately becomes a follower. Terms keep stale leaders harmless."},{"q":"Which workload is a poor fit for storing directly in etcd or ZooKeeper?","options":["Service leader and membership info","A cluster's feature-flag configuration","Millions of per-user order rows with heavy write traffic","A distributed lock's ownership record"],"answer":2,"why":"Every write goes through a single leader and waits for a quorum, and the whole dataset is replicated to each server. Coordination state is small, but bulk application data should live in a scalable database."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Consensus","def":"Getting a group of machines to agree on one value or one ordered history even when some crash or messages are lost."},{"term":"Term","def":"Numbered election period in Raft. At most one leader per term; a higher term always overrides a lower one."},{"term":"Quorum / majority","def":"More than half the servers: floor(N/2)+1. A cluster of 2f+1 servers survives f failures."},{"term":"Commit rule","def":"A leader may report an entry committed once a majority has stored it durably. Never earlier."},{"term":"Election timeout","def":"How long a follower waits without a heartbeat before running for leader. Randomized per server to avoid ties; must exceed the heartbeat interval by a wide margin."},{"term":"Vote restriction","def":"A server only votes for a candidate whose log is at least as up to date as its own, so the winner has every committed entry."},{"term":"etcd / ZooKeeper","def":"Small, strongly consistent stores built on Raft (etcd) or Zab (ZooKeeper). Used for config, leader election, membership and locks, not bulk data."},{"term":"Cluster size","def":"Use 3 or 5 servers. Even sizes add cost without adding failure tolerance, and more servers make every write slower."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"leader-election-locks","title":"Leader Election & Distributed Locks","subtitle":"Watch two workers fight over one lock, see a paused process corrupt data, and see a fencing token stop it.","minutes":12,"level":"Advanced","learn":["How a lock service picks one leader and why the lock is really a lease with an expiry time","Why a lock that only has a TTL is unsafe when a process pauses (GC, VM stall, slow network)","How a fencing token lets the protected resource reject a stale lock holder","Where the lock service itself gets its safety (consensus) and what that costs you"],"nodes":[{"id":"a","label":"Worker A","kind":"worker","x":10,"y":30},{"id":"b","label":"Worker B","kind":"worker","x":10,"y":80},{"id":"lock","label":"Lock service","kind":"service","x":47,"y":18},{"id":"store","label":"Shared storage","kind":"storage","x":86,"y":60}],"edges":[{"from":"a","to":"lock"},{"from":"b","to":"lock"},{"from":"a","to":"store"},{"from":"b","to":"store"}],"metrics":[{"id":"ttl","label":"Lease TTL","unit":"s"},{"id":"token","label":"Newest token","unit":""},{"id":"pause","label":"A frozen for","unit":"s"},{"id":"stale","label":"Stale accepted","unit":"writes"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Only one may write","body":"Two copies of a worker run for availability, but a nightly job must be run by only one at a time, or a file will be written twice and corrupted. Someone has to be chosen as leader, and if that leader dies, someone else must take over quickly. How do machines that cannot see each other's memory agree on who is in charge?","idea":"Leader election is mutual exclusion across machines: at most one holder, with a way to recover when the holder dies.","caption":"Two workers, one shared resource, one allowed writer.","focus":["a","b","store"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"ELECTION","title":"Race to create one key","body":"Both workers ask a lock service (etcd, ZooKeeper, or a database row with a unique key) to create the key /leader. The service handles requests one at a time, so exactly one create succeeds. The loser gets an error and either retries later or watches the key to be told when it disappears.","idea":"A central service that serialises requests turns a race between machines into a single winner.","caption":"A wins the create; B is told the key exists.","focus":["a","b","lock"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"lock","kind":"request","label":"create /leader"},{"from":"b","to":"lock","kind":"request","label":"create /leader"},{"from":"lock","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"OK"},{"from":"lock","to":"b","kind":"fail","label":"already exists"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"LEASE","title":"A lock must expire","body":"If A simply held the key forever and then crashed, nobody could ever take over. So the lock is a lease: it is valid for a TTL, here 10 seconds, and A must renew it before it runs out. The service also hands out a fencing token, a number that increases with every grant. A gets token 33.","idea":"A lease turns 'held until released' into 'held until it times out', so a dead holder cannot block everyone forever.","caption":"A holds a 10 s lease and token 33.","focus":["a","lock"],"flows":[{"from":"lock","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"10s lease, tok 33"},{"from":"a","to":"lock","kind":"async","label":"renew"}],"metrics":{"ttl":"10","token":"33","stale":"0"}},{"tag":"WORK","title":"The healthy case","body":"While A keeps renewing, B stays out and A writes to storage as the leader. Nothing is wrong here, and for many jobs a plain TTL lock would look fine in testing. The danger is not a crash, which the lease handles well. The danger is a holder that is slow but still alive.","idea":"TTL locks work in the happy path, which is why their flaw survives testing.","caption":"A renews its lease and writes with token 33.","focus":["a","lock","store"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"lock","kind":"async","label":"renew"},{"from":"a","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"write (token 33)"},{"from":"store","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"ok"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A freezes for 15 seconds","body":"Just after checking that it still holds the lock, A hits a long garbage-collection pause, a VM migration, or a stalled disk. The whole process is frozen, so it cannot renew. A has no idea any time passed. Frozen processes do not notice their own clocks stopping, so A cannot be trusted to check the lease itself.","idea":"A process cannot reliably know it was paused: a check like 'do I still hold the lock?' can be stale by the time the write happens.","caption":"A is frozen; its renewal never arrives.","focus":["a","lock"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"lock","kind":"fail","label":"renew lost"}],"metrics":{"pause":"15"}},{"tag":"EXPIRE","title":"The lease runs out","body":"After 10 seconds without a renewal, the lock service expires A's lease and deletes the key. B, which was watching, immediately creates it and becomes the new leader with token 34. From the service's point of view everything is correct: A looked dead, so B took over.","idea":"The service did the right thing; the problem is that A does not know yet.","caption":"B takes over with token 34.","focus":["b","lock"],"flows":[{"from":"lock","to":"b","kind":"async","label":"watch: key gone"},{"from":"b","to":"lock","kind":"request","label":"create /leader"},{"from":"lock","to":"b","kind":"response","label":"10s lease, tok 34"}],"metrics":{"token":"34"}},{"tag":"FAILURE","title":"Two writers, one file","body":"At second 15, A wakes up. Its last check said it was leader, so it finishes its write. B is also writing. If storage accepts anything from anyone, A's old write lands after B's newer one and overwrites it. No lock service bug happened: the lock was granted correctly. The unsafe part is trusting the holder.","idea":"Lock plus TTL alone cannot guarantee exclusion; a stale holder can still act after its lease is gone.","caption":"A's stale write lands next to B's write.","focus":["a","b","store"],"flows":[{"from":"b","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"write (token 34)"},{"from":"a","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"write (stale)"},{"from":"store","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"ok (bad!)"}],"metrics":{"stale":"1"}},{"tag":"FENCING","title":"Let the resource say no","body":"The fix is to check at the place that matters. Every write carries the fencing token, and storage remembers the highest token it has seen. B wrote with 34, so a later write with 33 is rejected as stale. The check happens in storage, so it still works however long A was frozen and whatever A believes.","idea":"A fencing token makes the protected resource, not the lock holder, the final judge of who is allowed to write.","caption":"Storage rejects token 33 because it has seen 34.","focus":["a","store"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"store","kind":"request","label":"write (token 33)"},{"from":"store","to":"a","kind":"fail","label":"rejected: 33 < 34"}],"metrics":{"stale":"0"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"What this costs","body":"The lock service must itself survive failures, so real ones (etcd, ZooKeeper) replicate through a consensus protocol and need a majority up, which is covered in the Raft lesson. Fencing also needs storage that can compare tokens, and not every system can. A lock that is only an efficiency hint, like avoiding duplicate emails, can skip fencing. A lock that protects correctness cannot.","idea":"Use fencing when correctness depends on the lock; accept a plain TTL lock only when a rare double run is harmless.","caption":"The lock service replicates its own state for safety.","focus":["lock","store"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Lease, token, check","body":"Election picks one winner by serialising a create. A lease with a TTL lets the system recover from a dead leader. But a paused leader can wake up after its lease ended, so every grant carries an increasing fencing token and the resource rejects any older token. TTL gives liveness; fencing gives safety.","idea":"Never trust 'I hold the lock' alone; make the resource verify the token.","caption":"Election, lease, and fencing together.","focus":["a","b","lock","store"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"ttl":"10","token":"34","pause":"15","stale":"0"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a lock that is just a key with a 10 s TTL unsafe for protecting a shared file?","options":["The lock service cannot create keys atomically","A holder may be paused past its TTL and still write, unaware that a new holder exists","TTLs make the lock service slower than 10 s","Two workers can never be granted the same key"],"answer":1,"why":"The service correctly expires the lease and grants it to someone else, but the old holder cannot tell that time passed. Its write can arrive after the new holder's."},{"q":"Worker A has token 33 and was frozen. B now has token 34 and has written. What does storage do with A's write if it implements fencing?","options":["Accepts it, since it arrived last","Accepts it if A's clock says its lease is still valid","Rejects it because 33 is lower than the highest token seen, 34","Asks the lock service whether A is leader before deciding"],"answer":2,"why":"Storage keeps the highest token it has seen and refuses any lower one. This does not depend on clocks or on A's beliefs."},{"q":"A worker checks 'am I leader?' and then writes immediately. Why does that check not make the TTL lock safe?","options":["The check itself takes too many milliseconds","The process can pause between the check and the write, making the answer stale","Leaders are not allowed to check their own status","The lock service rejects repeated checks"],"answer":1,"why":"A GC pause or stall can fall between the check and the write, so the result of the check may already be false when the write happens."},{"q":"You use a lock only to avoid sending the same daily email twice, and an occasional duplicate is acceptable. What is the reasonable choice?","options":["A plain TTL lock is enough, since a rare double run is harmless","You must add fencing tokens to the email provider","Use no lock, because locks cannot work across machines","Make the TTL 1 ms so it is always fresh"],"answer":0,"why":"When the lock is only an efficiency optimisation, an occasional violation costs little. Fencing matters when correctness depends on exclusion."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Lease","def":"A lock that is valid only for a fixed time and must be renewed. If the holder stops renewing, the service frees it so others can take over."},{"term":"Fencing token","def":"A number that grows with every lock grant. The protected resource rejects any request carrying a lower token than one it has already seen."},{"term":"Lock service","def":"A small replicated store (etcd, ZooKeeper, Consul) that serialises create/renew requests and uses consensus to stay correct if some nodes fail."},{"term":"Leader election","def":"Choosing one node to do a job. Common pattern: all nodes try to create one key, the winner is leader, the rest watch the key."},{"term":"Pause hazard","def":"GC pauses, VM stalls and slow I/O can freeze a process longer than the TTL. After waking it may still act as leader."},{"term":"Efficiency vs correctness","def":"A lock used to save work can be a simple TTL lock. A lock that protects data integrity needs fencing at the resource."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"clocks-ordering","title":"Clocks & Ordering","subtitle":"Watch two replicas disagree about which write came last, then fix it with logical clocks.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why wall clocks on different machines never agree exactly, and what NTP can and cannot fix","How last-write-wins can silently throw away the newer write","What happens-before means and how to tell ordered events from concurrent ones","How Lamport timestamps and vector clocks capture causality","Why hybrid logical clocks pair physical time with a counter"],"nodes":[{"id":"c1","label":"Client 1","kind":"client","x":10,"y":25},{"id":"c2","label":"Client 2","kind":"client","x":10,"y":75},{"id":"a","label":"Replica A","kind":"server","x":40,"y":25},{"id":"b","label":"Replica B","kind":"server","x":40,"y":75},{"id":"ntp","label":"NTP time server","kind":"external","x":72,"y":10},{"id":"store","label":"Merged record","kind":"db","x":80,"y":55}],"edges":[{"from":"c1","to":"a"},{"from":"c2","to":"b"},{"from":"a","to":"b"},{"from":"a","to":"store"},{"from":"b","to":"store"},{"from":"ntp","to":"a"},{"from":"ntp","to":"b"}],"metrics":[{"id":"skew","label":"Clock skew","unit":"ms"},{"id":"lamport","label":"Lamport clock","unit":""},{"id":"kept","label":"Writes kept","unit":"of 2"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Which write came last?","body":"A shopping-cart value is stored on two replicas. Client 1 sets it to \"red\" through Replica A. Fifty milliseconds later Client 2 reads \"red\", changes it to \"blue\", and writes through Replica B. Blue is clearly the newer intent. Can the system work that out by comparing timestamps?","idea":"Deciding what happened after what is the core problem; timestamps are only one way to answer it.","caption":"Two clients, two replicas, one value.","focus":["c1","c2","a","b"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Clocks drift and disagree","body":"Each machine counts time with a quartz crystal. A typical crystal is off by 10 to 100 parts per million, which is up to 8 seconds a day if left alone. So two servers always show slightly different times. Here Replica B's clock runs 120 ms behind Replica A's. That gap is called clock skew.","idea":"There is no single 'now' across machines; every clock is a slightly wrong local guess.","caption":"Replica B's clock is 120 ms behind A.","focus":["a","b"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"skew":"120"}},{"tag":"NTP","title":"NTP shrinks skew, never removes it","body":"NTP (Network Time Protocol) lets a machine ask a time server for the time, estimate the network delay, and nudge its own clock. Over the public internet it typically gets within a few to a few tens of milliseconds. Tuned datacenter setups using chrony or PTP can reach well under a millisecond. Errors still come back: a missed sync, a VM pause, or a leap second can jump a clock.","idea":"Synchronized clocks are close, not equal, so a timestamp comparison smaller than the error margin means nothing.","caption":"Replicas sync to NTP; skew drops to about 5 ms.","focus":["ntp","a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"ntp","kind":"request","label":"what time?"},{"from":"ntp","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"time + delay"},{"from":"b","to":"ntp","kind":"request","label":"what time?"},{"from":"ntp","to":"b","kind":"response","label":"time + delay"}],"metrics":{"skew":"5"}},{"tag":"FAILURE","title":"Last-write-wins loses data","body":"Suppose the sync lapsed and B is 120 ms behind again. Replica A stamps red at 10:00:00.100. Fifty ms later, B stamps blue with its slow clock: 10:00:00.030. The merge rule last-write-wins (LWW) keeps the biggest timestamp, so red wins and the newer blue write is silently discarded. No error, no log, just a lost update.","idea":"LWW trusts wall clocks to order events, so skew larger than the gap between writes makes it drop the real latest write.","caption":"Blue is dropped because B's clock is behind.","focus":["c1","c2","a","b","store"],"flows":[{"from":"c1","to":"a","kind":"request","label":"set red"},{"from":"a","to":"store","kind":"replicate","label":"red @ .100"},{"from":"c2","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"set blue"},{"from":"b","to":"store","kind":"fail","label":"blue @ .030"}],"metrics":{"skew":"120","kept":"1"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Happens-before","body":"Instead of asking what time it was, ask what could have influenced what. Event X happens before Y if they run on the same machine with X first, or X sends a message that Y receives, or a chain of such links connects them. If neither happens before the other, they are concurrent: neither could have known about the other. Red then blue is ordered because Client 2 read red first.","idea":"Causality, not wall-clock time, defines order: A happens-before B only if information could have flowed from A to B.","caption":"Client 2's write depends on red; it is causally later.","focus":["c1","a","c2","b"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"red"},{"from":"b","to":"c2","kind":"response","label":"read: red"},{"from":"c2","to":"b","kind":"request","label":"write blue"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"LAMPORT","title":"Lamport timestamps","body":"Leslie Lamport's trick needs no clocks at all. Each node keeps a counter. It adds 1 for every local event and attaches the counter to every message. On receiving a message the node sets its counter to max(its own, the message's) + 1. So if X happens before Y, X's number is smaller. The reverse does not hold: a smaller number does not prove causality.","idea":"A Lamport clock gives a total order consistent with causality, but cannot tell concurrent events from ordered ones.","caption":"Counters ride on messages; B jumps past A's value.","focus":["a","b"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"red, L=4"},{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"blue, L=5"}],"metrics":{"lamport":"5"}},{"tag":"VECTOR","title":"Vector clocks spot concurrency","body":"A vector clock keeps one counter per node. Red is written at A as [A1, B0]. B receives it and writes blue as [A1, B1]. Every entry is at least as large, so blue replaces red safely. But if A also takes a different write, giving [A2, B0], that and [A1, B1] are incomparable. The system keeps both as siblings for the app or a merge rule to resolve. Dynamo-style stores like Riak use this.","idea":"Comparing vectors tells you three things: older, newer, or truly concurrent. The cost is a vector that grows with the number of writers.","caption":"Ordered writes replace; concurrent ones are kept.","focus":["a","b","store"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"store","kind":"replicate","label":"[A2,B0]"},{"from":"b","to":"store","kind":"replicate","label":"[A1,B1]"}],"metrics":{"kept":"2"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Hybrid logical clocks","body":"Lamport and vector values mean nothing to a human and cannot answer 'show me data from 9am'. A hybrid logical clock (HLC) stores the physical time plus a small counter. It follows the wall clock closely, but if a message arrives from the future, it bumps the counter instead of going backwards. CockroachDB and YugabyteDB use HLCs. Google Spanner takes another route: its TrueTime API reports an uncertainty interval, and commits wait it out, typically a few milliseconds.","idea":"HLCs keep causal ordering and stay close to real time, at the cost of bounded, not zero, dependence on synchronized clocks.","caption":"Timestamps carry time plus a tiebreak counter.","focus":["a","b","ntp"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"t=.100, c=0"},{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"response","label":"t=.100, c=1"}],"metrics":{"skew":"5","kept":"2"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the right clock","body":"Wall clocks drift, and NTP only narrows the error, so comparing timestamps from different machines can reorder events and let LWW lose data. Lamport clocks give a causal-consistent order. Vector clocks also detect concurrency. HLCs mix both ideas with readable time. How to merge concurrent writes is left to the conflict-resolution lesson.","idea":"Use time for humans and logs, and use causality tracking when correctness depends on order.","caption":"Causality tracking keeps both writes visible.","focus":["c1","c2","a","b","ntp","store"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"skew":"5","lamport":"5","kept":"2"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Replica A and B are synced by NTP to within about 20 ms. Two writes to the same key happen 5 ms apart on different replicas. What can a timestamp comparison tell you?","options":["Which write is newer, because NTP is accurate","Which write is newer, as long as the clocks are in the same timezone","Nothing reliable, since the gap is smaller than the clock error","That the writes are concurrent"],"answer":2,"why":"Skew of 20 ms is larger than the 5 ms gap, so either replica's stamp could be ahead. The comparison is within the noise and cannot be trusted."},{"q":"Last-write-wins is used to merge two replicas. Which scenario most clearly causes silent data loss?","options":["Both replicas have exactly the same clock","The replica that took the later write has a clock running behind the other","The writes are to different keys","The network is slow but all messages arrive"],"answer":1,"why":"A later write stamped by a slow clock gets a smaller timestamp, so the merge discards it even though it was the latest in real time."},{"q":"Two vector clocks are [A:3, B:1] and [A:2, B:2]. What is their relationship?","options":["The first is older","The second is older","They are identical","They are concurrent"],"answer":3,"why":"Neither vector is greater than or equal in every entry: A is higher in the first, B is higher in the second. So neither write saw the other."},{"q":"Event X has Lamport timestamp 7 and event Y has Lamport timestamp 9. What can you conclude?","options":["X happened before Y","Y cannot have happened before X","X and Y are concurrent","Y happened before X"],"answer":1,"why":"If Y happened before X, then Y's timestamp would be smaller. So Y cannot precede X, but X may still be concurrent with Y, which is why 'X happened before Y' is not guaranteed."},{"q":"Why would a database choose a hybrid logical clock over a plain Lamport clock?","options":["HLCs need no counter","HLC timestamps stay close to real time, so you can query by time and still respect causality","HLCs eliminate clock skew","HLCs detect concurrent writes like vector clocks"],"answer":1,"why":"An HLC pairs physical time with a counter: readable, near wall-clock, and causality-safe. It does not remove skew or give per-node concurrency detection."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Clock skew","def":"The difference between two machines' clocks at the same real instant. Always non-zero; NTP keeps it to roughly ms, not zero."},{"term":"Clock drift","def":"How fast a clock gains or loses time. Cheap crystals drift tens of parts per million, several seconds per day if unsynced."},{"term":"Happens-before (→)","def":"X → Y if X precedes Y on one node, or X is a send whose receive is Y, or by chaining. Otherwise the events are concurrent."},{"term":"Lamport timestamp","def":"A counter bumped per event and set to max(local, received) + 1 on receive. If X → Y then L(X) < L(Y); the reverse is not guaranteed."},{"term":"Vector clock","def":"One counter per node. Compare entry-wise to find older, newer, or concurrent. Grows with the number of writers."},{"term":"Hybrid logical clock","def":"Physical time plus a logical counter. Stays near wall-clock time and never goes backwards. Used by CockroachDB, YugabyteDB."},{"term":"Last-write-wins (LWW)","def":"Keeps the highest timestamp. Simple, but loses updates under skew or true concurrency. Fine for idempotent overwrites like caches."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"gossip-failure-detection","title":"Gossip & Failure Detection","subtitle":"Watch a cluster notice a dead node, spread the news by rumor, and then get fooled by a network split.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How heartbeats let one node judge whether another is alive","Why a fixed timeout is a blunt tool and what a phi-accrual detector does instead","How gossip spreads membership news to every node in roughly log N rounds","Why a failure detector can only suspect, never know, and how that causes false alarms","What split brain is and why a majority rule contains it"],"nodes":[{"id":"a","label":"Node A","kind":"service","x":12,"y":50},{"id":"b","label":"Node B","kind":"service","x":38,"y":20},{"id":"c","label":"Node C","kind":"service","x":38,"y":80},{"id":"d","label":"Node D","kind":"service","x":64,"y":20},{"id":"e","label":"Node E","kind":"service","x":64,"y":80},{"id":"client","label":"Client","kind":"client","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"a","to":"b"},{"from":"a","to":"c"},{"from":"b","to":"c"},{"from":"b","to":"d"},{"from":"c","to":"e"},{"from":"c","to":"d"},{"from":"d","to":"e"},{"from":"client","to":"d"},{"from":"client","to":"e"}],"metrics":[{"id":"gap","label":"Silence","unit":"ms"},{"id":"phi","label":"Suspicion phi","unit":""},{"id":"aware","label":"Know E is down","unit":"of 4"},{"id":"leaders","label":"Active leaders","unit":""}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Is that node dead or just quiet?","body":"A five-node cluster stores data and must route around machines that fail. But no node can look at another's power light. All it can see is messages arriving or not arriving. Silence could mean a crash, a slow disk, a long garbage-collection pause, or a broken network cable. This lesson shows how a cluster turns silence into a decision.","idea":"Failure detection is inference from missing messages, never direct observation.","caption":"Five peers and a client; nobody can see another node's health.","focus":[],"flows":[],"metrics":{"gap":"0","phi":"0","aware":"4","leaders":"1"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Heartbeats","body":"Each node sends a tiny heartbeat to its peers on a schedule, say once per second. Every receiver records when the last one arrived. As long as beats keep landing about a second apart, the sender is presumed healthy. A heartbeat proves only that the sender could send a moment ago, not that it is doing useful work.","idea":"A heartbeat is a periodic I-am-alive message; its absence is the only signal of trouble.","caption":"E sends a heartbeat to D every second; D logs each arrival.","focus":["d","e"],"flows":[{"from":"e","to":"d","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat #41"},{"from":"e","to":"d","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat #42"}],"metrics":{"gap":"1000","phi":"0.4"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"The fixed timeout","body":"Now E crashes. The simplest rule is a fixed timeout: if no heartbeat arrives for 5 seconds, declare E dead. It is easy to reason about but rigid. On a quiet LAN, 5 seconds wastes time before reacting. On a congested or cross-region link where delays vary a lot, 5 seconds fires on healthy nodes. One number cannot fit every network or every moment.","idea":"A fixed timeout trades detection speed against false alarms, and the right value changes with conditions.","caption":"E has crashed; D waits for the 5 s timer to expire.","focus":["d","e"],"flows":[{"from":"e","to":"d","kind":"fail","label":"no heartbeat"}],"metrics":{"gap":"5000","phi":"2.2"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Phi-accrual detection","body":"A phi-accrual detector (used by Cassandra and Akka) learns how late beats normally are. It reports a suspicion score, phi, that grows the longer the silence lasts compared with history. Phi 1 means about a 10% chance the node is still fine, phi 2 about 1%, phi 8 about one in a hundred million. The application picks a threshold, commonly 8 in Cassandra, instead of a time.","idea":"Phi adapts to observed network jitter and outputs a graded suspicion level, not a yes or no.","caption":"Silence grows; phi climbs past the threshold of 8.","focus":["d","e"],"flows":[{"from":"e","to":"d","kind":"fail","label":"phi rising"}],"metrics":{"gap":"18400","phi":"8"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Gossip starts","body":"D now suspects E, but a cluster needs every node to agree on membership. Rather than D messaging everyone, nodes use gossip: every second each node picks a few random peers and swaps what it knows, including a version number per member. D tells B and C that E looks down. No coordinator is needed, so none can become a bottleneck.","idea":"Gossip is periodic random pairwise exchange of state, with no central coordinator.","caption":"D tells two random peers that E is suspected.","focus":["d","b","c"],"flows":[{"from":"d","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"E suspect v7"},{"from":"d","to":"c","kind":"replicate","label":"E suspect v7"}],"metrics":{"aware":"3"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"The rumor spreads","body":"In the next round, B and C each pick their own random peers, and A hears it. The set of informed nodes roughly doubles every round, so news reaches N nodes in about log2(N) rounds. With 1,000 nodes that is around 10 to 15 rounds, so roughly 10 to 15 seconds at a 1 s interval. Each node sends only a few small messages per round no matter how large the cluster is.","idea":"Informed nodes double each round, so spread time grows with log N while per-node load stays flat.","caption":"A, B and C now agree; the live cluster converges.","focus":["a","b","c","d"],"flows":[{"from":"b","to":"a","kind":"replicate","label":"E suspect v7"},{"from":"c","to":"a","kind":"replicate","label":"E suspect v7"}],"metrics":{"aware":"4"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"False alarms","body":"Suppose instead that E is fine, but a 12 s garbage-collection pause froze it. Its heartbeats stop, phi crosses the threshold, and the cluster marks it down. When E wakes up and resumes, it must be un-marked. Too sensitive a threshold causes flapping: nodes repeatedly shunned and readmitted, triggering needless data movement. Too lax a threshold leaves real failures undetected longer.","idea":"A detector is never certain; threshold choice balances slow detection against false positives.","caption":"A paused but healthy E looks identical to a dead one.","focus":["e","c","d"],"flows":[{"from":"c","to":"e","kind":"fail","label":"no reply (GC)"},{"from":"e","to":"c","kind":"async","label":"I am back"}],"metrics":{"gap":"12000","phi":"8","aware":"4"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Split brain","body":"Now a network partition cuts the cluster: A, B, C can talk to each other, as can D, E, but the two groups cannot. Each side sees the other go silent and concludes it is the survivor. If both sides then accept writes or elect their own leader, you get split brain: two active leaders with diverging data. Clients who reach different sides see different truths.","idea":"Split brain happens when both halves of a partition believe they alone are in charge.","caption":"Both sides suspect the other and each elects a leader.","focus":["b","d","c","e","client"],"flows":[{"from":"b","to":"d","kind":"fail","label":"partitioned"},{"from":"c","to":"e","kind":"fail","label":"partitioned"},{"from":"client","to":"e","kind":"request","label":"write x=2"}],"metrics":{"leaders":"2"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Containing it with a majority","body":"The usual defence is a quorum rule: only a group holding a strict majority of the configured members (3 of 5 here) may act as the leader or accept writes. The 2-node side can still detect that it lost contact, so it steps down or goes read-only. Two disjoint groups can never both hold a majority, so at most one leader exists. How elections reach that agreement is covered in the consensus lesson.","idea":"Detection only suspects; a majority rule turns suspicion into safe action, because two majorities always overlap.","caption":"A, B, C keep the majority; D and E refuse writes.","focus":["a","b","c","d","e"],"flows":[{"from":"a","to":"b","kind":"replicate","label":"commit 3 of 5"},{"from":"a","to":"c","kind":"replicate","label":"commit 3 of 5"},{"from":"client","to":"e","kind":"fail","label":"no quorum"}],"metrics":{"leaders":"1"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"From silence to decision","body":"Heartbeats make silence measurable. Phi-accrual turns the length of that silence into a graded suspicion that adapts to the network. Gossip spreads the verdict to every node in about log N rounds without a coordinator. Because any verdict can be wrong, safety-critical actions such as electing a leader need a majority, not just a detector's opinion.","idea":"Detect by heartbeat, spread by gossip, and act only with a majority.","caption":"Heartbeats, suspicion, gossip, then quorum.","focus":["a","b","c","d","e"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"gap":"1000","phi":"0.4","aware":"4","leaders":"1"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A fixed 5 s heartbeat timeout works in the data center but fires constantly on a cross-region link. What does a phi-accrual detector change?","options":["It sends heartbeats more often so silence is shorter","It judges silence against the observed arrival pattern, so tolerance adapts to jitter","It removes the need for heartbeats by using gossip","It guarantees a suspected node is really dead"],"answer":1,"why":"Phi compares the current silence with the learned distribution of past inter-arrival times, so a jittery link tolerates longer gaps while a steady one reacts faster."},{"q":"A 1,000-node cluster gossips once per second and each node contacts one random peer. About how long until a rumor reaches nearly everyone?","options":["A fraction of a second","Roughly 10 to 15 seconds","About 1,000 seconds","It never fully converges"],"answer":1,"why":"The informed set roughly doubles each round, so coverage needs about log2(1000), around 10 rounds, plus a few for stragglers."},{"q":"A node freezes for 12 s in a GC pause, is marked down, then resumes. What does this show about failure detectors?","options":["The detector had a bug","Heartbeats should be sent over TCP","Slow and dead are indistinguishable from outside, so verdicts are suspicions that can be wrong","Gossip should have blocked the verdict"],"answer":2,"why":"From a peer's view a paused node and a crashed node look identical, so any detector can only suspect and must tolerate being wrong."},{"q":"A 5-node cluster partitions into groups of 3 and 2. Which rule prevents two leaders?","options":["Both groups elect a leader, then merge by timestamp","Only the group with at least 3 of the 5 configured members may lead","The group whose heartbeats are fastest leads","The group containing the original leader always leads"],"answer":1,"why":"Requiring a strict majority of the full membership means two disjoint groups can never both qualify, so at most one leader exists."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Heartbeat","def":"A small periodic message meaning I am alive. Receivers track the time of the last one; missing beats raise suspicion."},{"term":"Fixed timeout","def":"Declare a node dead after N seconds of silence. Simple, but one value cannot suit both fast LANs and jittery WANs."},{"term":"Phi-accrual","def":"A detector outputting a continuous suspicion score from learned heartbeat timing. Phi 8 is about a one-in-100-million chance of a false alarm under the model."},{"term":"Gossip protocol","def":"Each round, every node swaps state with a few random peers. Spreads news in about log N rounds with flat per-node load."},{"term":"Membership list","def":"Each node's view of who is in the cluster and whether they look up or down, versioned so newer facts win in gossip."},{"term":"False positive","def":"A healthy node wrongly marked down, often after a GC pause or network blip. Aggressive thresholds cause flapping."},{"term":"Split brain","def":"Two partitions both act as the authority and accept conflicting writes. Prevent with majority quorums."},{"term":"Majority quorum","def":"More than half of the configured members, e.g. 3 of 5. Any two majorities overlap, so only one side can proceed."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"crdts","title":"Conflict Resolution & CRDTs","subtitle":"Watch two replicas accept writes while cut off from each other, then see why last-write-wins loses data and merge rules do not.","minutes":14,"level":"Advanced","learn":["Why multi-leader and offline-first systems must handle concurrent writes","How last-write-wins silently drops updates, and when that is acceptable","How a G-counter merges by taking the per-replica maximum","How an OR-set lets an add survive a concurrent remove","The idea behind collaborative text editing, and where CRDTs stop working"],"nodes":[{"id":"alice","label":"Alice's phone","kind":"client","x":15,"y":25},{"id":"ra","label":"Replica A","kind":"region","x":55,"y":25},{"id":"bob","label":"Bob's laptop","kind":"client","x":15,"y":75},{"id":"rb","label":"Replica B","kind":"region","x":55,"y":75}],"edges":[{"from":"alice","to":"ra"},{"from":"bob","to":"rb"},{"from":"ra","to":"rb","label":"sync link"}],"metrics":[{"id":"a","label":"Replica A","unit":"likes"},{"id":"b","label":"Replica B","unit":"likes"},{"id":"truth","label":"True total","unit":"likes"},{"id":"lost","label":"Lost updates","unit":"writes"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Two copies, one cable cut","body":"A post has 10 likes, stored on Replica A and Replica B so each user writes to a nearby copy. Now the link between them drops, or one user goes offline in a tunnel. Both replicas must keep accepting writes, because refusing them would make the app unusable. When the link returns, the copies will have diverged. What should the merged value be?","idea":"If replicas accept writes independently, conflicts are guaranteed; the only question is how you resolve them.","caption":"Both replicas start at 10 likes.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"replicate","label":"10 likes"}],"metrics":{"a":"10","b":"10","truth":"10","lost":"0"}},{"tag":"CONFLICT","title":"Concurrent writes","body":"While partitioned, Alice taps like once on Replica A, so A stores 11. Bob taps like twice on Replica B, so B stores 12. Neither write knew about the other, which is what concurrent means: neither happened before the other. The honest total is 13, but each replica now holds a different number and the sync link is down.","idea":"Concurrent means neither write had seen the other, not that they happened at the same instant.","caption":"A shows 11, B shows 12, truth is 13.","focus":["alice","ra","bob","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"like +1"},{"from":"bob","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"like +2"},{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"fail","label":"partitioned"}],"metrics":{"a":"11","b":"12","truth":"13"}},{"tag":"LWW","title":"Last write wins","body":"The simplest rule: every write carries a timestamp, and on sync the replica with the newest timestamp overwrites the other. Here B wrote last, so both settle on 12. The copies agree, but Alice's like is gone and nobody was told. LWW also trusts wall clocks, and clocks on different machines can disagree by tens of milliseconds or more, so the winner may not even be the truly later write.","idea":"LWW always converges, but it throws away one side of every conflict, so it is only safe when losing a write is fine.","caption":"Sync keeps B's value; Alice's like is lost.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"12 @ t=10:04"}],"metrics":{"a":"12","b":"12","lost":"1"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Merge instead of overwrite","body":"A CRDT (conflict-free replicated data type) is a data structure designed so that any two copies can be merged without a coordinator. The merge function must be commutative (order of the two inputs does not matter), associative (grouping does not matter), and idempotent (merging the same state twice changes nothing). If those hold, replicas that receive the same updates end up identical, in any order, with duplicates.","idea":"A CRDT turns conflict resolution into a math property of the merge function, so no write is arbitrarily discarded.","caption":"Replicas now exchange state and merge it.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"replicate","label":"state"},{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"state"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"G-COUNTER","title":"A counter that cannot lose","body":"Instead of one number, a G-counter (grow-only counter) keeps one slot per replica. Start at {A:5, B:5}, total 10. Alice's like bumps A's slot to 6. Bob's two likes bump B's slot to 7. Each replica only ever writes its own slot. Merge takes the maximum of each slot: {A:6, B:7}. The value is the sum, 13, which is the true total.","idea":"Give each replica its own slot and merge with max; the counter's value is the sum of the slots.","caption":"Slots merge to {A:6, B:7}, total 13.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"replicate","label":"{A:6,B:5}"},{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"{A:5,B:7}"}],"metrics":{"a":"13","b":"13","truth":"13","lost":"0"}},{"tag":"IDEMPOTENT","title":"Duplicates and reordering are safe","body":"Networks retry, duplicate and reorder messages. Replica B receives A's state a second time, late, after it already merged. Taking the max of values it already has changes nothing, so the result stays 13. This is why CRDT sync can use simple at-least-once delivery with no deduplication. A G-counter cannot go down; a PN-counter uses two G-counters, one for increments and one for decrements, and subtracts.","idea":"Idempotent merge means replaying old state is harmless, so sync can be sloppy and still correct.","caption":"A duplicate state message changes nothing.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"replicate","label":"{A:6,B:5} again"}],"metrics":{"a":"13","b":"13","truth":"13"}},{"tag":"OR-SET","title":"A set with add and remove","body":"Sets are harder: Alice removes milk from a shared cart on A while Bob, unaware, adds milk on B. An observed-remove set (OR-set) tags every add with a unique id. A remove deletes only the tags the remover has seen. Bob's add carries a brand new tag Alice never saw, so after merge milk stays in the cart. This is called add-wins semantics.","idea":"Unique tags let a remove cancel only the adds it knew about, so a concurrent add survives.","caption":"Remove cancels the old tag; the new add stays.","focus":["alice","ra","bob","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"remove milk"},{"from":"bob","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"add milk #t9"},{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"milk {#t9}"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"COLLAB","title":"Collaborative text editing","body":"Editors like Google Docs-style tools need two people typing in the same sentence without clobbering each other. Sequence CRDTs such as the ones in Yjs and Automerge give every character a unique id and a position defined relative to its neighbours, not an index. Concurrent inserts land in a deterministic order on every replica, and deletes leave a tombstone marker. The cost is metadata that grows with edit history.","idea":"Identify each character by a stable id instead of a shifting index, and concurrent edits can merge deterministically.","caption":"Concurrent inserts merge into one document.","focus":["alice","ra","bob","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"ra","kind":"request","label":"insert 'big '"},{"from":"bob","to":"rb","kind":"request","label":"insert 'red '"},{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"replicate","label":"ops"},{"from":"rb","to":"ra","kind":"replicate","label":"ops"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"What CRDTs cannot do","body":"Merge rules work for data where combining is meaningful: counters, sets, text, flags. They cannot enforce a global rule like a balance that must stay at or above zero, or a username that must be unique, because two replicas can each approve a valid-looking write that is invalid together. Those cases need coordination, such as consensus or a single owner, and you pay latency for it.","idea":"CRDTs give availability and convergence; invariants that span replicas still need coordination.","caption":"Two approvals that conflict cannot be merged away.","focus":["ra","rb"],"flows":[{"from":"ra","to":"rb","kind":"fail","label":"both approve"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick the merge rule on purpose","body":"Concurrent writes are unavoidable once replicas accept writes independently. LWW is simple but drops data and trusts clocks. CRDTs define a merge that is commutative, associative and idempotent, so every replica converges: G-counters take per-slot max, OR-sets let adds beat concurrent removes, and sequence CRDTs merge text. Use them when losing writes is unacceptable and coordination is too costly.","idea":"Choose the conflict rule deliberately; the default of overwrite loses data.","caption":"Both replicas converge on 13 with nothing lost.","focus":["alice","ra","bob","rb"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"a":"13","b":"13","truth":"13","lost":"0"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two replicas of a like counter each receive a different update while partitioned. After sync using last-write-wins, what happens?","options":["Both updates are combined into the sum","One replica's update is discarded and the copies agree","The replicas stay different until an admin picks one","The older timestamp wins because it was first"],"answer":1,"why":"LWW keeps the value with the newest timestamp and overwrites the other, so the copies converge but one write is silently lost."},{"q":"A G-counter is at {A:6, B:5} on one replica and {A:5, B:7} on another. What does a merge produce?","options":["{A:5, B:5}, total 10","{A:6, B:7}, total 13","{A:11, B:12}, total 23","{A:6, B:5}, because it arrived first"],"answer":1,"why":"Merge takes the maximum of each replica's own slot, giving {A:6, B:7}. Adding slots would double count updates that both sides already saw."},{"q":"Why is it safe for CRDT sync to deliver the same state message twice?","options":["The network guarantees exactly-once delivery","Each message is encrypted with a nonce","The merge is idempotent, so reapplying known state changes nothing","Replicas ignore messages older than one minute"],"answer":2,"why":"Idempotence is part of the merge contract. Taking max or union with state you already have yields the same state."},{"q":"In an OR-set, one user removes an item while another concurrently adds the same item. Why does the item survive?","options":["Adds always have a later wall-clock time","The remove only deletes the unique tags it had observed, and the new add has a tag it never saw","The set locks the item during removal","Removes are ignored if the network is partitioned"],"answer":1,"why":"Each add gets a unique tag. A remove cancels only the tags it knew about, so a concurrent add with a fresh tag is preserved."},{"q":"You must guarantee a bank balance never drops below zero across replicas. Which statement is true?","options":["A PN-counter enforces the limit automatically","A CRDT alone cannot enforce it; you need coordination for that invariant","LWW with synchronized clocks enforces it","Use a G-counter for withdrawals"],"answer":1,"why":"Two replicas can each approve a withdrawal that is valid alone but overdraws together. Cross-replica invariants need coordination such as consensus or a single owner."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Concurrent writes","def":"Writes where neither had seen the other when it was made. They are not about exact simultaneity, only missing knowledge."},{"term":"Last-write-wins (LWW)","def":"Keep the write with the newest timestamp and drop the rest. Simple and convergent, but loses data and depends on clocks."},{"term":"CRDT","def":"A data type whose merge is commutative, associative and idempotent, so replicas converge without a coordinator."},{"term":"G-counter","def":"Grow-only counter with one slot per replica. Merge is per-slot max; the value is the sum. Add a second one to make a PN-counter."},{"term":"OR-set","def":"Observed-remove set. Each add has a unique tag and a remove deletes only the tags it saw, so concurrent adds survive."},{"term":"State-based vs op-based","def":"State-based CRDTs ship full or delta state and merge it. Op-based ones ship operations and need reliable, causally ordered delivery."},{"term":"Tombstone","def":"A marker left in place of a deleted item so concurrent operations still resolve. It makes metadata grow until garbage collected."},{"term":"Where CRDTs fail","def":"Global invariants like non-negative balances or unique usernames still need coordination such as consensus."}],"isNew":true},{"slug":"unique-ids","title":"Unique ID Generation","subtitle":"Watch several servers mint IDs at the same time, and see why a plain counter stops working and what replaces it.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why a database auto-increment counter becomes a bottleneck and breaks when you add shards","How UUIDv4 and UUIDv7 differ, and why random keys hurt database indexes","How a Snowflake ID packs time, machine and sequence into 64 bits","How a ticket server hands out ID ranges, and what happens when a clock jumps backward","What sortable IDs buy you, and what they leak"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Clients","kind":"user","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"appa","label":"App server A","kind":"service","x":36,"y":22},{"id":"appb","label":"App server B","kind":"service","x":36,"y":78},{"id":"ticket","label":"Ticket server","kind":"server","x":64,"y":12},{"id":"db","label":"Orders database","kind":"db","x":64,"y":50},{"id":"idgen","label":"Snowflake generator","kind":"worker","x":64,"y":88},{"id":"idx","label":"Primary-key index","kind":"storage","x":90,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"appa"},{"from":"client","to":"appb"},{"from":"appa","to":"db"},{"from":"appb","to":"db"},{"from":"appa","to":"ticket"},{"from":"appb","to":"ticket"},{"from":"appa","to":"idgen"},{"from":"appb","to":"idgen"},{"from":"db","to":"idx"}],"metrics":[{"id":"rate","label":"Max IDs/s","unit":"IDs/s"},{"id":"hops","label":"Network hops","unit":"per ID"},{"id":"size","label":"ID size","unit":"bytes"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Every row needs a name","body":"Each new order, message or user needs an ID that no other row will ever have. With one server, a counter is enough. With many servers creating rows at the same moment, who decides the next number? The answer must be unique, cheap to produce, and ideally not a bottleneck or a single point of failure.","idea":"An ID scheme is judged on uniqueness, speed, size, sortability, and whether it needs coordination.","caption":"Two servers both need IDs right now.","focus":["client","appa","appb"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"appa","kind":"request","label":"create order"},{"from":"client","to":"appb","kind":"request","label":"create order"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"AUTO-INC","title":"Let the database count","body":"The simplest scheme is a database auto-increment column (a sequence). The app inserts a row and the database returns 1001, then 1002, and so on. IDs are small (8 bytes), perfectly ordered, and never collide. The cost is that every insert goes through one writer, so ID creation takes a network hop and is capped by that database. Assume roughly 10,000 inserts per second as a rule of thumb for one primary.","idea":"Auto-increment is correct and compact, but one counter means one choke point.","caption":"The single database hands out the next number.","focus":["appa","db"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT order"},{"from":"db","to":"appa","kind":"response","label":"id = 1001"}],"metrics":{"rate":"10000","hops":"1","size":"8"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Add a second writer","body":"Traffic grows, so you split the data across two databases, and each keeps its own counter. Both now hand out 1001, and the moment you merge, export, or reference rows across shards the IDs collide. You can patch it by giving shard 1 odd numbers and shard 2 even numbers, but adding a third shard means changing the scheme. Meanwhile app B waits in line behind app A.","idea":"A counter only works while exactly one thing owns it; sharding breaks that ownership.","caption":"Two writers, one counter: waiting or clashing.","focus":["appb","db"],"flows":[{"from":"appb","to":"db","kind":"fail","label":"wait or clash"}],"metrics":{"rate":"10000","hops":"1","size":"8"}},{"tag":"UUID V4","title":"Roll the dice locally","body":"A UUIDv4 is 128 bits with 122 of them random. Each server makes one on its own with zero coordination, millions per second. Collisions are possible in theory but absurdly unlikely: after a billion IDs the chance of any duplicate is around 1 in 10 quintillion. The price is size (16 bytes) and randomness: inserting random keys scatters writes across the whole primary-key index instead of one hot spot.","idea":"UUIDv4 trades coordination for size and index-unfriendly randomness.","caption":"Each server mints its own ID, no network call.","focus":["appa","appb","db"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT uuid4"},{"from":"appb","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT uuid4"}],"metrics":{"rate":"1000000","hops":"0","size":"16"}},{"tag":"UUID V7","title":"Put time at the front","body":"UUIDv7 (standardized in RFC 9562, 2024) keeps the 128-bit shape but starts with a 48-bit Unix timestamp in milliseconds, followed by about 74 bits of random and counter data. Still no coordination, and IDs created later sort later. In a B-tree index new keys land at the right edge, so pages stay hot in memory and splits are rare. Ordering is only millisecond-accurate, and the timestamp is readable by anyone who sees the ID.","idea":"UUIDv7 keeps UUID's no-coordination property and adds time ordering that indexes love.","caption":"Time-prefixed keys append at the index edge.","focus":["appa","db","idx"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT uuid7"},{"from":"db","to":"idx","kind":"async","label":"append at end"}],"metrics":{"rate":"1000000","hops":"0","size":"16"}},{"tag":"SNOWFLAKE","title":"Time + machine + sequence","body":"A Snowflake-style ID fits in 64 bits: 1 unused sign bit, 41 bits of milliseconds since a custom epoch (about 69 years), 10 bits of worker number (1,024 workers), and a 12-bit sequence. Within one millisecond a worker counts 0 to 4095, so one worker can issue 4,096,000 IDs per second. Because the worker number differs per machine, no two machines can ever produce the same ID, and no network call is needed.","idea":"Snowflake splits the ID into fields so uniqueness comes from who and when, not from a shared counter.","caption":"A local generator builds a sortable 64-bit ID.","focus":["appa","idgen"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"idgen","kind":"request","label":"next id"},{"from":"idgen","to":"appa","kind":"response","label":"64-bit id"}],"metrics":{"rate":"4096000","hops":"0","size":"8"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The clock goes backward","body":"Snowflake depends on the wall clock. If NTP corrects a machine and time jumps back 20 ms, the generator may repeat a timestamp it already used and could issue a duplicate ID. A safe generator remembers the last timestamp it used. When the clock is behind that, it waits for time to catch up, or refuses to issue IDs, rather than guess. Worker numbers must also be unique, so they are assigned by a coordinator or by config, never picked randomly.","idea":"Time-based IDs are only as safe as the clock and the uniqueness of worker numbers.","caption":"Generator refuses IDs until the clock recovers.","focus":["appa","idgen"],"flows":[{"from":"idgen","to":"appa","kind":"fail","label":"clock moved back"}],"metrics":{"rate":"4096000","hops":"0","size":"8"}},{"tag":"TICKET SERVER","title":"Lease ranges of IDs","body":"A ticket server is a small dedicated service, historically a database with an auto-increment table, that hands out numbers. Asking it for every ID would repeat the bottleneck, so apps request a block, say 1,000 IDs, and spend them locally. That is one hop per 1,000 IDs. Run two ticket servers, one issuing odd blocks and one even, so one can fail. IDs are small and mostly increasing, but a crashed app loses its unused block, leaving harmless gaps.","idea":"Handing out ranges turns one coordination call into thousands of free local IDs.","caption":"App A leases a block of 1,000 IDs.","focus":["appa","ticket"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"ticket","kind":"request","label":"need 1000 ids"},{"from":"ticket","to":"appa","kind":"response","label":"10000-10999"}],"metrics":{"rate":"1000000","hops":"0.001","size":"8"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Why sortable matters","body":"Time-ordered IDs (v7, Snowflake, ticket blocks) let you page with a simple cursor such as WHERE id < last_seen ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 50, and they keep inserts append-like. Ordering across machines is only approximate: two servers can create IDs in the same millisecond, and clock skew shifts things further, so never treat ID order as true event order. The creation time is also embedded, which can leak how fast you grow.","idea":"Sortable IDs make queries and indexes faster, but order is approximate and time becomes visible.","caption":"Both servers append to the same index edge.","focus":["appa","appb","db","idx"],"flows":[{"from":"appa","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT 7f3a..."},{"from":"appb","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"INSERT 7f3b..."},{"from":"db","to":"idx","kind":"async","label":"append at end"}],"metrics":{"hops":"0","size":"8"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Pick by constraint","body":"Use auto-increment for a single database where simplicity wins. Use UUIDv7 when you want no coordination and standard tooling. Use Snowflake-style 64-bit IDs when size and throughput matter and you can manage worker numbers and clocks. Use ticket servers or ID blocks when you want small numeric IDs with rare coordination. Avoid UUIDv4 as a primary key on large indexed tables unless you need unguessable, order-free values.","idea":"Every scheme trades coordination, size, ordering, and clock trust; choose the one whose weakness you can live with.","caption":"Four ways to mint IDs, one trade-off each.","focus":["client","appa","appb","ticket","db","idgen","idx"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"rate":"4096000","hops":"0","size":"8"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two database shards each auto-increment from 1, and you later merge their orders into one table. What goes wrong?","options":["Nothing, since each row has its own timestamp","The IDs become random and unsortable","Both shards produced the same IDs, so rows collide","Auto-increment IDs cannot exceed 32 bits"],"answer":2,"why":"Each counter is independent, so both shards issue 1001, 1002 and so on. Merged rows have duplicate keys, which is why a shared counter does not survive sharding."},{"q":"A Snowflake worker has a 12-bit sequence field and 1 ms timestamp resolution. What is the most IDs one worker can issue per second?","options":["1,024","40,960","65,536","4,096,000"],"answer":3,"why":"12 bits gives 4,096 values per millisecond, and there are 1,000 ms in a second, so 4,096 × 1,000 = 4,096,000. The 10-bit worker field (1,024) only sets how many workers exist."},{"q":"Why does inserting UUIDv7 keys usually perform better than UUIDv4 keys on a large B-tree primary-key index?","options":["New v7 keys land near the end of the index, so writes stay on a few hot pages","UUIDv7 is shorter than UUIDv4","UUIDv7 needs no unique check","UUIDv7 is generated by the database"],"answer":0,"why":"The leading timestamp makes keys increase over time, so inserts append at the right edge. Random v4 keys land on arbitrary pages, causing cache misses and page splits. Both are 16 bytes."},{"q":"A Snowflake generator notices the system clock is 20 ms behind the timestamp of the last ID it issued. What is the safest behavior?","options":["Use the current clock anyway and hope the sequence differs","Wait or refuse until the clock passes the last timestamp used","Pick a new random worker number","Reset the sequence to zero and continue"],"answer":1,"why":"Reusing an earlier millisecond risks repeating a timestamp, worker and sequence combination already issued, which would create a duplicate. Waiting for time to catch up preserves uniqueness."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Auto-increment","def":"A database counter. Small and ordered, but one writer owns it, so it caps throughput and breaks when you shard."},{"term":"UUID V4","def":"128 bits, 122 random. No coordination, collision odds negligible, but 16 bytes and random order that scatters index writes."},{"term":"UUID V7","def":"128 bits led by a 48-bit millisecond Unix timestamp plus random bits (RFC 9562). Time-sortable, index-friendly, no coordination."},{"term":"Snowflake ID","def":"64 bits: 41 time (ms), 10 worker, 12 sequence. About 4.1 million IDs per second per worker; needs unique worker numbers and sane clocks."},{"term":"Ticket server","def":"A small service that leases blocks of numbers to apps. Coordination happens once per block; run two with odd/even offsets for availability."},{"term":"K-sorted","def":"Roughly time-ordered: IDs sort by creation to within the clock and millisecond precision, not by exact event order across machines."},{"term":"Clock rollback","def":"The system clock moving backward after a sync. Generators must remember the last timestamp and wait or refuse rather than reuse it."},{"term":"Keyset pagination","def":"Paging with WHERE id < cursor ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT n. Fast with sortable IDs, no OFFSET scans."}],"isNew":false}]},{"id":"communication","title":"Communication","lessons":[{"slug":"apis","title":"API Styles: REST, RPC, GraphQL","subtitle":"Load one mobile order screen three different ways and see what each API style costs in round trips and bytes.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How REST models resources and why it leans on HTTP verbs and caching","Why gRPC and protobuf suit fast service-to-service calls","How GraphQL lets the client ask for exactly the fields it needs, and what that costs the server","Why cursor pagination beats offset pagination on large, changing data","How to evolve an API without breaking existing clients","A simple rule for choosing a style for a given caller"],"nodes":[{"id":"web","label":"Web client","kind":"client","x":10,"y":28},{"id":"mobile","label":"Mobile app","kind":"client","x":10,"y":75},{"id":"gw","label":"API gateway","kind":"gateway","x":34,"y":50},{"id":"orders","label":"Orders (REST)","kind":"service","x":60,"y":18},{"id":"inv","label":"Inventory (gRPC)","kind":"service","x":60,"y":50},{"id":"gql","label":"GraphQL server","kind":"server","x":60,"y":82},{"id":"db","label":"Database","kind":"db","x":86,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"web","to":"gw"},{"from":"mobile","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders"},{"from":"gw","to":"inv"},{"from":"gw","to":"gql"},{"from":"gql","to":"orders"},{"from":"gql","to":"inv"},{"from":"orders","to":"db"},{"from":"inv","to":"db"}],"metrics":[{"id":"trips","label":"Round trips","unit":"calls"},{"id":"payload","label":"Payload","unit":"KB"},{"id":"latency","label":"Screen load","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One screen, many needs","body":"A mobile app shows an order screen: the order, the customer's name, and each item with its stock status. The data lives in an Orders service and an Inventory service. How should the app ask for it? The answer depends on who is calling, how far away they are, and how often the shape of the data changes. We will assume a phone on a 60 ms round-trip (RTT) mobile network.","idea":"An API style is a contract for how callers ask for data; the right one depends on the caller.","caption":"The phone needs data from two backend services.","focus":["mobile","gw"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"REST","title":"REST: resources and verbs","body":"REST models things as resources with URLs: GET /orders/812 reads one, POST /orders creates one, PUT or PATCH changes it, DELETE removes it. Status codes carry the outcome (200, 404, 409). Because GET is safe and repeatable, browsers, CDNs, and proxies can cache it using standard headers. Assume this order response is about 18 KB of JSON.","idea":"REST maps nouns to URLs and actions to HTTP verbs, which makes it cacheable and easy to debug with curl.","caption":"One GET returns the order resource.","focus":["mobile","gw","orders"],"flows":[{"from":"mobile","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders/812"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders/812"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"200 order JSON"},{"from":"gw","to":"mobile","kind":"response","label":"200 order JSON"}],"metrics":{"trips":"1","payload":"18","latency":"70"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"REST's chattiness problem","body":"The order JSON holds only a customer id and item ids. To draw the screen the app must also GET the customer and then the stock for the items. That is under-fetching: three sequential round trips at 60 ms each. Each response also carries fields the screen never shows, which is over-fetching. Together they push the total to about 40 KB and 210 ms.","idea":"Fixed-shape resources force extra calls (under-fetching) or extra bytes (over-fetching) for screens that need a custom view.","caption":"Three sequential calls to build one screen.","focus":["mobile","gw","orders","inv"],"flows":[{"from":"mobile","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /customers/9"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"customer"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"customer JSON"},{"from":"mobile","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /stock?ids"},{"from":"gw","to":"inv","kind":"request","label":"stock"}],"metrics":{"trips":"3","payload":"40","latency":"210"}},{"tag":"RPC","title":"gRPC: fast calls between services","body":"Inside the data center the gateway calls Inventory with gRPC, a remote procedure call (RPC) framework. The contract is a .proto file that defines methods and typed messages. Protobuf encodes them as compact binary, often a third to a quarter the size of equivalent JSON, over HTTP/2 with many calls multiplexed on one connection. Generated client code makes the call look like a local function. Browsers cannot speak it directly without a proxy.","idea":"gRPC trades human readability and browser reach for speed, strict types, and generated clients, which fits internal traffic.","caption":"The gateway calls Inventory over binary gRPC.","focus":["gw","inv","db"],"flows":[{"from":"gw","to":"inv","kind":"request","label":"GetStock(ids)"},{"from":"inv","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"SELECT stock"},{"from":"db","to":"inv","kind":"response","label":"rows"},{"from":"inv","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"StockReply"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"GRAPHQL","title":"GraphQL: ask for exactly what you need","body":"With GraphQL the phone sends one query to a single endpoint, naming the exact fields: order id, customer name, and each item's name and in-stock flag. The GraphQL server resolves each field by calling Orders and Inventory, then returns only what was asked for. One round trip carries about 6 KB, so the screen loads in roughly 90 ms instead of 210.","idea":"GraphQL moves the choice of shape to the client, collapsing many calls into one precise response.","caption":"One query, fetched fan-out behind the server.","focus":["mobile","gw","gql","orders","inv"],"flows":[{"from":"mobile","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"POST /graphql"},{"from":"gw","to":"gql","kind":"request","label":"query { order }"},{"from":"gql","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"order, customer"},{"from":"gql","to":"inv","kind":"request","label":"stock"},{"from":"gql","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"6 KB JSON"},{"from":"gw","to":"mobile","kind":"response","label":"6 KB JSON"}],"metrics":{"trips":"1","payload":"6","latency":"90"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The hidden costs of GraphQL","body":"Flexibility moves work to the server. A client can ask for orders, each with items, each with reviews, and one query fans out into hundreds of backend calls (the N+1 problem) unless you batch lookups. Because everything is a POST to one URL, HTTP caching is mostly lost, so you cache by object id instead. You also need depth and cost limits so one query cannot take the system down.","idea":"GraphQL needs batching, query-cost limits, and its own caching; the server pays for the client's freedom.","caption":"A deep query fans out into many backend calls.","focus":["gql","orders","inv"],"flows":[{"from":"gql","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"item 1"},{"from":"gql","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"item 2"},{"from":"gql","to":"inv","kind":"fail","label":"N+1 calls"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"PAGINATION","title":"Listing without loading everything","body":"Any list endpoint needs pagination. Offset style (?limit=50&offset=100000) is simple but makes the database skip 100,000 rows each time, and rows inserted meanwhile shift pages so items repeat or vanish. Cursor style (?limit=50&after=ord_7f3a) resumes from the last row seen using an indexed key, so cost stays flat and pages stay stable. GraphQL conventionally exposes the same idea as cursor-based connections.","idea":"Prefer cursor pagination for large or changing lists; reserve offset for small, stable data.","caption":"The Orders service pages with a cursor.","focus":["web","gw","orders","db"],"flows":[{"from":"web","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /orders?after"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"after=c1"},{"from":"orders","to":"db","kind":"request","label":"WHERE id > c1"},{"from":"orders","to":"gw","kind":"response","label":"50 + next cursor"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"VERSIONING","title":"Changing an API without breaking callers","body":"Old app versions stay in the wild for years, so you cannot change a response and hope. Safe changes are additive: new optional fields, new endpoints. Breaking changes (rename, remove, retype) need a new version, such as /v2/orders or a header, with the old one kept until usage drops. Protobuf allows adding fields but never reusing a field number. GraphQL marks fields deprecated and removes them later.","idea":"Make changes additive, and run old and new versions side by side when a break is unavoidable.","caption":"Old and new clients hit different versions.","focus":["web","mobile","gw","orders"],"flows":[{"from":"mobile","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /v1/orders/812"},{"from":"web","to":"gw","kind":"request","label":"GET /v2/orders/812"},{"from":"gw","to":"orders","kind":"request","label":"v1 and v2"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Choosing a style","body":"Use REST for public, cacheable, resource-shaped APIs that many unknown clients will call. Use gRPC between your own services where speed and strict contracts matter. Use GraphQL when many client types need different views of connected data, such as phones and web. Many systems mix all three behind a gateway. Paginate lists with cursors and evolve additively.","idea":"Pick by caller: REST for the public, gRPC for internal hops, GraphQL for flexible client views.","caption":"Each style serves a different caller.","focus":["web","mobile","gw","orders","inv","gql"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"trips":"1","payload":"6","latency":"90"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A phone on a 100 ms RTT network needs data that takes three dependent REST calls. What is the most direct fix for load time?","options":["Compress the JSON with gzip","Return the combined data in one call, for example via a GraphQL query or a purpose-built endpoint","Switch the calls from GET to POST","Add more database replicas"],"answer":1,"why":"Dependent calls cost one RTT each, about 300 ms here. Cutting the number of round trips helps far more than shrinking bytes."},{"q":"Why is gRPC a better fit than REST for calls between your own backend services than for a public browser API?","options":["gRPC works natively in every browser","gRPC uses compact binary messages, HTTP/2 multiplexing, and typed generated clients, while browsers lack direct support","gRPC responses are cached by CDNs automatically","gRPC removes the need for a schema"],"answer":1,"why":"Binary protobuf and HTTP/2 reduce size and latency, and the .proto contract gives strict types. Browsers need a proxy, so public APIs usually stay REST or GraphQL."},{"q":"A GraphQL endpoint slows down after clients start requesting orders with nested items and reviews. Which is the likely cause and remedy?","options":["HTTP verbs are wrong; switch to PUT","N+1 resolver calls; batch lookups and add query depth or cost limits","The schema is too small; add more types","Protobuf encoding is too large"],"answer":1,"why":"Nested fields can trigger one backend call per parent item. Batching (such as a data loader) and cost limits keep fan-out under control."},{"q":"A feed is paged with ?offset=. While a user reads page 2, ten new posts are inserted at the top. What happens when they load page 3?","options":["Nothing; offsets are stable","Some posts from page 2 appear again, because everything shifted down","Ten posts are skipped","The server returns an error"],"answer":1,"why":"Inserts at the front shift every later row, so offset-based pages repeat items. A cursor anchored to the last seen id avoids this."},{"q":"You must rename the field total to totalCents in a REST response used by apps you cannot force to update. What is the safest approach?","options":["Rename it in place and announce the change","Add totalCents alongside total, keep both, and remove total only after old clients are gone or in a new version","Delete total and return an error to old clients","Change the field's type but keep its name"],"answer":1,"why":"Additive changes do not break existing callers. Removal or rename is a breaking change that needs a transition period or a new version."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"REST","def":"Resources at URLs, acted on with HTTP verbs and status codes. Easy to cache and debug; fixed response shapes can cause over- or under-fetching."},{"term":"gRPC / protobuf","def":"RPC over HTTP/2 with a .proto contract and compact binary messages. Fast and strictly typed; best for internal service calls."},{"term":"GraphQL","def":"One endpoint where the client sends a query naming exact fields. Fewer round trips; needs batching, cost limits, and id-based caching."},{"term":"Over/under-fetching","def":"Over-fetching returns unused fields; under-fetching forces extra calls because one response lacks needed data."},{"term":"N+1 problem","def":"One query for a list plus one extra call per item. Fix by batching lookups or joining."},{"term":"Cursor pagination","def":"Resume from the last seen key (?after=id). Stable under inserts and constant cost, unlike large offsets."},{"term":"Additive change","def":"Adding optional fields or endpoints. Does not break old clients; renames, removals, and type changes do."},{"term":"Idempotent","def":"Repeating the call has the same effect as doing it once. GET, PUT, and DELETE should be; POST usually is not, so use idempotency keys for payments."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"realtime","title":"Real-Time: Polling, SSE, WebSockets","subtitle":"Watch one chat message reach a user who never asked for it, using four different ways to let a server speak first.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why plain HTTP cannot push, and how short and long polling fake it","How Server-Sent Events and WebSockets keep one connection open","When to pick polling, SSE or WebSockets","Why connection gateways are stateful and what that does to scaling","How a message crosses gateways through a pub/sub bus, and what happens when a gateway dies"],"nodes":[{"id":"alice","label":"Alice (sender)","kind":"client","x":10,"y":24},{"id":"bob","label":"Bob (receiver)","kind":"client","x":10,"y":78},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":30,"y":50},{"id":"gw1","label":"Gateway A","kind":"gateway","x":51,"y":24},{"id":"gw2","label":"Gateway B","kind":"gateway","x":51,"y":78},{"id":"bus","label":"Pub/sub bus","kind":"queue","x":72,"y":50},{"id":"chat","label":"Chat service","kind":"service","x":90,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"alice","to":"lb"},{"from":"bob","to":"lb"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw1"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw2"},{"from":"gw1","to":"bus"},{"from":"gw2","to":"bus"},{"from":"bus","to":"chat"}],"metrics":[{"id":"reqs","label":"Requests/s","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"conns","label":"Open conns","unit":"conns"},{"id":"delay","label":"Avg delay","unit":"ms"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"The server has news, the client is silent","body":"Alice sends Bob a chat message. The server now holds something Bob wants, but HTTP only works one way: a client asks, the server answers. A server cannot start a conversation on its own. So how does Bob's screen update without him refreshing? Throughout this lesson we assume 100,000 connected users.","idea":"Plain HTTP is request/response, so real-time needs a trick or a different protocol.","caption":"Bob is idle; the news is on the server.","focus":["bob","gw2"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"reqs":"0","conns":"0","delay":"0"}},{"tag":"SHORT POLL","title":"Ask again every few seconds","body":"The simplest fix: Bob's app asks \"anything new?\" every 5 seconds. It works with any server and cache. But 100,000 users polling every 5 s is 20,000 requests per second, almost all returning nothing. And a message waits on average half the interval, about 2.5 s, before Bob sees it.","idea":"Short polling is trivially simple but wastes requests and adds delay equal to half the poll interval.","caption":"Most polls come back empty.","focus":["bob","lb","gw2"],"flows":[{"from":"bob","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"any news?"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw2","kind":"request","label":"any news?"},{"from":"gw2","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"nothing"},{"from":"lb","to":"bob","kind":"response","label":"empty 200"}],"metrics":{"reqs":"20000","conns":"0","delay":"2500"}},{"tag":"LONG POLL","title":"Ask, and the server waits","body":"With long polling the server does not answer an empty poll. It holds the request open, often up to 30 seconds, and replies the moment something arrives. Bob's app then immediately asks again. Delay drops to about a network trip, and idle traffic falls to roughly 100,000 / 30 = 3,300 requests per second, though every message still pays for a full new request.","idea":"Long polling gives near-instant delivery over plain HTTP by parking the request until there is news.","caption":"The request is held, then answered at once.","focus":["bob","lb","gw2"],"flows":[{"from":"bob","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"wait for news"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw2","kind":"request","label":"held up to 30 s"},{"from":"gw2","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"new message"},{"from":"lb","to":"bob","kind":"response","label":"new message"}],"metrics":{"reqs":"3333","conns":"100000","delay":"50"}},{"tag":"SSE","title":"Server-Sent Events: one long download","body":"Server-Sent Events (SSE) make that held response permanent. Bob opens one GET with Accept: text/event-stream, and the server keeps writing small text events into it. The browser's EventSource reconnects on its own and sends Last-Event-ID so the server can resume. It is one-way, server to client, which fits feeds, notifications and progress bars. Over HTTP/1.1 browsers allow only about 6 connections per host, so use HTTP/2.","idea":"SSE is a one-way push stream over ordinary HTTP with built-in reconnect and resume.","caption":"One open response; events flow down it.","focus":["bob","lb","gw2"],"flows":[{"from":"bob","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"GET /events"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw2","kind":"handshake","label":"stream opens"},{"from":"gw2","to":"lb","kind":"async","label":"event: msg"},{"from":"lb","to":"bob","kind":"async","label":"event: msg"}],"metrics":{"reqs":"0","conns":"100000","delay":"20"}},{"tag":"WEBSOCKET","title":"WebSockets: a two-way pipe","body":"A WebSocket starts as an HTTP request with an Upgrade header. The server answers 101 Switching Protocols and the same TCP connection becomes a full-duplex channel. Both sides now send small frames at any time, each with only 2 to 14 bytes of header. That suits chat input, games and collaborative editing, where the client talks as much as the server does.","idea":"WebSockets upgrade one HTTP connection into a bidirectional message channel with tiny per-message overhead.","caption":"Upgrade handshake, then frames both ways.","focus":["bob","lb","gw2"],"flows":[{"from":"bob","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"Upgrade: websocket"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw2","kind":"handshake","label":"Upgrade"},{"from":"gw2","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"101 Switching"},{"from":"lb","to":"bob","kind":"handshake","label":"101 Switching"}],"metrics":{"reqs":"0","conns":"100000","delay":"10"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"The gateway remembers who is connected","body":"Open connections make servers stateful. Gateway B holds Bob's socket in memory, and a request cannot reach Bob except through that process. Each idle connection costs roughly 10 to 50 KB of memory (a rule of thumb), so 100,000 connections is a few GB, and a tuned box can hold far more. Scaling means adding gateways and spreading connections across them.","idea":"A connection lives on one specific gateway, so you cannot simply send Bob's message from any server.","caption":"Bob is attached to Gateway B only.","focus":["gw1","gw2"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"FAN-OUT","title":"Delivering across gateways","body":"Alice is connected to Gateway A, Bob to Gateway B. Gateway A has no socket for Bob, so it publishes the message to a pub/sub bus. Every gateway that holds a matching user receives it, and Gateway B writes it down Bob's socket. The chat service also saves it so a reconnecting client can catch up. Bus delivery is usually at-most-once, so the saved copy is the source of truth.","idea":"Gateways share messages through a pub/sub bus, and a durable store backs up the fast path.","caption":"A to bus to B to Bob; the chat service stores it.","focus":["alice","gw1","bus","gw2","bob","chat"],"flows":[{"from":"alice","to":"lb","kind":"async","label":"send frame"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw1","kind":"async","label":"send frame"},{"from":"gw1","to":"bus","kind":"async","label":"publish room:42"},{"from":"bus","to":"chat","kind":"async","label":"persist"},{"from":"bus","to":"gw2","kind":"async","label":"room:42"},{"from":"gw2","to":"lb","kind":"async","label":"frame"},{"from":"lb","to":"bob","kind":"async","label":"new message"}],"metrics":{"delay":"30"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A gateway dies","body":"Gateway B crashes, or is redeployed. Its 50,000 sockets drop at the same instant and every client tries to reconnect at once, a thundering herd that can knock over the survivors. Clients should wait a random, growing delay (exponential backoff with jitter) and send their last seen message id to resume. Planned deploys should drain: stop accepting, then close sockets gradually.","idea":"Connections are fragile, so clients must reconnect with jitter and resume from a message id.","caption":"Gateway B fails; clients rush to reconnect.","focus":["gw2","lb","bob"],"flows":[{"from":"lb","to":"gw2","kind":"fail","label":"crash"},{"from":"bob","to":"lb","kind":"handshake","label":"reconnect, last id"},{"from":"lb","to":"gw1","kind":"handshake","label":"resume"}],"metrics":{"reqs":"50000","conns":"50000","delay":"800"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Choosing the tool","body":"Use short polling when updates are rare and caching matters, such as a job status check. Use SSE for one-way streams like notifications, prices or live logs. Use WebSockets when the client sends often and latency matters, such as chat, games or shared cursors. Whatever you pick, send a heartbeat every 20 to 30 seconds, because proxies and load balancers cut idle connections, often after 60 s.","idea":"Pick the simplest option that matches your direction of traffic, and always heartbeat.","caption":"Same message, three transports, different costs.","focus":["bob","lb","gw2"],"flows":[],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"From asking to being told","body":"HTTP cannot push, so short polling asks often and long polling parks the question. SSE keeps a one-way stream, and WebSockets give a two-way channel. Open connections pin users to a gateway, so messages cross gateways through a pub/sub bus, and clients must reconnect with backoff and resume. Queues, logs and API styles are covered in neighbouring lessons.","idea":"Real-time is a held-open connection plus a way to route messages to the gateway that owns it.","caption":"Connections at the edge, a bus in the middle.","focus":["alice","bob","lb","gw1","gw2","bus","chat"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"reqs":"0","conns":"100000","delay":"30"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"100,000 users short-poll every 10 seconds. Roughly how many requests per second does the server see, and what is the average delivery delay?","options":["About 1,000 req/s and 10 s delay","About 10,000 req/s and 5 s delay","About 10,000 req/s and 10 s delay","About 100,000 req/s and 5 s delay"],"answer":1,"why":"100,000 / 10 s = 10,000 requests per second. A message arrives at a random point in the interval, so the average wait is half of it, 5 s."},{"q":"A live stock ticker only sends prices from server to browser and must reconnect automatically after Wi-Fi drops. Which is the best fit?","options":["Short polling every second","Server-Sent Events","WebSockets with custom reconnect code","Long polling with a 5 s timeout"],"answer":1,"why":"Traffic is one-way, which is exactly what SSE does. EventSource reconnects and resumes with Last-Event-ID built in, so WebSockets add complexity without a benefit."},{"q":"Alice is on Gateway A and Bob on Gateway B. Why can't Gateway A just write to Bob directly?","options":["WebSocket frames cannot carry text","Bob's socket exists only in Gateway B's memory","The load balancer blocks gateway-to-gateway traffic","Gateways may only talk to the database"],"answer":1,"why":"A connection is held by one process. Gateway A has no handle for it, so the message must reach Gateway B, usually through a pub/sub bus."},{"q":"A gateway holding 50,000 sockets restarts and all clients reconnect instantly, overloading the other gateways. What is the standard defence?","options":["Close all sockets after 5 seconds","Reconnect with exponential backoff plus random jitter","Switch every client to short polling","Add a second load balancer"],"answer":1,"why":"Jitter spreads reconnects over time and backoff slows repeated tries, so the herd arrives gradually instead of as one spike."},{"q":"A WebSocket works on your laptop but drops after exactly 60 seconds of silence in production. The most likely cause?","options":["Browsers cap WebSocket age at 60 s","An idle timeout on a proxy or load balancer closing quiet connections","The server ran out of ports","TLS certificates expire every minute"],"answer":2,"why":"Intermediaries close connections that carry no traffic. Periodic ping or heartbeat frames every 20 to 30 seconds keep them open."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Short polling","def":"Client asks on a timer. Simple and cache-friendly, but wasteful when idle, and delay averages half the interval."},{"term":"Long polling","def":"Server holds the request until data exists or a timeout (about 30 s), then client re-asks. Low delay over plain HTTP."},{"term":"SSE","def":"One long HTTP response of text events, server to client only. Auto-reconnect and Last-Event-ID resume are built into EventSource."},{"term":"WebSocket","def":"HTTP Upgrade (101) turns the connection into a full-duplex frame channel. Best when both sides send often."},{"term":"Stateful gateway","def":"A server that holds open sockets in memory. Needs a way to route messages to the gateway owning each user."},{"term":"Fan-out via pub/sub","def":"Gateways subscribe to channels for their users; a publisher reaches every gateway without knowing who is where."},{"term":"Backoff with jitter","def":"Retry after a random, growing delay so mass reconnects do not stampede. Pair with a last-seen id to resume."},{"term":"Heartbeat","def":"Small periodic ping (every 20-30 s) that stops proxy idle timeouts and reveals dead peers early."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"queues","title":"Message Queues","subtitle":"Watch a checkout hand work to a queue, survive a traffic spike and a worker crash, and park a poison message.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a queue decouples producers from consumers and absorbs bursts","What at-least-once delivery means and why duplicates happen","How acknowledgements and the visibility timeout recover from crashed workers","Why consumers must be idempotent","What a dead-letter queue is for and when messages end up there"],"nodes":[{"id":"producer","label":"Checkout service","kind":"service","x":10,"y":52},{"id":"queue","label":"Email queue","kind":"queue","x":38,"y":52},{"id":"w1","label":"Worker A","kind":"worker","x":66,"y":20},{"id":"w2","label":"Worker B","kind":"worker","x":66,"y":52},{"id":"dlq","label":"Dead-letter queue","kind":"queue","x":66,"y":84},{"id":"mail","label":"Email provider","kind":"external","x":90,"y":36}],"edges":[{"from":"producer","to":"queue"},{"from":"queue","to":"w1"},{"from":"queue","to":"w2"},{"from":"w1","to":"mail"},{"from":"w2","to":"mail"},{"from":"queue","to":"dlq"}],"metrics":[{"id":"depth","label":"Queue depth","unit":"msgs"},{"id":"inflight","label":"In flight","unit":"msgs"},{"id":"sent","label":"Emails sent","unit":"emails"},{"id":"dead","label":"Dead letters","unit":"msgs"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Don't make checkout wait","body":"After a purchase, the checkout service must email a receipt. If it calls the email provider directly, the customer waits for that call (often 300 ms to 2 s), and if the provider is down, the order fails over an email. Sending a receipt can happen a few seconds later, so why should checkout depend on it right now?","idea":"Work that does not need to finish before you reply should not block the reply.","caption":"Checkout and email are tied together.","focus":["producer","mail"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"depth":"0","inflight":"0","sent":"0","dead":"0"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Hand the job to a queue","body":"Instead, checkout writes a small message such as 'send receipt for order 81' to a queue and gets a confirmation that the queue stored it, usually within a few milliseconds. The service that writes is the producer. Checkout can now finish immediately, even if no email worker is running at that moment.","idea":"A queue stores messages durably until someone is ready to handle them.","caption":"Producer enqueues; queue confirms it is stored.","focus":["producer","queue"],"flows":[{"from":"producer","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"send receipt #81"},{"from":"queue","to":"producer","kind":"response","label":"stored"}],"metrics":{"depth":"1"}},{"tag":"BUFFER","title":"Absorb a spike","body":"A flash sale sends 300 orders per second for 10 seconds, so 3,000 messages arrive. Each worker can send about 40 emails per second, so two workers handle 80 per second. The queue holds the difference: after the burst about 2,200 messages are waiting, and the backlog drains in roughly half a minute. Without the queue, the email path would have been overwhelmed.","idea":"A queue turns a short burst into a longer, steady flow the consumers can handle.","caption":"Depth climbs during the burst, then drains.","focus":["producer","queue"],"flows":[{"from":"producer","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"300 msgs/s"},{"from":"producer","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"300 msgs/s"}],"metrics":{"depth":"2200"}},{"tag":"DELIVER","title":"Receive hides the message","body":"Worker A asks the queue for a message. The queue does not delete it. It makes it invisible to other workers for a set period called the visibility timeout (30 seconds here) and counts one delivery attempt. The message is lent to Worker A, not given away. Meanwhile Worker B receives different messages, so work is shared.","idea":"Receiving only leases a message; it stays in the queue until acknowledged.","caption":"Worker A holds a lease on one message.","focus":["queue","w1"],"flows":[{"from":"w1","to":"queue","kind":"request","label":"receive"},{"from":"queue","to":"w1","kind":"response","label":"msg + 30s lease"}],"metrics":{"depth":"2199","inflight":"1"}},{"tag":"ACK","title":"Acknowledge on success","body":"Worker A calls the email provider, gets a success reply, and only then sends an acknowledgement (ack) to the queue. The ack deletes the message for good. The order matters: ack after the work is done, never before. Acking early would lose the email if the worker died a moment later.","idea":"Ack means 'done, delete it', so send it only after the work has really succeeded.","caption":"Email sent, then the message is deleted.","focus":["w1","mail","queue"],"flows":[{"from":"w1","to":"mail","kind":"request","label":"send email"},{"from":"mail","to":"w1","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"},{"from":"w1","to":"queue","kind":"response","label":"ack (delete)"}],"metrics":{"inflight":"0","sent":"1"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A worker crashes mid-job","body":"Now Worker A sends the email but crashes before it can ack. The queue never hears back. After the 30 second visibility timeout expires, the message becomes visible again and Worker B receives it. Nothing is lost, which is the good news. The bad news is that the customer's email may now be sent a second time.","idea":"At-least-once delivery means no loss but possible duplicates.","caption":"No ack arrives, so the message is redelivered.","focus":["w1","queue","w2","mail"],"flows":[{"from":"w1","to":"queue","kind":"fail","label":"crash, no ack"},{"from":"queue","to":"w2","kind":"request","label":"redeliver (try 2)"},{"from":"w2","to":"mail","kind":"request","label":"send email"}],"metrics":{"inflight":"1","sent":"2"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Make consumers idempotent","body":"Exactly-once delivery across a network is not something a plain queue can promise, so the safe design is to make handling a message twice harmless. This property is called idempotency. Worker B includes a key such as 'receipt-order-81' with the call, or first checks a table of processed keys, so the repeat becomes a no-op. Also set the visibility timeout longer than your slowest normal job, or a slow worker will cause duplicates by itself.","idea":"Pair at-least-once delivery with idempotent consumers to get an effectively-once result.","caption":"A dedupe key makes the repeat harmless.","focus":["w2","mail"],"flows":[{"from":"w2","to":"mail","kind":"request","label":"key: order-81"},{"from":"mail","to":"w2","kind":"response","label":"already sent"},{"from":"w2","to":"queue","kind":"response","label":"ack (delete)"}],"metrics":{"inflight":"0","sent":"2"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A poison message","body":"One message has a malformed address, so every attempt fails. Without a limit it would be retried forever, wasting capacity and hiding real problems. So the queue is configured with a maximum receive count, say 5. After the fifth failed delivery, the queue moves the message to a dead-letter queue (DLQ), where it waits for a human or a repair job.","idea":"A dead-letter queue parks messages that keep failing so they stop blocking healthy work.","caption":"After 5 failed tries the message is parked.","focus":["queue","dlq","w1"],"flows":[{"from":"queue","to":"w1","kind":"request","label":"try 5 of 5"},{"from":"w1","to":"queue","kind":"fail","label":"error, no ack"},{"from":"queue","to":"dlq","kind":"async","label":"move to DLQ"}],"metrics":{"inflight":"0","dead":"1"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"What the queue bought us","body":"Producers write and move on, consumers pull at their own pace, and the queue buffers any gap between them. Receive leases a message, ack deletes it, and the visibility timeout brings back work from dead workers. That gives at-least-once delivery, so consumers must be idempotent. A DLQ catches messages that never succeed, and a growing DLQ or queue depth is your alarm.","idea":"Decouple with a queue, ack after success, expect duplicates, and watch the DLQ.","caption":"The full path, with a safety net.","focus":["producer","queue","w1","w2","dlq","mail"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"depth":"0","inflight":"0","sent":"2","dead":"1"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A worker receives a message, finishes the job, but crashes just before sending the ack. What happens next with a standard at-least-once queue?","options":["The message is lost because the worker died","The message becomes visible again after the visibility timeout and is delivered to a worker again","The queue detects the crash and deletes the message","The producer is told to resend it"],"answer":1,"why":"Without an ack, the lease simply expires and the message returns to the queue. That is how at-least-once delivery avoids loss, at the cost of possible duplicate processing."},{"q":"Jobs normally take 20 seconds but sometimes 90 seconds. The visibility timeout is 30 seconds. What is the most likely symptom?","options":["Workers will run out of memory","Messages will be dropped silently","Slow jobs will be redelivered to another worker while still running, causing duplicates","The queue will stop accepting new messages"],"answer":2,"why":"A job that outlasts the lease becomes visible to others while the first worker is still going. Set the timeout above your slowest normal job, or extend the lease while working."},{"q":"Why should a consumer acknowledge a message only after the work succeeds?","options":["Acking early would lose the work if the consumer crashes afterwards","Acks are slow, so they should be delayed","The queue refuses early acks","Late acks prevent duplicates"],"answer":0,"why":"Ack deletes the message. If you ack first and crash, no copy remains to retry. Acking last trades possible duplicates for no loss."},{"q":"Your DLQ depth suddenly rises from 0 to 4,000 right after a deploy. What is the best reading?","options":["The queue is healthy; the DLQ is just a cache","Producers are sending too fast","The new consumer code probably fails on messages it should handle, so investigate and then redrive the messages","Visibility timeout is too long"],"answer":2,"why":"Messages only reach the DLQ after repeated failed attempts. A jump right after a deploy points at a consumer bug, and once it is fixed the parked messages can be moved back to the main queue."},{"q":"Orders arrive in a 10 second burst at 300/s. Two workers process 40/s each. What does the queue do for you here?","options":["It makes the workers faster","It holds the backlog (about 2,200 messages) and lets the workers drain it at their own pace","It rejects messages above 80 per second","It guarantees each email is sent once"],"answer":1,"why":"A queue buffers the gap between arrival rate and processing rate. It does not speed up consumers or guarantee exactly-once, but the producer is never blocked by the slow side."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Producer / consumer","def":"The producer writes messages to the queue; the consumer reads and handles them. Neither needs to know the other's speed or whether it is currently running."},{"term":"Visibility timeout","def":"How long a received message stays hidden from other consumers. If no ack arrives in that time, it reappears. Set it above your slowest normal job."},{"term":"Ack","def":"Tells the queue the work is done so the message can be deleted. Send it after success, not before."},{"term":"At-least-once delivery","def":"Every message is delivered one or more times. Nothing is lost, but duplicates can happen after crashes or timeouts."},{"term":"Idempotent consumer","def":"Handling the same message twice has the same effect as once, for example by using a dedupe key or an upsert."},{"term":"Dead-letter queue (DLQ)","def":"Holding area for messages that exceeded the maximum receive count. Alert on its depth, fix the cause, then redrive."},{"term":"Queue depth / age","def":"Messages waiting and the age of the oldest one. Rising depth means consumers are too slow or stuck; use it to autoscale workers."},{"term":"Backpressure","def":"When consumers fall behind, the queue grows. Limit its size or slow producers if the backlog could outlive its usefulness."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"pubsub","title":"Pub/Sub & Event Logs","subtitle":"Publish one event to a partitioned log and watch independent consumer groups read it at their own pace, then replay it.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a topic lets one published event reach many independent subscribers (fan-out)","How partitions split a topic for scale and why order is only guaranteed within one partition","How consumer groups share partitions and how offsets record each group's progress","What consumer lag means and what happens when a consumer crashes mid-stream","Why a retained log can be replayed, unlike a message that is deleted once read"],"nodes":[{"id":"orders","label":"Orders service","kind":"service","x":10,"y":30},{"id":"pay","label":"Payments service","kind":"service","x":10,"y":72},{"id":"p0","label":"Partition 0","kind":"queue","x":38,"y":18},{"id":"p1","label":"Partition 1","kind":"queue","x":38,"y":50},{"id":"p2","label":"Partition 2","kind":"queue","x":38,"y":82},{"id":"b1","label":"Billing worker 1","kind":"worker","x":66,"y":25},{"id":"b2","label":"Billing worker 2","kind":"worker","x":66,"y":75},{"id":"an","label":"Analytics (group B)","kind":"worker","x":88,"y":50}],"edges":[{"from":"orders","to":"p0"},{"from":"orders","to":"p1"},{"from":"pay","to":"p1"},{"from":"pay","to":"p2"},{"from":"p0","to":"b1"},{"from":"p1","to":"b1"},{"from":"p2","to":"b2"},{"from":"p2","to":"b1"},{"from":"p0","to":"an"},{"from":"p1","to":"an"},{"from":"p2","to":"an"}],"metrics":[{"id":"log","label":"Msgs in log","unit":"msgs"},{"id":"lagA","label":"Group A lag","unit":"msgs"},{"id":"lagB","label":"Group B lag","unit":"msgs"},{"id":"rate","label":"Publish rate","unit":"msg/s"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"One event, many readers","body":"When an order is placed, billing must charge it, analytics must count it, and a search index might want it too. If the Orders service called each of them directly, it would need to know every consumer and wait on the slowest one. Pub/sub flips this: the producer announces the event once and does not care who listens.","idea":"Publish-subscribe decouples who produces an event from who consumes it.","caption":"Orders service has one event and several unknown readers.","focus":["orders"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"log":"0","lagA":"0","lagB":"0","rate":"0"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Publish to a topic","body":"A topic is a named stream, such as orders. The producer sends an OrderPlaced event to the topic and gets an acknowledgement; it never learns who reads it. Every subscribed group receives its own copy of the stream. Adding a new reader later needs no change to the producer.","idea":"A topic is a named channel; fan-out means every subscriber group sees every event.","caption":"The event is appended to the orders topic.","focus":["orders","p1"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"p1","kind":"request","label":"OrderPlaced"},{"from":"p1","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"ack"}],"metrics":{"log":"1","rate":"1200"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Partitions split the topic","body":"One machine cannot absorb a busy topic, so the topic is cut into partitions, here three. The producer sets a key, for example the customer id, and hashes it to pick a partition. The same key always lands in the same partition, so one customer's events stay together. Different keys spread across partitions for parallel throughput.","idea":"The key decides the partition: same key, same partition; many keys, spread load.","caption":"Two producers write to three partitions by key hash.","focus":["orders","pay","p0","p1","p2"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"p0","kind":"async","label":"key=u17"},{"from":"orders","to":"p1","kind":"async","label":"key=u42"},{"from":"pay","to":"p1","kind":"async","label":"key=u42"},{"from":"pay","to":"p2","kind":"async","label":"key=u90"}],"metrics":{"log":"9000","rate":"1200"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"A log with offsets","body":"Each partition is an append-only log. Every record gets the next offset: 0, 1, 2, and so on. Reading does not remove a record; the broker simply keeps it until retention expires. Order is guaranteed inside a partition only. Records on different partitions have no defined order relative to each other.","idea":"Ordering is per partition, so put events that must stay ordered under one key.","caption":"Partition 1 holds u42's events in the order they were written.","focus":["p1"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"log":"9000"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Consumer groups share work","body":"Billing runs two workers in one consumer group named billing. The broker assigns each partition to exactly one worker in the group, so no record is handled twice by the group. Worker 1 owns partitions 0 and 1; worker 2 owns partition 2. Consumers pull records by offset at their own speed.","idea":"Within a group each partition has one owner, so partitions cap the group's parallelism.","caption":"Group A splits the three partitions between two workers.","focus":["p0","p1","p2","b1","b2"],"flows":[{"from":"b1","to":"p0","kind":"request","label":"poll from 4210"},{"from":"b1","to":"p1","kind":"request","label":"poll from 3977"},{"from":"b2","to":"p2","kind":"request","label":"poll from 4105"},{"from":"p2","to":"b2","kind":"response","label":"batch of 500"}],"metrics":{"lagA":"800"}},{"tag":"FLOW","title":"Another group, another copy","body":"Analytics reads the same topic in a different group. Groups are independent: each tracks its own offsets, so analytics sees all three partitions in full without affecting billing. This is the fan-out. A slow analytics job cannot slow billing, because neither group removes data for the other.","idea":"Each consumer group has its own position, so groups never compete for the same records.","caption":"Group B reads every partition independently of group A.","focus":["p0","p1","p2","an"],"flows":[{"from":"p0","to":"an","kind":"request","label":"group B"},{"from":"p1","to":"an","kind":"request","label":"group B"},{"from":"p2","to":"an","kind":"request","label":"group B"}],"metrics":{"lagB":"12000"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Offsets and lag","body":"After processing a batch a consumer commits its offset, saying everything before this point is done. Lag is the log end minus the committed offset. Billing at 800 messages behind is normal; analytics at 12,000 behind is catching up from a slower batch job. Rising lag that never falls means consumers are too slow for the publish rate.","idea":"Lag is the key health signal: it shows how far each group is behind the newest record.","caption":"Workers commit offsets; the gap to the log end is lag.","focus":["b1","b2","an"],"flows":[{"from":"b1","to":"p1","kind":"async","label":"commit 3977"},{"from":"b2","to":"p2","kind":"async","label":"commit 4105"}],"metrics":{"lagA":"0","lagB":"9000"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"A worker crashes","body":"Worker 2 dies after processing some records but before committing. The group notices missing heartbeats and rebalances: worker 1 takes over partition 2 and resumes from the last committed offset. Records handled after that commit are delivered again. This is at-least-once delivery, so handlers should be idempotent, meaning a repeat does no extra harm.","idea":"Crashes cause replays from the last commit, so consumers must tolerate duplicates.","caption":"Partition 2 moves to worker 1 after worker 2 fails.","focus":["p2","b2","b1"],"flows":[{"from":"p2","to":"b2","kind":"fail","label":"worker lost"},{"from":"p2","to":"b1","kind":"handshake","label":"reassigned"},{"from":"b1","to":"p2","kind":"request","label":"resume 4105"}],"metrics":{"lagA":"3000"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"Replay the past","body":"Suppose analytics shipped a bug and miscounted for three days. Because the broker retains records for a set time, say seven days, you fix the code and move group B's offsets back to three days ago. It re-reads and rebuilds its results. A classic queue deletes messages once handled, so it cannot do this.","idea":"Retention turns the topic into a replayable history, bounded by the retention window.","caption":"Group B rewinds its offsets and reads old records again.","focus":["p0","p1","p2","an"],"flows":[{"from":"an","to":"p0","kind":"request","label":"seek -3 days"},{"from":"p0","to":"an","kind":"replicate","label":"old records"},{"from":"p1","to":"an","kind":"replicate","label":"old records"},{"from":"p2","to":"an","kind":"replicate","label":"old records"}],"metrics":{"lagB":"2500000"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"The log is the contract","body":"Producers append keyed events to a topic. Partitions give scale, and order holds only within one partition. Each consumer group owns partitions, tracks offsets, and moves at its own speed, so many groups can read the same data. Retention makes replay possible, but plan for duplicates and watch lag.","idea":"Topic plus partitions plus offsets gives fan-out, scale, and replay with one stored log.","caption":"One retained log feeds many independent groups.","focus":["orders","pay","p0","p1","p2","b1","b2","an"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"log":"9000","lagA":"0","lagB":"0","rate":"1200"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Events for customer 42 sometimes reach a consumer out of order, even though one service publishes them in order. Which cause fits best?","options":["The broker sorts records by size","The consumer group has too many workers","The events were published without a key, so they spread over several partitions","Offsets reset to zero after each commit"],"answer":2,"why":"Order is guaranteed only within a partition. Without a consistent key, one customer's events land on different partitions and can be read in any relative order."},{"q":"A topic has 4 partitions and a consumer group runs 6 workers. What happens?","options":["All 6 share the work equally","4 workers each own a partition and 2 sit idle","Each record is delivered to all 6 workers","The broker creates 2 more partitions"],"answer":1,"why":"Within a group each partition is owned by one consumer, so useful parallelism is capped at the partition count. The extra workers are idle standbys."},{"q":"A worker handles a record, then crashes before committing its offset. After the rebalance, what should you expect?","options":["The record is lost for good","The record is silently skipped","The group stops until an operator intervenes","The record is delivered again, so the handler should tolerate repeats"],"answer":3,"why":"The new owner resumes from the last committed offset, so work done after that commit is redone. Idempotent handlers make this harmless."},{"q":"A new team wants every order from the last two days, and retention is seven days. What makes this possible?","options":["Reading does not delete records, so a new group can start from an earlier offset","The producer must republish the events","The broker copies messages to each new subscriber when it joins","Only the original group may read old offsets"],"answer":0,"why":"The log keeps records until retention expires and each group tracks its own offsets, so a new group can seek back and read the retained history."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Topic","def":"A named stream of events. Producers append to it; any number of consumer groups can subscribe and each gets the full stream."},{"term":"Partition","def":"One ordered, append-only slice of a topic. More partitions allow more parallel writers and readers."},{"term":"Message key","def":"Value hashed to choose a partition. Same key means same partition, which preserves per-key order."},{"term":"Offset","def":"A record's position number within its partition. Consumers commit offsets to remember their progress."},{"term":"Consumer group","def":"Consumers that share a topic's partitions; each partition has one owner in the group. Different groups are independent."},{"term":"Consumer lag","def":"Log end offset minus the group's committed offset. Growing lag means consumers cannot keep up."},{"term":"Retention","def":"How long, or how much, data the broker keeps, for example 7 days. Replay is only possible inside this window."},{"term":"Rebalance","def":"Reassigning partitions when consumers join or leave. Processing pauses briefly and records may be redelivered."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"batch-stream","title":"Batch vs Stream Processing","subtitle":"Watch one shop's click events get turned into hourly revenue two ways, and see what each way costs you.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How a MapReduce-style batch job splits, shuffles and reduces a big dataset","How a stream processor keeps running state and emits results continuously","What tumbling, sliding and session windows are and when to use each","Why late events happen and how watermarks decide when a window closes","How lambda and kappa architectures trade complexity for freshness and simplicity"],"nodes":[{"id":"src","label":"Event sources","kind":"user","x":7,"y":50},{"id":"log","label":"Event log","kind":"queue","x":28,"y":50},{"id":"lake","label":"Data lake","kind":"storage","x":28,"y":86},{"id":"batch","label":"Batch job","kind":"worker","x":50,"y":18},{"id":"stream","label":"Stream processor","kind":"worker","x":50,"y":56},{"id":"serving","label":"Serving store","kind":"db","x":72,"y":38},{"id":"dash","label":"Dashboard","kind":"client","x":93,"y":38}],"edges":[{"from":"src","to":"log"},{"from":"log","to":"lake"},{"from":"lake","to":"batch"},{"from":"log","to":"stream"},{"from":"batch","to":"serving"},{"from":"stream","to":"serving"},{"from":"serving","to":"dash"}],"metrics":[{"id":"rate","label":"Events in","unit":"/s"},{"id":"lag","label":"Result lag","unit":"s"},{"id":"late","label":"Late events","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Same events, two ways to answer","body":"A shop's site and apps emit about 5,000 events per second (clicks, carts, orders). That is roughly 430 million a day, or about 200 GB at 500 bytes each. The business wants revenue per hour on a dashboard. Do you let data pile up and crunch it in big jobs, or process each event the moment it arrives?","idea":"Batch processes a finished pile of data; stream processes data that never finishes.","caption":"Events flow from sources into a durable log.","focus":["src","log"],"flows":[{"from":"src","to":"log","kind":"request","label":"click, order"}],"metrics":{"rate":"5000"}},{"tag":"BATCH","title":"Batch: collect, then compute","body":"Batch works on a bounded dataset: a fixed set of files with a known start and end. Here the log is copied into a data lake as hourly files. Nothing is computed yet, the data just accumulates. Because the input is complete and unchanging, a failed job can simply be rerun on the same files and give the same answer.","idea":"A bounded input is finished and repeatable, so reruns are safe and simple.","caption":"Events are archived as hourly files.","focus":["log","lake"],"flows":[{"from":"log","to":"lake","kind":"async","label":"hourly files"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"MAPREDUCE","title":"MapReduce: split, shuffle, reduce","body":"The batch job cuts the day's 200 GB into 128 MB splits, about 1,600 pieces. Map tasks run in parallel on those splits and emit (hour, amount) pairs. The shuffle moves all pairs with the same key to one reducer, and each reducer sums its keys. Results land in the serving store. The hour must close and the job must finish first, so the dashboard can be an hour or more behind.","idea":"Batch scales by running many independent map tasks, then grouping by key; its cost is waiting.","caption":"Parallel map and reduce tasks write hourly totals.","focus":["lake","batch","serving"],"flows":[{"from":"lake","to":"batch","kind":"request","label":"1,600 splits"},{"from":"batch","to":"serving","kind":"response","label":"revenue by hour"}],"metrics":{"lag":"4200"}},{"tag":"STREAM","title":"Stream: compute as events arrive","body":"A stream processor reads the log continuously and treats the input as unbounded: it never ends, so there is no final answer, only a running one. It keeps state in memory, such as a running sum per hour, updates it on every event, and pushes fresh totals to the serving store. The dashboard is now a couple of seconds behind instead of an hour.","idea":"Streaming trades the simplicity of finished input for results that are seconds old.","caption":"Each event updates running totals immediately.","focus":["log","stream","serving","dash"],"flows":[{"from":"log","to":"stream","kind":"request","label":"each event"},{"from":"stream","to":"serving","kind":"async","label":"running sums"},{"from":"serving","to":"dash","kind":"response","label":"fresh totals"}],"metrics":{"lag":"2"}},{"tag":"WINDOWS","title":"Windows: cutting an endless stream","body":"To get an hourly number from a stream you group events into windows. A tumbling window is fixed and non-overlapping, like each clock hour. A sliding window overlaps, such as the last hour recomputed every 5 minutes, so each event lands in 12 windows. A session window groups one user's activity and closes after a gap, say 30 quiet minutes.","idea":"Pick the window shape from the question: fixed buckets, rolling view, or per-user activity.","caption":"The processor groups events into windows by time.","focus":["log","stream"],"flows":[{"from":"log","to":"stream","kind":"request","label":"10:00-11:00 bucket"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"LATE EVENTS","title":"Late events and watermarks","body":"A phone loses signal and sends an order stamped 10:58 only at 11:03. Windows should use event time (when it happened), not processing time (when it arrived), or this order lands in the wrong hour. But the processor needs a rule for when a window is done. A watermark says 'I assume no event older than this is still coming', often the newest event time minus an allowed lateness like 2 minutes. Anything later is dropped, sent to a side output, or used to correct the result.","idea":"A watermark is a guess about completeness: shorter waits give faster results but more late drops.","caption":"An order stamped 10:58 arrives after the window closed.","focus":["src","log","stream"],"flows":[{"from":"src","to":"log","kind":"async","label":"t=10:58, at 11:03"},{"from":"log","to":"stream","kind":"request","label":"late event"},{"from":"stream","to":"serving","kind":"fail","label":"after watermark"}],"metrics":{"late":"0.2"}},{"tag":"LAMBDA","title":"Lambda: run both side by side","body":"Early on, stream results were approximate and could be lost, so teams ran two paths over the same events. A speed layer (stream) gives quick but rough numbers. A batch layer recomputes accurate numbers later and overwrites them. The serving store merges the two. It works, but you now write, test and debug the same business logic twice, in two systems, and the results must agree.","idea":"Lambda buys accuracy plus speed at the price of maintaining two codebases that must match.","caption":"Batch and stream both write into one serving store.","focus":["log","lake","batch","stream","serving"],"flows":[{"from":"log","to":"stream","kind":"request","label":"speed layer"},{"from":"lake","to":"batch","kind":"request","label":"batch layer"},{"from":"stream","to":"serving","kind":"async","label":"fast, rough"},{"from":"batch","to":"serving","kind":"response","label":"slow, exact"}],"metrics":{"lag":"2"}},{"tag":"KAPPA","title":"Kappa: one stream, replay for fixes","body":"Modern stream engines handle state, checkpoints and event time well, so kappa drops the batch path. The log is the source of truth and keeps data for days or months. To fix a bug or change logic, deploy the new version, replay the log from the earliest offset into a new output table, then switch readers to it. A batch is just a stream that ends. The catch is retention: replay only works if the log still holds the history.","idea":"Kappa keeps one codebase by treating reprocessing as a replay of the log.","caption":"New code replays the log from offset 0.","focus":["log","stream","serving"],"flows":[{"from":"log","to":"stream","kind":"replicate","label":"replay offset 0"},{"from":"stream","to":"serving","kind":"async","label":"new table"}],"metrics":{}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The stream job crashes","body":"The processor dies at 11:20 with an hour of state in memory. A batch task would just rerun. A stream job instead takes periodic checkpoints: a snapshot of its state plus the log offset it had reached, say every minute. On restart it reloads the last checkpoint and re-reads from that offset. Some events are processed twice, so the output write must be idempotent (safe to repeat) or transactional to avoid double counting.","idea":"Checkpoint plus replay recovers a stream job; idempotent output keeps the replay from double counting.","caption":"The job restores a checkpoint and re-reads the log.","focus":["log","stream","serving"],"flows":[{"from":"stream","to":"serving","kind":"fail","label":"crash 11:20"},{"from":"log","to":"stream","kind":"replicate","label":"from checkpoint"}],"metrics":{"lag":"70"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Choosing between them","body":"Use batch when freshness of hours is fine and you need cheap, repeatable crunching of huge history, such as nightly reports or model training. Use stream when decisions need seconds, such as fraud checks or live dashboards. Streams need windows, event time and watermarks to cope with endless, out-of-order input. Prefer one codebase (kappa style) over lambda when your engine can replay the log.","idea":"Choose by how stale an answer you can tolerate, then pay for the extra machinery only if you need it.","caption":"Batch is exact and slow; stream is fast and needs care.","focus":["src","log","lake","batch","stream","serving","dash"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"lag":"2"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"An order happened at 10:58 on a phone with no signal and reached the stream processor at 11:03. Why should hourly windows use event time here?","options":["Event time is always faster to compute","It removes the need for a log","The order belongs to the 10:00-11:00 hour, and arrival time would put it in the wrong one","Processing time cannot be used with windows"],"answer":2,"why":"Event time records when something actually happened, so results stay correct despite delays. Processing time would count the order in 11:00-12:00 and change results whenever the pipeline is slow."},{"q":"What is the main ongoing cost of a lambda architecture?","options":["The log cannot store more than a day of data","The same business logic must be written and kept consistent in a batch system and a stream system","Batch jobs cannot read from the data lake","Serving stores cannot accept two writers"],"answer":1,"why":"Lambda runs two code paths over the same events. Any logic change must be made twice, and the two paths can disagree, which is the main reason teams move to kappa."},{"q":"Which workload fits batch processing best?","options":["Recomputing a product-recommendation model over three years of purchase history each night","Blocking a stolen card within one second of a purchase","A live dashboard of orders in the last minute","Alerting when a sensor reads above a threshold"],"answer":0,"why":"Large, bounded history with no need for second-level freshness is where batch is cheapest and simplest. The other three need results in seconds."},{"q":"You find a bug in a kappa pipeline's revenue logic from last week. What is the sound fix?","options":["Patch the running job and accept that last week's numbers stay wrong","Delete the log and wait for new events","Convert the pipeline to lambda immediately","Deploy the fixed job, replay the log from the needed offset into a new output, then switch readers to it"],"answer":3,"why":"Kappa treats reprocessing as replay. This works only if the log's retention still covers the affected period, so retention is a design decision."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Bounded vs unbounded","def":"Bounded data has a known end (a day's files), so batch can finish. Unbounded data never ends (live events), so stream jobs run forever."},{"term":"MapReduce","def":"Map tasks process input splits in parallel and emit key/value pairs; the shuffle groups equal keys; reduce tasks combine each group's values."},{"term":"Tumbling window","def":"Fixed-size, non-overlapping buckets, such as each clock hour. Every event belongs to exactly one window."},{"term":"Sliding window","def":"Fixed size but advancing in smaller steps (1 hour every 5 minutes). Windows overlap, so each event counts in several."},{"term":"Session window","def":"Groups one key's events until a gap of inactivity (for example 30 minutes). Length varies per user."},{"term":"Event time vs processing time","def":"Event time is when it happened; processing time is when the system sees it. Use event time for correct results under delays."},{"term":"Watermark","def":"A moving estimate that no earlier events are still coming, often newest event time minus allowed lateness. It closes windows; later events are dropped or handled separately."},{"term":"Lambda vs kappa","def":"Lambda runs batch and stream paths and merges them. Kappa uses only a stream over a replayable log and reprocesses by replaying. Checkpoints plus idempotent output make recovery safe."}],"isNew":false},{"slug":"service-discovery","title":"Service Discovery","subtitle":"Watch an Orders service find, health-check and call a Payments service whose instances keep changing addresses.","minutes":10,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["Why hard-coded addresses break when instances autoscale, crash and redeploy","How registration, heartbeats and TTLs keep a registry honest","The difference between client-side and server-side discovery, and what each costs","How DNS-based discovery works and why caching makes it sluggish","What happens to callers during the stale window after an instance dies"],"nodes":[{"id":"orders","label":"Orders service","kind":"service","x":10,"y":48},{"id":"dns","label":"Internal DNS","kind":"dns","x":38,"y":48},{"id":"registry","label":"Service registry","kind":"db","x":38,"y":14},{"id":"lb","label":"Load balancer","kind":"lb","x":38,"y":82},{"id":"pay1","label":"payments-1","kind":"server","x":74,"y":14},{"id":"pay2","label":"payments-2","kind":"server","x":74,"y":48},{"id":"pay3","label":"payments-3","kind":"server","x":74,"y":82}],"edges":[{"from":"orders","to":"registry"},{"from":"orders","to":"dns"},{"from":"orders","to":"lb"},{"from":"orders","to":"pay2"},{"from":"orders","to":"pay3"},{"from":"pay1","to":"registry"},{"from":"pay2","to":"registry"},{"from":"pay3","to":"registry"},{"from":"lb","to":"registry"},{"from":"lb","to":"pay1"},{"from":"lb","to":"pay3"},{"from":"dns","to":"registry"}],"metrics":[{"id":"instances","label":"Registered","unit":"inst"},{"id":"extra","label":"Extra hop","unit":"ms"},{"id":"stale","label":"Stale window","unit":"s"},{"id":"failed","label":"Failed calls","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"Where is Payments right now?","body":"Orders must call Payments. In a fixed setup you would write payments-1's address into a config file. But in a cloud setup instances are replaced on every deploy, added when traffic grows, and removed when they crash, and each new one gets a new IP address. A config file cannot keep up. Service discovery is how callers find the current, healthy instances by name.","idea":"Instances are temporary, so callers need a live lookup from a stable name to current addresses.","caption":"Orders knows a name, not any address.","focus":["orders"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"instances":"0","failed":"0"}},{"tag":"REGISTER","title":"Instances announce themselves","body":"When an instance finishes starting and is ready for traffic, it tells the registry its service name and address, such as payments at 10.0.1.4:8080. Some systems have the instance register itself; others have a platform agent do it for it. Registering only when ready matters: an instance still loading its cache should not get traffic yet.","idea":"A registry is a live map from service name to the addresses that are ready right now.","caption":"Three Payments instances register.","focus":["pay1","pay2","pay3","registry"],"flows":[{"from":"pay1","to":"registry","kind":"request","label":"register 10.0.1.4"},{"from":"pay2","to":"registry","kind":"request","label":"register 10.0.1.5"},{"from":"pay3","to":"registry","kind":"request","label":"register 10.0.1.6"}],"metrics":{"instances":"3"}},{"tag":"HEALTH","title":"Staying registered means proving you are alive","body":"A crashed instance cannot politely deregister, so the registry needs another way to know. Each instance sends a heartbeat every 5 seconds, or the registry polls a health endpoint instead. After 3 missed beats (15 seconds here, a typical choice) the entry is removed. Short intervals detect failure faster but add load and risk flapping on a brief network blip.","idea":"Heartbeats with a timeout turn silence into a removal, so dead instances age out automatically.","caption":"Healthy instances keep sending heartbeats.","focus":["pay1","pay2","pay3","registry"],"flows":[{"from":"pay1","to":"registry","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat"},{"from":"pay2","to":"registry","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat"},{"from":"pay3","to":"registry","kind":"async","label":"heartbeat"}],"metrics":{"stale":"15"}},{"tag":"CLIENT-SIDE","title":"The caller picks the instance","body":"In client-side discovery, Orders asks the registry for the list of healthy Payments addresses, keeps it in memory, and chooses one itself, for example round-robin. The call then goes straight to that instance with no middle hop. The cost is that every client in every language needs this lookup, caching and balancing logic, and each one needs to be kept up to date.","idea":"Client-side discovery saves a network hop but spreads discovery logic into every caller.","caption":"Orders looks up the list, then calls payments-2 directly.","focus":["orders","registry","pay2"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"registry","kind":"request","label":"payments?"},{"from":"registry","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"3 addresses"},{"from":"orders","to":"pay2","kind":"request","label":"POST /charge"},{"from":"pay2","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"extra":"0"}},{"tag":"SERVER-SIDE","title":"A proxy picks for the caller","body":"In server-side discovery, Orders calls one stable address: a load balancer or proxy. That proxy reads the registry, picks a healthy instance and forwards the request. Callers stay simple and need no discovery code. The trade-off is an extra hop, often around 1 ms inside one data center, and the proxy becomes something you must keep highly available.","idea":"Server-side discovery keeps callers simple but adds a hop and a component that must not fail.","caption":"Orders calls the load balancer, which routes to a healthy instance.","focus":["orders","lb","registry","pay1"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"lb","kind":"request","label":"POST /charge"},{"from":"registry","to":"lb","kind":"async","label":"healthy list"},{"from":"lb","to":"pay1","kind":"request","label":"POST /charge"},{"from":"pay1","to":"lb","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"},{"from":"lb","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"extra":"1"}},{"tag":"DNS","title":"Using DNS as the registry","body":"The simplest discovery tool is DNS. Payments gets a name such as payments.internal, and the registry or platform keeps it pointing at the healthy addresses, returning several A records. Every language already knows how to resolve names, so no new client library is needed. The weakness is caching: answers live for their TTL (say 30 seconds), and some runtimes cache even longer, so clients can keep using old addresses.","idea":"DNS discovery works everywhere, but caching makes it slow to notice change.","caption":"DNS returns the current addresses, valid for the TTL.","focus":["orders","dns","registry"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"dns","kind":"request","label":"A payments?"},{"from":"dns","to":"registry","kind":"request","label":"healthy?"},{"from":"registry","to":"dns","kind":"response","label":"3 addresses"},{"from":"dns","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"3 IPs, TTL 30"}],"metrics":{"stale":"30"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"An instance dies","body":"Now payments-3 crashes without warning. It stops sending heartbeats, but the registry only removes it after 15 seconds, and a client that cached the list may keep it even longer. During that window, about one call in three, the share that round-robin sends to the dead instance, fails. Retrying the call on a different instance hides most of this from users.","idea":"Detection is never instant, so callers need timeouts and retries to cover the stale window.","caption":"Calls to the dead instance fail until it is removed.","focus":["orders","pay3","registry"],"flows":[{"from":"pay3","to":"registry","kind":"fail","label":"no heartbeat"},{"from":"orders","to":"pay3","kind":"fail","label":"connect timeout"}],"metrics":{"instances":"2","failed":"33","stale":"15"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The registry itself is down","body":"What if the registry becomes unreachable? A client that cached the last good list can keep calling instances, which is why caches are a feature and not just a shortcut. New instances will not be found and dead ones will not be removed until the registry returns, but existing traffic keeps flowing. Registries are therefore built as replicated clusters, and callers should fail open to their cached list.","idea":"Run the registry as a cluster and let callers keep serving from their last known list.","caption":"Orders keeps calling payments-2 from its cached list.","focus":["orders","registry","pay2"],"flows":[{"from":"orders","to":"registry","kind":"fail","label":"unreachable"},{"from":"orders","to":"pay2","kind":"request","label":"cached address"},{"from":"pay2","to":"orders","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"failed":"0"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"Name in, healthy address out","body":"Instances register when ready and prove they are alive with heartbeats. Callers find them through the registry directly (client-side), through a proxy (server-side), or through DNS. Every approach has a delay between a failure and everyone noticing, so timeouts, retries and caches that survive a registry outage are part of the design. Kubernetes, for instance, combines DNS names with readiness checks to do all of this for you.","idea":"Discovery maps names to healthy addresses; the real design work is handling staleness.","caption":"Register, heartbeat, look up, call, retry.","focus":["orders","dns","registry","lb","pay1","pay2","pay3"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"instances":"3","extra":"1","stale":"15","failed":"0"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"A service sends heartbeats every 5 seconds and is removed after 3 missed beats. It crashes. About how long can the registry still list it?","options":["Under 1 second","About 5 seconds","Up to about 15 seconds","Until someone deregisters it by hand"],"answer":2,"why":"A crash gives no goodbye. The registry waits for 3 missed 5-second beats, so the dead entry can remain for up to roughly 15 seconds."},{"q":"Your team uses five languages and wants no discovery code in callers. Which fits best, and what do you pay?","options":["Client-side discovery; you pay with a stale cache","Server-side discovery; you pay an extra hop and must keep the proxy available","Hard-coded IPs; you pay nothing","Heartbeats; you pay with longer TTLs"],"answer":1,"why":"A proxy keeps logic out of every client, at the cost of one more hop and one more component that must stay highly available."},{"q":"Users report errors for about a minute after each deploy removes old instances, though DNS records update within seconds. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The registry is storing too many instances","Clients cache DNS answers longer than the TTL, so they keep calling old addresses","TLS handshakes are too slow","The load balancer has too many healthy backends"],"answer":1,"why":"Some runtimes cache DNS beyond the TTL, so they keep using addresses that no longer exist. Check resolver caching and add retries."},{"q":"The registry goes down for two minutes. Orders cached the instance list 10 seconds ago. What should happen?","options":["Orders stops calling Payments until the registry is back","Orders keeps calling instances from its cached list","Payments instances shut themselves down","Orders rebuilds the list using a broadcast"],"answer":1,"why":"Existing traffic can continue from the last known list. Only changes, such as new or dead instances, go unnoticed until the registry returns."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Service registry","def":"A store mapping a service name to the addresses of its currently healthy instances. Examples: Consul, etcd, Eureka, the Kubernetes API."},{"term":"Heartbeat and TTL","def":"Instances report regularly; if reports stop for a set time, the entry is dropped. Shorter means faster detection but more load and false removals."},{"term":"Client-side discovery","def":"The caller queries the registry and picks an instance itself. No extra hop, but every client needs the logic."},{"term":"Server-side discovery","def":"The caller uses a stable proxy address, and the proxy looks up and picks an instance. Simpler clients, one extra hop."},{"term":"DNS-based discovery","def":"A service name resolves to the current addresses. Works with every client, but TTLs and client caches delay updates."},{"term":"Readiness vs liveness","def":"Liveness asks whether the process is alive; readiness asks whether it can take traffic now. Register on readiness, restart on liveness."},{"term":"Stale window","def":"The time between an instance failing and callers stopping use of it. Cover it with timeouts, retries and sensible TTLs."}],"isNew":false}]},{"id":"reliability","title":"Reliability","lessons":[{"slug":"timeouts-retries","title":"Timeouts & Retries","subtitle":"Watch a checkout service survive a flaky payment API, then watch careless retries knock that API over.","minutes":12,"level":"Intermediate","learn":["How to pick a timeout from observed latency instead of guessing","How exponential backoff with jitter spaces retries out","Why layered retries multiply load into a retry storm","How retry budgets and deadlines cap the damage","Which failures are safe to retry and which are not"],"nodes":[{"id":"client","label":"Mobile client","kind":"client","x":8,"y":50},{"id":"checkout","label":"Checkout service","kind":"service","x":32,"y":50},{"id":"payment","label":"Payment API","kind":"service","x":57,"y":50},{"id":"db","label":"Payments DB","kind":"db","x":84,"y":50},{"id":"others","label":"Other callers","kind":"client","x":57,"y":14},{"id":"cache","label":"Cached price list","kind":"cache","x":32,"y":86}],"edges":[{"from":"client","to":"checkout"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment"},{"from":"payment","to":"db"},{"from":"others","to":"payment"},{"from":"checkout","to":"cache"}],"metrics":[{"id":"timeout","label":"Timeout","unit":"ms"},{"id":"attempts","label":"Attempts","unit":"tries"},{"id":"load","label":"Payment load","unit":"req/s"},{"id":"ok","label":"Success","unit":"%"}],"steps":[{"tag":"QUESTION","title":"The payment API gets flaky","body":"A checkout service calls a payment API. Normally it answers in about 80 ms, and the 99th percentile (p99, the slowest 1 in 100 calls) is 400 ms. Today about 30 percent of calls fail or hang for a moment. What should checkout do: wait forever, give up at once, or try again? Each choice has a cost, and this lesson walks through them.","idea":"Any call over a network can be slow, fail, or never answer, so the caller needs a plan for each case.","caption":"Checkout depends on a payment API that now fails 3 calls in 10.","focus":["checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"charge $42"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"fail","label":"502 / no reply"}],"metrics":{"timeout":"none","attempts":"1","load":"600","ok":"70"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Set a timeout first","body":"With no timeout, a hung call holds a thread and a connection until the OS gives up, often after minutes. Enough hung calls and checkout itself runs out of threads. A timeout caps the wait. A common rule of thumb is to set it a little above the dependency's p99, so 500 ms here, then adjust from real data. Too low and you abort calls that would have worked.","idea":"A timeout turns an unbounded wait into a bounded one; base it on measured p99, not on hope.","caption":"Checkout stops waiting at 500 ms and frees its thread.","focus":["checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"charge $42"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"fail","label":"timeout @500ms"}],"metrics":{"timeout":"500","attempts":"1","ok":"70"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Retry the transient failure","body":"Many failures are brief: a dropped packet, a server restarting, a momentary overload. Trying again often works. If each attempt fails 30 percent of the time and failures are independent, three attempts all fail only 0.3 x 0.3 x 0.3, about 3 percent. Success climbs from 70 to roughly 97 percent. This only holds when the cause is temporary. A dead dependency fails every time.","idea":"Retries convert short-lived faults into successes, but only if the next attempt has a real chance of working.","caption":"A second attempt reaches the Payment API and succeeds.","focus":["checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"attempt 1"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"fail","label":"503"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"attempt 2"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"attempts":"3","load":"780","ok":"97"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Back off exponentially","body":"Retrying instantly hammers a service that is already struggling. Exponential backoff waits longer after each failure: for example 100 ms, 200 ms, 400 ms, 800 ms, doubling until a cap such as 2 seconds. The growing gap gives the dependency room to recover. It also keeps one stubborn caller from spending its whole effort on a service that is down.","idea":"Doubling the wait after every failure gives a struggling service breathing room.","caption":"Each retry waits twice as long as the previous one.","focus":["checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"try 1"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"after 100 ms"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"after 200 ms"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"after 400 ms"}],"metrics":{"attempts":"4"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Add jitter","body":"Suppose the payment API blips for one second and 5,000 callers all fail together. With plain backoff they all retry at exactly 100 ms, then all at 200 ms, arriving in synchronized waves. Jitter adds randomness. With full jitter each caller sleeps a random time between 0 and its current backoff value, so the retries spread into a smooth trickle instead of spikes.","idea":"Jitter breaks up synchronized retry waves; always add it to backoff.","caption":"Many callers retry at random moments instead of all at once.","focus":["others","checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"others","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"retry +37 ms"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"retry +91 ms"},{"from":"others","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"retry +64 ms"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"load":"700","ok":"97"}},{"tag":"WHAT IF","title":"The retry storm","body":"Now the payment API is truly overloaded: it slows down and capacity halves. Callers time out and retry. Worse, both the client app and checkout each allow 3 attempts, so one user tap can become up to 9 calls to payment. Normal load of 600 requests per second balloons to about 5,400, which slows payment further and causes more retries. The helper has become the attacker.","idea":"Retries at every layer multiply: 3 attempts across 2 layers is up to 9 calls per user action.","caption":"Layered retries pile load onto the already struggling Payment API.","focus":["client","checkout","payment","others"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"checkout","kind":"request","label":"3 tries"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"3 x 3 tries"},{"from":"others","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"more retries"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"fail","label":"overloaded"}],"metrics":{"attempts":"9","load":"5400","ok":"12"}},{"tag":"TRADE-OFF","title":"Cap it with a retry budget","body":"A retry budget limits retries as a share of total traffic, for example no more than 10 percent of recent requests may be retries. It is usually a token bucket: each first attempt earns a fraction of a token, and each retry spends one. When the bucket is empty, the call fails right away. During a real outage load rises only about 10 percent, so the dependency can recover.","idea":"A retry budget puts a hard ceiling on extra load no matter how many things fail.","caption":"Once the budget is spent, checkout fails fast instead of retrying.","focus":["checkout","payment","cache"],"flows":[{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"first attempts"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"fail","label":"503"},{"from":"checkout","to":"cache","kind":"request","label":"budget empty"},{"from":"cache","to":"checkout","kind":"response","label":"fallback"}],"metrics":{"attempts":"1.1","load":"660","ok":"70"}},{"tag":"CONCEPT","title":"Retry only what is safe, within a deadline","body":"Retry connection errors, timeouts, 429 and 503. Do not retry 400 or 404, because the same request fails again. Retrying a charge can bill twice unless the operation is idempotent, which the Idempotency lesson covers. Also give the whole user action a deadline, say 2 seconds, and pass the remaining time downstream so no layer keeps retrying after the user has left.","idea":"Retry only transient, safe errors, and stop when the overall deadline runs out.","caption":"The remaining time shrinks as the request moves downstream.","focus":["client","checkout","payment"],"flows":[{"from":"client","to":"checkout","kind":"request","label":"deadline 2000ms"},{"from":"checkout","to":"payment","kind":"request","label":"left: 1400ms"},{"from":"payment","to":"checkout","kind":"response","label":"200 OK"}],"metrics":{"timeout":"500","attempts":"2","load":"660","ok":"96"}},{"tag":"RECAP","title":"A safe retry recipe","body":"Set a timeout near p99. Retry only transient errors, with exponential backoff and full jitter. Retry at one layer, not at every layer. Cap total retries with a budget of around 10 percent, and bound everything with an overall deadline. Done well, retries hide small faults. Done badly, they turn a small fault into an outage.","idea":"Timeouts bound the wait, backoff and jitter space retries, and budgets and deadlines bound the damage.","caption":"All pieces together keep the system stable when payment stumbles.","focus":["client","checkout","payment","db","cache"],"flows":[],"metrics":{"timeout":"500","attempts":"1.1","load":"660","ok":"96"}}],"quiz":[{"q":"Backoff starts at 100 ms and doubles each time, with full jitter. Roughly how long might a caller wait before its 3rd retry?","options":["Exactly 400 ms","A random time between 0 and 400 ms","A random time between 400 and 800 ms","A random time between 0 and 100 ms"],"answer":1,"why":"The delays are 100, 200, then 400 ms before the 3rd retry. Full jitter picks a random value between 0 and that ceiling."},{"q":"A user taps Pay. The app, the checkout service, and the payment gateway each make up to 3 attempts. What is the worst-case number of calls reaching the payment gateway's backend?","options":["3","9","12","27"],"answer":3,"why":"Attempts multiply across layers: 3 x 3 x 3 = 27. This is why retries should usually happen at only one layer."},{"q":"Which failure is the best candidate for an automatic retry?","options":["A 503 response from an idempotent GET request","A 400 response saying the card number is malformed","A 404 for an order id that does not exist","A 403 because the API key is revoked"],"answer":0,"why":"A 503 is often temporary, so a later attempt may succeed. The other errors are caused by the request or credentials and will fail the same way every time."},{"q":"Traffic is 1,000 requests per second and the dependency is completely down. With a retry budget of 10 percent, about how many calls per second does it receive?","options":["3,000","2,000","1,100","4,000"],"answer":2,"why":"Every first attempt still goes out (1,000) and retries are capped at 10 percent of that (about 100), for roughly 1,100. Without a budget, 3 attempts each would send about 3,000."},{"q":"Why does a timeout set far below the dependency's p99 cause trouble?","options":["It makes backoff delays too long","It disables jitter","It aborts calls that would have succeeded and triggers extra retries","It prevents the connection from being reused"],"answer":2,"why":"If the timeout is under p99, a large share of healthy but slow calls get cancelled and retried, which adds load and wastes the work already done."}],"cheatsheet":[{"term":"Timeout","def":"Maximum time to wait for a reply. Set slightly above the dependency's measured p99, and set one on every network call."},{"term":"Exponential backoff","def":"Wait base x 2^n before retry n, up to a cap. Example: 100, 200, 400, 800 ms, capped near 2 s."},{"term":"Full jitter","def":"Sleep a random time between 0 and the current backoff ceiling. Spreads out synchronized retries from many callers."},{"term":"Retry storm","def":"Retries add load to a struggling service, which fails more calls, which causes more retries. Layered retries multiply it."},{"term":"Retry budget","def":"A cap on retries as a share of traffic, often about 10 percent. Past the cap, fail fast instead of retrying."},{"term":"Deadline","def":"One time limit for the whole user action, passed downstream so each layer knows how much time is left."},{"term":"Safe to retry","def":"Timeouts, connection errors, 429, 503, and idempotent operations. Never blindly retry 4xx client errors or non-idempotent writes."}],"isNew":false}]}]};
